        StaticBook("Cleric's Journal", "Valmir_", "A Cleric’s Journal\n\nBy Ari’ker Telios\n1440, Malin’s Welcome\n\nIt has grown cold. Very cold. I am not sure how much longer I can keep in this cave.\n\nThe dark creatures are relentless with their attacks. As of now I have been able to prevent them from getting to the outside world.\nI have received no help from any other Clerics or Paladins. We are all but dead, the order is scattered. I have had to retreat from the main chambers and had to leave some valuable artefacts, although I shall try to retrieve these soon.\n\nJournal 94 (no recorded date)\n\nThese creatures – they are coming up. They have breached through the barrier, and I am unable to hold them off on my own. This may be my last day, but I will fight the stop these creatures. \n\nWhoever finds this journal,\nleave while there is still hope.\n\nI pray that Tahariae will aid me now, as I have lost all faith due to recent events.", ""),
        StaticBook("Cloud Temple", "Leyu", "Cloud Temple\nThe cloud temple is a historical fortress that rest in peace, away from all sign of life in Aegis. It is the primary spot where the king's or Oren would reside in time of great need, or in severe warfare conditions between an unfightable\nfortress. Cloud temple also once was the home of Count Valkmore, whom dissapeared several hundred years ago. It was said that Count Vlakmore was the origonal creator of the temple, in hope that Aegis would forgive him for commiting\nan ungly sin.\nOriginally written in Aegis by an author Unknown.\nRe-scribed in Vailor by Leyu'Maehr from memory.", ""),
        StaticBook("Cloud Temple", "Leyu'Maehr", "Cloud Temple\nBy Leyu’Maehr\nThe Cloud Temple is a historical fortress that rest in peace, away from all sign of life in Aegis. It is the primary spot where the king’s or Oren would reside in time of great need, or in severe warfare conditions between an unfightable fortress.\nThe Cloud Temple also once was the home of Count Valkmore, who disappeared several hundred years ago. It was said that Count Valkmore was the original creator of the Temple, in hope that Aegis would forgive him for committing an ugly sin.\n        --------", ""),
        StaticBook("CloudTemplePoem", "Dooble_Brick", "Natures Beauty, A druidic Poem\n-------------------This poem is written to describe the beautiful waters of The Cloud Temple Docks.\nThe serenity of the nature so quiet,\nThe sound of the enchanting tide,\nThe pictorial view takes your breath away, \nThe scenic beauty of the bay, \nThe beauty of nature is a captivating sight, \nLooking at it, you can forget your entire plight!", ""),
        StaticBook("Clue 2", "Niera Acal'elor", "Gather around with all of your friends, \nTo the place in town where the party never ends. \nUnder the shadows of the great Sky whale, \nAnd where the great white ships set their sails.", ""),
        StaticBook("clue 3", "Niera Acal'elor", "This is where the Hierarch goes, \nWhen he wishes to wash away his woes. \nYou can bring a friend or maybe two, so they can relax in the bubbles with you.", ""),
        StaticBook("clue 4", "Niera Acal'elor", "Treasure can be found wherever you look, \nIf you take a moment with a lovely book.", ""),
        StaticBook("Cmplt Wrks of GS", "Farley III Stafyr", "The Complete Works of Godwein Stafyr.\n\nTheorem of Wisdom-2\n\nKnowledge and Experience-10\n\nElements of Mind-21\n\nPowers of Being-28\nTheorem of Wisdom\n\nWhat is wisdom?\n\nWisdom is not easy to define, likely similar to eternity. Every person has its own views of wisdom. To elaborate this further, a row of examples.\nIs knowledge wisdom? \nPerhaps.\n\nIs knowing the right thing to do wisdom? \nPerhaps.\n\nIs doing something good wisdom?\n Perhaps.\nIs learning wisdom? \nPerhaps.\n\nAs aforementioned you cannot simply define wisdom. Wisdom is multitude, colorful, differently shaped. Wisdom can and does exist in many of us.\nAs aforementioned you cannot simply define wisdom. Wisdom is multitude, colorful, differently shaped. Wisdom can and does exist in many of us. \n\nTo different degrees, in different shapes.\nA forester can have the wisdom of knowing how to treat his plants and trees properly, when to grow what to reach the best result. As can the farmer. A sorcerer or wizard can know a lot about tge divine art of magic and has through this his\nvery own wisdom.\n\nStill, one would say, that in these examples wisdom turns out to be rather knowledge, which is only partially right, if it’s right at all.\nWisdom requires more than simply having knowledge. Wisdom requires understanding the spirit, the being, the living and many things more. The inner peace and silence may bring wisdom. On the other side, a warrior may achieve a new level of\ncombat by finding his inner stability in combat. To be calm in the face of the storm and evil.\nKnowledge and Experience Elaborated.\n\nUpon a recent interesting discussion about knowledge with an elf, I considered onto writing down, what the difference between knowledge and the often related\nexperience is.\n\nTo note at this place, there are enough people who assume that knowledge and experience are the same, although this is a wrong statement, as experience and knowledge are different.\nTo point this out, an example is required.\n\nFire. Fire burns and hurts, consumes burnable things such as wood and coal.\n\nNow let's assume you learned about fire and its properties out of a book. You studied the texts.\nThis merely is knowledge, theoretical knowing or to put it in different terms, theoretical experience about something, respectively fire in this example.\nOn the other hand, if you never read a book about fire and get burned heavily or see it burning in front of you, consuming something, you learn from it directly. This is experience, practical knowledge.\nThe difference should be clearly visible by now.\n\nNow we have to elaborate the relation between these two elements of wisdom, mental capabilities, sense. The origin of knowledge is clearly, through the\nexperiment and therefore through experience. Knowledge is an unpractical independent manifestation of experience, written down into books, teached through them or teached through a teacher, master.\nThat was knowledge is, to know, but not to know.\n\nExperience itself may not always be referred as knowledge in this case, because knowledge can go deeper than experience, although knowledge\norigins from it. Knowledge itself may include to know about the progress and process behind the visible, such as the burning of fire.\n\nStill, it must be said, that experience can create knowledge under circumstances,\nexperience can also increase knowledge. Experience can support and enrich knowledge. The relation between these two elements is vastly huge and not to be underestimated.\nIt is best to acquire both of them to roughly equal terms in cases, helping you on your way through the life.\nElements of Mind\n\nUpon the writing of the Theorem of Wisdom, my very own mind came to the conclusion that there must be more to it. Every person knows of elements such as fire, earth, water, wind or air and lightning.\nBut aside from external elements which we can feel and use, there are also other elements, in a completely different range, of which we unknowingly make use and into which we tap, without realizing it.\n\nThe Elements of Mind.\nThe mind is a strange fabric, giving us the ability to think, to consider, to act, react, to learn, to remember and many things more. But the mind itself, such as the outer world we experience, has its own elements.\nTo underline this, an example will be given.\n\nFeelings. Feelings, such as love, guilt, hate, happiness and many more. This is one of the elements of the mind, we are able to use it, express ourselves with it, to tap into the power of this\nelement.\n\nFurther explanation is not necessary, I presume, as everyone has at least one feeling within him.\n\nOther elements do exist. Wisdom is one of them, as I aforementioned.\nAlso knowledge and experience are elements of the mind. But there are more!\n\nConcentration, Purity, Peace and Balance, and Common Sense.\n\nHow each of these work, will be explained in further\nupcoming books. For the element of wisdom, I refer myself to my work “Theorem of Wisdom” for further information. The book “Knowledge and Experience – Elaborated” may give additional hints about two further elements.\nThe Powers of Being\n\nAs a theoretical study, my writings began about wisdom, leading me to the difference between knowledge and experience and their relation, furthermore bringing me to write a first draft of the\nelements of mind.\n\nBy now I have realized that there must be more. The mind is part of the being itself. A power within everyone of us. Logically though, the being consists of more than only the mind.\nPhysically we have the body as the second power of being. The body is one power we use every day, its elements are blood, organs, muscles and the resulting strength… The elements belonging to the body shall be elaborated by me in\nfurther writings.\n\nTo note, that this is the point where my studies will go over into the studies of doctors and healers, likely to be seamless. For this I must also refer to a man of biological knowledge in the future.\nAs for the third and last power of being, I hereby mean the soul. The elements of soul are yet to be elaborated and studied by me, although I can definitely align an element to the soul. In another work of mine, I say that feelings are an element of\nmind. By now, I must correct myself and say, that feelings are a definite element of the soul. The same may go for purity as well. For this we will see further in the future, what my studies and writings bring up.", ""),
        StaticBook("Cobbler and Crow", "Sofetios", "The Cobbler and the Crow\nBy Sofetios\nI was working in the night,\nAs I bent over a shoe,\nI was working very hard,\nTo finish before the morning dew.\n \nI was just about finished,\nWhen what was this I hear?\nA tapping, slight tapping,\nAnd it seemed to be so near.\nI turned to the window,\nAnd what did I see?\nA crow tapping at my glass,\nStaring at me.\n \n“Go away.” I said,\nSternly as I did stand.\n“Don’t you know I have work to do?”\nThough I know it didn’t understand.\nBut the crow kept tapping at the glass,\nAnd I began to get mad,\nThis crow just would not stop,\nNot even a tad.\n\nI turned to my unfinished shoe,\nTo work as long as I could bear,\nBut then I noticed something missing\nThat would stop me from completing the pair.\n \n\nThe golden lace that I needed,\nFor this order to be complete,\nI couldn’t find it anywhere,\nAs the glass the crow did beat.\nI checked my table, checked my pockets,\nAnd then to my surprise,\nThere was a hole in one,\nThat was just the right size.\n\nI would never finish this shoe,\nBy the morning light,\nNow things were dire,\nAnd my situation was quite tight.\n“I must have dropped it,\nOn my way back from the store!”\nBut it was too far I thought,\nAs my confidence tore.\n \nBut then I realized, “What’s this?”\n“Where was the tapping I couldn’t bare?”\nI looked to the window,\nAnd the crow wasn’t there.\nI went to the window,\nAnd what was in its place?\nIt was simple and divine,\nThe golden lace.\n \nI opened the window,\nAnd took it with care,\nBecause of who gave this gift,\nI was certainly aware.\nI went to my table,\nAnd finished the shoe,\nBut then thought of the crow,\nIf I only knew!\n \nI then went to bed,\nWith not much I could say,\nI only did hope,\nThe crow would come back some day.", ""),
        StaticBook("Code of Chivalry", "Jacob Chapel", "Knight's Code of Chivalry\nThe Knight's Code of Chivalry, as revised on the 7th of The Amber Cold, 1470, by Noble Ser Maric II Varodyr, the Winged Knight and scribed by Ser Akal Ipos.\n\nPrinted at 'Books of Oren', Petrus.\nA Knight shall live by honour, for glory, and guard the honour of fellow knights.\n\nHonour The Creator and maintain His Church.\n\nKeep the faith and act in righteousness.\nHe shall serve his liege lord valorously and with conviction.\n\nAnd persevere to the end in any enterprise begun and never turn his back upon an equal foe.\nA Knight shall refrain from the wanton giving of offence and despise pecuniary reward.\n\nGive succour to widows and orphans and regard the honor of women.\nHe shall offer that which is not needed, to those who are needy.\n\nAnd safeguard the helpless and uphold the weak.\nA Knight shall respect those placed in authority but fight for the welfare of all.\n\nAbstain from Injustice, cruelty and deceit.\n\nHe shall be vigilant and show courage, even in the face of evil.\nResolve not with swords what can be with words.\n\nAnd at all times speak the truth, even if faced with death.\nIn their blood the creator’s will shall be written.", ""),
        StaticBook("Cognitism", "oblivionsbane", "<>*<>*<>*<>*<>*<>*<>*<>          Cognitism\n<>*<>*<>*<>*<>*<>*<>*<>\n\nCognitism is a magic that allows one to see their own illusions. As any illusionist knows, this is normally impossible due to the nature of illusionary magics. Normally, an illusion would\nvanish if someone were to realize that it was, in fact, an illusion. Getting around this done through a technique known as the observers eye, and it is no over-estimation to say this is the key to this magic. It is done by separating the mind in two,\nwhere one views the illusion and the other manipulates it. They communicate through a subconscious connection, never truly being separate. This can cause mild to severe mental issues at first, but eventually as the mage progresses these subside or at\nlessen to the point that they aren't noteworthy.\n\nThe ability to see your own illusions through Cognitism creates many uses. I have categorized these uses into three subtypes: Tools, Virtual Representations, and Visual Assistances.\nTools are the simplest of the three. They are anything you create that allows you to perform a specific function. Examples of these would be illusionary meter-sticks, measuring squares, and protractors. These are the simple, beginners tools, however.\nExperienced Cognats are able to create their own tools which perform significantly better, but they are only possible through Cognitism. An example of this is an illusionary bar that can extend and tell you visually the exact height of a certain object.\nThis leads us into our next subtype, Virtual Representations. These are separated into two types, models and overlays. Models are detailed three-dimensional illusions. They can be as large and intricate as a cathedral, and as small and simplistic as a\ntack. One use of models is when designing something. For instance, a blacksmith would be able to plainly view the sword he wants to design and make real time alterations to said design rather than simply using the foggy picture in his head.\nThen we have overlays, which are basically flat, two-dimensional models. These are useful for scribing and creating blueprints or planning out paintings. Anything that would be have to do with work done on a flat surface could be aided using overlays.\nIt is also possible to convert one to another, which makes this particular part of the magic work well with blueprints, since an overlay of a blueprint could be made, then changed into a model.\n\nFinally, we have the real magical aspect of Cognitism, Visual Assistances. This is where all of the \"legendary\" aspects of Cognitism reside. The most basic use of this subtype is creating numbers and symbols to do mathematical operations visually.\nThis allows one to not have to worry about paper, and it removed the physical lag between writing the next part of an operation down. If done enough, this can get to the point moderate calculations can be done in a mere moment, and advanced ones might\nonly take half a minute or less, depending on the individual. It should be noted that Cognitism does not make one \"smarter\". The person is as smart as they will ever be, but what it does allow is it allows them to do things faster, which is why so many\npeople seem to think it can, in fact, make someone smarter. Visual Assistances also allow someone to, technically, improve their memory. This is done through the storing of Visual Assistances, which can give a Cognat highly detailed photographic memories.\nNow, we come to a part of Cognitism that is indeed considered legendary, Hyper Thought. Hyper thought is achieved through a state of meditation, where a Cognat speeds up their thinking using Cognitism in an attempt to discover a solution or revelation\nover a problem. There is not much more I can say about it, other than it can occasionally leave one mentally fried, with a severe migraine, inability to organize thoughts, and loss of some motor capabilities.\n\nIt also cannot be used in a fight. It takes a\ngood toll on your concentration to even use Cognitism due to the spectators mind, and any extreme stresses would interrupt the fragile technique. \n\nOverall, if you are a tradesman or simply someone wishing to learn a magic that will enhance your ability\nto think and work rather than destroy or manipulate, I would say Cognitism is right for you.\n\n- - - - - - - - - - - \n\nWritten by the master Cognitism mage, Mark Lander\n\n- - - - - - - - - - -", ""),
        StaticBook("Combat Manual", "Temp Thersist", "Combat     Basics\n\n         The 3 Stances\n\nHigh - Form is leaned in, Knees bent. Arms arced in pre-wind-up. Blade is to be pointed skyward. Referred to as the \"Counter,\" stance. Used to counter-act Mid-guard and stanceless men.\nMid-Guard - Form is relaxed, blade pommel parallel to the user's waistline. Knees bent and arms relax. Referred to as the \"Assaulting,\" stance. Attacks vary on situation, be it arc or thrust. Counters Low stance and untrained fodder.\nLow - Form is lowered, knees bent and body leaned in. The arms are left in a half-wind-up with the blade parallel to the flooring. Referred to as the \"Wounding,\" stance. Counters High Guard, spearsmaan and isn't typically used to kill a target.\nMovements\n\n\nWhen in combat, keep your foe in direct line of sight. Movements are circular in nature, there are to be no steps forward or backward, instead, side-step in or out.\nWhen Assaulting - Nature may say rush at the target head-on. Instead, aim to the target's left or right with movement. Aim to strike the target and take place on either flank or rear of the target. Once flanked, strike either the neck or lower back.\nWhen Countering - Side-step, parry, or retaliate. Know the range of the opponent's weapon. IF your range is superior, retaliate with a side-step and aim to cleave limb or behead the enemy. If your range is lesser, parry the blow of the enemy and close\nWhen Countering cont... \n\nthe gap, making an effort to disarm or hinder.\nCrossbow Usage\n\nThe following is a petty guide for loading and firing a crossbow.\n\nSet weapon, if required. Simply setting it grip-first onto the ground.\n\nDraw back string, with device or hand.\nMove foot from grip.\n\nLift weapon and load with bolt/munition.\n\nShoulder weapon and aim.\n\nFire.\n\nRepeat as needed.\n\nNote loading device.\nCreated for usage by the Caerngard of Kaedrin. \n\n - Temp Thersist of Kaedrin.", ""),
        StaticBook("Combat: Volume I", "Gerald Elgan", "===================\n\n\nMarked Combat:\nVolume I\n\nTranscribed by\nGerald Elgan\n\n\n\n\n==================\nThe Magical Versus the Mundane- The Knight's Way of Defense Against the Supernatural:\n===================Through the ages, mankind has faced countless threats, both mundane and magical. The wars against the undead have taught us techniques and-\n===================countermeasures that can be- with ample experience, skill and prayer- successfully utilized against the unholy and the profane.\n===================What is Recommended:\n-Numbers\n-Ranged Weaponry\n-Light Armor\n\nExcerpt taken from \"The Guide to Hunting Mages\"\nMarked Combat\n===================Combat Basics:\n------------\nThe 3 Stances\nHigh- Form is leaned in, knees bent. Arms arced in pre-wind-up. Blade is to be pointed skyward.\nReferred to as the \"Counter\" stance. Used in an effort to -\n===================counter-act movements from mid-guard and stanceless efforts.\nMid- Form is relaxed, blade pommel is parallel to one's waistline. Knees bent and arms relaxed. Known as the \"Assaulting\" stance.\n===================Attacks vary on situation, be in Arc or thrust. Used to counter low stance and when assaulting shielded or untrained foes.\nLow- Form is lowered, knees bent, body leaned in. The arms are left in a -\n===================half-wind-up with the blade parallel to the flooring. Referred to as, \"Wounding\", stance. Used to counteract High-guard, spearmen and is only used to hinder movement or knock a foe off balance. This stance is used with two handed.\nMovement\n===================When in combat, keep the foe in your direct line of sight. Movements are circular in nature, there are to be no steps taken forward or backward, instead side-step in.\nMovement, cont.\n===================When Assaulting, nature may say rush the target head on, instead, aim to the target's right or left with movements. Aim to strike the target and take place on either flank or rear of the target. Should the target-\nMovement, cont.\n===================continue to stand after the inital blow, aim for immediate execution, striking to either the neck or lower-spine.\nMovement, cont.\n==================\nWhen countering, side-step, parry or retaliate. Know the range of the Foe's weapon, if the range of your own counter-acts that of the enemy's, retaliate with side-step, aiming to either cleave limb or behead the foe.-\nMovement, cont.\n===================Should the weapon be lesser or equivalent, parry and close the gap. The strike won't likely be lethal. Instead, aim to disarm or hinder.\nCombat Stance\n===================So, think of the three stances as a game of Rock, Paper, Scissors. When on even grounds, and not taking situation at hand, the three stances are as such\nHigh-Rock\nMid- Scissors\nLow-Paper\nCombat Stance, cont.\n===================When starting combat, take stance. Do not suddenly switch stance, since it exposes yourself, parry or sidestep while shifting. Shifting to counter will result in a half stance, which is a stance of valuable parries.", ""),
        StaticBook("Combos/Dessert", "Sofetios Jayamen", "Sofetios's\n       Restaurant\n \n      ===========\n         Specials\n            and\n         Dessert\n           Menu\n     =============\nSpecials\n\nMeat Lover’s Feast (1 per visit)\n\nAn assortment of cooked meats including, Seasoned Pork chop, A Prime Rib, Some Cooked Mutton, and two loaves of Bread.\n\n15 Minas\nVegetable Lover’s Feast (1 per visit)\n\nAn assortment of vegetable dishes including, a baked potato, Two Carrots, A Vegetable Salad, some Cheese fondue, and two loafs of bread.\n\n15 Minas\nThe Sweet Lover’s Feast (1 per visit)\n\nAn assortment of Sweets and delicious goodness including, 4 Caramel Cookies, 8 Butter Biscuits, Cream Pie, some Sweet cheese fondue, and two loafs of caramel bread.\n\n20 Minas\nDesserts\nCake with Frosting (1 per visit)\n\nA fresh baked cake with creamy white frosting a few small fruits on top.\n\n15 Minas\nCheesecake\n\nA Classic, Cheesy and sweet Cheesecake.\n\n20 Minas\nPlease remember to turn in your menu when you are done!\nThanks!\n~Sofetios Jayamen", ""),
