        StaticBook("Menu of TSO", "The Sleeping Ocelot", "The menu of the Sleeping Ocelot teahouse and bakery, owned by Faieya Vallei'onn and Arabella Rosalie Delevoye. Feel free to enquire about any of the items on this menu.\nCakes!\n~*~*~*~*~*~*~\n\nCheesecake: Seven minas\n\nChocolate cake: Five minas\n\nBrownies: Four minas\nBiscuits!\n~*~*~*~*~*~*~\n\nCaramel cookies: Three minas\n\nButter biscuits: Five minas\n\nLemon cookies: Six minas\n\nChocolate cookie: Four minas\nPies!\n~*~*~*~*~*~*~\n\nPumpkin pie: Four minas\n\nApple pie: Six minas\n\nLemon pie: Six minas\nTea!\n~*~*~*~*~*~*~\n\nDandelion tea: Two minas\n\nPoppy tea: Two minas\n\nPine tea: Three minas\n\nBirch tea: Three minas\n\nBlue orchid tea: Three minas\nChamomile tea: Three minas\n\nRose tea: Four minas\n\nBrown cap mushroom tea: Three minas\n\nCactus tea: Three minas\nSpecials!\n~*~*~*~*~*~*~\n\nBreakfast special: Pancakes or omelette with a glass of juice for five minas\n\nLunch special: A sandwich and a glass of juice for five minas\n\n(Juice of your choice!)\nJuices!\n~*~*~*~*~*~*~\n\nPumpkin juice: Two minas\n\nCarrot juice: Two minas\n\nApple juice: Two minas\n\nOrange juice: Two minas\n\nMelon juice: Three minas\nIf you order a cup of tea and a biscuit at the same time, a mina will be taken off of your overall price!", ""),
        StaticBook("Merchants Handbk", "Julius Weston", "The Merchant's\n       Handbook\n===================\n\n by:\n      Julius Weston\nTOC\n3.......................................Basics6................Things to Know\n10...................................Method19..........................................Tips\n\nThe Basics:\n\nMerchanting is a skill. Unlike others, it does not require strong arms or a suit of armor. But rather a silver tongue. One capable of painting pictures with words alone. \n\nMerchanting is about buying a product for cheap and selling it for a higher price. Or we are also known as distributors. Taking ones product and selling it for them. Charging them a small fee for our services.\nThings to know:\n\nThere are sets of questions you should ask yourself before venturing out and trying to sell your product.\n\nSupply - Can I get this product for cheaper than I can sell it?\nCan I sell this? Would I pay minas for this?\n\nDemand - Who would want this product? Why would they want this product? When would they want this product?\n\nAll these questions are important. For instance, you don't\nwant to buy 1000 logs for 1 apiece and expect them to sell at 2 apiece the next day. You are now sitting on your investment. This is a slow investment. But if I knew a new settlement needed spruce logs, maybe I should head there and try to sell them.\nAlways ask yourself, \"Would I pay for this?\" before buying an item to sell. It's hard to sell a product that you don't even want.\nThe Method:\n\nEngage\nIntroduce\nThe Sale\n\nThis 3 step process has worked for me multiple times. It's all about engaging the customer first. Get to know them.\n1. Engage\n\nFirst off, you must engange the customer. Start off with a friendly hello and introduce yourself, ask them how they are doing and maybe about a recent event. Sometimes this will give you a clue on what sort of items they\nwould be interested in. This is a very, very important step and should not be overlooked. Be sure not to introduce your product from the start.\n\nEase into it.\n2. Introduce\n\nNow, we introduce the product we want to sell them. But not like its just another ordinary item. It has to have fluff. Get creative from its origin, history, make, or even special abilities.\nTrying to sell an apple might be hard. But an apple that can grow your hair back? That might sell. \n\nA simple rose for a holiday gift? Or how about a rose plucked from the highest peak in all of Vailor. \n\nIt's all about in the\ndescription. \n\nFluffier the better.\n3. The Sale\n\nNow comes the time to announce the price. There are many ways to get creative with this as well. First off know the value of your item. Now you have to make it seem like they are getting the best deal in town.\nYou could compare the quality of your product to that of your competitor. \n\nYou could tell them that the product usually goes for more and tell them they are getting a steal.\n\nYou could play the pity card and tell them\nyou need to sell it or ______. \n\nThere is always the straight up deal. A haggleable price is sometimes the best way to go. \n\nThe more creative, the better!\nTips and Tricks:\n\nThe more people you talk to per day, the higher chance you have to sell your product!\n\nNever burn bridges. They might want a product from you in the future!\nCreate the want! Cater your product to the needs of the customer! Do what it takes to make a sale!\n\nInteract! Actually focus on the people and not just about selling your product. It will make things much less awkward and much more smooth!\n Thanks for Reading!\n\n\n I hope to see other\n succesful merchants\n out there!\n\n\n ~Julius Weston", ""),
        StaticBook("Merry Christmas", "Nicolas Headsted", "Hey Sofetios, its NicktheRed20, or Nick. I just wanted to write you this note to make christmas a little more personal in Sutica. \n\nI wanted to thank you for everything you do in this town, even though your internet is complete ass, I try and tune the\npianos as best as I can.\n\nYou brought such a great thing to LotC, not just this town. Never have I thought I would be able to play music in minecraft without redstone and a bunch of note blocks. What you did was just make it so much easier with a 3x3 note\nblock piano. It's honestly astonishing. Thank you Sofetios, I hope you have a very Merry Christmas.\n\nYour Friend, \nNick Pechersky", ""),
