        StaticBook("Note-Block Organ", "The Musician", "How To Tune A\n  Note-Block Organ\n\nA note-block organ is a simple instrument, actually composed of several complex boxes which produce a single note each. These boxes can be tuned to produce most any note on the scale.\nIn order to tune each box, one must turn a crank once to the right for each step up the octave.\n\nFor simplicity's sake, this book only records the C major scale, but their range begins at F# below C, and can be turned 24 times before returning to F#.\nIt is interesting to note that the box's tone changes depending on what material they sit upon. Wood produced a deep, resonating sound, while simple dirt and most other materials creates a sound that is the closest to an actual organ or harp.\nC MAJOR SCALE\n\nC: 6 turns\nD: 8 turns\nE: 10 turns\nF: 11 turns\nG: 13 turns\nA: 15 turns\nB 17 turns\nC: 18 turns", ""),
        StaticBook("Notes on Inwe", "Tristanm1", "Notes on Inwe\nAuthor Unknown\nJoma, 20th of The Second Seed, 1316: \n\nThe Original Dark Elves of Inwe seemed to live within the forest, having trees for homes. In conflict with their orc neighbours, they hid themselves amongst the thick darkwood trees and found shelter and\npeace here. \n\nTheir houses are made of simple wooden constructions, preferring to live in the inside of thicker trees. Despite this preference, they were not keen on climbing, and dug cavernousdwellings below their houses as\npossible shelter and storage.\n\nThe grove within Inwe was the reason the Dark Elves split from Malinor. They worshipped the moon over the sun. This is evident in seeing their sacred pool, as it was built so moonlight would reflect in it during\ntheir nightly gatherings. \n\nIn time, Dark Elves learned to weave forests and perhaps even draw the moon's power into magical abilities. Their chantings of old seem to echo within the pool still. It was possible that the Dark Elves\nhad an order much like the Druids, but this is unconfirmed.\nTolta, 24th of The Second Seed, 1316 : \n\nThere is a strange sort of energy wafting this place. It surrounds the pool like mist. I feel it strongest in the mornings, as the dew hangs from the leaves and the moon's touch is still recent upon\nthe pool.\n\nIt is revitalizing, like waking from a long sleep. Some traces of the Mali'Ker magic might still linger here.\nIrba, 4th of Sun's Smile, 1316: \n\nI have found an old tome amongst the ruins of the Dark Elven settlement. It denotes that the Dark Elves in Inwe were hunted by a people they called 'Marauders', a group that finally found and attacked the village.\nThis may have been the cause of their disappearance, but that's anything but confirmed. \n\nThis book is not written in old elvish, but in common Aegis tongue. We might be able to derive a time period from this, or a taboo for using elvish in\nwritten work. Either way, the journal uses the word 'Velulaei', which possibly means 'Moon'. They speak of the moon as if she is an entity, like a goddess or a spirit. I wonder if this could be more than silly superstition of a primitive race.\nSabet, 28th of Snows Maiden, 1316: \n\nWe are starting to uncover multiple grotto's that may have served as cave dwellings and hiding spaces for the dark elves. These caverns seem damp and cramped, and I would abhor spending just\none night in one of them. The original inhabitants must have used them out of necessity, perhaps a fear of being found if they spend too much time above the surface.\n\nOne of the caverns is situated directly below the largest tree of\nthe village, baring its roots and the water that feeds it. I wonder if the elves tampered with this pool of water in some way, in order to have the tree grow to its unusual vastness.\n\nSpending too much time in this grotto makes me ill at ease, my senses\nurging me to leave.. This feeling might be the remnant of an ancient spell ment to keep intruders out. If this is true, I can only assume that within this grotto is something the former owners did not want to be found.", ""),
        StaticBook("NOTICE", "Armin Ambertoe", "On the 16th of Sun's Smile, 1556, a group of three dwarves broke into the thanhium lab. The way they managed to enter was by lockpicking the doors at the side entrance. THE SIDE ENTRANCE GATE HAS TO BE SHUT AT ALL TIMES. I noticed the lab had been broken\ninto when I went downstairs. Lyeth and Orias showed up shortly after this discovery, and we proceeded to the entrance. Orias and I went on the walls surrounding the entrance with buckets of acid, in case the thieves were hostile, and Lyeth stood at the\nfirst set of doors, lowering the bridge but not after trying to open the glass door herself. This action by Lyeth likely jammed the door system, and it trapped the dwarves inside. We did manage to spot them once they came back up to the doors, and we\ntalked with them, attempting to convince them to surrender which they failed to do. So they sat trapped in the lab, slowly freezing to death. Then, out of no where, the cracking of the large thanhium shard that was previously stolen, or at least that is\nwhat I suspect it was, could be heard. Orias had left at this point and so Lyeth and I dashed to the entrance to the vault to take cover. Revion arrived then and we huddled by the vault until the explosion happened. The glass door contained the blast, but\nended up cracking, and thanhium dust began to slowly leak out. The dwarves all likely died from the blast, and so Lyeth, Revion and I went to work attempting to seal the crack. Lyeth first piled stone in front, but that failed to contain it completly.\nNext she gathered up some gold she had lying around in her room and melted it down. With the melted down gold she made her way to the glass door and poured it on the stone barrier, sealing it shut. With the crack sealed, we made our way back up to the\nupstairs. Once there Orithur arrived and went swiftly down to the lab once notified about the situation. He began quite angry, and luckily that magical amulet managed to keep him in check. He then left the library, seemingly quite sad and upset and walked\noff to the west of the library into the woods. At this time, the downstairs area is flooded with thanhium dust, and so no one should enter without a mask on. Also, the seal on the lab should NOT be touched under ANY circumstances. It is also advised that\nany items of significant value in the downstairs rooms be moved to a different place for the time being. Lastly, venting of the dust will occur within the next few stone days so it is advised to have a mask on at all times when walking about the library\nuntil further notice.\n\nIf you have any questions direct them to Lyeth or myself. \n\n-Armin", ""),
        StaticBook("Notification", "Froy LaValètte", "Your order has been delivered, my beloved. I've taken the liberty to make you some extra- only for you.\n\nI will be sure to remove this chest tomorrow, when I wake up.\n\nYours truly,\n420blazerxdxd", ""),
        StaticBook("Nursery Rhymes", "Mariana", "-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=--=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=--=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\n\n\n\nChildren `s Nursery \n         Rhymes\n\n\n\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=--=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=--=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=\n*On each page would be a picture in relation to each rhyme- along with blue rims around the corners\n-=-=-= Camels -=-=-\n\nFor each mina to tell;\nNever to cry a camel for sale;\nOld, and young;\nNever to cry camels for sale.\nv-v Milkey Roads v-v\n\nOne bucket, two bucket full;\nCows around; \nThree buckets full;\nOne more cow, and\nthe last bucket is full;\nOff now, leaving the milky road with five buckets filled.\n0-0Rose and Popy0-0\n\nTis I enjoy rose;\nMy sister enjoys poppies;\nNever would we agree;\nFor rose or poppy tea.\nLove and Mommy`s\n          Hugs\n\n\nFor each tear I cry;\nMommy is standing right by;\nHolding me as she kisses my tears bye bye.\nl-l-l River Dame l-l-l\n\n\nDown the the River Dame; \nAll of us children play;\nDown by River Dame\n*-*-*-Blue Sky-*-*-*\n\nEverywhere I go;\nBlue sky, blue sky:\nWith a yellow ball up high;\nBlue sky, blue sky:\nI`ll do anything if I have my blue sky.\n*The back cover would have a picture of a child holding a flower- along with the author`s name written on the corner", ""),
        StaticBook("NV-02", "liam_barnett", "10th of the Amber Cold, 1567\n\nNemir Valnelis\n\nThe following is the account of what took place on the night of the 15th of Sun's Smile, 1567\nThis will be a transcript of Miss Valnelis' own testimony, word for word.\nSection I:\n\nVictim claims that she arrived at the gates, alone, hoping to enter in order to purchase a drink. She found two guards arguing, presumably Corporal Crider and Lieutenant Smyth.\nAs the woman was arrested, on the grounds of being an 'Aheral, she was taken to the cells beneath Ancelcourt, strapped into the chair by Smyth, accompanied by Sano, Zenik, and Crider.\nAs the victim was trapped in the chair, Crider and Sano protested, protecting the woman. Crider lunged towards Smyth, fed up with his, quote, \"Abnormal acts of violence,\" and Zenik knocked him out cold, using\na glass bottle, breaking it against his head. They proceeded to hang Crider up on the wall, and Sano departed.\nSection II:\n\nAfter the departure, Smyth took out his knife, gliding it across the victim's cheek. He began asking the woman questions, such as the following:\n\"What's your name?\"\n\n\"How badly do you want to live?\"\n\n\"Who would miss you if you were to die?\"\nAfter answering these questions, Crider came to, starting to look around the room. As he was already pinned to the wall, unable to move, Smyth ordered Zenik to hit his head with a rock.\nAfter Zenik had done so, Smyth ordered him to do it again, but harder this time, and that is where Crider's head was removed. Zenik then took Valnelis' own dagger, and returned it by darting it at her feet.\nSection III:\n\nAfter being released, Valnelis ran outside, greeted by Sano, Crawley, accompanied by Zenik, and met with a man named Vargen, a concerned friend.\nThis is a compiled report of the actions of Lieutenant Smyth on the night of the 15th of Sun's Smile, 1567. Recorded by Bell Sano, and accompanied by Michael Raher and Erren Crawley.", ""),
        StaticBook("O. Studies I", "Orithur", "~:;: The Voidal Egg :;:~\n\n- Orithur's Studies-\n------------------- Entry 1: \nI was recently approached by a resident of Hualthal known as 'Archembalt'. He claimed to have been approached by a mysterious and seemingly powerful\nstranger known as 'Errant'. Archembalt revealed to me that this Errant had given him some sort of orb covered with intricate yet unknown markings. Together we studied the object for some time before it was decided we should attempt to open it. Upon\nseveral strikes to the orb, it split open perfectly down the middle, causing a gooey creature resembling that of a large leech to fall out. The creature for some time remained motionless. Archembalt then remarked that the orb in question seemed to warm to\nthe touch of a magic-user, but remain cold to the touch of a non-magic user. Then, when going to touch the 'leech', it sprang up and attached itself to his arm. I had seen such a creature documented before in the library of Dragur, seen on several\noccasions to be feeding off objects and people of significant mana. As such, I am naming the creature, the Mana Leech.\n\nThe Mana Leech could not be pulled from the man's arm due to its toughness and slipper hide, but\nstrangely, but a matter of chance we inflicted pain upon Archembalt, and as a reaction the creature somewhat evolved, growing small legs and expanding in size. We continued to beat the man and watch as the evolution process continued to work, assumedly as\nan act of self-defence for its host.\n\nHowever, when the creature reached the size of a large dog, it sprang from Archembalt's arm and fled. We were unable to find it.\nEntry 2:\nSeveral days have passed and the creature has been sighted outside Hualthal. It was chased and followed across the snow plane until it suddenly stopped, and dug straight downwards. After digging up the earth in pursuit, we\nencountered something so bizarre and extraordinary, that words can barely describe. We encountered a massive, large black orb the size of a small house, hiding all this time beneath the earth. The creature was then never seen,  it either entered the orb\nor became one with its mass.\n\nThe egg, and an egg I assume it to be due to the similar marking as the previous smaller orb, was excavated and a small fortification was constructed around it so that it may be studied. There is a runic symbol\natop the egg, red in colour. The pattern is detailed below:\n\n             +\n      +     +     +\n             +\n             +\n             +\n        +       +\nI have seen no pattern or symbol like it. I will study further.\nEntry 3:\nI have concluded that the larger egg and the leech from before are both Voidal in nature, as much like other noted voidal creatures they are resiliant to physical attack and magical attack only fuels them. However, the markings and the rune\nappear to suggest that an intelligent force is at work here - these creatures are unlike any other voidal beings encountered in the past.\n\nThe leech has not been sighted again and the egg shows no change.\nEntry 4:\nAmazingly, the egg has changed and altered. Four large tentacle-like chitinous growths have extended at equal distances apart around its edge, extending about 3 meters into the air. As well as this, the rune in the centre has now\nchanged from the previous design, to this:\n\n             +\n      ++          ++\n      +             +\n             +\n         +      +\n         ++++++\n\nI have also noticed that when in proximity\nto the egg, I could hear a faint sound, similar to a whisper on a gust of wind. At first I believed it to be interference on my telepathic field that connects me to my brother, but alas the mortals about me confirmed to hear the whispering.\nEntry 5: The 'egg' has transformed once more. It now stands as a large obelisk, breaking the structure it was contained within.\n\nIt has remained in this state for a very long peroid of change. No further change has occured.", ""),
        StaticBook("O. Studies II", "Orithur", "~:;: Weather Spire :;:~\n\n- Orithur's Studies -\n-------------------Entry 1:\nI have produced a scale model of a device I believe may potentially hold the capability to alter the very weather in the sky. Admittedly, only to a state of snowstorm.\nThe reasoning, as I am often questioned about, is merely for the sake of curiosity. If it can be done, why shouldn't it be done?\n\nThe scale model is only that, but the concept is that I will construct a large wooden tower with a large mechanism atop\nit that holds the sole purpose to grind down Thanhium into a powder. Then, releasing this into the atmosphere may cool the air to such a state that snowcloud form. Allowing for, in a sense, control over elements previously only known to bow to the whim of\nGods, Aenguls and Daemons.\n\nShould this project succeed even in the slightest manner, it will be a great leap for the scientific world, and perhaps bring us one step closer to fully understanding the world we live in, and how it functions.\nEntry 2:\nWith the help of one of my scholars and his Frost Witch companion (make no mistake, I am aware of the Witch's intentions for the device even if she will not admit it), I have allocated an area to begin building the prototype. For the best\nresults, I believe we must build the structure as clost to the cloud layer as possible for you see I wish to distribute Thanhic dust into the existing clouds rather than attempt to create clouds myself.\n\nThe scaffold is now currently\nunder construction, and I am making a variety of precautions to ensure that it will be strong enough to resist the weight of the mechanism atop it, the might of the wind, and the side-effects that the Thanhium may have upon the structure's integrity.\nEntry 3: Ser Ashur, a Scholar of Dragur has found what he believes to be a far more efficient use of Thanhium, and more practical than a tower. He would instead seek to build a large 'cannon' that fires Thanhium high into the cloud layer, where it would\ndetonate into a dust.\n\nI am inclined to believe that this solution may indeed be more practical than building a tower, and far less likely to collapse. The cannon will need sufficient reinforcement to prevent damage to it by\nthe launching of Thanhic shards, and the effects of Thanhium itself. But this can be quite easily done.  Construction has currently been altered with the new design of a 'cannon' in mind.\n*Entry 4 has been torn from the book.*\n*Entry 5 has been torn from the book.*\n*Entry 6 has been torn from the book.*\nEntry 7:\nDisaster. Utter disaster. The device is quite functional, to lengths I could never have imagined. It as intended, alters the weather to create snow. But I failed to see that by using Thanhium in the process, I had turned the air\nquite toxic. It seems I am never able to escape the fact that I am a weapon, and my every creation can be nothing more than used to bring about devastation - there is truly no escape. The device has one use - destruction: even from a desire so simple, it\nhas brought about chaos. I will not see something of my own creation be used as a weapon, and as a result, I have both destroyed the device and torn the pages from this journal that would allow for its duplication. I consider the experiment, a failure.", ""),
        StaticBook("O. Studies III", "Orithur", "~:;: Devourer Ruins:;:~\n\n- Orithur's Studies -\n-------------------Entry 1:\nThe scholars of Dragur and I have expeditioned to a peculiar set of ruins located within the Elven realm within Vailor. The ruins are significantly worn,\nbut we were able to make out a few important, or so I believe, details pertaining to its purpose and its history. Upon entering ruins, I am told one is immediately struck with a deathly stench, as I am incapable of smelling it myself, but more\non that later. To the immediate left of the entrance to the ruins are some ancient elven inscriptions, however the dialect is worn and faded to the point where only two things are clear from it: The writings indicate to a portal, of sorts, that will lead\nthe group or 'cult' that resided in the place to their paradise.\n\nEntry 2:\nIt would seem a cannibal has taken up the ruins as a home, we quickly found several mutilated bodies, all half-eaten. The bite marks\nindicate that the person or creature that did it, was certainly humanoid in nature. Hence the conclusion of cannibalism. Due to the fact that we could find no evidence of cannibalism within the various scriptures on the walls, I believe the cannibal\nis an irrelevant factor to our study, and holds no relevance to the ruins whatsoever.\n\nEntry 3:\nThe most prominent part of the ruins would be a strange mural, detailing the existence of a large squid-like beast. Sometimes referred to\nas the 'Squid Lord', but more commonly known as the 'Devourer'. The fact that this creature is known to exist today, and was seemingly worshipped so many years ago, indicates that the creature is either not unique, and there exist a species of its ilk, or\nit is simply very, very old. The latter personally makes more sense to me, seeing as only one beast has ever been seen to this day. The ruins indicate to the fact that the Devourer was worshipped by them, and it would somehow open this 'portal to\nparadise'. That would explain the very cult-like room I will detail in the next entry.\n\nEntry 4:\nThe room is quite small, and is located but a short distance away from the mural. It appears elven in design, as do the rest\nof the ruins, once again indicating to the presence of ancient elves. Around the room is a balcony, with the purpose to allow onlookers to view the centerpiece of the room - some sort of sacrificial podium. This would suggest cultist activity in these\nruins. It would seem that these ancient elves somehow linked this beast with an afterlife, believing him to be some form of God. Interestingly, the ruins are located very close to the elven shoreline, suggesting that these sacrifices may have\nindeed reached the Devourer.\n\nEntry 5:\nSeveral bookshelves were discovered within the ruins, but time has not been kind to them. We were unable to make out any information from any of them. They are all too worn to be legible.\nEntry 6:\nIt has been mentioned that appearances of these 'Ancient Elves' have occured within the elven land. Portals opening up to release them, seeminly bent on destruction. It is certainly interesting that they use portals, as described in the ruins.\nI will investigate further into these occurances, as the likelihood that these elves are connected to both the ruins and the Devourer is high indeed.", ""),
        StaticBook("O. Studies IV", "Orithur", ":;: Basilisk Hunt :;:\n\n- Orithur's Studies -\n-------------------Entry 1:\nA group of Scholars and company have departed to hunt down and slay a basilisk, while I would like to say it was for scientific measures, it was in fact for mina.\nThe creature is said to be located somewhere within a cliff at the location known as 'Saltstone'.\n\nWe depart for these cliffs immediately.\n\nEntry 2: We have found the cavern where the basilisk is said to have been\nlocated, alas we have waited here some time with no change nor appearances of large green snakes. We believe the creature is lurking within a large pool of water of water of questionable depth at the end of the cavern. Alas, we are standing well clear\ndue to this assumption. None of us are particularly eager to investigate if it is the case.\n\nEntry 3: I write this entry after the beast has appeared and lay slain. I will document the events that occured now over the following pages.\nIt would seem that the Basilisk resembles that of a dragon with its head, and it also bears the ability to suck up water and blast it out at a boiling temperature, but seems to lack the ability to produce a breath of fire - this is further indication that\nthe beast is a distant relative of dragons.\n\nIt as expected, resembles a large snake, green of scales, bearing no limbs whatsoever, but this does not slow the creature, it is able to move at quite the alarming speed through both\nthe tunnels of the cavern and the pool of water itself.\n", ""),
        StaticBook("O. Studies V", "Orithur", "~:;: Imp Parasite :;:~\n\n- Orithur's Studies -\n-------------------Entry 1:\nI have been approached by a dark elf infected with something known only as the 'Imp Parasite'. It requires the host to constantly consume the blood of a living\ncreature or animal. The man in question acquired the parsite from being bitten by an orc also infected with it. It allows for a variety of useful benefits, preventing the user from falling ill or being poisoned, are but some of the uses the Imp Parasite\nprovides. But in this journal I will not be analysing the properties of the Imp Parasite, but instead how to completely remove it from its host.\n\nThe parasite has grown and multiplied within the host body, so removing\nthe creature is no easy task. It cannot be cut out due to it being so woven within the host's being. \n\nMy hypothysis on how to overcome this obstacle is as follows: Draw a sample from the host and exposure the Imp parasite sample to\nvarious Thanhic alloys, each one a very small, but different strength. Using this system I may potentially be able to analyse the minimum level of Thanic exposure required to eliminate the Imp Parasite without harming the host.\n\nHuman and other\ndescendants usually die to Thanic exposure at 'Alloy level six', whereas rabbits die at 'Alloy level 3'. I am hoping that the parasite is unresilient to cold, and will perish before level 6, where the host will be put in danger.\nEntry 2:\nI have now acquired a sample of the 'Imp Parasite' from the infected host. When performing the removal surgery, the parasite visibly seemed to withdraw from my scalpel. Never before have I witnessed such intelligence in a\nbeing of its nature. The parasite has mastered the art of survival, to the point where the host is often quite aware of its existence, and chooses to accept it.\n\nWhether or not the host accepts it due to a biological function of the parasite, forcing\nthem to do so, or whether they simply choose to do so due to its benefits, is as of yet unclear. On this I will study further.\n\nI originally believed it necessary to preserve the specemin in ice through Thanhic exposure, but instead I have found a safer\nalternative: to prevent harm or even death of the creature, as we know so little about it, I have instead left it within a large jar of blood, and for the moment it seems to be quite content.\n\nI will commence my study soon, however, as I am unsure of how\nlong the creature can survive this way without a host. I promptly find a source of heat to warm the blood, to attempt to match the conditions it would face inside a mortal body, this shall hopefully prolong its life for me to experiment upon it.\nEntry 3:\nThe activity of the parasite seemed to slow of recent, but after heating the blood under a small flame to mimic the conditions it would face inside a body, it has returned to its alarmingly active self. It consumes blood at an immeasurable\nrate, and thus I have had to take a quantity of blood from a willing scholar to keep its container filled until I find the time to complete its experiment.\n\nEntry 4:\nI have discovered the minimum level of thanhic alloy required\nto efficiently kill off the Red Imp Parasite. The percentages of the alloy are as follows: 38% Thanhium, 62% Ferrum. Anything lower seems will likely kill the parasite after some time, but this measurement killed mine at a distance of 0.5 meters, in a\ntotal of eleven minutes. Previous experiment would dictate that this level of Thanhic exposure will not prove fatal to the host I wish to rid of the parasite. I now prepare for the final stage of the parasite's removal.\nEntry 5:\nSuccess. The host was kept at the low exposure to Thanhium for almost a complete cycle of the sun, and when they arose, none of the black veins that previously indicated the presence of the parasite could be found. Unless there exists a small\namount of the parasite within the hosts body very gradually replicating once again, the parasite should no longer exist to be a problem. If however that is the case then I have discovered but a temporary solution to the problem, and frequent returns to\nthe proceedure will be required. At this time, it is too early to tell.\n\nRegardless, I consider the experiment a great success.", ""),
        StaticBook("O. Studies VI", "Orithur", "~:;: Harbinger Study :;:\n\n- Orithur's Studies -\n-------------------Entry 1:\nI am a troubled mind. This study has become an absolute priority, and I am delaying all other works so that I may entirely focus on the study and perhaps cure for my own\nmind. The psyche of ghouls and the creation of Bloodshards shall be put on hold until I believe I have sufficiently learned all there is to on my own nature, and the nature of fellow Harbingers.\n\nEntry 2:\n\nThe problem:\nHarbingers, some more than most, were forged by a Drakaar, which is an entity of extreme corruption. But countering this is our previous lives before we were immolated and turned into Harbingers. We\nare divided personalities now that Setherien's hold of us is over. Part of us exists as the man before, in my case, a paladin of Xan. But another part of us is constantly pushing to emerge, and it is the Harbinger Setherien created. Should our emotions\nconsume us, it is incredibly likely that we become unable to resist the inner urge to release the hatred of the Drakaar, Setherien - our armour returns to its blue shade, and we know only hatred. For any Harbinger attempting to find a new life in the\nmodern world, this is a significant problem.\n\nUsually the change is not by choice, and as beings of undisputed power and occasionally; influence, we pose real danger to friend and foe alike. Thus far in every instance of combat, this change has been\nforced upon me. \n\nThe Harbingers are scratches in the music sheet of life, we are mistakes, we are stories that collide and tangle. We are creatures out of time, and we are tormented. There is nothing beautiful about a Harbinger, and\nnever before my transformation to one could I have believed that immortality could be worse than death.\n\nIf I am to maintain my work as the Library of Dragur's librarian, I must work to either control my urges, or find a way to ensure they never occur.\nEntry 3:\n\nThe solution?:\nMy theories are nothing more currently, but I turn my gaze to the holy orders that litter the world. There may exist a way of controlling my other self, and if I do discover it, I could extend it to the\nvarious other Harbingers that linger in hiding, so that we may truly find a place within the world to settle, and become anew. With Setherien's legacy constantly rearing its vile head, this currently exists as an impossibility.\n\nMy fears however, are\nthat the mentioned holy orders will learn of this state of mine, and simply seek to eliminate me. While I believe I have built a strong relationship with some of their leaders and many of their members, my recent activity in my corrupt state may have\ndirtied my image. And even if they were to assist, I find it likely that their magical intervention would simply force a demanifestation, my entity being particular weak to holy magic.\n\nEntry 4:\nIt has been some time since my previous\nentry for a lot has occured during that time. Shortly after finishing the last entry, a small army of various holy groups arrived at the door of the library - Ascended, Clerics and Paladins of Xan. They had heard of my flashes of corruption and arrived\nquite heavily armed. Knowing full well that resisting would do me no end of trouble, I followed them to their stronghold on the Isle of Leone.\n\nThere I was given two choices, be killed or be 'cleansed'. But I am not like the corrupt creatures they\nhave faced before, for I am fragmented. My soul cannot be cleansed for it is shattered, a segment existing within a Bloodshard, a segment dying with Setherien, and the remainder writing this now. To cleanse me, would, to the best of my knowledge, kill me.\nThus when given the choice of death or... Death, I sought to convince them to assist me in finding a solution, to my luck, they obliged. I have Herun Athna, descendant of an old friend of mine; Hilan Athna, has gifted me with a magical amulet\nworn around the neck. The necklace dampans my magical prowess, which is no real bother as I seldom used it regardless, but also assists to keep Setherien's corruption at bay. I am yet to see it function, but the holy orders seemed satisfied that I may\ncontinue my work if I am to keep the amulet on my person at all times.\n\nEntry 5:\nToday a rather distressing event occured, some important work was stolen from my laboratory beneath the library,\nand thus my anger did swell and make way to my corrupted form. Alas I am pleased to note that upon the start of my transformation, the amulet did glow with power, and reverse it. I was quite surprised at the speed at which the trinket functioned, and\nhow effective it appeared to be. This worries me, for I am unsure what empowers the amulet, and dread to think what will occur if its power is drained. \nI am hopeful that with the continued cooperation between Herun and the leaders of the other\ndivine orders, may corrupt counterpart may be kept at bay with pleasing regularity. Perhaps even, permanently. Alas, only time will tell.\n\n- Orithur", ""),
        StaticBook("O. Studies VII", "Wretched", "~:;:    The Abyss    :;:~\n\n- Orithur's Studies -\n-------------------Entry 1:\nRecently, I found myself drawn to a distant location, in the mountains to the North of the Human Capital of Felsen. What drew me there, I could not say, perhaps the\nwill of a greater entity. But what I found was truly extraordinary. Before me stood a great stone arch, utterly devoid of life. After several minutes of inspection, the arch burst into life, and it became clear that the structure was the frame for an\nimmense, black portal. Stepping through, I was taken to a new world. A land most peculiar, and most dark.\n\nEntry 2:\nI had discovered the Abyss - or at least part of it, known only as the 'Sunless Sanctum'. There was darkness\nall about me, but that was to be expected. It wasn't called Sunless for no reason. For those unaware: the Abyss is a great pit that stretches a continent wide, or more specifically, the continent of Aegis, which imploded to form the Abyss after what is\ntold to be a battle between Aenguls and Daemons. However no Descendant was alive to witness it, for we had already abandoned Aegis under the attacks of Iblees and his undead. The residents of the Abyss, which I shall come to explain later, refer to the\nbirth of the Abyss as the 'Sunless Day'.\n\nEntry 3:\nThere are structures all around the Sunless Sanctum, and they each server a very different purpose. But surprisingly, these buildings are all in pristine condition. After extensive\nresearch I discovered that these buildings had been remade and maintained by the residents of the Abyss - They call themselves the 'Remnant Dead'.\n\nEntry 4:\nThe Remnant Dead are citizens of Aegis that never made it to\nthe portal that allowed for the descendants escape of the collapsing continent. The remaining, somehow became consumed with the power of the forming of the Abyss,\nand survived the great cataclysm they know as the Sunless Day. According to studies of the\ntomes within the Sanctum's library, these Remnant dead were for a long time crazed creatures, entirely mad and without purpose - however almost immediately as these tomes begin to document the presence of the Drakaar Mordring, who\nnow rules the Abyss, the writing became very coherent and dare I say, fanatical.\n\nIt would seem this Drakaar gave the Remnant Dead something to believe in and aspire to. They are now devouted to him, and act as speakers of his will.\nEntry 5:\nA notable point about the Remnant Dead, is that they are husks of the descendants, with time their minds have degraded and become simple. Most are only known to be able to utter a single phrase, even with the controlling influence of Mordring.\nThere is however a single exception to this rule. One of the Remnant Dead is significantly smarter than the others and fully capable of speech and communication. He led the others to construct the buildings you see located around the\nAbyss, and exists as a servant to Mordring. I talked at length with this Remnant Dead, who, having forgotten his name from centuries ago, named himself 'The Paver'.\n\nThe Paver did not know of a world outside the Abyss, and so believed it was all\nthat existed. Curiously, he thought the world was beautiful. I visit him on occasion, he usually resides at the highest point in the large spire within the Abyss.\n\nEntry 6\nNot a great deal is known about the reclusive master of the Abyss known\nas 'Mordring', who I have come to believe is a verly magically potent Drakaar. I have discovered that Mordring, after discussing with The Paver, is a skeleton, in a very literal sense. Corruption has seeped so far into his being that the very flesh from\nhis bones has fallen off. He is incredibly weak, but only physically. Mordring has had the time and capability to harness great magical potential.\n\nMordring's will is a simple, and clear one. Mordring wishes to provide a sense of 'love' and\nprotection to a type of people who had never recieved it. These people, are of a darker afflication, and find the Abyss to be the location their souls are drawn to, myself included.\n\nSome tomes almost vaguely mention Mordring having a deep\nlove for Dragon-kind, saddened by how the descendents have driven them to near extinction. It is rumoured that Mordring searches for a way to bring about an era of the dragon. At the time of writing, this remains a simple rumour.\nEntry 7:\nPatrolling the Abyss, there is a lesser Dragon, a Drake, by the name of Satar. The tomes within the Sanctum describe this Drake as the footsoldier of Mordring and guardian of the Abyss. He is able to be summoned be speaking a certain\nphrase (the phrase is located within one of the books at the Sanctum) by a peculiar Obelisk. Doing so will bring the Drake to the Sunless Sanctum - but beware, doing this without reason only seems to agitate the creature. Satar does not seem capable of\nharming those bound to the Abyss, however.\n\nEntry 8:\nOne thing that must be made clear; the Sunless Sanctum is not an evil place, it is a dark place. Despite its darkness, there is a great peace and a great warmth there. It is difficult not to feel\nat home when standing within its majesty. No doubt this only applies to entities such as myself, but that truth remains regardless. The Sanctum is a place of gathering, and of calm. It is the counter to evil, and I believe it holds more secrets than meet\nthe eye.\n\nEntry 9:\n'The Deep Below'. A phrase used in whispers around the Abyss, it has something to do with a... power, deep below the surface of the Sunless Sanctum. However, anything I find within tomes\nis extremely vague, and the Paver is mostly unwilling to talk about it entirely.\n\nI can only begin to make assumptions about what could possibly be beneath the Abyss, below the deepest point in the known world. But it is feared nontheless.\nEntry 10:\nAt the top of the largest tower in the Sunless Sanctum, a tower known as the 'Witching Spire', there exists a room that is closed off to all who seek to enter. After studying the Abyss for some time, a force granted my access to this room, and\nwhat I discovered was beyond anything I could have anticipated. Within the chamber -\n\n*The rest of the page has been torn from the book.*\nEntry 11:\nThe Black Sun is the sigil of Mordring, the mark of the Abyss. I believe it is the embodiment of a great metaphor - the warmth of the sun, without its blinding rays. There is truth in the dark, if one's eyes can see through the shroud.", ""),
        StaticBook("Observations", "[Dedicant] Thickfeet", "Observations\n            on\n        Enchantments\n\n      \n\n\n\n\nWritten by\nLethius Thickfeet\nAs my learnings during my druid dedicancy continue, it has come to my attention the unnatural tendencies of the magical realm.\n\nIt its known that the source of most arcane magics is the great endless Void.\nIt is also believed by some the damaged cause by such usage is severe and slowly affects the greater realms that we live in.\n\nI was tasked to witness this in person. \n\nI spent a few days looking to the locals of Kal'Archon for\na regular magic user. Unforunately I found noone in the safety of the newly built city.\n\nOf course within the Druid Grove itself that kind of search would be futile.\nSurprisingly my search led me right under my nose.\n\nI was sifting through old belongings and came across a small journal I had purchased decades before.\n\nAmong my collection stood a very unique\nitem. A mage's journal had described the usage and discoveries she had made involving Enchantments.\n\nI glanced through the pages where a short collection of glyphs and its attributes were described in detail.\nIt was clear to me that such magical imbuement must have come from the same source as most Arcane magics.\n\nWhat I found was not wrong, and what found me was unnatural in every way.\nI began by searching the city markets for the magical reagents needed to perform enchantments.\n\nAfter collecting a few cheap tools and swords, the natural emerald stone which takes most precidence in the profession, and a mixture of other\npowders and artifacts required, I set out into the mountains to find a secluded and safe place to examine and study.\n\nThe first few days of attempts yielded nothing of value. \n\nMost attempts ended with absolutely no\nchange to the item in question. Some attempts the enchantments didn't hold but I had started to gain progress.\n\nFor most of those days, deep meditation and rebalance of the area is how I kept my focus. For every single attempt I\nmeditated to find signs to stop my continued attempts. It seemed to me that nature itself approved of me finding out the dangerous truth of the Arcane.\n\nI also repaid my attempts with various planting of flowers and herbs. Thankfully my work in gathering\nleft me with the stores to do so.\n\nEventually my labor bore fruit. I managed to enchant an old sword with a magical sharpness. It was almost as if the enchantment itself took precidence to the edge of the old blade. Ahead of the blade\nwas a magical barrier that cut through most things much easier than the obvious dull blade would have ever done alone. \n\nI rested that night after planting a healthy sapling nearby.\nThat night was some of the worst sleep I could have imagined. I dreamed of a darkness swallowing all around me. I couldn't outrun it, and eventually it over came me as I ran for my life.\n\nI awoke in the middle of the night\nknowing that something dark was coming due to the actions I had taken in those mountains. \n\nThe sounds of the area were too silent. The life of the area had fled and I could feel an unnatural emittion from the sword.\nI picked up the sword with both hands and felt the great weight atop the used hilt.\n\nI turned to see that a group of undead abominations had found me.  Running up the hill I felt the sweat and pain in my legs as I gained hieight but not distance.\nTaking a few wild swings I was able to stop a few of them as I ran. \n\nThe feeling of futility overcame me as the more I used the sword, the more monsters I had seemed to see. \n\nWhat had I unleashed? This was obviously\nno intent behind the enchantment, but I was starting to believe the author of that journal might have known exactly what they were doing when writing down a 'Sharpness' enchantment.\n\nI had been tricked and I was paying the price.\nI began slashing more wildly at everything in sight.  If I could wear out the blade and break it, I knew the enchantment would cease.  \n\nWhile this included some of the monsters around, I also slashed at the stoney earth.\nThe clangs of metal and stone probably agitated more of these monsters and the pain seared in my arms with every swing.\n\nI thought of death and how I had truly deserved it by naively jumping into a magic I didn't understand.\nA piercing wail filled my ears and a satisfying relaxation overcame me as the weight was lifted from my hands.\n\nThe enchantment had ended and the old sword was scrap at my feet.\n\nAs I stood watching the creatures come\nfor me, I decided to earn my fate. \n\nMy pulse filled my hearing, my body shook with fright, and I realized my folly.\n\nI closed my eyes and asked the aspects for forgiveness as the footsteps came closer and closer...\nA sudden silence filled my ears as powerful as a dragon's roar.\n\nIt felt like an age passed before I gained the courage to open my eyes. \n\nI sat on my knees completely astonished. As swiftly as the creatures appeared,\nthey had suddenly dispersed into the darkness.\n\nI spent a few days longer within the trees just thinking of the events. I hid the remainder of the items I purchased around the area hoping they would be lost with time.\nOnce I returned to the Grove I immediately penned what you have just read.  I hope that my story can help in those that come after me. \n\nDo not delve into what we know least about without guidance and carefulness.\nThe void contains a power that we are simply unable to wield elegently and it is not to be toyed with.\n\nI pray that my actions do not reflect poorly on the order, nor did I do too much damage to nature itself.\nDedicant\nLethius Thickfeet\n\n13th of the Grand Harvest.\n\n1457", ""),
        StaticBook("Odd Events C1", "Sofetios Jayamen", "Odd Events\n\n             ~By Sofetios\n                  Jayamen\nPrologue\n\nMy name is James Geedin. My family recently settled in a new land without a name, so I call it Genway.  We were lucky enough to stumble upon an old house.\nWhoever built it, I don’t know. Some odd stuff seems to happen here, but I’m determined to stay at my new home. This is my story.\nChapter One\n\n         The Light\n\nI woke up in the middle of the night with chills running down my spine. I was around 17 at the time. I went downstairs to get something to drink.\nThat’s when I noticed it. I looked out the window and noticed a light in the distance at the woods. Though it wasn’t yellow like a fire, it was white. I thought about checking it out, but decided I should go to bed instead.\nThe next day in the morning, I went downstairs to have breakfast, but the thing was, my parents weren’t awake. That was unusual, so I went upstairs to their room and knocked on the door.\nWhen I knocked on the door it simply opened slowly from the force of my knock. I looked in, but no one was there. I spent the rest of the day looking for them, but to no avail.\nSoon it became nighttime and I was in the kitchen. That’s when I noticed it again, the same light as before. This time I went out towards the light. I came to the forest clearing where the light was coming from.\nThere was a small mirror in the center, angled towards my house. Then I noticed something, the white light wasn’t coming from the mirror, It was coming from the attic window of my house being reflected by the mirror.\nI saw a word carved into the mirror. It said “Truth”. I looked back towards the house. Could my parents be were that light is? I took the mirror and made my way back the house.\nI got to the house and made my way upstairs to look for a way up into the attic. I looked different places until I got to my parents room. I walked in and there was a small white light coming from the bookcase in there, going around the books.\nI walked up to the bookcase and looked through the cracks between the books. There was a white stairway that lead upwards.  I shifted the bookcase over and started walking up the white stairs. Until I got to a white trapdoor above me.\nCautiously, I lifted it up a crack and looked in the attic room.  There were two statues that looked just like…  I then opened the trapdoor fully and rushed into the white room towards the two statues.\nThey were exact replicas of my parents, but they had faces that looked like they were scared of something. Suddenly there was the sound of the trapdoor shutting behind me.\nI looked back to see something in white robes that covered its whole body and a light coming from under its hood.\n“Who are you?” I said scared. It simply stood there. I looked back towards my parents for a second and back again.\nThen it was right in front of me. Startled I fell back and the mirror dropped on the floor face up but didn’t break. I got up and backed up. It didn’t move.\nThen I noticed something. In the reflection on the mirror on the ground, where the thing was supposed to be was a person. The person in the reflection had light coming from its eyes.\nI took out my short sword. Getting ready to fight whatever this was. I lunged at its chest, but as it made contact with the light coming from it, the blade turned white. I dropped my sword from it happening.\nI was about to panic when I remembered the mirror, then got an idea. Keeping my eyes on the person, I picked up the mirror, careful not to get in the light.\nThen with one hand, I slid it under the light coming from the person’s hood, trying to aim it back at it. Suddenly the light coming from under the hood disappeared. Then the person in the robes simply fell over, solid as a stone.\nI turned to the statues of my parents. I wasn’t sure what to do. I talked, I yelled, but there was no response. I had never felt so alone before. My parents turned into stone, not knowing how to turn them back to normal.\nBut then that made me realize something. Something I should have thought from the start. \n\nWho had left the mirror in the woods?\nThe next morning, I awoke to see I was in the white room. It hadn’t been a dream. I was still clutching the hand of the statue of my mother. I looked back to the robed man who turned to stone the night before.\nCautiously, I walked over and got my now stone white sword. I then walked over to the robed man and used the tip of my sword to lift the hood, which was facedown. As I lifted it, no light came from under it as it had before.\nI brought my sword back and put it in its sheath, then looked around the room. Everything was just a bright, stone white. I noticed the window wasn’t white and was still clear. I saw the sunlight coming in from it.\nI turned around and then saw something that wasn’t there last night. A black dot, on the far wall. It couldn’t be more than the size of my pinky finger going around. I walked up to the black dot but then stopped.\nSomething was… coming out of the dot. What looked like a small claw was starting to come out of the dot, but it stopped as if it couldn’t fit through. Then it retracted and went back inside. When I say it is a dot.\nI do not mean a hole. I mean like… liquid… like there was a fresh stain on the wall.  Suddenly, another dot appeared. And another. All on the same wall. Rapidly, more dots started appearing. The whole back wall was about half black dots now.\nThen I heard it. A deep sinister laugh, coming from the wall. I attempted to move back, but found that my feet were stuck to the ground. Next dots started… moving, to the center almost as if forming a kind of giant stain on the wall.\nWhen the giant dot was formed, a claw filled hand come out, though bigger than the last one. It clasped the left side of the dot connected to the wall. Then another one came out, connecting to the other wall.\nThen, in the middle, a giant dark blob starting slowly coming out. I looked down and say the mirror from before. I bent and picked it up. Then I pointed it at the blob. There was only another bit of sinister laughter.\nThe unnatural voice said, “I already know what I am fool.” Then it began reaching with one of its claws slowly towards me. Then… I woke up.\nI opened my eyes, hoping to be in bed but realizing I was in the white room, clutching the mirror in one hand and the statue of my mother’s hand in the other. I immediately got up and looked at the back wall. And… there was a small black dot.\nEnd of Chapter One\n\n\n“Fear mainly occurs in the absence of knowledge.” ~ Sofetios Jayamen\nThank you for taking the time to read this book! I will be writing more in the future!\n\nIf you like my writing, please let me know in person or by bird!\n\n~Sofetios Jayamen\n((Sofetios))", ""),
        StaticBook("Ode to Fiandria", "Rindir", "Ode to Fiandria\n          by: Rindir\nOde to the great white stag adorned here.\nAnd to the wonderful marvel of those without fear.\n\nOde to the bears, and elk, and 'coons.\nAnd to all the marvelous life that looms.\n\nOde to Fiandria.\nOde to the enormous trees so tall.\nIn hopes that they will surely never fall.\n\nOde to the great mystical forest.\nAnd to the flowers loved by a magnificent florist.\n\nOde to Fiandria.\nOde to the wildlife that comes and goes.\nAnd to the many plants that grows.\n\nOde to the friends that we may make.\nIn hopes we will always cherish their wake.\n\nOde to Fiandria.", ""),
        StaticBook("Of Shades", "Maleos Ilindith", "-------------------Just in! Taeleh himself recruited a Mali'ker into the Golden Owl! It seems we are more and more mingling with the impure, such as Taeleh. Going by the name of Dram, and at the age of over 400, a well-engrained impure, he is to join the\nOwls that merely go about catching rodents and calling them beasts.\n\nTaeleh was immediately seen trying to coax the Mali'ker into joining, accepting even the newest of members straight out the gates without much inspection.\nDram was also to be given a home by Taeleh to live inside the walls while the many pure Mali'aheral slump by the docks on the waiting list.\n-------------------We are also to see a heated argument between Maleos and Ikur revolving around the history of the\nGolden Owl and its sacrificial nature as shown in year 1112, as it is recorded. \n\nIkur being the Owl was to yell that all lies spread of the Elibar'acals was a felony began to retreat after realizing that there existed plenty that witnessed\nthe sacrifice of a Elibar'acal by another in an attempt to further their power in Fi'hiiran'tanya, the magic of the Elibar'acal bloodline carried on by Ikur and Taeleh when magic first began with the belief that magic was a weakness.\nNot only do the Golden Owl believe the dark arts are evil, they, at their roots, wish to rid all magic from Fi'Ceru, as that is how they originally arose.\n\nAs of now, Ikur is to admit that what Maleos had said was indeed true about his bloodline.\n-------------------Taeleh is also to defend his hiring of the Golden Owl, in that slaying beasts in the wild is more important than protecting our walls from the recently increasing Uruk raids, as it was stated.\nWhile he continues to openly recruit individuals as he merely attempts to fill their fantasies of slaying dragons and hunting frost witches, he cares not for Fi'Ceru's health nor quality of guarding.\nAfter not being able to respond to claims of not caring of his people, he merely fled the scene minus dignity. We will publish when he is able to get back on as to why guarding is not more important.\n\nReported by Fi'Ceru san", ""),
        StaticBook("Offical Document", "Wolf Druid Ouity", "I, King Brett Perea of Oren hereby declare that the shop \"Danbolt's Smithy- Good Quality, Low Prices!\" Belongs to the good Druid Gildun.\n\nA talented, handsome man, strong in his faith, smithing, and ravishing good looks.", ""),
        StaticBook("Old Elven Part 1", "Anthron", "Table of contents\n------------------\nAffixes: 1-13\n-------------------Seed words: 14-part 2\n-------------------\nContent provided by\nSporadic:\nhttps://www.lordofthecraft.net/topic/19254-recovering-the-ancient-tongue-of-the-elves/\nAFFIXES\nAffixes  served a very important purpose in the Elven language. You are recommended to learn of them and their purpose before anything else,  for many words are formed by the addition of affixes to the so- called 'seed words', which are listed\nbelow.\nWe further discuss our findings on affixes in the grammatical section of this volume. Furthermore, words deserving extra explanation will be handled in the 'curious words' section.\nNote: The dash indicates the location of the seed word to the affix\nac- (aca-) Honorific for an accursed.\n-------------------ah- (ahe-): Honorific for reverence. Often used in front of names to denote sainthood.\n-------------------ael-: Vile, unnatural, beastly.\n--------------------al: -Designates the completion\nof an action.\n------------------\n-an: many, full of -Mostly designates plurality.\n------------------\nan*h- (a/o/i/e/u): To direct (up/down/left/right/rotate) (more on this in 'curious words').\n-------------------\nav-: wandering.\n-------------------ay: for.\n-------------------\n-ante (-nte): Last.\n-------------------div-: Empty, devoid of (antecendent to -an).\n--------------------ento (-nto): next.\n-------------------\n-ehya: and - used at the beginning of a sentence for 'but...'\n-------------------\nel- (ele-): - Changes a noun into a pronoun, making it a title of someone, or possibly something.\n-------------------\nfi-: new, anew - Often used as prefix, but not exclusively.\n-------------------heya- (hey-): what, how, who.\n-------------------\nil- (ilu-): with\n--------------------ii: little Designated the diminutive form. Plural: iian.\n-------------------\n-ir: - Designates the purpose of someone.\n-------------------iyl-: this usually a prefix, though not exclusively.\n-------------------iyul-: that usually a prefix, though not exclusively.\n--------------------leh: -Designates affiliation.\n------------------\nma- (mar-): very -Intensifier. More on this in 'curious words'\n--------------------n: - Sometimes put behind an adjective or verb to create a noun.\n--------------------ne (-ane): not - A suffix used to indicate the negative.\n-------------------\n-onn: from, born, kin - Often a suffix, but not always. More on this in 'curious words'.\n-------------------par- (pra-): before, pre-\n-------------------res- (re-): wayward, capricious, unpredictable.\n-------------------\n-sae: Spreading, development management. - More on this in 'curious words'\n-------------------tal- (tali-): Part (of) - More on this in 'curious words'\n-------------------u-: or - Always a prefix and always used with apostrophe.\n-------------------\n-yem (-hyem): again -Can be used to make a 're-' family of verbs\n-------------------vul- (vule-): clever, intuitive, deceptive.\n-------------------\nSEED WORDS\nThese words make up the brunt of the Elven language, and may be modified by various affixes to create new words.\nacal: valuable, golden, cursed - From ac-al: Completion of the word\nac: Cursed. Beware of this double meaning. Because of this double meaning,  Elves of old may give those they wish ill a nugget of gold to declare their hostility.\nThe gesture was often wasted on other races, who considered the gold a tribute.\n-------------------acalin: shop\n-------------------acaln: gold\n-------------------\n(a)chikr: (without the a:) ***** - denotes a woman who murders her children (or neglects them to death), which is considered a horrible crime due to the value of fertility and the rarity of Elf children. Without the 'a', the word degenerates\ninto a grave insult. Know that intentional cursing in Elven attracts ill-willing aspects.\n-------------------(a)dil: needy, desiring, wanton - When one forfeits the 'a' it becomes as of a vice.\n-------------------\n(a)dont: requesting, awaiting - Without the 'a' it becomes 'demanding', -er makes verb as always.\n-------------------adri: evil, immoral, hate.\n-------------------(a)elor: abomination, faithless, demon. -Denotes any non-humanoid of intelligence.\nWithout the 'a' the word becomes an insult to call someone disfigured. Know that intentional cursing in Elven attracts ill-willing aspects.\n-------------------Aengul: Aengul - race of semi-divine beings affiliated with light.\n-------------------aetah: age\n-------------------aeth: aether\n-------------------aher: to bless.\n-------------------ahern: blessing.\n-------------------akal: valuable, golden - less ominous.\n-------------------akaleh: wealth, money, valuable.\n-------------------akalin: shop\n-------------------\nakaln: gold - less ominous way to speak of gold.\n-------------------allim: sin  - allimar: to sin; alliman: sinful.\n-------------------andria: hope\n-------------------annyer: to dance\n-------------------Asul: Sun\n------------------\nata: ugly, tainted\n-------------------athri: serpent, snake\n-------------------a)iler: Wizard, conjurer, heretic -Any wizard or spiritual humanoid that's not a druid. When one forfeits the 'a' in the word it becomes an insult.\nIt should be noted that the word 'Ailer' is not used for your everyday magic user, but reserved for a powerful, exclusive brand of spellcasters.\n-------------------alor: salmon -aloronn/alonn: salty\n-------------------ame: forest\n-------------------\nannil: guiding -anniler: to guide, direct\n-------------------ante: Last\n-------------------arche: infrequent, sporadic, rare.\n-------------------(a)rhil: just fair (without the a:) vengeful, scorned.\n-------------------\nasimulum: sanctuary, glade, asylum, dedicated place (esp. one of nature).\n-------------------aver: to wander, to roam around aimlessly, to be confused.\n-------------------avern: wanderer, confused person.\n-------------------ayal: yellow\n------------------\nay(i)lu: liked, likable - may(i)lu: loved. The 'i' is optional, but may not be used if the object is not a person.\n-------------------ayla: good\n-------------------barbu: dark, black, hedgehog\n-------------------bilok: to create, to make.\n----\nBortu: dwarf\n-------------------(a)caele: sky. - The plural, caelan, is used to reference to the Seven skies and with that, God. The lack of an 'a' in front makes the word disrespectful or insulting.\n-------------------celia: star\n-------------------\nceru: strong, mighty\n-------------------chirr: wolf\n-------------------chul: to must\n-------------------cihi: city\n-------------------cinh: to stop, to cease\n-------------------ciw: to sleep\n-------------------\ncrua: hurting, suffering - which makes cruan: pain\n-------------------Daemon: Daemon - race of semi-divines affiliated with smoke and fire.\n-------------------dion: long - dionne: short\n-------------------divu: empty\n------------------\ndrui: Druid\n-------------------draoi: Evil/corrupted druid. Warlock. Sorcerer.\n-------------------ehier: to find\n-------------------enaer: guest\n-------------------ento: next\n-------------------ern: to can, to be able to\n-------------------eth: end, to end, to die -word can be a noun, passive verb and active verb depending on usage.\n-------------------evar: to protect, preserve\n-------------------eyr: to use, apply\n-------------------faesu: horse\n------------------\nfer: tool - This word creates a wide array of words for various tools, such as torches and pickaxes. They are discussed further in 'curious words'.\n-------------------fenn: ice\n-------------------feta: cold\n-------------------fih: to eat, to feed\n--------\nfin: region\n-------------------fiyem: reborn, renewed, revitalized\n-------------------fyu: full\n-------------------hae: her\n-------------------haelun: mother\n-------------------haler: to promise, to pledge\n------------------\nharriel: square\n-------------------heial: circle, council\n-------------------hier: near\n-------------------hileia: Peace. - Has no plural\n-------------------hiuw: to twitch, to move\n-------------------hiylu: Healthy / spirited\n------------------\nibar: white, owl\n-------------------igne: flame, fire - igne'sae: destruction, war\n-------------------iheiuh: wind\n-------------------iheiuhii: voice, breath\n-------------------ihn: stick\n-------------------ihnsil: spear\n------------------\nikur: wrought, cold, gray\n-------------------ikurn: iron\n-------------------ikru: foolish\n-------------------iller: to give, to grand\n-------------------ilum: glowing - when something only reflects light. ilumr: to glow.\n------------------\ninder: to read\n-------------------indor: book\n-------------------irrin: glass\n-------------------ito: to, here, within - Can also be a suffix. More on this in 'curious words'\n-------------------iyat: thought\n-------------------iyath: to think\n-------------------iylanh: to place\n-------------------kae: me\n-------------------kalem: Malin's year, - Roughly 26 Anthos years, denotes the length of a High Prince's reign.\n-------------------kaliri: Event, party\n-------------------\nkarim: Fortday, - The measure of time from one Dwarven sleeping cycle to the next, roughly 24 Anthos days.\n------------------\nkarin(ah): sunrise, - Also used to speak of day\n-------------------kathir: foe\n-------------------\nker: dark, sunset - Also used to speak of night\n-------------------khel: darkness - Strictly a noun\n-------------------kina: murdered, killed, death, in mortal danger-a noun used for someone who is marked for death by someone or something.\n---------------\nkrafeh: corpse\n-------------------krey: single. kreyn - pair.\n-------------------lae: him\n-------------------lar: sturdy, steadfast.\n-------------------lareh: boulder, mountain, rock\n-------------------larebil: anvil\n------------------\nlarueth: netherrack\n-------------------laureh: chosen, favored, noble\n-------------------laurir: Noble\n-------------------len: song\n-------------------lenaern: inn\n-------------------lenniel: melody\n-------------------lent: to (over)grow\n-------------------\nlenti: growth, moss.\n-------------------lentos: brown\n-------------------leyu: graceful, beautiful.\n-------------------liewyn: smile - liewynh: to smile, liewyn'saer: to entertain, to amuse; maliewynh: to laugh\n-------------------\nlle: kindness, good intention\n-------------------lie: dishonest, untrue, false, deceitful -lien: lie , liear: to lie; maliear: to betray (malieir: traitor)\n-------------------llir: friend\n-------------------lin: home, place -linan'sae: town\n--------------\nllum: sorrowful, mourning - llum'saer: to sadden\n-------------------llyt: aggressive, intimidating, -llyth: to strike\n-------------------lom: to fragment, to splinter\n-------------------lomi: fragment, splinter, pebble\n------------------\nlomal: flint\n-------------------luci: grape - lucionn: sour\n-------------------luer:  to imprison, to cage \n-------------------luerlent: flowerpot\n-------------------lye: we - Used to speak of elves, a people, or even the world as a whole. Often misused.\n-------------------lyu: silent - leads to the words lyun: silence; lyuer: to silence and lyu'saer: to censor \n-------------------maehr: wisdom, knowledge\n-------------------maelu: to know\n-------------------mahnih: wall\n-------------------\nMali: elf/elven - Word is both singular and plural, noun and adjective.\n-------------------maln: father\n-------------------marn: more\n-------------------marenne: less\n-------------------masuleh: anvil\n-------------------medi: helping, helpful\n-------------------meracahe: balance\n-------------------miar: lava miar'sae: griefing, harming, spiting\n-------------------mira: warm\n-------------------miruel: red\n-------------------myumier: to carry, to bring\n------------------", ""),
        StaticBook("Old Elven Part 2", "Anthron", "Continuation of Seed words from part 1\n1 - 27\n-------------------\nCurious Words 28- and on to part 3\n-------------------The credit for this dictionary goes to the maker, Sporadic whom made the post to make this book possible on the forums.\nnae: you\n-------------------naeri: devout one, cleric\n-------------------narn: to walk, travel through\n-------------------nealu: to teach\n-------------------nor: land - nor'sae: farming\n-------------------\no: why\n-------------------ohn: like - Either a prefix or stand-alone\n-------------------oment: to meet\n-------------------okar: to safeguard\n-------------------okarn: oasis\n-------------------orrar: to hunt\n-------------------\nothelu: sharp\n-------------------parir: harbinger\n-------------------pern: potato\n-------------------parsaer: to conceive, to kindle. \"to have an idea\". \n-------------------perith: to remember\n-------------------puer: to write, to draw, to scribble.\n-------------------puerith: to write down (of books), to memorize. to record (more formal than 'puer')\n-------------------riv: claw \n-------------------riurn: swamp\n-------------------\nraell: carrot, root, herb. (refers to subterranean parts of plants possessing healing or nourishing properties)\n-------------------san: word, news - saner: to speak\n-------------------saneyr: to debate, discuss. - saneyir: debater, public speaker, orator.\n-------------------salum: to feel, to sense\n-------------------seth: void\n-------------------sew: soup, stew\n-------------------siimah: tower\n-------------------sil: sword\n-------------------siol: lone, lonely\n-------------------\nsirame: Honor; connection to the forest. - Has no plural\n-------------------siru: mushroom -siruonn: bitter\n-------------------siyuer: to play\n-------------------\nsohae: devout one, cleric - In a militant or executive sense. sohaer: ruler (through teachings, more positive than uthir.)\n-------------------suika: sugar -\n-------------------sul: light - strictly a noun.\n-------------------sulier: to see\n-------\nsulii: eye\n-------------------sulirrin: glowstone\n-------------------taeleh: mind\n-------------------taelu: to learn\n-------------------tahorran: hawk\n-------------------tahu: high\n-------------------tahn: peak, height\n------------------\ntalar: horizon - considered a place between the heavens and earth;\n-------------------talareh: stone - evolved from the words tali and lareh.\n-------------------\ntaliyna: body, living being, aspect -'taliiyna' can be used to denoted the physical parts that allow the body to live, i.e. organ, heart, mind and blood\n-------------------taliiyh: to bleed\n-------------------taliyu: secret\n-------------------\nTalonnii: family, blood(line) - always capped\n-------------------tarem: Elven week -Equivalent to 7 'karim'\n-------------------tayna: life, energy of living\n-------------------taynei: green\n-------------------\ntaynuel: vitality, energetic, alive (noun or adverb dependent on positioning)\n-------------------tenna: until\n-------------------tennallar: ocean\n-------------------\nterh: to grind, process, churn - wehn'tehral: bread; valein'tehral: cheese; raell'tehral: salve, potion), luci'terhal: wine\n-------------------\ntiuth: triangle - the triangular shape is found as a symbol for oppression, unlike the circle, which is used to describe (just) councils; - taliuth: oppressor\n-------------------thruer: to explode\n-------------------thrur: explosive, volatile\n------------\nthrurn: explosive, dynamite\n-------------------thill: pure, refined, delicate\n-------------------thilln: silver\n-------------------thul: raw, unrefined, brusque\n-------------------thulith: metal\n-------------------thuln: ore, raw material\n-------------------tilru: serving, submissive\n-------------------tir: law\n-------------------tos: desert, wasteland, barren ground - tos'sae: razing\n-------------------tufer: to construct, to prop\n-------------------\ntur: bone\n-------------------turr: sheep\n-------------------tuva: everything, all\n-------------------\ntuveh: wool, something soft\n-------------------ueth: nether\n-------------------uhier: to seek\n-------------------\nulln: rot, withering - strictly a noun.\n-------------------ullr: rot, decay, wither - Often of dead flora.\n-------------------ullral: monster, undead -lit: decayed. Used for living beings that have severed themselves from the natural way.\n----------------\nuradh: to scowl.\n-------------------uradir: naysayer\n-------------------uriar: to shout\n-------------------urth: to rule - negative connotation to this word.\n-------------------urthir: ruler\n-------------------maruthir: tyrant\n------------------\nUruk: Orc\n-------------------ut: because\n-------------------uvul: powder\n-------------------Valah: human\n-------------------vallei: water\n-------------------valein: milk\n-------------------vallel: blue\n------------------\nvallum: crying, griefing\n-------------------valmir: artist - from purple, the color of art.\n-------------------valmuel: purple\n-------------------van: to fare\n-------------------Velulaei: moon - Named after the moon goddess worshiped by the dark elves.\n-------------------vihai: whisper\n-------------------vilut: cow\n-------------------vira: vigilant, mournful - Traditionally used for attending the wake of vigil for a deceased Elf.\n-------------------vularale: bat\n-------------------\nvull: to deceive, to trick\n-------------------vuln: fox\n-------------------waleh: to heal\n-------------------wehn:  grass, grain.\n-------------------welier: to listen, to hear - rewelier: to repeat, to echo\n-------------------\nwy: appendage - more useful are the words that can be formed in combination with 'an*h': anah'wy: head; anoh'wy: foot, anih'wy: left hand, aneh'wy: right hand; anuh'wy: hand (either)\n-------------------wyl: while\n-------------------wyn: happy\n------------\ny: upon\n-------------------yallr: to live.\n-------------------yuln: gemstone - Used as the seed for many words, including maleyuln: diamond\n-------------------\nCURIOUS WORDS\nNo doubt you are left with many questions as you skim through the Elven wordlist. Indeed, their mystique and unique language has many intricacies which are not readily understood by those who did not take ample time studying them.\nFor perusal at your leniency, I have taken apart the words with most reason to spark confusion, and gave them the special attention they require in this section.\n-------------------\nito:\nThis particle is a means to denote location. However, it has many different meanings, depending on its location and accompanying words in a sentence.\nThe simplest case:\nito narne: I/he walk(s) here / I/he walk(s) within.\nNo ambiguity arises here. However, when we add a noun to this:\name ito narne: The forests walks here\nito ame narne: I/he walk(s) to the forest.\name'ito narne: I/He walk(s) within the forest\nameto'eyha narne: And I/he walk(s) within the forest\name'eyha ito narne: And the forest walks here.\nNote that the location of ito becomes crucial here. Putting it on the place of a subject aligns it to the meaning 'to'. On the object's location\n(to understand this, educate yourself on the elven sentence order), the noun 'ame' becomes the subject instead, and ito regains the meaning 'within' or 'here'.\nIto can also be used as a suffix, which is demonstrated by the last examples. In this case, the\naddition of ito restricts the noun to an object.\nThis word is also used to create the elvish equivalent to the words 'to be' and 'to have'. This particularly might confuse a novice to the language. to read upon this, I refer you to the grammar section of\nthis tome.\n-------------------tali-:\nThis word can do two very different things to the word it is connected to. In the simplest case, it adds the modifier 'part of' to the linked noun. But sometimes, the addition of this prefix creates a whole new word:\ntalibortu: Part dwarf -> Halfling\ntalivalah: Part human -> Halfling\ntal'uruk: Part Orc -> Goblin.\n\nHowever, sometimes ambiguity can abound from this word. For example:\ntaliame: Tree\ntal'ame: Part of the forest.\n\nIt would seem that in confusing cases, elves add an apostrophe between to denote they mean multiple words rather than a single noun. However, this is not always a safe rule,\nas it may clash with other rules for using apostrophes. Caution is urged, and ambiguity may indeed arise.\n--------------------sae:\nThis word, meaning the development, spreading or management of something, can be used to create many new words in elvish\nSome of these may not seem obvious. Examples include:\n\nnor'sae: development of land -> farming\ntos'sae: spreading of desert -> pillaging\nThe combinations grow even more numerous when we consider that sae can be combined with\nir to create sair, lending a word for, exemplum gratia, 'farmer' and 'pillager'. It can also be turned to saean to create a \nplural (where applicable) and saer, turning the word into the stem of a verb, effectively giving us the words 'to farm' and\n'to pillage'.\nThese further combinations exist almost always wherever a word was created from the suffix sae. Knowledge of this fact will allow the reader much more affluency in this language.\n-------------------\nma-:\nApart from being an intensifier, used at your leisure, the prefix ma- often finds its home in places where the intense use of affix would cause the same affix to start. An example of this is found in the word 'log': The elves would consider a log\n'part of tree' thus 'part of a part of the forest'. However, talitaliame is NOT a correct word to use in Elven.\nInstead, the second tali is implied by the use of the intesifier. Thus:\nmataliame: Log\nIf used in an event where a repeated suffix is used, ma- is placed after the seed word, but before the suffix one wishes to repeat.\nInterestingly enough, ma- is one of the rare affixes that does stack on certain occasions, as seen in the word marmatelu.\n-------------------onn:\n\nThis word is much like tali, in that it can be used to further define a certain word, as well as be used to create a noun of it's own. Also like tali, he inclusion of an apostrophe in a confusing case will imply the speaker is\nmeaning to talk of multiple words\nAn example:\n\name'onn: Born of the Forest, from the forest.\namonn: wood\nThe suffix will create a myriad of new words for the advanced speaker. Study of its usage will surely be beneficial.\n-------------------fer:\n\ngrammatically, nothing is strange about this word. However, its uses in combinations are so numerous that a separate section may be devoted to it.\nfer'ame: Axe\nfer'thuln: Pickaxe\nfer'nor: shovel\nfer'sul: torch\nfer'bilok: hammer\nfer'norsae: hoe\nfer'puer: quill. Pen. A tool for writing.\nOther uses can be thought up by the cunning mind. However, bear in mind that the word fer is mostly used to speak of handheld tools, not of crafting tables, anvils or furnaces.\n--", ""),
        StaticBook("Old Elven Part 3", "Anthron", "Curious words as a continuation from part 2. 1 - 4\n-------------------Elven Grammar 5 - 41\n------------------\nElven counting 42 - 45\n-------------------more on part 3\n-------------------\nAgain, more thanks to Sporadic for making the post!\nan*h- (a/o/i/e/u):\n\nBy adding one these five vowels into the place of the asterisk the prefix takes on a new usage. This prefix gains many new meanings when combined with other affixes and relies heavily on context.\nSentence order is especially important when using this prefix multiple times.\nElan*h [a/o/i/e]: North, South, East and West respectively.\n\nanh'sae: expanding, stretching - replacing the apostrophe with a vowel specifies in which direction.\nan*h il'an*h: - refers to a diagonal path, defined by the vowel used.\n-------------------\nElven Grammar.\n-------------------Sentence Order: The common order of elvish sentences is Subject-object-verb.\n\nPlural nouns: indicated by the suffix -an.\nThe apostrophe: Used in certain cases and possibly dictates a certain pronunciation.\nCurrent known uses are:\n\n*Denoting certain honorifics (such as sainted)\n*Between a modifier and a verb\n*Between a word and its suffixes (more on this below).\n*With the word 'eyha' (and) to connect sentences and and in terms of a list.\nUse of affixes: This has shown to be a very important factor in Elven language. Elves, tend to use multiple affixes to further define their nouns and verbs, rather than using separate words. For these, the following rules exist:\n*Most affixes seems to have two forms, depending on wether or not the word it relates to ends or begins with a vowel\n*Suffixes often get added with an apostrophe between it and the word.\n*If a word has multiple suffixes, there will only be an apostrophe between the full word and its last suffix. The priority in ordering the suffixes remains unknown.\n*If a suffix is seperated by the apostrophe, it inflects as if the last letter of the word it relates to was a consonant.\n\nRecombination of words:\nWhen one understands the heavy use of affixes and their combining to forge words,\nit is not surprising that the elves relied on a dictionary where this recombination was prevalent in creating new words. \nTheir core words were little, but their abundant use of affixes and linking words still allowed them to describe nearly anything\nunder the sun.\nExamples of this are:\nnarn'vallei: travel'water -> swim\nnarn'acaele: travel'sky -> fly\nathrinarn'acaele: serpenttraveling'sky -> Dragon.\npar'karinah: Before the sunrise -> Dawn\npar'ker: Before the sunset -> Dusk.\nuvul'igne: Firepowder -> Ash\nuvul'thrurn: Explosive powder -> Gunpowder\n\nWe also see this occuring a lot when the word tali is involved.\nMany more examples may be discovered by the studious scholar soon. Take heed, however, that any particular combination, while perhaps comprehensible, may not be correct. The language of nature seems very particular on what words may coexist in harmony.\n-------------------Affiliation: The suffix 'leh (note the apostrophe) designates affiliation, the possession of the object by the subject.\nNote: If no object is present, the suffix is implied to mean something similar to 'lives' or 'spirit'. However,\nit is not placed in translation\nEx: part of a common farewell 'kaean'leh evareh', means 'may (it) protect our spirit' literally, but is translated to 'may (it) protect us'.\n-------------------\nKnown inflections of the elven verbs:\n\nIn this section, the dash indicates the stem of the word.\n\n*-e: First person singular\n\n*-a: Second person singular\n*-e: Third person singular\n*-ae: First person plural\n*-an: second person plural\n*-eyae: Third person plural\n*-eh: Optative singular\n*-erih: Optative plural The optative inflection changes slightly depending on the ending letters of a\nverb. Most verbs end in -er, but if one ends in -ar the plural inflection of the optative becomes -arih ( the -ar- syllable is not repeated!). A verb ending in -h will have the -h- replaced by the full inflection of the optative (thus eth becoming eteh\nand eterih. Other irregular verbs have the inflection simply added to the end.\n\n*The stem of a verb functions as a singular imperative.\n*The stem of a verb used as a suffix to a noun (apostrophe where required) is a modifier\n*The optative serves as a wish ('may' + verb) in most cases. However, adding the negative (-ne) affix after the verb indicates the lack of permission.\n-------------------Pronouns as a subject:\nA pronoun is usually not used to denote the subject of a word, as the inflection of the verb itself would usually denote in which person the verb is denoted. However, on occasion a rather critical ambiguity may arise, like in the following example:\name ignere: The forest burns.\nShould we 'assume' a pronoun here, we can suggest that this sentence may also be translated as 'I burn the forest' or even 'he burns the forest'. This ambiguity is too serious to ignore, and therefore we are expected\nto place another pronoun here as the subject if we wish to denote an action like such:\n\nkae ame ignere: I burn the forest.\nKnow that a pronoun used as a subject alone never gains the suffix 'leh' (this is possible when it is an object, making the implied translation \"spirit\".)\n-------------------Infinitive:\nAn infinitive can be made by tying two verbs together with an apostrophe and adding an 'o' ('or-') prefix to the second verb (the infinitive). The first verb is usually the assisting verb (possibilities include can, must, will) and needs to be the only\nstem.\nThe infinitive verb receives the inflections similar to the way to a simple, single verbed sentence would.\nBy Example:\nito ame ern'onarna: You can travel to the woods\nAn infinitve can also occur without an assisting verb. In this case, you shall add an o prefix to the verb, but no inflection. An example of this:\nito ame onarn: to travel to the woods.\nTake note that the infinitive must not be used to express a wish, as only the optative form may serve this purpose.\nPast and future tense:\n\nTo create a past or future tense, we first need to take notice to the time of day. The words an elf uses for tenses depends on whether it's day or night at the time of speaking.\nFor a past or future tense, we add one of these four words at the very end of a sentence:\nkarin'ento: next sunrise\nkarin'ante: last sunrise\nker'ento: next sunset\nker'ante: last sunset\nThough elves have taken to shortening this to karinto, karinte, kento and kente in more casual speech.\nIf it is night, we use ker'ante for past, and karin'ento for future tense.\nIf it is day, we use karin'ante for past, and ker'ento for future tense.\n\nThis remains true even if the time period one wishes to denote is more than one sunset or sunrise from the time of speaking.\n-------------------\nThe verbs 'to be' and 'to have'\nBefore reading this, you might find it beneficial to have read about the word 'ito' in the 'CURIOUS WORDS' section.\nNote that:\nito kae: To him / on him\nito kae'leh: To his spirit / on his spirit ('spirit' being implied)\nNow, should we place a noun in front of this:\nmali ito kae: An elf to him. -In elvish, this is the equivalent of 'He has an elf'.\nmali ito kae'leh: An elf to his spirit. The equivalent of 'He is an elf'.\nIt becomes easy to mix these meanings up. After all, kae'leh is the possessive form and seems the logical candidate for the verb 'to have'.\nHowever, the implied addition of 'spirit' is very important here. Elves speak of their spirit, or essence, as\nsomething deeply intimate. When something belongs to you as a person, you have it. But when something belongs to your essence, you are it.\n-------------------\nCAPITALIZATION\n\nElves do not capitalize the word at the beginning of a sentence the way we do in common. Instead, only on a select few occasions are words capitalized, namely:\n\n*Names (of persons, places, titles)\n*Races (Mali, Valah, etc.)\n*Objects of worship (Usul, Velulaei), even when the word's not used to denote the divine being tied to the object\n*When denoting aspects of nature (such as Vuln being the aspect fox.)\n*Words of significant concepts are sometimes capitalized, such as 'lye', 'talar', 'acaele' and 'tuva'\n-------------------\nELVEN COUNTING\n\nElves, while their numerical system remains a subject of discussion, were able to write numbers 'in full' like we are. That is to say, they could name numbers of any size in regular conversation.\nAn Elven number, when expressed in full, consists of multiple words. Each word represents an order of magnitude. They were written in descending order, meaning the largest part of the number came first.\nElves had words for the numbers one to ten, and words for a hundred, a thousand, and so on. To denote multiples of hundred,\nhey would take a number from one to ten, and put it in front of a word denoting an order of magnitude, linking the two with an\naprostrophe\nDiscovered words for elven numbers are as follows:\n1: oem\n2: niut\n3: hael\n4: vailu\n5: kulin\n6: banih\n7: laier\n8: esun\n9: moiel\n10: telu\n100: matelu [ma-(mar-) a preffix for 'very']\n1000: marmatelu\n-------------------CONVENTIONS OF THE ELVEN LANGUAGE \n-continues to part 4-", ""),
        StaticBook("Old Elven Part 4", "Anthron", "Continuarion of part 3!\n-------------------CONVENTIONS OF THE ELVEN LANGUAGE 1 - 7\n-------------------\nThis is the last book, so final thanks to Sporadic for making these books possible on the forums!\nThe elven tongue was almost exclusively spoken. Evidence for this is found in the ruins of Inwe, where the writings of early Dark Elves were in an ancient dialect of the common tongue. The most often used elven words in writing are names\nsuch as Mali'nor (now Malinor) and Velulaei.\nWhatever caused the language to change so drastically through the ages is not known, but is especially remarkable due to the long lives of elves, which would presumably make evolution of a language go much slower.\nIt is suggested that a taboo on elvish was enforced, though why and by who remains unknown.\nIt has been rumored that elvish was also the language of nature, which the elves either learned through study or was taught to them by certain deities.\nThey then had the power to converse with nature directly, allowing\nthem to guide the growth of trees and migration of animals as if by magic. If this were true, elvish, assuming someone was capable of mastering the long-forgotten cadence of the words, would be a very potent language indeed.", ""),
        StaticBook("Old Report", "William Roswell", "Palkasatori\nManpower:\n5 perm. 10 on call\n\nLarge HQ in Alsace\n\nThey serve the Falkenraths and De Solas\n\nRelations must be confidential\nthanks for the gear by the way", ""),
        StaticBook("On ArcaneJournal", "Jerry_Man", "The Arcane Mage could feel the node of Bellus Turris at incredible distance. Perhaps faintly, but direct enough to make a long distance jump across a journey of many weeks by boat. If his path could be directed so straightly then my own senses have\nmuch farther to come. I sat in that ancient library for hours and must have been within a few yards of the node itself, yet was I not consumed by its energy. I do pray that I have grown since such days and that my foolishness now is not so great to\nupon a stabilised connection to the Void in ignorance of its incomparable power. By the creator, I hope my studies have proven themselves.\nThe node's effect upon the surrounds were minimal considering the time it had remained. That is under the assumption that the dating of that library was accurate at 1100 years. Such symmetry is not typical of a Void Hollows, so I hope that\nI was not so blind to have been sat in a creation of that node and beleived it to have been the remains of an Ancient Mage Sect. Such detail. Every book, every page, every word. If it was an illusion, or a morphing of reality, its existence was too real.\nFloating chunks of land, and strange beasts. It is not so uncommon. Certainly a Hollows will commonly generate such oddities. So why after such a length of time would so few peculiarities arisen? One can only assert that the Node had\nbeen previously stable, perhaps even dormant. The Arcane Mage's seal was probably the only thing that stopped it from graduating to a Void Hollows. But the power could not be contained. We knew the explosion was going to happen days before it did.\nFor all that time while the original inhabitants of that tower conducted studies into the arcane not dissimilar from our own, could the Void Node really have existed harmoniously? Perhaps the thing that upset the Node's balance was what caused\nthem to flee. Perhaps they knew the dangers unfolding as the Node became unstable. Or perhaps they were not so knowledgable and were somehow consumed by its great power and exist now as nothing more than a current of energy within the Void Realm.\nIf I were to create the tiniest rift near this new Node that would be the end. All the marvels of its instability would be removed instantaneously. Even though this one is not nearly as powerful as Asulon's Bellus Turris, the connection is still the same.\nThe void can slip into our world and we have known who know how to stop it. Arcane Mage sealed such a place so that none could enter. We have done likewise, yet there are too many curious eyes that dare to push that boundary.\nThey know not what they seek. Nor do I. Perhaps I could journey deeper to discover the secrets he spoke of. Perhaps my understanding is now great enough.\nAn explosion outward that pulls everything inward. And then nothing. A peace. Towers levelled. Lives devoured. The Void Node is a supply of such great power, yet we know so little. The Void has no enemies. It harbors no discrimination.\nOnly perfect destruction the likes of which none can escape. All is consumed and turned into energy, an energy which feeds this world. Operating in a cycle too great for our fraction of time to perceive.\nAre we not just waiting for the next surge to destroy even us?\nI journey deeper.", ""),
        StaticBook("On Dragonkin", "Aruzond, Warden of the North", "On Dragonkin\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWritten and recorded by Aruzond, Warden of the North\nThere are two species of dragonkin known to have descended from Dragur, though corrupted and defiled forms of both have formerly existed. Dragur, the Daemon of Knowledge, oftentimes appears as a dragon in his true form.\nDragur’s chief descendants, the Dragaar, are sentient “greater dragons” that seek to collect and share knowledge with mortals. They can conceal themselves under the guise of mortals and other creatures, though it is a rare occasion to meet a Dragaar in\nperson. Dragons, on the other hand, are more common -- though proportionately rare compared to other living beings -- than greater dragons. They are generally referred to as dragons, though the proper classification\nis “lesser dragon.” They are non sentient, and are commonly viewed as natural predators that roam our world. However, like all living beings, they are susceptible to taint and corruption. Though a rare occasion, dragons have been known to alter their\nvery nature into a twisted, foul portrayal consumed by hatred and rage for the mortal races. The outcome of this calamity is referred to as a drake. Little is known about the purpose of the dragonkin as a whole, except that each species' intentions vary\ngreatly. \n\nDragon\n \n    The dragon (or lesser dragon) species is beastlike in nature, and generally accepted to be non sentient. Though they cannot process thought or comprehend concepts, they are highly\nintelligent in regards to instinct. The lesser dragon was once a rare sight to mortals, but they have appeared to exist in greater numbers in Anthos. Particularly, dragons roam the frozen wastelands of the North. Though they live in other regions\nof Anthos, the frigid North is home to many deep caves and caverns, which they prefer for nesting purposes. The region is cold, secluded, and tribesmen allow the food supply to remain steady. Dragons may roam the land of Anthos in search of cattle\nor mortals if they grow tired of pork flesh, and they continue to hunt until they are satisfied. Although lesser dragons have been frequently encountered in recent times, their numbers are slowly dwindling as they have begun to retreat to the deep,\ncold North.\n\nDrake\n \n    “Do not underestimate the allure of darkness, mortal. Your kind has yet to discover that even the purest of souls are drawn to it.” -Aruzond.\nThrough the malevolent desires of twisted corruption, the dragon has the potential to unwillingly alter its very existence. When a dragon degrades into a drake, its body decays into a shadowy skeleton of its former self.\nThe scales of a drake turn black as midnight, and their fangs cloak themselves in putrefying poison. While a drake does not develop sentience, its desires can be manipulated to follow any malignant alignment. Drakes forfeit any connection to their\nformer selves, and their lust for eternal damnation of life causes them to instill fear into those who would oppose it. The drake is slightly smaller in size compared to a dragon, but what it lacks in size and physical strength is compensated\nby maneuverability and lethality. It is rare to encounter an evil powerful enough to twist and taint the mind of the mighty dragon, but these influences communicate through our world more readily than we may often realize.\nDragaar\n \n    Bestowed with the gift of immortality and sentience, the Dragaar exist chiefly to seek and gather knowledge. Each Dragaar is assigned the role of gathering a distinct aspect of knowledge, and this role defines their characteristics\nand demeanor. However, they do not solely desire knowledge and wisdom. The greater dragons, or Dragaar, act as ever watching wardens over the mortal races. However, most prefer to live in seclusion  in deep thought and meditation, only to emerge when\nthe races call upon them in their time of need. The Dragaar can and often disguise themselves under the guise of a mortal or creature. This is their preferred method of watching over mortals, as it is both subtle and inconspicuous. There are only\ntwo Dragaar that have revealed themselves to the mortal races, and the decision to do so was not lightly concluded. Though the Dragaar seek to protect the mortal races, they often decide to do so by living in secrecy. Many discern that if they\nchose to associate themselves with mortal affairs too frequently, dire consequences would arise. The primary concern held by Dragaar, in regards to involvement in the matters of mortals, is that the races would eventually resort to\nrelying on them to protect mortals from any form of evil. However, the greater dragons aspire to teach the mortal races self-preservation, without them depending on the intervention of immortals.\nDrakaar\n\nTo attempt to bend the will of a Dragaar would be all for naught, and thus what can cause a Dragaar to devolve itself into a twisted abomination of hatred is unknown. The ancient ancestors of the four mortal races once believed that\nthe choice for this horrendous transformation to occur was their own, though the aftermath of such a decision would contradict their entrusted duty. The greater dragon itself is a terrifying opponent, but a Dragaar that feels no remorse and fears\nno consequence? That is the true fear of corruption; the perversion of the mind and distortion of one’s morality is a dreadful circumstance. In past times, the outcome of such an event would often be referred to as a “greater drake.”\nIt is the combination of a Dragaar’s sentience and a drake’s all-consuming hatred. The likelihood of a Dragaar devolving into a being tormented by rage is next to none, as most would, in their right mind, turn themselves into stone to prevent the\nconversion. The only verifiable event recorded of a Dragaar turning to stone is that of Velketzar. The Guardian Velketzar, consumed by self-condemnation and despair due to his failure, dramatically transformed into a stone statue\nto prevent the inevitable calamity. If a Dragaar were to fall under the hand of corruption, it would hold the power to politically corrupt nations with ease. Additionally, detecting and pursuing such a foe would be toilsome, all due to its shape shifting\nability. Fortunately, deep meditation and concentration can inhibit this rare occurrence, along with immersing themselves in their aspect of knowledge.", ""),
        StaticBook("On Dreadknights", "The Eternal Librarian", "On Dreadknights\n\n by Cir'dian Nymphea\nIf this book is found anywhere else than the Eternal Library of Annil’sul, the Enclave of Haelun’or, it is stolen.\n\nBoth buyers and sellers will be punished.\n\nSigned, the Eternal Librarian\nGenesis\nThe dreadknights are beings comprised of suits of armor bound together through the engraving of specific runes unique to the blood mages, of whom draw their magic from a deity presently unknown to me. They originated in the land of Asulon,\nas did their constructs (The dreadknights). As it stands however, it is somewhat of a mystery as to where the blood mages came from, and how their magic came to be. However it is known that the dreadknights are the tools of the blood mages. They are the\nstrength behind them.\n\nCreation\nThe dreadknights are created over the process of several months through a long and arduous ritual involving the removal of freedom and physical flesh. The process begins with the abduction of a\nspecific choice being. The selection is not bound to physical aspects (Which would be removed in the end anyway) but rather decided by the quality of those taken. Usually of whom are stubborn and loyal to whom they serve. In post of their abduction- the\npatron is taken to which ever stronghold the dreadknights would presently occupy. From there they would be bound. The dreadlord then begins the process with the destruction of both knee caps, as to prevent escape and standing. No sedatives are used in\nthis process. The patron is then fitted with a suit of armor usually comprised of a metal known as black iron (Known for being particularly dense, and durable). The leader then engraves several runic symbols into the armor of the victim. This can be done\nby both dreadlord, and blood mage. These runes bind the soul of said victim to the suit of armor. Once finished, the subject is cast into a cell, and left for several days before further work is done. For the next few weeks the victim is tortured, their\nrunes left open to rot. Flesh eventually gives way, leaving nothing but the victim’s skeletal form beneath. Alongside this, the will of the victim is broken, and they are taught to love pain. By two months, the process is complete, and the new dreadknight\nmay now set out to do the will of their dreadlord or blood mage.", ""),
        StaticBook("On Fire Evoking", "ScribeOfMilo", "[!] Delwyn Daeynore is scratched out cruedly in the cover of the book.\nBasic learning technique for Fire Evocation-\n\nAt first I should begin with learning the basics of flame in this plain of existence. \nOnce I gain enough information I shall spend the first five elven days in deep meditation, to-\n- improve my new found connection with the void. I should remember that meditation may be done in any state of peace. Thirty minutes each day. Once in the morning, once after breakfast, etc. Now, once I begin my lessons, I should deeply observe-\n-the abilities of fire, how it spreads and such. Perhaps I shall find a mage of high caliber who may demonstrate. After the first elven week, I should begin to conjure up small flames- or at least attempt. Once every two days, after my third-\n- meditation. This will keep me practiced, but also improve my connection with the void. I must be strong willed. After this elven week, I may work to conjure larger flames (Note: The volume states that these may be no larger then an elven fist.)\nIt may take years after this before I am able to correctly hurl a ball of evoked flame.\nImportant notes from this days study- \n\nEvoked flame is not truly real, it only appears that way- the effects are real.\n\nIt is important to rest frequently when experiencing mana fatigue.\n'A Study of Flame'\n\n[!] A small painting is pinned to the remainder of the page. It depicts a blazing fire, though artfully draw as if it were a rune.\nAt a chemical basis-\n\nFire is the reaction of air, friction, and a sort of fuel. Without one of these three elements, the fire would never light- and if not, you would produce sparks at the very least.\nWhen a flame is lit, it eats away at the air around it, using it as a life source which it cannot replenish. So, on this notion, if you were to light a fire in a place with no existing air- if you were ever able to atleast- it would cause nothing.\nA statement taken from a book titled\n 'The Elements' on Fire.\n\n 'It has been an important part of all cultures and religions in the past to modern day Aegis. Fire is bright, strong, takes charge, and wants control. Fire is-\n- never subtle, it proudly shows itself. Fire never stops unless acted upon by another element or force, and it consumes everything it touches.'\n\nEnd statement.\n", ""),
        StaticBook("On Mali'Aheral", "Jistuma", "The Mali’Aheral, the blessed elves. Or as others call them: High elves.\nThis elven people is one of the most hated by their own race. People see them as racists and don’t think they’ll do any good. They kill members of their own race, for they are ‘impure’. However is this true?\nI say not.\n\nThe culture of the Mali’Aheral goes back to a specific elf known as Larihei lomahnih. She was one of elven kind who bathed in water that contained enchanted golden\nParticles, which had changed their appearance. Her elven kind had a paler skin and longer ears than the other elven kinds. They were also taller and their hair had become fiarer.\nThe change that these particles had made to them also was psychological, these early Mali’aheral had shifted away from their original beliefs and thought more logical.\nThey became scolars, one of which was Larihei.\nAs a child of her kin she learned their traditions and ideals. And soon she appeared to be a master in all subjects a Mali’Aheral was expected to learn. She grew up to become\nthe leader  of the Mali’Aheral and to be their support in the council of princes in Malinor. However she promoted the Mali’Aheral’s believes in the council: That elves were the superior race of Aegis and that they should also rule the other races.\nHowever the other princes and princesses said that she was only promoting war and was a danger to the elves. Larihei was banished from Malinor.\nHalf of the Mali’Aheral followed Larihei when she went to find a new home for their race. The other half remained with Malinor, slowly losing their culture. However when those high elves found the ruins of their kin in Asulon, the old culture was revived.\nMaehr’sae Hiylun’ehya That is the core of the Mali’Aheral culture. It can be translated as “Progress and Heath”.\nBreaking that sentence down, you can split it up in two parts: Maehr’sae, or Progress and Hiylun’ehya, or Heath.\nMaehr’sae refers to the development of knowledge and the value of it. It is the base of the position of science in their society, as scientist stand in a high position die to their ability to recover truth and knowledge.\nHiylun’ehya stands for the heath and purity of the Mali’Aheral race. They wish to keep their race from mixing blood, because they believe that their body is the perfect design. A Mali’Aheral is not to ruin his body, he shall not damage it in any way.\nThis means that he or she will not tattoo nor scar their Body out of free will. They are also not to mix blood with other races, because they produce children who are not ‘perfect’ anymore. This includes intimate contact with other races, because they\nthey would risk creating a ‘failure’, a child of mixed blood.\nAnother important part of their culture is that they believe to be superior to other races, die to them able to think clear because they do not follow any god.\nThey frown down upon the religions of other races, however they respect those who show to be wise and able to think logical.\nCombat is another thing that is frowned down upon, as it is an action that does not require thinking and will only cause the loss of people. That last one is very important: The Mali’Aheral are, of course also subject to the curse of Malin's children,\ntherefor all lives are valued high and loss of one is a waste. It is therefore also expected of all Mali’Aheral to create children at some point in their life.\nThe Mali’Aheral frown down upon those of mixed blood, they moght discriminate them, they are not violent however. Their only violent people are the sillumir, the mourning blades, they are the Mali who give their sacrifice to the race by taking up the\nsword. Their name comes from the need for them to fight, a sad thing to do in their society. They are respected for their sacrifice and remain few in number, due to the morals of their race. A Mali’Aheral does not fight unless necessary.\nI hope this has enlighted you about the Mali’Aheral.\n\nThink about this next time you see them, and try to understand their actions.\n\nMaehr’sae Hiylin’eyha\n\n -Vallel’Yuln Aeléyèlsa\nOn Mali’Aheral\n\nWritten by Vallel’Yuln\nCopied by Rosso\nVaerHaven", ""),
        StaticBook("On MaliAheral", "Leyu", "On Mali'Aheral\nThe Mali'Aheral, the blessed elves. Or as others call them: High Elves.\nThese Elven people are one of the most hated by their own race. People see them as racists and don't think they'll do any good. They kill members of thier\nown race, for they are 'impure'. However is this true?\nI say not. The culture of the Mali'Aheral goes back to a specific elf known as Larihei Lomahnih. She was one of Elven kind who bathed in water that contained\nenchanted golden particles, which had changed their appearance. Her Elven kind had a paler skin and longer ears than the other elven kinds. They were also taller and their hair had become fairer. The change that\nthese particles had made to them also was psycological. These early Mali'Aheral had shifted away from their original beliefs and thought more logical. They became scholars, one of which was Larihei.\nAs a child of her kin she learned their\ntraditions and ideals. And soon she appeared to be a master in all subjects a Mali'Aheral was expected to learn. She grew up to become the leader of the council of Princes in Malinor. However she prmoted the Mali'Aheral's\nbelif in the council: That Elves were the superior race of Aegis and that they should also rule the other races. However the other Princes and Princesses said that she was only promoting war and was a danger to the Elves. Larihei was\nbanished from Malinor. Half of the Mali'Aheral followed Larihei when she went to find a new home for their race. The other half remained with Malinor, slowly losing their culture. However when those High Elves found the ruins of their kin\nin Asulon, the old culture was revived. Maehr'sae Hiylun'ehya That is the core of the Mali'Aheral culture. It can be translated as \"Progress and Health\".\nBreaking that sentence down, you can split it up two parts: Maehr'sae,\nor Progress and Hiylun'ehya, or Health. Maehr'sae refers to the development of knowledge and the value of it. It is the base of the position of science in their society, as scientist stand in a high position due\nto their ability to recover truth and knowledge. Hiylun'ehya stands for the health and purity of the Mali'Aheral race. They wish to keep their race from mixing blood, because they believe that their body is the perfect design. A\nMali'Aheral is not to ruin his body, he shall not damage it in any way. This means that he or she will not tatoo nor scar their body out of free will. They are also not to mix blood with other races, because they produce children who are not 'perfect'\nanymore. This includes intimate contact with other races, because they woudl risk creating a 'failure' a child of mixed blood. Another important part of their culture is that they believe to be superior to other races, due to them able to think\nclear because they do not follow any god. They frown down upon the religions of other races, however they respect those who show to be wise and able to think logical. Combat is another thing that is frowned down upon. as it is an action that does\nnot require thinking and will only cause the loss of people that last one is very important: The mali'Aheral are, of course also subject to the curse of Malin's children, therefor all lives are valued high and loss of one is the waste. It is therefore\nalso expected of all Mali'Aheral to create children at some point in their life.\nThe Mali'Aheral frown down upon those of mixed blood, they might discriminate\nthem, they are not violent however. Their only violent people are sillumir, the Mourning\nBlades, they are the Mali who give their sacrifice to the race by taking up the sword. Thier name comes from the need for them to fight, a sad thing to do in their society, They are respected for their sacrifice and\nremain few in number, due to the morals of their race. A Mali'Aheral does not fight unless necessary. I hope this has enlighted you about the Mali'Aheral.\nThink about this next time you see them. and try to understand their actions.\nMaerh'sae Hiylun'eyha\n-Vallel'Yuln Aeleyelsa\nOriginally written in Aegis by an author Unknown.\nRe-scribed in Vailor by Leyu'Maehr from memory.", ""),
        StaticBook("On Man's Humors", "Jule Farmer", "\n\n\n\n      On Man's\n       Humours\n\n\n       By: Jule\n       Farmer\nMan likes to tell jokes.\n\nSometimes these jokes might seem serious.\n\nIf you're not aware that it's a joke, you might think man was intentionally stupid.\n\nReally, man was trying to be funny.\nMan will sometimes tell a joke to try to ease tension in a situation.\n\nOthertimes man would like to make friends.\n\nOccasionally humor can be an attempt to impress a potential mate.\nMan has developed humor for the purpose of bonding with his fellow man.\n\nHe will in these cases, try to use humor to bond with those who are not man.\n\nThis can sometimes lead to misunderstandings, and even insults.\nIf you come across a man who attempts to tell you a joke, and you cannot recognize the joke, there will be awkwardness.\n\nIf you recognize the joke, laugh at it, in an attempt to appease the man.\n\nSometimes he will even leave you alone.\nThis has been a guide to Man's Humors, in a non-medical sense.\n\nThank you for reading, please share with your friends and family.\n\n\n\n            FIN", ""),
        StaticBook("On Orcs", "Leyu", "On Orcs\nNo race is more adapted to the harsh deserts and its predators than the muscular Orcs. Masters o f combat and wrestling these 7ft high Goliaths can destroy most prey with their bare hands. To an Orc the most important\nthing is the hunt and the protection of his tribe and clan. For that reason, t they do not intermix with other races much. They have a mutual hatred and respect for the Dwarves of they do not intermix with other races much. They have a mutual hatred\nand respect for the Dwarves of Kal'Urguan because of their ferocity and strength. However, they heavily disdain other races simply because because of their ferocity and strength. However, they heavily disdain other races simply because because\nof their ferocity and strength. However, they heavily disdain other races simply because they're not big enough. Where other races establish cities and build monuments to their predecessors, Orcs establish tribes and build strong clans around the\nmythic figures of their ancestors. Orcs tend toward nomadic life, but what villages they do choose to establish are usually built from the ruins of other cities, swallowed and spat forth again by the drifting dunes of the desert. :: Although Orcs can\nnaturally live longer than humans, this rarely becomes a point of debate. The oldest Orc to ever live was Karugk of Clan Pyrathon, who died in glourious battle on the turning of his two hundredth year (Eight Hundred Orcish Seasons). Orcs are just as\nprolific as Dwarves and Humans, but their children have the shortest period of puberty, somestimes reaching full adulthood in only fifteen years. Orcs are also the race in Aegis most likely to cull their own, whether purposefully\nleaving the incapable behind in the desert, or removing the weak through ritual trials. However, the strength of the clan is paramount in an Orc's eyes, and they will defend their young and wise to their very last breath - even if it is only to give\nenough time for the wise to show the young how to best attack after their elder clansmen have fallen. :: Orcs are usually brownskinned with green tints, varying from sandy to dark green and rarely black, and their hair is usually very dark, with\nbrown and black the most common. Most Orcs follow the practice of coloring their hair with the blood of their enemies. Because their hair is so dark, this coloring rarely shows until many dousings, and it is considered a sign of a great warrior\nfor your hair to glow red with the blood of your enemies. Orcs of the Shaman bloodline tend more toward green skin and black hair, usually distinguished from other Orcs by :: Size and musculature set them apart from all other races on Aegis, sometimes\nconsidered giants at two and a half meters tall. Orcs of the goblin bloodline, are most notably smaller most notably smaller than other Orcs, usually only reaching a meter and a half. Goblins are the most likely of all Orcs to have black skin and fairer\nhair. Orc eyes are usually brown or black, with a rare few blessed with blood-red.\nOriginally written in Aegis by an author Unknown.\nRe-scribed in Vailor by Leyu'Maehr from memory.", ""),
        StaticBook("On Pigmen", "Tristanm1", "A Brief History of Pigmen: Aegis - Kalos\n\nBy Sunshine Druid Verden\nThe appearance of Zombie Pigmen was first documented during the Second Iblees Invasion, several hundred years ago. These unnatural monsters were used by the Fallen Daemon as powerful soldiers during his war of conquest against the mortal races of Aegis,\nwhere their group mentality and shear ferocity was crippling to the defenders.\n\nUnlike other lesser Undead, the Zombie Pigmen possessed an intellect far superior to any common Zombie or Skeleton. They would always be seen in possession of\njagged, golden swords and very rarely were encountered alone. Their speed was almost on par with that of the living, a trait that allowed few the luxury of escaping.\n\nAfter the invasion was ended and the Dark One had been sealed away for good, many\nstrange things were observed by the population of Aegis. While the mindless Zombies and Skeletons continued to attack anyone on sight, the Pigmen did not. They had become passive, almost completely indifferent to the world around them. Instead of\nmindlessly killing, they began to aimlessly roam the world, staring blindly at the things around them as if they weren't truly there.\n\nAfter Aegis had crumbled and sank into the sea, many thought they would never see the creatures again. And indeed, they\ndidn't. For a time. But, again, the Pigmen emerged from the unknown, and they were furious. For many years the Pigmen were found roaming the darkest corners of Asulon and attacked anyone foolish enough to get close. The reason for this is not clear, and\nmany theories have arisen to explain. However, none have been proven.\n\nYet another strange event was observed on the island of Elysium. A large volcano near the temporary Elven settlement erupted and spewed lava over the Malinor farms,\nand the Pigmen rode it down the slope. They were, thankfully, peaceful this time; but they appeared in numbers never seen before. Hundreds of them began to blindly wander from their lair, spilling over the border into the Orcish deserts. There they were\ndestroyed not by men, nor by beast, but by cacti. The creatures tore themselves to shreds over the next few months as they walked through the cacti farms, and they probably would still if it wasn't for the piles of their dead brethren impeding their\nmovement.\n\nSince arriving in Anthos there has only been one reported case of Pigmen. This was a very small issue, where only three or four Pigmen would wander around a border wall in the Wilds. A magical fountain was placed\nat this site, and all has been quiet since then.\n\nIf the Pigmen are here on Anthos, they are hiding very well.", ""),
        StaticBook("On Shamanism V1", "Thurak'Yar", "Scribed by the Farseer Thurak,\n14'th of SUn's Smile, 1487.\n1. Those Who Practise\n\n2. The Connection\n\n3. Traversal\n\n4. Utilisation\nThose Who Practise\n\nA magic almost strictly wielded by those of Krug’s descent, Shamanism is to the Orcs as Muuntrivazja is to the priests of the Kha’s; narrowly practised and highly revered. Those that fully understand the ways\nof the magic, divided into the sects of Elementalists, Lataumans, Witchdocters and Farseers, each have their own role in the Uruks’ society and all put the needs of their race above their own or any singular individual. Dark Shamanism,\na forbidden topic, shall not be discussed in this tome, as it holds no relevance to the topic being held. The first, and debatably most simple form of Shamanism, Elementalism, is based on making a connection to the Elemental Plane and the Spirits that\nreside there. A Shaman of this type, with the aid of his or her ‘bonded’ spirit(s), may control an element such as Earth or Fire that is present nearby in their own realm. It should be noted that this differs from Arcane Magic as it requires no\nconnection to the void and, rather than creating matter, simply manipulates that already existing. The next form of Shamanism, the practitioners of which are dubbed ‘Lataumans’, is much more complex. By moving their consciousness into\nthe realm of Ancestral Spirits, these shamans can contact the forms of those who were chosen as ‘honourable’ in life, and reside in a place more connected to ours than any other. The long dead such as Lur or even Krug himself (Although it has never\nbeen done) could be contacted for advice or information, and stronger Lataumans can gain blessings from these spirits; their strength or courage being ‘boosted’ for a short, albeit valuable, time, although this can’t be reproduced on\nothers by these Shamans.\nWitchdocters, the more ritualistic group of the subtypes, choose to meddle with the Immortal Spirits- those that live in a plane dedicated to the aspects of the mortal races.\nEvery emotion, every ideal and every thought can be categorised to a spirit, there, and the power of such spirits is used by Witchdocters to curse and plague those who are seen as a threat to the Shamans’ beliefs. They can do this on anything\nliving- including animals and plants, and as such these shamans are held in wary consideration by the Druids. They may also do the same with the Elementals, surprisingly- being able to call upon the lesser of such spirits to curse whomever\nthey wish. Another important part of Witchdoctery is the production of potions, which, although similar to alchemy, instead focuses on using ingredients that represent a certain spirit to call upon its power, and requires more thought on\nthe individual pieces than the brew as a whole.\nLastly, there are the Farseers. Sometimes called the Elders of the Uruks, these shamans spend their days traversing the Immortal Plane in search of enlightenment in cultural and\nhistorical matters. They bring their council to the Rex or their race as a whole, and act as the balancing force between the two planes. In much the same way as Witchdocters can curse, Farseers have the ability to bless\nplants or animals; being able to increase favourable attributes such as strength and resilience, but also those more intangible, such as hope. It is this reason why Farseers are so widely valued, as these blessings before battles or klomps prove to be\ninvaluable assets.\nThe Connection\n\nFor the following section, we’ll be referring to the connection of the Farseers to the Spirit Realm, as it is held as the both the most complex and developed form of the art among those who practise it; being one\nthat these Shamans use to constantly and fully transport their consciousness to another plane. This forced passage between realms begins when a Farseer cuts off the link between his consciousness and his body, and ends when he, or in turn\na Spirit controlling him, returns the stream to his mortal person. Unlike any other magic, the beginning of Shamanism actually requires the limitation of Mana being spent, rather than an increase in such. The regulation of mana use is vital in both\nentering and departing the spirit realm, and as such is vigorously practised by all Farseers. To let their mind wander to the place of spirits, they must almost entirely cut off the connection between their mind and their body-\nleaving only a trickle of mana as an anchor to return. When they wish to come back, they increase the mana flow to their mortal person, and their thoughts will begin to travel back to their body. Now, that being said, although controlling mana plays\na massive part in connection to the Spirit Realms, it isn’t so that anyone that can do such may actually make this connection. When an aspiring shaman is first taken on as a student, they will be put through several trials and tests, dictated\nby their teacher. Although they are sometimes seen as a filter to weed out the dedicated from the short-sighted, they are indeed much more; as a pupil’s action in these tests decide whether or not the spirits themselves will allow him or\nher to move their consciousness into their realm. An intangible filter of sorts is present, at which a group of minor spirits regulate the influx of those entering and leaving the Spirit Realm. Those that have been seen bearing positive\nattributes during these tests may be, for the first time, allowed to enter this new realm. It should be noted that the longer a Shaman has been connecting to the Spirit Realms for, the easier it will be for them to pass through the filter without\nthe spirits scrutinising over them- and as such it’s important for these Elder Shamans to do their best to regulate their own actions as honourable or otherwise.\nTraversal\n\nOnce a Shaman has connected to the Spirit Realm, an incomprehensible variety of circumstances may occur. For the first few times, chances are their senses will be dulled or non-existent as their\nmind struggles with the lack of bodily connection. Their vision may remain pitch black, and the only ‘input’ they receive may be a soft, niggling feeling of being watched. As time progresses, aspiring Shamans learn to deal with what\ninformation they’re given, and soon the senses return, albeit in situations that differ massively from those of the Mortal Realm. Their vision may be hued; the world they seen filtered as if through coloured glass. Hearing may\nbe non-existent, or they may hear cries far away as close as if whispered in their ears. The sense of feel usually comes last for those that travel the spirit realm, and it can be the most confusing of all to deal with. Sometimes, should they come\nacross a vision of a man being slaughtered, they would feel a phantom sense of pain- like a slash across their neck as they’re decapitated. It’s important for a Shaman to differentiate these Empathetic feelings to others from their\nown, especially in the Spirit Realm, as it’s been known to drive some blindly mad. Now, on to what actually occurs in this realm. When a Farseer, presumably with full use of his senses first enters the Spirit Realm, they'll find themselves, most likely,\nin an environment they haven’t yet seen. It might be a dense forest or a dank swamp; either way, a Shaman learns quickly to adapt when they traverse the spirit realm. With them is usually their Spirit Guide; a creature in a form important\nto them or their heritage which will lead them through this realm away from, or into danger, depending on the situation. A member of the Ugluk clan, for example, might have a Spirit Guide in the form of a bull, as it is Ugluk Bulls that their clan\nis so famous for- and the same can be said for Lur Wolves or Braduk Rhinos. So, led, or not, by their respective guides, a Shaman will eventually come across what they will refer to as a vision. It may be an event of the long past, the distant\nfuture, or the twisted present- usually representing something that has or will play a major part in the development of either the Uruks or the Shamans. These won’t be straightforward events, however; one may see only the form of a crying Uruk\nmother and her child, and from there they must learn to decipher its potential meaning. And it is from that that a Farseer’s real skill is discovered; the ability to learn from these small snippets of information and then advise their leaders or people\nfor or against certain actions that could, potentially, bring massive consequences for their race. \nAnother form of visions, much rarer in comparison to these odd events, are those brought forth by a spirit himself. These are no tricks to\nbe deciphered; if a spirit goes to enough effort to force a Shaman into a vision, their vision will be straightforward and commending. Usually, these occur when the Spirit feels as if their influence in the Mortal Realm could be\nincreased, and they are to call upon the aid of a Shaman to spread their influence. The Shaman, of course, has no real choice in the matter, but those stronger in the way of words may be able to convince a nagging Spirit to change their\nmind on a matter which might be otherwise impossible, or at least very consequential, to perform.\nUtilisation\n\nLast but very much not least, we come to a Shaman’s real use in Orcish culture- their ability to manipulate the Mortal Realm around them. As said earlier, each sect of Shamanism has their own ways of doing\nthis: Elementalists by meddling with nearby elemental, Lutaumans by blessing themselves, Witchdocters by cursing others or creating tribal elixers, and Farseer by blessing Orcs or others around them who are in need.\nUsually, they go about these things in similar ways. Elementalists must bind themselves to an Elemental by first impressing them by going on certain quests to draw their attention. Then, they promise the Spirit something that it seeks that they can\ngive, and then a bond will be made. As more Elemental Spirits are bonded to, sometimes a Elementalist’s proficiency in the control of a certain element may be hindered; like if one Shaman has bonded to a spirit of water as well fire, or earth\nas well as lightning. It’s up to the Shaman himself to decide what they’ll sacrifice, or if they’ll instead focus only on one element to become utmost proficient at it over all others.\nLutaumans go about creating their influence similarly. They first must call upon an Ancestral Spirit to meet with, and then they will try and gain their favour by promises of impressive feats or proof of their past honour. Once a Spirit\nagrees, they will temporarily bind their essence to the Shaman; giving them massively increased attributes such as strength or bravery for a short amount of time, which may just turn the tide of battle in their favour.\n*the book cuts off- seeming to have run out of space. Down the bottom a small entry has been scribbled in*\n\n\n\n\"See Volume Two for the remaining information of this Tome.\"", ""),
        StaticBook("On Shamanism V2", "Thurak'Yar", "Volume Two of \nOn Shamanism, Scribed by the Farseer Thurak, 14'th of Sun's Smile, 1487.\n... The more they do this, the more word of their feats will spread among the Ancestral Plane and the more likely it is for these Spirits to accept their deals.\nWitchdocters go about things a bit differently than others. During the rituals,\nthey’ll present certain objects which hold relevance to the Spirit they are trying to evoke. For example, should they be trying to create a potion which causes the drinker to have insatiable hunger, they would perhaps need something\nsuch as the stomach of a large animal, such as a bull, or they may burn and char excessive amounts of savouries in hopes of gaining the attention of the spirit of Greed and Gluttony. Should they do so, they’ll begin to call to their desired\nspirit in the tongue of Old Blah, asking for their aid and promising that the attributes that they represent will somehow be more prominent after the use of said potion. Should the spirit agree, the mixture will be cursed, and the Witchdoctor can use\nuse it as he will on his unfortunate victims. However, these Shamans must be careful, as it’s sometimes not apparent how a spirit has cursed the potion, and unintended effects are rampant in the brews of unexperienced Witchdocters.\nFinally, the Farseers’ blessings are actually quite similar to that of the Lataumans’, in the way that it requires sharp tongue and wit to convince the spirits to give them their boon. When a Farseer decides he wishes to bless something, he must travel\nto the Spirit Realm and begin calling the name of the spirit that hold the attribute he needs for the blessing. Should the spirit answer, then the Farseer must try and convince it that the blessing will, in some way, benefit the spirit.\nIf this outright fails, and the Spirit refuses, a Farseer may do something else that no other Shaman may do; that being fight the spirit for his blessing. This is very dangerous, and is usually done only in times of desperation, as when a Shaman\ndies or is injured in the Spirit Realm, he dies or is injured back in the mortal realm. Once he’s either convinced the Spirit of the need of the blessing or beaten him in battle, it will then begin to take over a Shaman’s body. Back in the Mortal Realm\na Farseer’s limbs may start to move under its will, and words in Old Blah will be said to give the blessing direction. A mist, usually hued the colour that best represents the spirit doing the blessing, will pour from the Shaman's orifices\nand encompass the person, animal or plant he wishes to bless. When its finished, the Shaman need only to return his connection to his body and admire his handywork; the fabric of the mortal world being shifted in his favour, albeit in only a small front.", ""),
        StaticBook("On Telekinesis", "Sarrion", "On Telekinesis\n------------->\nBy Sarrion Zytiaear\n\nThe following is written from my knowledge as a master of the art of Telekinesis. I was taught by the Infamous Beltran, one of the best at the art I know to this day.\n-Sarrion\nTelekinesis is the arcane art of alteration on the environment around the mage by changing a materials gravity and velocity. Simply enough it’s achieved by intently studying the world around the mage by understanding\na material inside out and able to weight this material just by a glance. The mage then establishes a mana anchor between them and the object they’re casting on and giving an intense focus\non the thought of changing the property of the materials gravity. Distance of the caster and object vary between the skill of the caster, where one who is a student may not be able to lift an object unless it’s a foot in front of them\nto a master who may lift an object on the other side of a tea room. When casting the mage is rooted in place due to the intense amount of focus it requires to shift an object, even if it’s just a cup of tea the user must stand still.\nIt’s also known that like any other alteration the mage may not lift organic material, fluids, or sands due to how complex the materials are.\nCommon combative uses of Telekinesis is the use of throwing knives which can be thrown at the speed of a crossbow bolt by changing it’s velocity, or any other normal day object as a projectile.", ""),
        StaticBook("On the Void", "Thereon Ireheart", "Notice: These books are rushed copies of tomes in the Library of the Orkish capital, as made during the raid of the 16th of The Grand Harvest, 1532. The following is in no way a complete nor perfect copy of said book.\nOn the Void\n\nAs potential, waiting to be shaped and formed. Such is the role of an arcane mage.\n\nChapter II\n\nIn the previous chapter, everything that exists in our world exists as potential in the void.\nBut within the void, it is useless to us. Therefore, through trainning a mage can learn to connect to the void and harness its potential in various ways. One example, is evocation, the art of evoking a certain element. An evocanionist uses the void to\nForm their element out of the voids potential and then pull it into our physical world. They will have control over this evoked element (mind you the level of control depends of their skill) and it will be able to effect the physical plane (evoked flames\nwill burn, evoked winds will blow). make no mistake however, that which comes from the void does not belong in our physical plane. As such, as soon as a mage looses connention to the void, be it because he is too tired to continue, his concentration is\nbroken or he simply servers the connention, that which came from the void will return there. this applies to all arcane magic, not just evocation. You way be wondering, if the void is full of everything, why can't mages /make/ everything. The anser lies\nwith knowladge. You see, creating something from the void is no walk in the park. A mage requires absoloute knowlage of what they are attempting to create before they can create it. For this reason, arcane mages focus on one area of harnessing the void\n(or more for advanced mages). An evocationist will spend time learning and experiencing all they can about their element, a conjurer will disect creatures so as to understand how they work and an illusionist need to know how something feels before they\ncan make someone elsefeel it. So, how is it we can connect to the void?\n\nChapter III\n\nRelatively simple to learn but takes a lot of time to master. An arcane mage just starting out learning his craft (regardless of what that craft is)\nwill need to spend a lot of time meditating. Why do they meditate? To achieve the state  of nothingness needed to connect to the void. A connection to the void requires your mind to be empty. All thoughts, feelings amd emotions must be brushed aside. For\na mage just starting out, this will be quite rather difficult and requires a comfortable and secluded area in which they can meditate with extreme focus. To a mage just starting out meditating, it is best if you find somewhere quiet as when you hear a\nnoise your mind will subconsciously focus on it, which disrupts your focus. When meditation for the first time, to not expect to summon anything. When a mage has /truly/ gotten a grasp for this state nothingness they will feel a connecting to the void for\nthe very first time. Nothing will physically happen but you, the mage will most surely feel the power of the void. I can not say how exactly you will feel it. the result differs from person to person with some fainting, some feeling immense power surge\nthrough them and some having belling noses. But you /will/ feel it. After making your first connection it will slowly get easier from there as you progress now that you know what you need to achieve. You can now start experimenting with your art, assuming\nyou already have extensive knowledge on what you intend to create. Bare in mind that pulling something from the void, wether it be a feeling, an element or a creature, requires larger amounts of mana. Large and of longer sustained spells requre larger\namounts of mana to keep them from returning to the void. It is importaint that you recognise your limits, for mana has a physical strain on the body, leaving you fatigued. A mage that does not recognise his own limits may end up knocking himself out from\nhis own spell which is quite counter productive. As a new mage, Do not try anything large or long. You will not be able to sustain the provide the mana needed. Start small and work your way up. with lots of meditation and practice, there will come a day\nwhen you may connect with the void in an instant.\n\nThe way of a mage is lifelong. Mastery is simply staying the path. So stay the path.\n\nElers, Regent of the Arcane Delvers and Aeromancer 1st of suns smile 1445.", ""),
        StaticBook("On the Void", "miningtilldeath", "Index\n\nChapter I What is the void\n\nChapter II How we can use the void\n\nChapter III Connecting to the void\nChapter I\n\nWhat is the void? No doubt you as the reader know it to be the source of arcane magic (which includes the art of evocation, illusion and alteration). But what actually /is/ it? The void is a plane of existance that contains absoloutely\nnothing. Yet at the same time, it contains everything. Strange and complex as this may sound, it is in fact relatively simple. You see, the void is a place of /potential/. Thoughts, feelings, elements, everything really exists within the void yet puerly\nas potential, waiting to be shaped and formed. Such is the role of an arcane mage\nChapter II\n\nHow can we use the void? As mentioned in the previous chapter, everything that exists in our world exists as potential in the void. But within the void, it is useless to us. Therefore, through trainning a mage can learn to connect to\nthe void and harness its potential in various ways. One example, is evocation, the art of evoking a certain element. An evocationist uses the void to form their element out of the voids potential and then pull it into our physical world. They will have\ncontrol over this evoked element (mind you the level of control depends on their skill) and it will be able to effect the physical plane (evoked flames will burn, evoked winds will blow). Make no mistake however, that which comes from the void does not\nbelong in our physical plane. As such, as soon as a mage looses connection to the void, be it because he is too tired to continue, his concentration is broken or he simply severs the connection, that which came from the void will return there. This\napplies to all arcane magic, not just evocation. You may be wondering, if the void is full of everything, why can't mages /make/ everything. The answer lies with knowledge. You see, creating something from the void is no walk in the park. A mage requires\nabsoloute knowledge of what they are attempting to create before they can create it. For this reason, arcane mages focus on one area of harnessing the void (or more for advanced mages). An evocationist will spend time learning and experiencing all they\ncan about their element, a conjurer will disect creatures so as to understand how they work and an illusionist needs to know how something feels before they can make someone else feel it. So, how is it we can connect to the void?\nChapter III\n\nConnecting to the void itself is relatively simple to learn but takes a lot of time to master. An arcane mage just starting out learning his craft (regardless of what that craft is) will need to spend a lot of time meditating. Why do\nthey meditate? To achieve the state of nothingness needed to connect to the void. A connection to the void requires your mind to be  empty. All thoughts, feelings and emotions must be brushed aside. For a mage just starting out, this will be quite rather\ndifficult and requires a comfortable and secluded area in which they can meditate with extreme focus. To a mage just starting out meditating, it is best if you find somewhere quiet as when you hear a noise your mind will subconsciously focus on it, which\ndisrupts your focus. When meditating for the first time, to not expect to summon anything. When a mage has /truly/ gotten a grasp for this state of nothingness, they will feel a connection to the void for the very first time. Nothing will physically\nhappen but you, the mage will most surely feel the power of the void. I can not say how exactly you will feel it. The result differs from person to person, with some fainting, some feeling immense power surge through them and some having bleeding noses.\nBut you /will/ feel it. After making your first connection it will slowly get easier from there as you progress, now that you know what you need to achieve. You can now start experimenting with your art, assuming you already have extensive\nknowledge on what you intend to create. Bare in mind that pulling something from the void, wether it be a feeling, an element or a creature, requires mana. Large and or longer sustained spells require larger amounts of mana to keep them from returning to\nthe void. It is important that you recognise your limits, for mana has a physicall strain on the body, leaving you fatigued. A mage that does not recognise his limits may end up knocking himself out from his own spell which is quite counter productive. As\na new mage, DO NOT try anything large or long. You will not be able to sustain and provide the mana needed. Start small and work your way up. With lots of meditation and practice, there will come a day when you may connect to the void in an instant.\nThe way of the mage is lifelong. Mastery is simply staying the path. So stay the path\n\n\n\nJonathan Elers, Regent of the Arcane Delvers and Aeromancer\n\nTranscribed 1st of sun's smile 1445", ""),
        StaticBook("On Wights", "Revion Tathron", "On Wights\nBy Revion Tathron\nWights, first and foremost, are the creations of a Dark Art known as Mysticism, on which a book shall be written at a later date. They are, as it was put to me, \"Transcendent spectral entities\". They boast a wealth of powers from their state; these\ninclude:\n-Telepathy\n-Telekinesis\n-Manifestation Control\n-Better prowess in Mysticism\n-The ability to \"Haunt\" husks.\nWhat is haunting, you may ask?\nHaunting is the ability of Wights to 'possess' empty bodies. These can range from empty suits of armor to corpses. In this state, their magic is weaker depending on how heavy their 'body' is. However, it does provide\nsome protection against that enemy of all undead, Gold.\nAs Wights have the ability to control what they look like when they manifest, and they can possess husks, there are no common defining features, if you're merely looking at them. Wights are mostly made of 'Ectoplasm', which is a mixture of mana and\nlife-force that all spectral beings consist of.\nAs stated in the beginning of this tome, Wights are created through Mysticism. Only a few details are known about this ritual; firstly, that the to-be Wight is a master over Mysticism and ectoplasm, whereupon the 'sacrifice' themselves to\ntranscend to their spectral state from their bodies. However; their bodies turn as hard as rock, and if their former body is destroyed, the Wight perishes.\nThe odds are, you will never see a Wight; this is not due to the fact that they have so much control over their forms; rather, that there is only one currently in existence. This may change, however.\nThe Wight I talked with was perfectly reasonable, and did not attempt to kill me at all (as other things I've studied had.) It seemed relatively free from the instability that other undead are known to possess (See: Ghouls). \n\n\nEND.", ""),
        StaticBook("On Wisdom", "Whimsycal", "\n\n\n            On Wisdom\n             by Shanka\nWub is wisdom? It is a mixture of things. Gruk'n, experience agh wits all come together to define wub wisdom is.\n\nAnyash may gruk that to catch a fish, latz must use bait. But only the wise ash gruks wub kind of bait will catch wub kind of fish in\nwub river or lake.\n\nAnyash gruks that scaddernaks are bub'hozh agh dangerous beasts.\nBut only ash who has faced the beast will gruk wub it is that makes it so dangerous agh fearsome. Agh only the wise will gruk how to defeat agh tame the beast.\nTo be wise is to have experienced, to have learned, to have dared.\n\nThat is wub wisdom is.", ""),
        StaticBook("On Wraiths", "Toodles", "On Wraiths\n\nBy: Someone who isn't stupid enough to reveal their true name.\nNot much is known about the actual physical characteristics of Wraiths by the author, but what they do know shall be written down. Wraiths (also known as Harbingers) are shadowy undead beings with a proclivity for killing.\nThe most recent incarnation of Wraiths are produced by the machinations of the Drakaar Mordring, through the utilization of certain altars, one of which is in the Sunless Sanctum. (The process of becoming a Wraith is described in another book.)\nThe Wraith, once created, is bound to Mordring's Will; basically, to bring about a resurgence of the Dragonkin. Wraiths have the ability to summon some form of Vessel, which fill with lifeforce when a mortal dies in the vicinity of it. It takes fifteen\ndeaths for the Vessel to be full, upon which a Dragon or Drake will spawn. If the Vessel is destroyed before the amount of lifeforce is acquired, all the accumulated lifeforce will vanish, but the Vessel can still be summoned. If the Wraith attempts to\nharm the newly-made Dragon or Drake, the Wraith will perish. After thirty years, Wraiths are released from Mordring's service, but they retain the ability to summon Vessels. Wraiths have the ability to husk\nempty bodies, much like Wights; but there are unknown complications regarding the eyes of such a body.", ""),
        StaticBook("One set of eyes", "ChampionGreen", "There is some thing I would like to tell you but this only for our ears and no one elses I fell as though my punishment wasn't caried out and am very disapointed this time I will be leaving and never will come back the pain I caused to her and her little\ngirl shall no longer hold me in bind I am \n- Rolen Candi I have lied to you all and it has worked  I have stolen and taken from you  and do not feel bad about my intentions to kill you and Dan are still the same", ""),
        StaticBook("OOC Keep Notes", "Evelyn Alexander", "This entire book is OOC:\n\nI'm not entirely sure what's wrong with the keep but the Staunton's say it's ugly?\n\nNotes on the keep;\n\n-Not a fan of the ballistas\n- Flag colors need to be changed\n-Farm fields extended\n-Sam's house torn down\n-Shrine and graveyard need to be built\n-The kepp isn't ugly?????\n-Ask TG to lock Evelyn's Study\n-Probs eventually get rid of everyone's Private Quarters\n\n-idek what the barracks are used for tbh\n\n-Move the armory somewhere else with more room\n+Make TG lock le room\n- Get rid of the library\n\n-put bed in the maybe temp guest wing\n\n-sleep\n\n-don't sleep\n\n-Wait for NotCharles to respond to my damn msg\n- Sleep is for the weak\n\n-I'm I must sleeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeep\n\n-Just noticed I forogt to put 'wea' in front of I. . . Too lazy to delete and change it smh\n-Our Lord and Savior Wiseacres is on\n\n-Why did I think this would be a good idea?\n\n-Figure out what to do with the room before the \"maybe\" new armory\n\n-maybe\n- It can't be Eve's study cause I dun want anyone but Eve allowed to get in there.\n\n-Louge area?\n+No\n\n-Empty space?\n\n-Sparring area?\n+Why tf we would have a sparring area inside idek\n-Get rid of the Spruce wood where Cookie's room used to be\n+ Fuck that guy\n\n-What even are private quarters\n\n-Maybe get rid of the rooms outside of Joseph and Camryn????? idke what to do with the emoty space tho\n-idke\n\n-GG Athena\n\n-What was I doing?\n\n-Maybe make it a louge area?\n\n-smh\n\n-NotCharles nickname status bout to get revoked\n-This book is ten pages now\n\n-How tf\n\n-I'm so tired\n\n-SLEEP IS FOR THE WEAK\n\n-What even is the area under the master bedroom\n-Master bedroom lil' weird, why's it only half the entire space?\n\n-Why do we even have so many rooms\n\n-No one ever visits us\n\n-Except Joe\n\n-Y'Know, when we're kissing his ass to get noble status\n-Don't tell him I said that\n\n-idek know man, I don't have the eyes of an interior designer\n\n-These are all just ideas\n\n-I really hope my ideas don't get shot the fuck down by Luke\n-Why do we even' need barracks?\n\n-OOC chat is quiet\n\n-Sansa didn't pk Cassandra. Damn.\n\n- *listening to music intensifies*\n\n- *feelings of anger intensify*\n-Sleep is for the weak, and I am weak.\n\n\nThe end of my notes maybe hopefully probably why did I even think writing this was a good idea idek there are probs so many typos I'm ashamed.", ""),
        StaticBook("Opinion On Truth", "Sofetios", "An Opinion On Truth\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n                        -By Sofetios\nBefore we begin, I just wanted to say that this book will shortly cover a variety of subjects regarding my opinion of fear, doubt, truth, belief, lies, and how I think they correlate with each other. This book is just my opinion.\nYou don’t need to believe it or not believe it. To begin we will start with fear.\n\n                    Fear\n\nWhat is Fear? Fear is when you are afraid of something. But what does it mean to be afraid of something?\nI think fear occurs most often in the absence of knowledge, when you aren’t sure about something. Fear of the unknown is a very common thing, as the unknown can possibly do anything.\nIf you had complete confidence that something will happen a certain way, then you likely will have little fear about it unless it is a bad thing that will occur.\nThough if something goes the wrong way when you are fully confident about something, That may cause the next subject to happen. Doubt.\n\n                   Doubt\n\nDoubt is something that can cause fear.\nAs I explained earlier it ties in with the lack of knowledge in something. Doubt can be a very powerful thing. It changes, or attempts to change your perspective on things, for better or worse, but usually worse.\nTo avoid both fear and doubt, I think it is best to find one of the things that defeats both. Truth.\n\n                  Truth\n\nTruth is a very simple thing, Though it can be very hard, or easy, to obtain.\nIf you know the truth of something, that means you have true knowledge in a certain part or all aspects of it.  This defeats both doubt and fear, because fear is caused mainly by doubt, and doubt requires uncertainty in something.\nThere is, however, something that increases doubt. Most all of us do it. Lie.\n\n                     Lie\n\nLies corrupt the truth, they make things harder to make out.\nIn my perspective I think that the truth is a very simple thing to understand, it is lies that corrupt it and makes it more unnecessarily complicated.  In my opinion, lying causes most of doubt and fear.\nThe next and last subject I like to call absolute truth. \n\n           Absolute Truth\n\nThe thing with how doubt works is it tries to prevent us from getting to the truth.\nLies and doubt have happened so much that sometimes even if we find the absolute truth, we still doubt it, Unless of course we believe it’s the truth. And finally, what to believe is the truth. Well, that’s for you to decide.\n(Please let me know if you like my philosophical writing by telling me in person or sending a bird! ((Sofetios)) )\n\n-Sofetios", ""),
        StaticBook("Orc Babies", "Rosso", "Where do orc babies come from?\nYou imagine orc babies and you imagine a green thing being born from the hot sands of the war nation, somewhat like a plant. Well, that is just not true. Believe it or not, orc woman exist and actually get pregnant like humans and elves.\nThe great aspect of orc baby creation lies in the special mating ritual that is performed before the orc woman is impregnated. I had the misfortune of being made a slave to the orcs, but it did let me witness this unusual event.\nFirst of all, Klomps are made between all of the orc women, as the strongest will have the first choice on the males. Then came to men, and this is where it gets weird. In the normal Klomps, the orcs just hit each other until one is on the ground almost\ndying. The mating Klomp is quite different.\n\nIt was to my surprise that instead of punching and kicking, the male orc came dressed in what I assume was dwarven beard, and started doing other things. One chanted poetry in blah, I still do not know how\nthat is possible but it did happen. Another one started dancing, another brought a warthog, already dead, and started cooking it, using a variety of herbs and spices. I was stunt in my chains.\n\nIt appears that orc women fight only for first choice, while\nthe orc men fight to impress the women. One would think it would be by fighting each other in terms of combat, but the orc women know that their men are strong, so they chose the orc which seems to have other skills. As such, the orc man will try his best\nto impress the women, yet of course they do not like doing such.\n\nAfter the women chose their man, the orc males will have one night before they gather with their mating partners. On this night they smoke the biggest amount of cactus green that they can,\nand then trade blows, taking each punch to the face with honor. An orc which I saw that danced like a fairy, stood still while he received blows from another for more than half the night.\n\nThey do this ritual as to recover their honor after the greatly\nunhonorable performance. None laugh of others, they respect themselves for doing so, but I will never forget the orc singing in broken common, it still to this day makes me laugh. I was to be executed after the next day’s mating, which was done inside a\ntent of course, but luckily was saved by an attack of the dwarves which happened shortly after. This has changed my view on all orcs, as I never knew they could clean so many bowls in such a small amount of time, as the first chosen orc male had done.\nWhere do Orc babies come from\n\nOriginal author unknown\nCoppied by Rosso\nVaerHaven", ""),
        StaticBook("Orcish Doom Gate", "Granantar Fournier Ateus", "Orcish Doom Door  ~~~~~~~~~~~\n\nUpon entering the frayed forest beside the orcish mainland, I came across a ruined temple. Inside I found an inferno, to the degree that breathing was difficult. Eventually I came upon a few pools of molten lava.\n- The  lava seemed to incinerate everything it touched, so I managed to use the protruding boulders to jump across. Without a doubt, I was overly burdened by the scent of burnt flesh and traumatized by fatigue.\nAnd most of all, I was distraught, by-\n-the massive door that stood before me. A Doom Gate, I call it. For it loomed above me, dark, cold and mysterious. Upon entering, I felt as if I had entered another land...It was...putrid. The entire area was tunneled, and burnt. With lava splurging-\n- everywhere. Eventually I came across what appeared to be a levitating arena...above an inferno. The tunnel was life-less...As I approached the exit. ----------------------------------------------------------\nUpon Exiting, I found a locked chest.\nThis Doom Gate was...most definitely a promising place...for dark arts of sorts. There was blood and flesh, signs of past life. But none apparently in the present....", ""),
        StaticBook("Orcish Plague", "riverfyre", "The Orcish Plague has become a serious and debilitating disease that can kill those infected. With several having perished from the disease, new information has been given. While the information is not complete nor fully understood, what can be pieced\ntogether is ways to prevent further spread of this infectious disease. As such it is important to know how the disease is spread, what the symptoms are, and how to be treated. If this plague continues to spread at its\npace, entire cities could be wiped away within days of initial contact.\nHow it is Spread\n\nThe Orcish Plague has been know to spread in many various ways. How one becomes infected can be done through contact. Any contact with an infected person, creature, food, or area are at high risk of infection.\nThose with weaker immunities, children, and elderly are more prone to the disease. With just vicinity being an issue, the spread is far more concerning. The disease seems to be spread through air, consumption,\nand contact with poisoned areas. The disease seems to enter through the mouth and nose when exposed to the spores or infected air expelled from an infected individual.\nThe disease then spreads in the body from the lungs into the kidneys and other organs. When consumed, it starts with the stomach, spreading into the lungs and other organs. It is unknown what the plague does to the epidermal\nwhen touched, but the disease does pose serious health concerns. Bodies that have been infected risk infecting those that touch them. Avoid burying the dead as it can spread through the ground\nand can lead to infection of the water supply. Cremation seems to be the best solution for the dead, but take precautions with the smoke.\nSymptoms\n\nThe plague slowly devours the body internally. The organs fall one by one until the body can no longer sustain itself. As the body slowly decomposes inside, the pain becomes unbearable and the infected dies slowly and painfully.\n- Coughing within the first day\n- Lungs begin to inflame and fill with fluid\n- Lethargy\n- Eyes and flesh begin to turn yellow\n- Kidneys fail\n- Stomach rots\n- Internal organs slowly eaten away by the acid and rot\n- Death\nEven after treatment, the infected is left weak and in pain. If you see any sign of a cough or lethargy in children, immediately seek help.\nTreatment\n\nThere seems to be no normal way of treatment to spare one from this plague. However, it does appear that holy magic can be used to remove the taint from the bodies. While the plague can do severe damage, only powerful healers can\naccomplish the task on the more severe cases. Once healed, the infected can return to their normal duties after they have rested. The infection takes much of their strength and energy, leaving them exhausted\nfor several days. It appears even after treatment, one can become infected again.", ""),
        StaticBook("Order - Volume 1", "Aerxuis", "Order-Volume 1\nWritten by Silir'ilume\nCopied by Aerx\nEvery inquiry and aspect of art, every fundamental action and every deep-rooted pursuit. All are thought to aim for betterment, accomplishment and overall ‘good’.\nThe term itself is relative, yet is constantly, albeit rightly and accurately, declared to be the source and goal of all things. These aims and goals differ; some take the form of an activity, whilst others merely act as products separate from the\nactions that produce them. But of course, where aims exist separate from action, it is the essence of the aim to supersede the importance of the means. Multiple pairs of such a relationship exist. The product of a medical science is\nlife or health, whilst that of a war strategist is victory. However, where such activities are similarly categorized, the aims and goals and accomplishments of the master art will always out purpose that of the subordinates, for\nit is but the nature of the former for that the latter is pursued. No difference shall ever be perceived as to whether action itself takes place as an end or a part of the action, in which the cases of science are constantly mentioned.\nIf there is a resolution to the diversions that we undertake, which we as individuals strive for nothing but the sake of doing so, and if we do not opt for all for the goal of something else, intelligibly this must take form as both the most prominent\ngood. In such a case, would the knowledge of this not inherently strive for the betterment of life? Would we, not unlike an archer aiming for a target, have any higher chance upon striking what is right? If such is true, all together we must attempt, if\nonly in outline and précis, to ascertain what exactly it is and of which function or sciences it is an object of.\nIndeed, perhaps it would seem to belong to the most canonical of crafts, the most adroit and masterful art. One such aspect of this art\ntakes from as the nature of politics; an object which enjoins that this science be studied in such a state, and which aspect of this science each residential echelon shall become aware of, and to which level they shall understand; and\nas we take note of the fact that the most ardently revered of these objects, in the end, legislate the means.\nThey dictate what we are to do and what shall be avoided, for the ends of such sciences must include the ends of others, so that the end\ndesign shall be, for it must, for the benefit of all.", ""),
        StaticBook("Order of Nine", "Jack", "The Order of Nine was first established to protect the realm of Athera against any threats that may have came from across the sea, where the Betrayor lived.\n\nFounded by the Nine Heralds, they soon diversified into various chapters\nThe most notable ones being the Medraut the Skybreaker, Borz the Holy, Artorigus the Sacred. \n\nTheir deeds and valor spanned the continent, but as all things they too must have met their end.\nThough they sacrifice themselves to the Seal, their will shall forever remain. The Path of Nine is open to those who wish to protect. \n\n\n-Howard Guiles", ""),
        StaticBook("Order Placement", "Judas 'John Locke' Agnarsson", "Jack Dolan ((Reeg))\n\n20 bottles, 10 chests, 20 planks, 30 cobblestone, 1 cauldron, 1 furnace ALL BY TOMORROW!!!", ""),
        StaticBook("Oren History: I", "Jacob Chapel", "History of Oren\n       Part I\n\n\nThis history has been collected by me, Jacob Chapel. Sources are various scrolls and texts and the museum of Petrus, beside common knowledge and tales. Enjoy.\n\n((Written by Pepernoot))\nIf you're interested in the history before Al'Khazar, I suggest you pick up the first part of the Holy Scrolls, the Nativities. If you're interested in the history past Siegmund Carrion's election, then you need to find part two of The History of Oren.\nThe days of Aegis\nThe history of Oren begins in Aegis, the land of the Descendants. To the west of the Cloud Temple laid its lands. The capital of Al’Khazar was surrounded by pastures, farmlands and forests, and to the north laid the snowy lands,\ncontaining cities and towns such as Alstion and Snowy Fields. In the south were the islands known as the Whisper Isles, surrounded by the calm waters of the Whisper Lake, and filled with fishermen and craftsmen such as glassblowers.\nThe first king was Horen himself. Many Kings ruled the Kingdom wisely after him, Kings such as Saint Daniel. The Kingdom’s grand city was built by this King Daniel in 1300. Eventually King Pampo Perea, Saint Daniel’s seneschal, took power, with Edmund\nSheffield as his seneschal.\nAs time passed the Undead became stronger and bolder in their attacks on the Kingdom. Snowy Fields fell and was razed, but soldiers from Alstion retook it and the town was rebuilt. This turned out to be futile eventually as\nthe northern towns fell one by one. Even King Perea himself was killed by the Undead, within the tall walls of Al’Khazar. Edmund Sheffield took his place, but was corrupted by the dark forces and turned to the Nether. His “abduction” was accompanied by a\ngreat siege of Al’Khazar, in which a portal to the Nether opened in the city square. After a great battle the City of Man was sacked. Human refugees poured across the Kingdom and the city of Galahar, built on the ruins of the old town of Kramoroe, was\nnamed capital.\n\nEdmund’s son Enor Sheffield took his place, but under his rule the situation worsened drastically, the corruption of his father and his own weakness led to great dissent in the Kingdom.\nMeanwhile Gaius Marius, Hochmeister of the Teutons had carved out a realm for himself to the east of the Kingdom, and had even participated in wars against Oren, allying with its enemies. It is important to note that even though the Undead threatened\nOren, and although Oren was the strong defence between the Undead and the rest of the races, this didn’t deter them to wage wars against the Kingdom. Infighting between the nations of Malinor, Krug, Urguan and Oren was common.\nRebellion brew in the\nKingdom, and in Galahar the Phoenix Rebellion formed. As the weak King Enor saw his position wasn’t tenable anymore he named Gaius Marius as King of Oren. This treason lead to the rise of the rebellion and a war between the part of Oren controlled by\nthe Teutons and the Phoenix Rebellion, led by Eze’kiel Tarus as the Phoenix King. Eventually the two sides reach an agreement, splitting the realm into two parts. The Kingdom of Renatus, ruled by King Tarus, and the Realm of Hanseti, ruled by Gaius.\nAfter giving up his crown the mad King Enor and his followers settled on an island, and together with Queen Dawn Perea, wife of the old King Perea he created the nation of Salvus.\nHumanity could not rest though, as the Undead continued their march\nrelentlessly. In 1349 Galahar was attacked by the forces of evil, but even though the attack was repelled, the city was evacuated for fears of Miasma. Eventually Aegis fell in 1351, and the humans fled through the portal to the verge,\nalong with the other Descendants. \n\nA new dawn in Asulon\nAfter a brief stay in The Verge, the Descendants settled in the new lands of Asulon. Oren was still divided into the three Kingdoms, and Renatus settled around their new capital of Arethor.\nIn these turbulent times a new human nation sprung up, the Kingdom of Seventis.\n\nWhen King Eze’kiel resigned from the throne, Renatus was ruled by the Regent King Reynard Lycian until Godfrey Horen appeared. Godfrey claimed direct decent from King Horen\nand quickly received support from the Dukes and other nobles of the realm. He consolidated power and strengthened the Kingdom, which was plagued by instability and attacks from the other nations.\n\nSoon after Godfrey started his reign a coalition against\nSalvus formed by The Kingdom of Seventis, Alras, the Orcs and most importantly what was called the Twilight Army. This coalition did not have official support from Renatus, but most of its nobles supported it. Opposing the coalition were Salvus itself,\nthe Dwarves of Urguan and the Realm of Hanseti. After the humiliating defeat, and the betrayal of Queen Dawn’s guards, which murdered her, Salvus was absorbed into Renatus.\n\nAs the great plague hit Asulon, the Kingdom of Seventis was hit most severely,\npresumably due to its location close to the Cloud Temple. Most of its inhabitants died, allowing for an absorption into the Kingdom of Renatus to protect the weakened towns.\n\nThe Teutons could not stand by idly as Renatus grew, and went to war against\nthe Kingdom. Swiftly the war turned against their favour, and it became clear that they were losing the war. To avoid destruction of his nation, Hochmeister Mirtok surrendered it to King Godfrey, saying the words “I will die for this nation, but I will\nnot let it die for me”.\n\nThe annexation of Hanseti meant the unification of humanity, and the Holy Oren Empire was formed. In the Empire the Crownlands, Salvus and Hanseti were ruled as separate kingdoms, with the Emperor presiding over the individual\nkings, while also being king of the crownlands.\n\nAfter a rebellion against the crown, House Tarus, House Elendil and House Norsem were shunned and fell out of favour in the Empire. Soon though, the lands of Asulon were submerged by floods,\nand a second flight was needed.\n\nAnthos, a land plagued by war\nJust as the flight from Aegis, the new land was not reached in one trip. There were stops at the isles of Elysium and Kalos. One notable thing in Kalos was the formation of the\nPhoenix Kingdom, by Eze’kiel Tarus. The kingdom was quickly destroyed, even before leaving for Anthos.\n\nIn Asulon the Teutonic Order was nigh destroyed by the Flays, and the Hochmeister killed. A few remnants of the Order made it to Anthos, albeit\nwithout a Hochmeister to guide them. The Teutons settled on the southern side of the ice wall of the North. Meanwhile the Empire of Oren settled a bit further south, with Abresi as their capital.\n\nSoon after arriving it turned out this land was not as\nsafe as the Descendants had hoped. North of the wall the Teutons discovered a savage race of boarmen and a land filled with dragons.\n\nThis was not the most pressing issue to Oren though, as the Empress of Oren was kidnapped by the Orcs, and a joint\nHuman and Dwarf force invaded their capital and massacred nearly every orc present.\n\nEver present Elven aggression led to a crusade against the Princedom of Malinor. The Order of the White Rose quickly destroyed their forces and burned the Malenorian\nfarms. The elves eventually surrendered to the siege, proving the enormous strength of a united Oren.\n\nUnited it would not be for long, as the Teutons rebelled. In the first battle, at Kralta, the Teutons were crushed. At the second battle the\nBlackmonts stood alone against the Teutons on the Iron Fields, outnumbered two to one. The Teutonic Order was victorious.\n\nThe civil war was decided at the Siege of the Dreadfort, where the Chivays, and the Carrions (then bannermen of the Blackmonts)\ndistinguished themselves. The defeat at the Battle of Iron fields had led to close cooperation between the White Roses and the Blackmonts, which turned out to be more than the Teutons could handle. Although the Dreadfort was severely damaged the siege\nwas broken and the Teutonic Order defeated. Soon afterwards the Teutonic Order was disbanded by Emperor Godfrey.\n\nThe Scourge, the name of the goatmen and the dragons from the north made their first move south beyond the wall, destroying the old\nTeutonic castle of Greywyn with flames, and taking it for their own.\n\nThen, in a huge shock to the empire, Emperor Godfrey died. He was succeeded by his son, Horen.\n\nAs the princedom of Malinor was absorbed into the Empire as\nprotectorate, the expansion of Oren led to great concern with the other races. They formed a coalition and in the War of Anthos they attacked Oren, putting a halt to the expansion. Most prominent in the coalition were the Dwarves, angered by the\nexecution of one of their Dwarven lords by nobles of Oren. In the Battle of the Crossroads the coalition was defeated though, by a brilliant Orenian flanking manoeuvre.\n\nIn 1415 the Empire was left in disarray as Emperor Horen left, along with his\nmost loyal houses. The dwarves used this weakness to form their Grand Kingdom of Urguan in the War of Asulon. They dissolved the Empire of Oren and many parts, including Salvus and Adunia swear loyalty to the dwarves as vassal states. What remains of the\nEmpire was renamed to the Holy Kingdom of Oren and ruled by William Horen. After more chaos and disarray many more little human states sprung up or seceded from the Kingdom, leading to the current prominent Houses, states and cultures. \n \nEventually the\nGrand King of Urguan, Thorin Grandaxe dies. This caused the human vassal states to be released from their oaths to the dwarves. This made way for a renewed unification. As King William was pushing his powers and support from his nobles to the limit,\nHouse Winter made a secret pact with Ruska. This forced King William to step down, giving control of Oren to his cousin Silus. This was only temporary, as the states of Salvus, Oren, Ruska and the new state of Herendul were confederated into an empire,\nwith an elective monarchy. At the election Siegmund Carrion was voted in as Emperor of Oren.\n\nThis concludes part one of Oren's history. The events after Siegmund Carrion's election, up to the first years in Athera will be discussed in part two.\n((So you made it to the end. I hope you liked it. You can leave a message to me on the forums, username: Pepernoot))", ""),
