        StaticBook("Primacy of E. V1", "Kardel Irongut", "-The PRIMACY-\n        -of the-\n      -ELEMENTS-\n-0-0-0-0-0-0-0-0-0-A manuscript on alchemical philosophy\n-0-0-0-0-0-0-0-0-0-\n     Kardel Irongut\nPrelude: the Spiritual Importance of Alchemy\n-0-0-0-0-0-0-0-0-0-                             Often times, I find myself introducing that very self first, as Kardel, son of Heron, and second, as an Alchemist. My being of the Dwedmar species seems apparent\nenough that I do not need to adress it. And, just as often, I find people asking me about my alchemy. Some have delusions that it is a romantic art, full of tales of industry and discovery. Others view it in a much more negative light, as a demonic art\nakin to the dark practices of the cultists of Iblees.\n\nYet, no matter how a man or woman views alchemy in regards to their morals, one thing is certain: it is a subject that engenders much curiosity in the layman.\nTo put it in the terms of the aforementioned layman, Alchemy is not the art of brewing potions, as the uneducated in this craft would claim. Rather, it is the art of creation, the very mechanic of the world. Alchemy is the manipulation of the four\nelements in order to produce works that fuse the aforementioned humors to create more than simple potions. Alchemy, at its present state, can concoct anything from simple balms to artificial illusions of life. It can elongate lives, or truncate them at\nthe swig of a dram. However, what we presently know about alchemy is far from the art's true potential.\n\nThe reason why I have set to write this book is to delineate those unknowns. In ancient dwarven myth, it is said that Yemekarr used\nthe four base elements, which predated even his exalted self, to forge the world. Might this have been a diluted interpretation of a primordial \"world\" alchemy? Some medical professionals even claim that all sickness originates from four bodily humours,\neach of which is represented by one of the four base elements (fire, water, earth, and air) and a seperate season. Might this be a layman's interpretation of a much more complex alchemical process? The answers to these questions are all but certain, and\nthis text shall attempt to shed light upon these uncertainties by corroborating them with accepted fact.\n\n-0-0-0-0-0-0-0-0-0-\nChapter 1: Elementalism\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\n                            The most fundamental step in understanding the complexity of alchemy is understanding the four base elements. These elements are fire, water, earth and air. Each element represents\ncertain aspects. Fire represents emotion, chaos, power, and destruction. Water, on the contrary, represents tranquility, healing, fluidity, and flexibility. Earth conveys strength, fauna, flora, and spirit. Air represents wisdom, agility, speed, and the\nephemeral.\n\nThese characteristics are then expressed in the natural world through symbols. For example, the beautiful Swamp Blossom, growing in the canopies of the wetlands, has a nectar with an elusive property alike that\nof quicksilver, perfectly encapsulating the meanings of air, and thus representing air in the natural world. However, this principle is far from limited to herbs. Inorganic things, like precious ores, can be also said to represent aspects of the elements.\nAnd, when this application of alchemy is widened, symbolism can be also attributed to abstract things, like behavior, or the complex tangible, like organs and tendons. \n\nTherefore, can it not then be inferred that, if the elementalist explanation can be\nattributed to such convoluted concepts, alchemy seems to have an inherent place in the world? Furthermore, if alchemy is nestled so close to the heart of our natural existence, can then the unexplainable, such as diseases or temperaments be at\nleast partly awarded as symptoms of this chemical culprit? This is the basic inference of Alchemical Philosophy, which claims that alchemy is the ultimate mechanism of the universe, and that the creator used alchemy to create the tangible existence. This\nwill be the main topic of this manuscript.\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\nChapter 2: The Creation Deduction\n~=~=~=~=~=~=~=~=~\n                            The supernatural, interplanar entity called the Creator utilized antediluvian alchemy to create the universe; this is the thesis of this manuscript. And it only makes sense.\nLong have alchemists been able to create artificial approximations of life known as Homunculi. Even longer have alchemists been able to alter bodily functions, such as strength or speed, through potions. Alchemy, in this sense, seems to be the very\nessence of living function, the genesis of existance. Therefore, due to its inherent ability to modify such properties, can it not be said that Alchemy contains the key to create such properties as well? For how can one modify that which he is completely\nunfamiliar with?! \n\nAlchemy as a tool is extremely volatile: it can do anything from strengthening skin to engendering a new breed of creature (via Chimaeric creation.) According to myth, Alchemy might have the ability to generate completely unknown\nelements, the philosopher's stone the prime traditional example of this. Rumor has it that ancient alchemists were able to craft gold from steel and wine from water. This advanced level of functioning cannot be explained by anything short of the\nsupernatural.\n\nNo matter how hard sophists and theorists have tried, alchemy seems to have no scientific mechanism, no set of principles (With exception to the four elements) regarding how it functions. It simply functions, hinting that the art may be\ninherent to our plane of existance, encoded into it by a greater being much like a runesmith encodes runic information into a tool. The only creature ever reported to be able to do this is the creator, of who's existance we know of because of the sermons\nof the Aengulic Wandering Wizard. Therefore, can it not be said that this volatile, mysteriously encoded tool was in the posessions of a greater being?\n\nAnd if this greater being had this tool at his disposition, would he not utilize its extreme\nmalleability?\n\nNature follows the path of least resistance. Animals thrive best where it is warm. One rarely sees a doe grazing the tundra. If nature was made by this apocryphal creator, would it not be made in his image? And if it\nwere made in his image, would then the Creator himself not be a creature that would follow the path of least resistance? So why, then, would the Creator use any other art but alchemy? Magic takes mana and exhausts the caster, and hand-forging is outright\ninapplicable. Alchemy takes the angelic skills that such a creator would have had, and it is the most convenient tool of the ones presented. Therefore, would the creator not have followed the path of least resistance, as does all nature, and used alchemy\nto create the universe, at least in part? \n\nThe above would explain many, many things. It would explain why elementalism in encoded into the world. It would explain the impossibly vast continents the races have encountered, that no single creature could\nhave ever hoped to shape without aid. Furthermore, it would make natural sense. Therefore, it can be DEDUCED that Alchemy was the tool used to create the universe.\nChapter 3: The Implications of Alchemical Genesis.\n-0-0-0-0-0-0-0-0-0-                             Any sophist reading this would immediately question as to why the alchemical nature of our tangible plane matters. To that, I would answer with confidence\nthat such an affirmation would then change the entire course of mortal achievement and direction. If a tool, controllable by mortality, has the power to forge a plane, to create a world, then what stops mortals from becoming Gods themselves?\nThis question shall be answered in another volume, but for now, I would like to stress the sheer importance of the plausible assumption that the world might be an alchemical creation. An alchemical genesis would make it possible for mortality to have a\nreadily decryptable answer to all illness and disease. It would allow for the creation of more optimally adapted mortals. It would allow for the possible shattering of the Iblical Curses, and perpetual, near perfect (as perfection is impossible through\ncurrent alchemy) for all. The world would be at the fingertips of the educated, ready to mold in whichever ways they should please to do so. Indeed, a definitive alchemical genesis would offer possibilities as far fetched but theoretically plausible as\nthe ascension of the Descendant races to Aengulity. \n\nBut such far off hopes, while entertaining to the wondering mind, are too aloof. An alchemical genesis would more realistically offer simpler things, such as better understanding\nof the nature around us, and ready explanations to natural phenomena. It would make disease more manageable, and increase life quality. \n\nOn the contrary, such a genesis might decrease life's pleasures: with all speculation definitively\nanswered, there would be no true room for philosophy. Weapons so destructive could be made as to potentially cause wild tremors in response to alchemical reaction. Alchemy has always had two sides: a malevolent one and a benefactory one. A definitive\ndiscovery of this magnitude would make the art something perhaps beyond our intended power.\nEpilogue:\n-@-@-@-@-@-@-@-@-\n\nAs my parchment vanishes like the reagent in a cauldron and as my inkwell runs as dry as the environment fostering the Goblin's Ivy, I must preserve my thoughts for another volume. The purpose of this volume was to\nintroduce the basis of Alchemical Philosophy to any interested. I would recommend further reasearch into Alchemical Lore before continuing onto the next volume of this series, which will deal with the theoretical specifics of world forging and the planar\ndisparity of the Void and our Plane of Residence, as well as the dire difference between Alchemy, Magic, Runesmithing, and Shamanism. With these words of promise, I bid the reader a scholar's farewell, and encourage him (or, as improbable as this\nmay sound, /her/, as females are naturally a duller and more simple sex) to further reasearch into this field and to never cease questioning the environment around them. Do not settle for banal religious explanations, or for far-fetched, so called\n\"Scientific\" babble. Stay curious and adhere to the definites, and only make assumption when proof is ready to build upon, as a building with a faulty foundation will collapse at first tempest. And with these last words of wisdom, I bid you, the reader,\nfarewell. May your curiosity be sated only by the most definitive of explanations.\n-@-@-@-@-@-@-@-@-\n\n\n\n*A signature sprawls an entire page*\nKardel Irongut\nAlchemist\nTheologist\nScholar\nIllusionist\nFire Evoker\nBrthmdkn Prophet\nNaturalist\nElder\n\nand most importantly\n\nCurious Mortal", ""),
        StaticBook("Progenitor Is: 1", "Taenost Ihnsil'dionn", "\\The Progenitor/\n\nThe Progenitor is a publication that stands to represent an unbiased report accompanied by historical accounts recorded by Taenost Ihnsil'dionn. These historical accounts are classified as primary, through the eyes,\nhands, and participation of the recorder hisself. What you will read within is as accurate of accounts as an 'aheral may achieve, no spin - no adjective to spice nor distract from the history wrought between peoples of the Fringe.\nThe Voice of the Alrasians\n\nThe High King Syrio has attested that the start of his expeditionary camp's involvement in the Zionist War came when Valesmark merchants were attacked by human bandits. Dwarven details protected the merchants and\nrebuked the attempted highwaymen attempts, allowing the Valesmark to deliver their goods to the Cloud Temple of Anthos. Thereafter, High King Syrio explained that he felt drawn towards the Zionist treaty to deter further highwaymen attempts on his own.\nHigh King Syrio remained unfidgeting with concerns of the proposed treaty from High Chancellor Wilfriche Buron of the Orenites, not revealing what he nor his council members felt or decided. \n\nThe Alrasians bound themselves to secrecy in that they did not\ndetail or reveal any of their future hopes as to their participation in the theatre of war. All that could be ascertained was that the Alrasians insist that their perch atop the karst of Kal'Arkon was temporary and that they have land speculations\nunderway. \n\nHigh King Syrio attested lastly to an attack that the Orenites committed that led the camp to be slaughtered mostly. His description bore witness that men, women, and children were put to the sword by the Orenites.\nInterviewed High King Syrio\n -2nd of Malin's Welcome, 1456\n\nTemple of Vallendar Accosted\n\nThe Temple of Vallendar currently stands along the grounds of an old, yet ruined settlement named Montefleur. A clericess by the name of Lenia Ith'ael\nbore witness to an accosting from a supposed Ruskan named Harrison de Silvarois and a polemic from Lucienists which concluded the temple to be of a heretical nature.\n\nThe Clericess has confirmed that besides those two isolated incidents, no other\nconfrontations have occurred despite the Temple being in the current theatre of the Zionist War. Lenia Ith'ael confirmed that the temple is monastic in nature, adherents of Tahariae as the Archangel of Purity and patron of the Clerical Order farm produce\non Vallendar's grounds.\n\nInterviewed Lenia Ith'ael\n -5th of Malin's Welcome, 1456\n\nThe Dwarven Casus Bellum\n\nBalek Irongut detailed the reason for their involvement in the Zionist War. A summoned emissary dispatch arrived at Vekaro from\nKal'Arkon consisting of Lords Dizzy and Zahrer along with dwarfish legionnaires. \n\nThe Orenites examined the relation between the Orkish Warnation and Dwarfish Kingdom by relating that the Warnation attacked Vekaro in the near past, date untold, and asked\nthe Dwarves to allow them to return the same treatment without intervention. The Dwarfish emissaries requested evidence of such an event and the Orenites failed to produce. Balek Irongut described the confrontation as ending with Wilfriche cursing the\nDwarfish dispatch and besetting his soldiers of Vekaro on them in which all Dwarves successfully escaped except for Lord Dizzy who was injured by a pikeman.\n\nBalek Irongut described this instance as the casus bellum that King Wulfgar acquired which led\nDwarfish Kingdom to the current Zionist War.\n\nInterviewed Balek Irongut\n -15th of Malin's Welcome, 1456\n\nAn Attempt on Wilfriche Buron\n\nUpon winning a battle at dusk against the Dwarfish, Alrasian, and Orkish forces, the Orenites sent a\ndetail along with their High Chancellor to confront Syrio on what they perceived as a betrayal of expectations as they had given a truce to the Alrasians which Syrio had to confirm with his council. Upon arriving in their expeditionary camp, Wilfriche\nbegan his warnings to High King Syrio before having an axe crash down upon hisself. An assassin of Mali'ker ethnicity informed those around of his militant dislike for the Orenites before being slain by Wilfriche's detail.\n\nThe Orenites immediately drew\nsuspicions that High King Syrio had organized the plot as they escorted Wilfriche away from Kal'Akron as legionnaires attempted to rally for what gave the Orenites fear as being a further attack on the High Chancellor.\nAccosting of the Mali'aheral\n\nThe elcihi of the Mali'aheral, Tahn'siol have been accosted and accused of taking a political stance in the Zionist War. The Mali'aheral have a reverence for neutrality, as insisted by the Sohaer himself. An issue of\nanother publication known as The Flagship has been countered by the 'aheral as being libellous and request that the publisher remove any content that attempts to tie a neutral political entity into a war. \n\nBounty on High King Syrio\n\nA bounty\nissued by the Orenites, specifically High Chancellor Wilfriche Buron has been announced on High King Syrio Forel. The current paying price for his head stands at 2,000 minas.\n\nPok'Ugluk Alive?\n\nAn infamous and key figure in Uruk culture and history\nhas been reported to being found in the Fringe. Whether reports are true could not be confirmed, although the reports reveal that Pok'Ugluk stated a disdain for the ruling Azog clan of the Warnation and a desire to remove them for their seat of power.\nScourge at Kal'Akron\n\nThis subject matter remains to being very controversial with differing narratives. The Zionists upon being capitulated and slaughtered as they fled into Kal'Akron brought about an unsubstantiated accusation that the Scourge aided\nthe Orenites. The Progenitor\nshares a statement of a witness who corrects this narrative by explaining the following: The Zionists insist that they were struck by frostfire along the fields north of Kal'Akron, but failed to remember that they had\nfailed to hold position to be struck - the Orenites had already pushed, killed, and defeated the Zionist army. In fact, it was the Orenites who had been assaulted by Harbingers in which the trajectories of their frost-fire led to some of the Orenites\nidentifying the distinct, grim appearances of Harbingers who sat along the karst of Kal'Akron nearest the Alrasian Expeditionary Camp.\n\nAn 'aheral historian recommends the following statement: When you study events in history, your best evidence\nare primary sources. Primary sources are accounts from those who lived and witnessed the particular event in person and were typically affected by said event in some way and has emotions, thoughts, and experiences to recount. The Orenites had succeeded\nin the battle whilst most, if not all of the Zionist forces had been felled. The best judgement to make in this case would be to take the Orenites' accounts as solid while the Zionist accounts are mythic explanation as to their failure in the battle at\nKal'Akron. It is typical for  collective memory to weave up an explanation of either realistic or mythical proportions to define the moments in which those people had gone through a hardship.\n\nThe Progenitor, in the next issue, welcomes editorial and\nopinionated content that will be clearly and visually separated from our journalism. The Progenitor in and of itself remains separated from the conflict and those who write for the Progenitor uphold a standard of refusing any attempts to persuasively or\nforcibly put forth libellous, flowery, and scandallous content. \n\nThank You for Reading\n\nProgenitor\n Issue I\n  Printed on:\nThe 20th of Malin's Welcome\n                      1456", ""),
        StaticBook("Promotion: SGT", "arkantos1279", "By decree of Commander Rickard Barrow of the Gold Corps, William Worix for his incredible service, fortitude, discipline and determination in improving the standard of the Gold Corps, he is hereby promoted to Sergeant. With this seal of promotion he is\nnow an official member of the Gold Corps officer class. He is now granted the authority to train soldiers and command men of the rank and file class.", ""),
        StaticBook("Prophecy.", "Muildir", "Second Extract of Prophecy From the Kha.\n\nThe priests gain a prophecy not of prophecy of images, but of words. The shamans do as well.\n\nThe first cryptic message is as follows: \nOrigins are all but absolute.\nWe've not past my own dispute. \n\nStrands of fate now do entwine, both minds and soul are my design. \n\nSafeguard not my children near, mine is the vessel of love and fear.\nPity not the weight of strain, I do this time and time again.\"\n\nEnd first message.\n\nBegin second message:\n\nFruits of life do beg my pardon, roaming astray my endless garden.\nMy will sees metal turn to rest, and grow tall from all but dust.\n\nFind me not in book nor tome, for I'm the breath of life and home.\n\nForge noble beasts and kinds that reign. I do this time and time again.\n\nEnd second message", ""),
        StaticBook("Protection Writ", "John Sigismund", "This writ indicates the person of Vanessa Longridge is a servant of the crown; all assaults upon her person will be treated doubly, and she should be aided by all subjects and allies of the empire.", ""),
