        StaticBook("Taxes", "Melodystar", "Our taxes haven't been collected out of the box from last week, so I put taxes for this week in as well. It is 07/15/16 today and there is 10 vouchers in the box. I will continue to pay the taxes every week even though they haven't been collected", ""),
        StaticBook("TEB Vol. I", "VonEbs", "The Eldest Brother:\n A History of Malinor\n       Volume I\n    The First Sky\n===================\nMalinor as we know it began after the Collapse, an obscure event which lead to the collapse of any civilization that may have existed before 1300. When one of\nthe symptoms of the Collapse, the erasing of the collective memory of all who survived the Collapse, wore off, High Prince Native banded together what was left of a wood elven colony, as well as the dark elves and high elves in the region surrounding\nLaurelin. He set about consolidating his position in Laurelin, assisting many elves in setting up lives in the capital. Very few elves are left from this time, almost all having gone missing at some point during Aegis, so accounts of the early days are\nsecondhand at best, many times having been passed from pointed ear to pointed ear until reaching the writer. By most accounts, Native picked an array of Princes to help him govern the growing elven population, some notable ones being\nPrince Toren the Farmer, Prince Flefal the Engineer, Princess Arianna, and Prince Lafthi the Diplomat. The mining paradise of Ravenhold was founded, home to such notable elves as Khel Oussana, Haelphon Adecchio, and future Prince Ebs Telrunya.\nAfter the initial years in Laurelin, most of the High Council, including High Prince Native, were barely ever seen in Laurelin, and Malinor suffered. The Gauntlet, an obstacle course that offered a large minas reward, was opened rarely, and almost every\nelf immediately entered the Wardens or the Druidic Order upon entry into Malinor. The only functioning part of Malinor’s government were indeed the Wardens, the elite defenders of the forests of Malinor. Lead at first by the ruthless Blackthorn, they\nprotected the forests while the High Council squandered away the Princedom. Native’s only notable action during this time was nearly destroying Ravenhold when he caused the mine to collapse.\nWhen Kal’Bryst was destroyed during the onset of the Undead invasion, the High Council’s reaction was nonexistent. During the great northern wars, in which the humans struggled for survival against the undead invaders, the elves hid in the south, with\nvery few participating in the defense of Alstion.  The Wardens instead focused on removing Wrath from Wrath’s Clutch, an Undead citadel in the South that threatened to cut off Alras and Al’Khazar from Laurelin and Kal’Urguan. Countless wardens and other\nelves perished in attacks on Wrath’s Clutch, which was repeatedly assaulted so that prisoners being held there, most notably Princess Indelwehn, could be freed. No matter how many times the walls were breached, the forces of the United Aegis Coalition\n(UAC) could not dislodge Wrath until very late in the war. Indelwehn became notable for leading the wardens in the defense of Sanjezel, leading to the signing of the Malin-Krug pact, a long-lasting defensive treaty between the Holy Princedom and\nKrugmar, which was signed by Princess Indelwehn and Wargoth Mogroka’Gorkil. \n\nMalinor had a token presence in the Verge, its official settlement, overseen by Princess Lirinya, lacking utility and security. More enterprising elves and a few humans united\nunder Viscount Ebs, who founded the city of Fionn, which flourished for a time, until all but the mentally unstable abandoned the Verge during the massive slime and spider invasion. At this time, Laurelin abandoned its old Council Chambers to allow the\nMages Guild to have a place to set up away from the unstable north.\n\nFor a time, Malinor had a High Princess, Sorious, the wife of Native. After her death, the High Council passed a law stating that being the spouse of a Prince does not\ninherently make you a Prince. Native later fell in love with the owner of the Treetop Inn, which flourished in the trees North of Malinor. The owner, Lirinya, quickly rose to prominence in Malinor, gaining the title of Princess quite quickly. Her down to\nearth nature and her humbleness disguised that she was Undead. Gaining trust from a besmitten Native, she betrayed Malinor, destroying Ravenhold during the rule of High Princess Indelwehn.\n\nWith the appointments of Princess Indelwehn and Prince Mylas,\na new generation of Elven leaders took the helm of the nation. Ebs, a high elf, and Sythra, a dark elf, convinced High Prince Native to disband the corrupted Wardens, who were being used by the cruel Prince Quazar as an aggressive force, sparking conflict\nwith the other nations. The Vanguard was formed, with Sythra in command. With the appointment of Prince Ebs as High Prince Native’s final act, the High Council, guided by the still mostly absent Princes Toren and Lafthi elected High Princess Indelwehn to\nthe highest position in Malinor. They then added then Treelord Haelphon and Sythra to the Council, and removed the Archdruids from the Council, as in that time, the druids grove was a pit of filth and crime, all but sanctioned, and most certainly indulged\nin by Respiren and Dusk. This was a Golden Age for the elves, as their international power and influence waxed to a point where it could have been argued, after the fall of Al’Khazar, that Malinor was the stablest and most powerful state in Aegis.\nUnknown at the time of her election was the deep taint hidden in the heart of Indelwehn, installed during one of her stays in Wrath’s Clutch. Her second husband, Bircalin, and her friends watched in horror as she changed. It is still not clear if she was\naware she was tainted, however, she broke the Malin-Krug pact, refusing to aid Krugmar in their war against Urguan and Oren, and made a series of disastrous mistakes. She attempted to have Prince Ebs assassinated for questioning her,\nhowever he barely survived, and after a year recovering in Hanseti, he returned when Indelwehn was charged with the murder of a small child. The High Council attempted to cover up her crimes, but realized she had to go, and voted unanimously to remove her\n, revealing to the public only that she had resigned. In a near tie, Haelphon was elected High Prince, with Sythra losing by one vote. By this point, Prince Mylas had been killed during an Undead attack, and his body had been laid to rest outside the\nSister City, a suburb of Laurelin. At this time, the High Council met in the Sister City, as it was too easy for Undead spies to listen in on proceedings in Laurelin.\n\nMalinor established good relations with the Kingdom of Renatus after it emerged from\nthe Phoenix Rebellion, but the final days in Aegis were characterized by fear among the High Council. After the falls of both Al’Khazar and Alras, refugees flocked to Malinor. Prince Ebs set up the Alrasian refugee camp East of Laurelin, but the Alrasian\nofficers and Vanguard struggled to keep order in the once peaceful city of Laurelin. During a magical experiment by the druids and mages, Arkelos, and Undead, destroyed the Gauntlet, spreading miasma around Laurelin. As elves prepared to flee the\ncity, the druids managed to restore the air, however the damage was staggering. The High Council, despite the pleading of a few members, refused to build additional fortifications around Laurelin, instead opting to give Deltaro Black, an elf who\nthought he was much more competent than he actually was, permission to remodel the front entrance to make it more “naturey.” Laurelin did not stand a chance in the final battle, and as the dragons leveled the once proud jewel of Laurelin, the elves fled\nfor the Verge, many of which were openly weeping, where they were met by chaotic conditions, as the elven government has fallen apart. Treelord Alan managed to build an elven ship, but travel conditions were horrid, and many elves were lost in the voyage.\nAnd so the elves set out for Asulon, to brave a strange new world full of dangers they could not have possibly conceived as they left Laurelin.\n\n~Ebs Telrunya, Former Fourth Prince of Malinor", ""),
        StaticBook("Telekinesis", "Sanguine", "~+~+~+~+~+~+~+~+~       Telekinesis\nA guide to learning\n\nWritten by Magister\n      Sanguine\n~+~+~+~+~+~+~+~+~\nFirst off Telekinesis is a form of Alteration magic, Alteration magic impacts the world around it unlike its void counterparts.\nTelekinesis works by altering the force of gravity pushing down on an object. The process of telekinesis is rather simple Focus on an object then with the help of the void alter its gravity allowing it to float.\nAlthough at higher progression you will have a better grasp of the object being able to redirect, push and stop different objects.\nStarting out you should only be able to lift a few small objects such as a Pebble, but with time you will be able to lift more and heavier objects and that pebble you started with will now be a boulder.\nKnow you must have learned a thing or two but here are some Practical uses of Telekinesis: \n~Throwing knifes: They are light allowing you to throw them at your enemies with ease\n~Doors: No problem for any telekinetics just apply force against it.\n~People: Now the living are a bit tricky to manipulate so you must indirectly using clothing is a good bypass for it.", ""),
        StaticBook("test", "Lyeth", "test", ""),
        StaticBook("test", "Armin Ambertoe", "", ""),
        StaticBook("Testimony of War", "Daniel Nodal", "*---*---*---*---*\n\nThe first dwarven civil war of Anthos\n\n\nTestimony By Rosso\n\n  *---*---*---*---*\nWe first heard of the rebellion when their leader, a dwarf called Duregar, sent the Hold of VaerHaven a letter asking for the hold to join them. It didn’t impress me, the letter seemed poorly written, small, and with information that should not had been\nsaid in such a way, like his name and those of his allies. A meeting was done between the council of VaerHaven, which consisted of I, Rosso, the youngest of all councilman, a human, yet the one alive who has served as a councilman the longest, going back\nto the time of Silva Insula, then there is Killeki, he has been a councilman since the formation of Anthos, and a long time member of Silva Insula before, and to end we have Kardel, Jarl of the hold, took over when Ferron died, he had been around since\nthe time of Silva Insula, yet had never been on the council.\n\nMany things were discussed, the seeming inability of the leader of the rebellion to think and act correctly, what would happen if we joined, what happened if we didn’t, and the\nconsequences of it all. Before the meeting we had heard from the adunians, they were former members of VaerHaven, and banished by the legion and the king of the dwarves. They served a great deal in the decision of the council.\nIn the end, we had three choices:\n\n1. Remain with the king of the dwarves\n\n2. Remain neutral\n\n3. Join the rebels\nOne would think the first choice would be the safest, but they would be wrong. VaerHaven would be the town closest to the rebels, and all members of the hold would be made to take up arms, including the scribes and the healers, perhaps even the children.\nThe king had not been kind to us since we started, he went around the decision of the former king, and started made the town pay taxes. We were expecting as such, as long as they didn’t ask huge amount of taxes, we could pay, without having the residents\nwork. \n\nYet it continued, next they banished the adunians, the ones acting as guards of VaerHaven, and friends to many of us. Their charges were only against one member, and the other charges were not worthy of being\nbanished for. What angered me the most, was that the adunians had banished the accused adunian way before the dwarves placed charges on him, making the charges on the whole race pointless.\n\nIt didn’t stop there, after removing those\nthat were acting as guards, they ordered us to create a guard force. We had little men who would fight left, but we did as asked. We got the FrostShields, who were part of the Mageshields of the Delvers, and of course it didn’t work. In the end they were\nremoved for causing too much trouble. We were creating a new guard force when the dwarves decided to pass a law making every citizen join the auxiliars, and make them join with the legion’s training. We had no choice but to ignore it, but the\nlegion came almost every elven day to enforce it.\n\nThen once more the legion placed it’s nose on VaerHaven, this when I was away with my daughter in Malinor. They accused one of the town guilds, the Fallen, of Anarchism. When I asked what\ndid they do to be seen that way, all they told me was that they were told that they were plotting things. So, instead of wanting to question them, and search of the truth, they decided it would be better to just completely arrest every member without\nproof and for no crime committed. They were not let. I had a talk to some of the members of the Fallen, and knew most of them well, those rumors were false, and the legion didn’t even care for it.\nThen the rebels appeared, and the decision had to be done.\nKeep with the king, and continue to suffer in the hands of the legion, and be the frontline, with scribes made to fight, and those that would be against it, suffer as a traitor.\nKeep as neutral, and be attacked by the king without any allied forces, they would see neutrals as traitors.\nJoin the rebels, fight for our freedom, recover our friends the adunians, and try to end the reign of that king. We could decide who would fight, those that didn’t want to could decide to leave without being called traitors, or stay and hope for the best.\nWe could show, that VaerHaven didn’t think less of the dwarves of the king, only that we want our freedom.\n\nThe first battle is yet to happen, when it comes, I will try my hardest to keep try for the fight not to happen, but if it does happen, we have\npowerful forces in the free men of VaerHaven, who will give aid to us. I hope we do not have to use them.\n\nPerhaps we are being too selfish with our decision, perhaps we should have tried harder, have tried and perhaps even tried to\npay off the king for them to leave us alone. We’re a poor town, as all we produce is for the residents, but perhaps we would have been able to pay for it. But we believe the dwarves want military dominance over all the holds, we believe paying off the\nking would be pointless, maybe make him even remove the leadership of VaerHaven by force.\n((10/05))\nThe problems have already started in town, the condition that we accepted the rebels in VaerHaven was that they would be less than residents, which means they would have no authority. Instead, they call everyone a spy, and break the peace of\nthe hold. If it continues as such, we will have to remove the rebel forces from VaerHaven, which would make much of our decisions null. In the event of it happening, I have thought of solutions, and will have no problem enforcing them. The rebels are\nguests in VaerHaven, and they shall be treated as guests. If they act like they are in charge of VaerHaven, they will be removed.\n\nAn elven day ago I have asked for a dear friend of mine to leave town, and with her another two, perhaps\nthree residents. They are not meant to fight, nor to see war happen. They should have their own life, a happy life, in peace. I’ve seen one of the residents send a message to Malinor talking about the adunians, so I decided to go talk to the elven princes\nmyself. After saying a bit with my daughter I went to talk with one of the elven princes, and we discussed the war. They are with the dwarven king, and I understand their choice. They do not understand much our side,and I also do not blame them, as our\nway of managing is unique in every nation of Anthos. Even as elves, they are more similar to the dwarves when it comes to managing, then to a town of free men. The talk ended with an agreement. Those that do not wish to fight in the war, our residents,\nwill be allowed to head to Malinor, if they are not wanted there and do not cause trouble. It’s a way out for our residents if they wish, and I am happy this agreement was made.\n(12/05)\nI’m writing now from a jail room in the dwarven capital, I was captured when I went out to look for the wolf who has been going around killing in the forests of the dwarven lands. Fast capture I guess, got shoot in the back. I was taken to the\ndwarven capital and placed in a cell after my wounds were healed. After being questioned, I was let as a diplomatic prisoner. They wanted all the information I had, but that information is pretty much not secret. They asked for who our allies were, they\nknew that already. They asked for our defenses, they are allowed to enter VaerHaven if they don’t cause trouble, and see for the defenses themselves. Number of men as well, I told them the truth, we don’t have many fighters.\nBefore I was captured, I saw Kjell in town. I had a talk to him about the reasons as to why we were rebels, he doesn’t seem to be impressed, not many are. Nothing was accomplished in that talk. The buildings of the drawbridge was complete, we now have the\nplace protected, not that will do us any good. Soon a small army of the legion came in armor and captured one of the residents. And then a friend of mine lost her daughter in a fight, which I don’t know about. I should had told her to leave town before.\nAfter a night in the cell, I was made a diplomatic prisoner, which meant that I could walk around town with a guard, and had access to the great and big room I am writing in now. They then took me to a tavern where I bought a drink. They did remove\neverything from me, but left me with my minas then returned this book. My staff is still missing though, might have to get a new one. These guards don’t seem very good, they just left me to wander alone in town.\nI was able to escape when they left me alone. I came to back to VaerHaven and shortly after, the rebel king was captured again. He was released after torture, and then once more was he captured. The civil war is over now, nothing much was done, not\neven a single battle happened, I guess I’m glad for it.\nTestimony of War\n\nWritten in VaerHaven by Rosso\nCoppied by Daniel Nodal", ""),
        StaticBook("Thalia", "Barry Winterleaf", "Dedicant: Thalia\nDate: 04/09/2015\nGuide: Barry\nTask: Grow a tree\n\nNurture a tree to full health over the span of a few elven days", ""),
        StaticBook("Thalia Rank Task", "Thalia", "Interviews - Task 4\n-------------------\nWARNING: May have gramatical errors inside if so, sorry.\n\nDruids\n\nGuides\n\nArchdruids\n\nHeirophants\nDruids\n-------\n\nWho: Henry Winterleaf\n\nAs a druid you have know specific role within the grove so to speak, but all druids are tasked with upholding the balance of nature around the world.\nAt the rank of a standard druid your pretty much free to do as you please as long as you protect the balance of nature and follow the orders rules. \n\nGuides Next Page >\nGuides\n-------\n\nWho: Taynuel\n\nSame as a druid but in charge of teaching dedicants what they need to know in order to become Druids along with attuning dedicants that have been deemed ready\nto become Druids. Also help making decisions in the Inner circle.\n\n\nArchdruids next page               >\nArchdruids\n-----------\n\nWho: Beth\n\nArchdruids are the heads of the Inner circle, they have many responsabilities like maintaining the order as well as keeping the druids, guides and dedicants in check.\nHeirophant\n-----------\n\nWho: Garun Ibar'ker\n\nA heirophant is an elder, no longer bound by the tasks set by the order, the only tasks are the ones they set themselves, the only questions are the ones they ask themselves.\nNot over seen by archdruids for a heirophant has moved past that. A heirophant may have been an Archdruid. \n\nThey are wise, the image of a druid who has greater tasks, ones who have acquired knowledge that must be preserved.", ""),
        StaticBook("The 'Ame Way", "Lorena Faeliel", "~~~~~~~~~~~~~~Faith and Fortitude\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nThe Ame Way\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n\n\n\nThis book and its contents was written originally by Artimec Camoryn\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~I have been a servant of my people for well over a century now.\n\nWeathered the tides of war, oppression and slaughter. \n\nThe lawlessness of Malinor, the\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~helplessness of Lenniel, the squalid lifestyle of Lin'ame as our high elven masters lived on.\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nIt has been a long journey, but I firmly believe that we 'ame and the other mali who chose to live with us finally have a place we can be proud to call home.\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~Laureh'lin's walls stand strong against those who would do us harm, and the sirame fight off the unsavoury. Our kin have abandoned the lawlessness of Malinor and have finally learned to live their day to day life with a\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~modicum of quiet dignity, perhaps even gaining a sliver of respect from the world as we fought with the Oren crown against the rebels of Barrowyk.\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~We mali'ame have reached a stable golden era after a long journey of strife and conflict. But it is my duty to ask: What got us here? And what must we do to maintain it?\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~  Faith,\nFortitude,\nFamily.\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~Why Faith?\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~Faith sets us apart from the other races. It is the base of our traditions and our way of life. Without our faith, you take the taste out of our tongues, the meaning out of our race.\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~As all good 'ame know, our kin revere the spirits of nature, better know as the Aspects Cernunnos and Cerridwen. But why? What purpose does recerence to these two aenguls serve?\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~For every reason. The Aspects are the embodiment of nature. The trees we weave our homes in grow strong by their grace. Our crops are plentiful and the game in our forests bountiful on their whim. They are natural order, and as elves of\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~nature it is our place to follow them.\n\nTheir presence is also rooted in all our traditions. We invoke their names in marriage, in praying for fertility to bare a child, for coming of age and sending\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~off the dead. Traditions make us unified and proud. Pride breeds unity, unity breeds strenght. And it all starts with faith.\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~Why Fortitude?\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nFortitude is endurance, it is resilience and persistence. We mali are long lived people, prone to individualism. Our millenia of life exposes us to more hardship and grief than all others.\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~For this reason is why we must have fortitude.\n\nWe can never abandon our ways, our people and our family. No matter what the centuries throw at us. While the ride can wear down a rock, it must never grind it into\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~sand. Our moral fiber and backbone keeps us united.\n\nWe are the mali that did not flee to the savannah, nor scatter to the four corners of the continent when things got hard. We are the ones who clung to our ways\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~and our pride and did what we had to to keep our people and our ways alive.\n\nFortitude keeps us going.\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~Family\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~Must I elaborate here?\n\nPerhaps the only thing more sacre to us than the Aspects is our family. Laureh'lin is a state free of the chaos of Malinor because we care\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~for one another. We have thrown away the individualism that plagued past states and become devoted to our kin.\n\nWhile I speak of blood brothers, haelun, maln and oem ii, it is important to note\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~that all Mali are oem Talonnii - One family. We all exist for each other, live for our people, fight for our people, die for our people.\n\nOne we elves discard our desire to improve only ourselves and\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~begin to live for oem Talonnii, we will find that infact, our individual lives will markedly improve.\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~And there lies the three pillars that make us strong. More challanges and hardships undoubtably wait to test our people in the future, but with our newfound values we now possess the strength and unity to face them head\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~on. No longer will we be scattered with the four winds, the mali will now stand firm in the face of adversity.\n\n\niheiuhii narne narneyem'ehya", ""),
        StaticBook("The 'Good' Stuff", "Heshakomeu", "Records on the Alchemical Experiments of Heshakomeu de Campari Shidari\nIt should be noted that the concoctions recorded here were all tested on the Charley chickens and Heshakomeu himself and deemed safe for Mortal usage.\nDreki\n\nDreki is the pinnacle of my work with hallucinogenic substances and is meant for recreational usage. When consumed, the user will experience vivid hallucinations for two to three hours, then fall asleep, upon which they will fall into an\nincredibly vivid dream that lasts for six to eight hours. It is important to note nothing can be expected in these dreams other than that they will appear as real as the reality you live in while sober, no matter how exotic, bizarre, or unnatural they\nbecome. When one awakes, the next day will be filled with periodic migraines, light blindness, constant hunger, fatigue, uncontrollable shaking, and a strong desire for sugar.\n\nTo make, follow these instructions:\nPut a few ladles of water to boil.\nSprinkle ground-up dust of a ‘gram’ of cactus green into water.\nAdd a few drops of zawabate nectar.\nAdd a few drops of drilltongue sap.\nGrind several dried shadeleaf leaves – no more than four – and add them to the\nmixture.\n\nStir until thick, sludgy texture is achieved and concoction is a deep green.\nPour mixture onto a pan and leave in warm, dry place until all moisture is gone.\nGrind up dried mixture into dust.\nWith dreki dust, one can inhale it directly through the nostril for the soonest start of effects. One can also mix it into a drink for easier consumption, though the effects will be delayed. If comfortable, one can smoke it, resulting in a faster\narrival of effects than when drunk but slower than when inhaled.\nReykja\n\nReykja is intended for recreational usage, specifically for group smoking. It induces light hallucinations, usually no more than flashing colors, sounds that aren’t there, synesthesia, and distorted depth perception. It also puts the user into\nan energetic state, giving them an almost obsessive fascination with anything they focus on for more than a few seconds. These effects last for around four hours. After the effects wear off, the user will experience extreme fatigue, hunger, and a\ndryness of the mouth that lasts for around half a day.\n\nTo make, follow these instructions:\nChop head of dried speckled crimson mushroom into tiny bits and put in a bowl.\nGrind 'gram' of cactus green into dust and add to bowl.\nPour a few spoonfuls of water onto the dust and mushrooms and mix until mixture is sticky and stuck together.\nFlatten pipeweed leaf and dab crimson-green mixture along central stem of the leaf. Wrap the leaf around the mixture.\nWrap pipeweed wrap in\nthin paper and keep it closed using pinewood sap.\nSet in warm-dry place until consumption.\n\nThese cigar wraps can be smoked directly, or they can be cut into pieces and put in the bowl of a pipe. The cigars can alternatively be mounted on candles,\nfilling the room with smoke. The cigars, prior to being wrapped in paper, can be cut into pieces and put into the dough of bread or pastry and consumed through the food, resulting in a slower but just as potent high.\nSprunga\n\nSprunga is still rather confusing to me. I have only done it a few times, for the experience is not nearly as pleasant as with dreki or reykja; or rather, not pleasant in the same ways. Sprunga induces an intense euphoric sensation, coupled\nwith an unnatural feeling of lightness, as though one would float away at a gust of wind. The user will also experience extreme energy and excitement, which cannot be quelled until the five to seven-hour inducement is over. Depth perception is virtually\ngone, and the ear is much more sensitive to noise, an effect that continues to plague the user for another day. Dryness of mouth, jitters, and numbness of fingers and toes also accompany the pleasant effects.\n\nTo make, follow these instructions:\nExtract juice of King’s Ivy flower into a glass bottle.\nExtract juice of Mershin fungus into glass bottle.\nClose bottle and shake until contents are mixed.\nLight fire beneath bottle and cook until brown crystals accumulate on bottom of bottle.\nDrain bottle and remove crystals.\nGrind crystals into fine dust.\n\nThe dust can be inhaled through the nostrils, delivering a quick high. Lighting it afire to smoke or diluting it in a drink will severely dim or completely eradicate the positive effects.\nSpeckled Crimson Mushroom\n\nThis mushroom I first discovered growing in the storage cavern underneath Ac’Talerah, the fortress of the Arcane Delvers. Overwhelmed with curiosity after being locked inside, I ate a mushroom, experiencing\na remarkably vivid hallucination as well as a nearly-obsessive fascination with whatever held my attention for more than a few moments. Any potions it is used in will have the same effects. It grows in damp, dark places on stone, and can be recognized\nby its brilliant red coloration dotted with white splotches.\nMershin Fungus\n\nOriginally, I thought this mushroom to be an appendage of the speckled crimson, but it is in fact its own fungus. If consumed, it gives the eater a feeling of extreme lightness and happiness, coupled with an unquenchable energy. However,\none can hardly concentrate on anything, and the side effects are mentioned in the description of Sprunga. It has a tan head with thick ruffles underneath, and covers cave floors with a squichy, vine-like growth.\nZawabate\n\nMy experiments with this flower’s nectar have been a wonderful experience. It puts users in a hallucinogenic state that detaches perception from the body, if that makes sense. The shaman who introduced it to me called it an “out-of-body”\nexperience. The nectar can be put in tea, which makes taking it quite simple and pleasant. The user must be warned, however; overdosing on this can result in permanent damage to the mind, addling the senses and perception of what is real even when not\nhaving recently consumed the herb. Zawabate can be recognized by its short, curved flowers and tall stalk, both in hues of yellow and green, and grows primarily by desert oases.\nDrilltongue\n\nI have determined this to be a lovely addition to my array of recreational dosages. The sap gives the user light hallucinations and dulls the senses, leaving one disoriented and surrounded by visions. Drilltongue resembles a thick tangle of\ngreen roots that grip the stone it grows on tightly. It can be found in mountains and tundras.\nShadeleaf\n\nShadeleaf gives users hallucinations, which is accompanied with heightened emotional sensitivity and a deep sense of calm. The leaves can be smoked, eaten, and boiled into tea, all of which grant users the psychedelic adventure this herb\nprovides. Shadeleaf resembles a common weed, with short leaves and yellow flowers that grow during springtime, and grows primarily in forests. They can be distinguished from other weeds by the popping noise their leaves make that can be heard after\nrainstorms, which is caused by an overabundance of water being sent through their thin veins.", ""),
        StaticBook("The 3 Princesses", "Jory Marbrand", "In a land many thousand leagues away, there was a kingdom ruled by a just an honorable king.\nThis king had three beautiful daughters, the princesses were said to be angels sent from the heavens.\nTheir names were Selyse, Myranda, and\nNeryda.\nThe royal princesses were known across the lands of the kingdom, and all of them were loved by the commonfolk.\nThe king however, as just an honorable as he was, had not gained such love from his people, rather they had disliked him very much.\nKing Vayon had been unaware of the many lords that had plotted against him, them eager to overthrow the crown.\nThe royal house of Yorrice were not to survive for much longer if Vayon had continued to displease his commonfolk and vassal lords.\nFortunately,\ntheir foreign gods had graced the Yorrice family, bringing the princesses much wisdom and purity.\nSelyse, Myranda, and Neryda set off upon their elegent destriers to bring good word to their father's people, in hope to stop the usurping of the family\ncrown.\nSelyse rode to the riverlands of their father's kingdom with  a grand host of fair maids and polite folk. She spoke with the river duke, pleading for him to consider this predestined rebellion. With her great diplomatic dialect, fair beauty,\nand wondrous host, the river duke solemnly agreed to accept her terms, kneeling before his ducal bishop to swear his oaths of fealty once again.\nHer Highness was awfully pleased with the river duke, gracing his courts with the many fair maidens and\ncourtiers she had brought. \nFar in the westerlands, the princess Neryda rode to the western duke with a pious host of holy men and women, as well as many minstrels and jesters. She sought audience with the western duke, to ensure he had no need to usurp\nher kingly father. The two powers had many debates and arguments, many resulting in sour rages. At last, the western duke agreed reluctantly to pull out of the rebellion, swearing his oaths once again in the presence of Neryda's holy host. To ensure\nthe rather sly western duke would keep his holy promise, Neryda peppered her pious people all over his westerlands, keeping her minstrels close for pleasure.\nThe princess Myranda rode to the stormlands upon the thunderous coast of the kingdom. She\nbrought with her a mighty host of warriors, knights, and soldiers to the stormlands, she herself donning her own silvery armor and longsword. Myranda had been a warrior since the days of her childhood, a woman prone to victory. The storm duke had been\nknown for his reluctant and crude manner, therefore resulting in Myranda riding to his lands to ensure his fealty. Myranda's host or army, was met with fire and blood.\nThunder rained down upon the warrior folk, stormlanders rushing from their briny walls\nto attack.\nMyranda was repelled back in defeat, her face left red with blood and rage. The storm duke had laid waste to her armies, leaving her trapped upon the stony shores of his lands. Many of her men were lost to the fire and blood, yet some still\nremained. Many had thought the princess and her armies were defeated on the battlefield, the king and his daughters astraught. \nYet such was not the case.\nMyranda healed over time as she sat upon the secluded stony shores, planning her next attack\nwith the remnants of her host. She received word that the western duke had broken his holy oaths to aid the stormlanders, slaying the many holy men the pious princess had installed. \nMyranda was only fueled by such, drilling her men 'till they were\ndrowning within their own sweat. She gathered the fisherfolk that briganded the stormlands, installing them into her armies. They knew much about the coastline, and brought ships and dromonds along with them. \nIn a few more months,\nMyranda would be ready to attack the storm duke.\n           -=-\nThose few weeks passed, and Myranda rose from the seas to greet her foes. The many castles and holdfasts were laid to waste by Myranda, the storm duke being flayed and tortured within his own\nhalls. The stormlanders were eliminated, the commonfolk rising from their poor fishing hamlets to aid the princess.\nThe river duke was amazed, sending half of his forces to aid the warrior princess.\nIt was upon that thunderous night that the commonfolk\ndubbed their savior as the duchess of the stormlands.\nDuchess Myranda was pleased by such, sending over half of the kingdom's forces west to besiege the westerlands.\nThe wester duke forfeited the battle, never to be seen again. He ran, like the true\ncraven and coward he was.\nIt was then, that the kingdom had been secured. King Vayon was overjoyed by his daughters, his throne ensured to last for many decades.\nThe river duke abdicated his titles in awe of the princesses, offering his duchy to the\nprincess Selyse. She gladly took over the riverlands, being ascended to duchess.\nThe westerlands were left without heirs, so it was mutually decreed that the princess Neryda would arise to be duchess of the westerlands.\n          -=-\nThe kingdom\nhad been rescued from its rebellious state, now the realm entering a fruitful and rather promising age.", ""),
        StaticBook("The 3 Sisters", "[Teen] Katie Mantisuku", "There were once three sisters who were traveling along a lonely, winding road at twilight. In time, the sisters reach a river too deep to wade through and too dangerous to swim across. However, these sisters were learned in all void magics,\nso they simply connected to the void and brought the earth across to make a bridge over the treacherous water. They were halfway across when they found their path blocked by a hooded figure.\nAnd Death spoke to them. He was angry that he had been cheated out of three new victims, for travelers usually drowned in the river. But Death was cunning. He pretended to congratulate the three sisters upon their magic,\nand said that each had earned a prize for having been clever enough to evade him.\n\nSo the eldest sister, who was very protective over the younger two, asked for the ability to cast more powerful magic than any other in the land,\nmagic that must always win duels for its owner and magic worthy of one who had conquered Death! So death crossed to an elder tree on the banks of the river, and fashioned a wand from a branch that hung there, and gave it to the eldest sister.\nThen the second sister, who was an arrogant woman, with much loss in her life, decided that she wanted to humiliate Death still further, and asked for the power to recall others from Death. So Death picked up a stone from the riverbank\nand gave it to the second sister, and told him that the stone would have the power to bring back the dead.\n\nAnd then Death asked the third and youngest sister what she would like. The youngest sister was the humblest and also the wisest of the sisters,\nand he did not trust Death. So she asked for something that would enable her to go forth from that place without being followed by Death. And Death, must unwillingly, handed over his own Cloak of Invisibility.\n\nThen Death stood aside and allowed\nthe three sisters to continue on their way, and so they did, talking with wonder of the adventure they had had, and admiring Death’s gifts.\n\nIn due course the sisters separated, each for her own destination.\nThe eldest sister traveled on for a week or more, and reaching a distant village, sought out a fellow magic user with whom had harmed her sisters. Naturally, with the magics of Death in the wand, she could not fail to win the duel that followed.\nLeaving her enemy dead upon the floor, the eldest sister proceeded to an inn, where she told the tale of how she and her sisters had defeated death and how the wand and magics made her invincible.\n\nThat very night, another magic user\ncrept upon the eldest sister where she lay upon her bed. The thief took the wand and, for good measure, slit the eldest sister’s throat.\n\nAnd so Death took the eldest sister for his own.\n\nMeanwhile, the second\nsister journeyed to her own home, where she lived alone. Here she took out the stone that had the power to recall the dead, and turned it thrice in her hand. To her amazement and her delight, the figure of the girl’s mother appeared at once before her.\nYet she was sad and cold, separated from her as by a veil. though she had returned to the mortal world, she did not truly belong there and suffered. Finally the second sister, driven mad with hopeless longing, killed herself as truly to join her.\nAnd so Death took the second sister for his own.\n\nBut though Death searched for the third sister for many years, he was never able to find her.\n\nIt was only when she had attained a great age that the youngest\nsister finally took off the Cloak of Invisibility and gave it to her son. \nAnd then she greeted Death as an old friend, and went with him gladly, and, equals, they departed this life.\n((A parody of A tale of Three Brothers by Beedle the Bard/J.K.Rowling. I own nothing and she owns all.))", ""),
        StaticBook("The All-Theory", "Archibald", "The Theory of Everything, by Archibald Vallero\n-------------------\n\n    In this tome, I wish to explain to all my beliefs on why things exist with direct connection to the realm known as the Void. At times, you might be confused. This is entirely\nunderstandable given just how abstract this idea of mine might be.\nIf any of this offends you, I will let you know right now that I did not intend to offend or undermine anyone's religious beliefs. If that is what you believe I intended to do by the\ntime you've finished reading this book, then sorry. \n\nNow, let's begin.\nThe Void\n-------------------\n\n    Many readers probably have no idea what the Void is, which is why I've decided to begin this with an explanation so that non-mages can be at the same level of understanding with mages as to what the Void is.\nThe Void, in short is a dimension where everything exists simultaneously along with nothing. It is all that is and all that will ever be, but at the same time there is nothing there. One point I should emphasize while explaining this is that the Void\nIS another dimension. Implant this into your mind: the Void is a completely separate dimension to ours. Now, the use of the Void to mages is simply to draw the ability to create from it. Another keyword I'd like for you to remember is 'creation.' It will\nbe mentioned continually throughout this tome. As said before, voidal mages are able to create an array of elements and entities by exhausting their mana, which is essentially every living being's life force. Mana flows through our veins, and with it we\nare able to connect to the Void and bring about lightning, flesh, fire, water, air, earth, 'raw' energy (arcane energy), and the ability to alter the world or the people around us through different types of illusion or alteration. We use the Void to bring\nabout the CREATION of something or another that would otherwise be inaccessible to non-mages. Now, all of us have a mana anchor in the void, which is what it sounds like. Our mana, while in our bodies, is also connected to the Void, and this is what\nallows all non-mages to become mages. Now, with this explanation, I wish to bring up my theory.\nThe All-Theory \n-------------------\n\n    So, now you understand what the Void basically is. Good. Now, what if I told you that, since the Void is a dimension where anything is possible and, while it is in a constant state of balance and\nunbalance, it was still able to create /everything/. What if the dimension is the epicenter of ALL creations? What if it was even the creator of /our/ Creator? Think about it like this . . .  Mana can be found in all living, organic beings. This means\neven grass, trees, all abundances of animals, and us. This mana is what allows for there to be a mana anchor, weak or strong, to the Void for all beings. While the grass you stand on might have an extremely weak mana anchor, there is mana there\nnonetheless. Necromancers can drain the life from anything with mana in order to power themselves, meaning that, indeed, all things have mana. Even the stone of castles or the earth beneath us might have mana, though it might be in such an insignificant\namount that nothing can really be done with it. If we move up the chain and start with trees, there's more than likely more mana in them than both grass and stone, since Graven's are capable of manipulating their own mana to creator a connection or bond\nwith the tree that allows it to stay there. The tree is also involved in this process, though, by allowing for their to be a connection in the first place. Perhaps they have enough mana to have such a thing be done. Next is us Descendant races.\nWe are able to cast spells that closely resemble elements we can observe in modern day life, but with more manipulation involved. I won't go into specifics, but it requires the touch of our minds to create ice sculptures, fiery animals, and other such\nabnormal things. \n\n    Now, I did say that mana flows through our veins, but what if, just like stone, our skin, bones, and other bodily particles also contained mana? We are beings of mana, from the bottom up. All other things are made of mana,\neven if it is difficult for mages to tell. This leads me to believe that we were all created by the Void, but with middle-men inbetween the process of our creation. \n\n    Let's delve further into the nature of the Void for just a moment. We can use it\nto create a myriad of things. Here comes the confusing part of this theory, but I will try to summarize it as such first before explaining.\n\nInanimate materials have low mana, not able to create anything. We are animate along with animals, and some\nanimals are capable of being magical while us Descendant races can connect with the Void to create. Aengudaemons are powerful, capable of creating amazing and wondrous things, an example being the Arch-aengul Aeriel creating the Ascended, or Metzli\ncreating the entire race known as the Kharajyr. This type of creation is far beyond the Descendant races' abilities. Then there is the Creator, which many believe has created the aengudaemons and all of us. This could be true. But now, answer this for\nme: what created the Creator? The answer to this, I have deduced, is the only dimension capable of bringing about everything material and animate, or the Void itself. It is to my belief that the Creator is simply a powerful entity that has a immensely\npowerful connection to the void, allowing for this being to acquire a massive amount of mana and the ability to create the aengudaemons and us all. Essentially, the Void brought about the powerful being known to us as the Creator, which had a greater,\nintense mana anchor and mana pool, that this creator of ours was able to use everything available to it in the Void (which is everything conceivable) to create this world, and the aengudaemons. \n\n    There's also a pecking order, I've noticed. The\nVoid is the creator of all, it is a dimension/entity/being capable of granting power and creating any and everything, and as it had created our Creator, since the Creator has such a close connection to this dimension, this being's powers closely\nresembled the Void's. Then there are the Aengudaemons, some ranking higher than others. They have an immense amount of power, but have physical restrictions. An example would be the fact that they can only shape our realm through us lower beings, as\nthe paladins resemble Xan's hand, or the clerics resemble Tahariae's hand, or the cultists of Ikuras resemble Ikuras's hand. Then there is us. We do have a connection to the Void, but at only at a great cost. The more we master arts that allow us to\ncreate wards, elements, or bring things back to life or even drain mana from other living beings, the more our muscle mass and sanity disappears. We are thinning out. We are losing ourselves as we continue. This leads me to my next theory.\nThe All-Pure\n-------------------\n\n    As said before, the more one attempts to bring something into this realm, the more mass, or particles of themselves, they lose. I've lost around twenty pounds while still maintaining a healthy diet as I\nattempted to master conjuration, but if I were to try and learn even more evocations or magicks of another kind, I would be even far more thin. Weak. Decrepit. We lose our corporeal selves as we delve into the power that allows us to create. This leads me\nto the other observation that the more closely connected one entity is to the almighty Void, the more of 'themselves' they lose, or, rather, the more they lose their body, the greater their ability to manipulate elements and, naturally, everthing else.\nWhy this occurs I'm not /entirely/ sure, but an idea that's come to my mind is that it's the cost, as everything has a cost. The cost for becoming more god-like in being is that you lose what we know as ourselves. We lose our body and our 'mind.' This can\npotentially explain why Aengudaemons are able to have such great power in their own realm, creating what they want at their own whim, but as soon as they enter an earthly vessel to interact with us, they only become the equivalent of a powerful mage,\nrather than some being with the capabilities to destroy and create. The cost for having a body like ours is that they lose some of the powers that makes them 'them.' At the same time, once they are killed in this world, they are still able to ascend back\nto their own and retain their original powers. All very interesting, indeed.\n\n    The real aspect of the All-Pure theory involves 'purity.' In my belief, the closer one is to obtaining the ultimate power that the Void grants, creation, the more\n'pure' they are. In this sense, non-mages are not as pure as voidal mages. Inanimate objects aren't entirely as pure either, and are on the bottom of the order. This does not mean that one being deserves to live and one to die. It is simply my outlook on\nthings. Now, with this new theory, it is fact that mages who are granted their power from an aengudaemonic force, such as paladins, clerics, and cultists, are not as pure as voidal mages. The purest one can be is when they are able to siphon\ntheir spells of creation from the Void, the dimension where, believe, all life truly originates! The reason why paladins and clerics are not as pure as voidal mages is due to the fact that they are drawing their power from a being lower than the Void,\nand rather from an aengudaemon which had been created by the Void. They are drawing their power from a weaker source. The Void is the most powerful, truly. This could also explain why they, the aengudaemonic siphoners, are able to retain some muscle\nmass and weight while us voidal mages are weakened. We're closer to reaching the nature of the 'nothing-yet-everything' Void, while they are granted their power from a lower and less powerful being which takes its power from the Void. If they were to cut\nout the middle-man in their magicks, they would end up being like the voidal sorcerers: able to create more elements and manipulate this world further, yet at the cost of them losing more and more of their bodily matter. \n\n    With this in consideration,\nI do believe that there is a Creator. I believe, though, that our Creator is, frankly, a being who has such a powerful connection with the Void that it was capable of bringing our kind about along with the aengudaemons. But the true Creator is the voidal\ndimension. \n\n    With this, I conclude my novel. I hope you, brave scholar, found this piece of literature interesting and profound in its content. Thank you.\n\n         -Archibald V.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Ancient City", "Yden", "-------------------Chapter One\n-------------------\n\nThe cold breeze was gentle tonight, the leaves blew gently against their branches and the birds chirped as the sun began to set, he set his blade into the grass with a soft thud as it pierced\nthe ground. The evening was coming to a close and the weather was beautiful. Elidyr spoke with authority in his voice \n\"vee' er re gurthe another naa reborn\".\nTurning sharply to view the small band of mali'ame on his rear\n\"We set up camp for tonight, get some rest, we have a long day ahead of us\". They unpacked their satchels and carts and began setting up small tents for the groups of men, women and children. The construction of the small tents continued through to the\nend of the evening. Before long the open clearing was filled with tents of all shapes and sizes, the clearing littered with small green and white tents and within them, families many of them with childre. They were huddled up together trying to\nstay warm in the cold midnight air. Hours passed and the camp fell into silence, owls could be heard hooting as the moon began to rise. Elidyr was the last awake in the small camp, he gripped a small locket close to his chest and spoke under his breath\n\"atara, amin naa sorry ten' ilya tanya has marte nan' amin will tanka amin mistakes ar' brien seere a' sina ndor once au'\" Elidyr placed the locket back into his pocket and began poking at the fire. The fire began to die out and turn to embers, in the\ndying light of the fire another figure emerged from the tent behind Elidyr, it was a small slender woman by the  name of Lamruil. She placed herself inches away from Elidyr and opened her mouth to speak \"What is bothering you? You you can't hide\nanything from me brother.\" she spoke in a hushed tone. Elidyr responded hesitantly \"N-nothing is wrong, I was just thinking about mo-\" before he could finish his sentence Lamruil spoke in a gentle tone \"You must not worry about that, the past is the past\nwe must focus on the task at hand, these people are relying on us to take them to safety. We must not get hung up on foolish emotions!\" She placed a hand on his shoulder comforting him \"Perhaps you are right sister, perhaps you are right\" Elidyr whispered\nThey put out the remaining flames of the fire and started for their tent Lamruil stopped him \"You know... if there is anything bothering you just tell me?\" She smiles with concern but it was enough to reassure Elidyr, they returned to their tent\nand slept with the knowledge that tomorrow would be the final day on their journey. The night drew to a close, the owls stopped hooting and the birds began to chirp again in the vibrant sunshine. The camp was packed up and equipment stored on their\ncarts. They continued their journey east out of the mountains and into the open plains of the east, they tailed one and another for miles, snaking across the landscape, armed men with their wives and children at the front followed closely by the fighters,\namong the small group of fighters, the group consisted of two dozen archers followed by another dozen unskilled swordsman wearing patchwork leather tunics. They marched as one across shallow streams and hiked mountains stopping only to replenish\ntheir water barrels at fresh water. They reached the peak of the great valley and paused to rest as they admired the view, this city was named Gondolin, it had been their birth place and it was to be their salvage, the city had long since been forgotten\nthe only noises that filled the streets were that of rats in search of any remaining scraps to feed on but they began the journey and decended down the slope slowly, only a few fell on the decent but were quickly aided by the constant stream of people at\ntheir heel. They walked and walked until the sun began to set once more, they set up camp once again and spent another quite night under the stars, again Elidyr could not sleep, he sat alone next to the campfire starring deeply into the embers that\nescaped. While the whole camp slept Elidyr sat and waited for the night to end. The moon eventually passed over head and the sun began to rise allowing light onto the world once again and waking up all who saw its glory. They did not pack their tents this\ntime for they were so close to the promised city of Gondolin that that just left the tents were they were and vowed to return and collect them in the future, this also meant that they traveled faster than they had previously carrying all of their heavy\nequipment. The sun had barely reached the peak of its journey through the sky when they reached the ruined gates of Gondolin, vines grew from the walls surrounding the city but they still looked strong enough to hold back any army.\nThey admired the beauty until Lamruil stepped forward and passed through the ancient gates, she failed to notice the string placed across the entrance and before she could react *CRACK* ...\n\nSee chapter two to see what happens next!\n-By Yden", ""),
        StaticBook("The Ancient Mali", "Esgäleth Vynatis", "The Ancient Mali.\n\nRecorded on the 17th of the First Seed, 1522.\n\nBy: Esgäleth Vynatis\nThe research around the occurring events were recorded from the view of oem Mali’ame, as well as the limited knowledge and research done around the ruins found.\n\nFurther inquiries in the future should be made to amend the fragile evidence.\nIntroduction\nAround the year of 1517, in the newly found land of Elmali’ame in Vailor - a group of ‘ame researches discovered a tomb.\n\nThey first saw the dead corpse of things that seem to be mali - as well as things of certain interest.\nAmongst these ‘things’ was an emerald key; and whilst they knew not of it’s purpose, it was taken nonetheless.\n\nSide note: A dragon was also found, though this has no solid proof. It may or may not be dead.\nOf the Ancient Mali\n\nIt was only a few months after the recent tomb incident, when a new ruin was discovered.\n\nPrior to this event, the emerald key was found - as mentioned before.\nWhen the ‘door’  - or seal - was opened (using said key), ElMali’ame were greeted by an army of what they call Feral elves.\n\nThough victory was theirs to take - it was after the battle that they met ElMali’thilln.\n(Do not confuse this with ElMali’aheral or Mali’thill.) Amongst them was a necromancer able to raise the dead at will.\nIt was then that the city was laid in siege - literally set on fire. Many events took place, and the Necromancer grew quiet. \n\nUntil, he opened a portal (A certain Mali’ame deemed that it led to the void.) inside the tavern of the ‘ame.\nWhilst no permanent - or grande - harm was done, he dragged a visiting marked man with him through his portal. \n\nIt was said that he began appearing with a henchman. I would have thought that the Marked man was turned -\nbut the description of this henchman did not suit. This ‘henchman’ seem to have been a 6’5 mali, covered head to toe in iron armor.\nWeakness & attacks\nThese Mali attacked once every month for the past 2 years.\n\nThrough many fights and collisions with them, it was found that their weakness was under their chin. As well as this, Ballistae seems to work relatively well on them.\nRecorded fights, other than the ones mentioned above:\n\nAeroch’nor : A fight between ElMali’thilln and the Paladins.\n\nThe last fight: which consisted of Mali’ame, some Mali’aheral and Elvalah.\nWithin the last fight was where the necromancer was last seen. Though they’ve successfully killed two soldiers of the Necromancer - they failed to do any harm to the necromancer himself, before he slipped into his portal once again with a random Valah.\nLast note\nIn total, five ruins were found.\nBe aware that these ruins were inactive until the seal, or door, was opened. \nAlas, no further notes were drafted.\n\n\nEsgäleth Vynatis.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Arcane Arts", "Shariana", "-------------------The Arcane Arts\n    By Shariana TWW\n-------------------\nIntroduction.\n\nIn this book, you will get an understanding of the different areas around arcane void magic. This can be useful, if you practice these arts, or fight against an Evocationist, Thaumaturge or Conjurationist.\nChapter 1.\n\n     - Evocation -\n     The Sorcerer\n\nPerhaps the most well known of the four arch-types of magic.  Evocation involves evoking the elements of air, fire, earth, water, and lightning from the void.\n-Sorcerers cannot manipulate pre-existing elements, only summon them.\n\n-Each element is considered one arch-type of magic on its own.\n\n-The elements must follow the same laws as they do in nature.\n-Sorcerers must be ‘attuned’ to their element, spend time around their element, understand its scientific properties, how it feels etc…\n\n-Sorcerer’s must see the area they wish to summon their element in.\n-Sorcerers can only summon their element in its rawest form.\n\n-Hydromancers can also be Cryomancer (Ice Mage).\n\n-Sorcerers must imagine the element in their mind and bend it in their mind before evoking it.\nChapter 2.\n\n      - Alteration -\n    The Thaumaturge\n\nA true science of magic, Alteration involves altering the physical world around the mage.\n* Enchanting *\n-------------------The art of imbuing magical effects upon an object.\n-------------------\n\n-Changes what the item does rather than what it is.\n-Thaumaturge must be touching the item to enchant it.\n\n-The enchantment cannot alter the object physically.\n\n-Enchantments only last as long as they have a mana source.\n-Thaumaturges must imagine the object they see, in great detail, with the enchantment on it in their mind.\n* Transfiguration *\n-------------------The art of changing an object’s appearance.\n-------------------\n\n-Objects must keep their original mass.\n-Objects can only change their state, not their chemical makeup. (Ie. Ice to water, not water to wine.)\n\n-Thaumaturge must be touching and see  the object to transfigure it.\n-Thaumaturges must imagine the object altering,exactly how they want it, in their mind and the steps it would need to go through for the transfiguration to be possible.\n* Abjuration *\n-------------------The art of protection.\n-------------------\n\n-Wards are barriers of mana which cancel out other, aggressive mana.\n-Wards only block spells which have less mana than the ward was made with.\n\n-Wards only last so long as they have a mana source.\n-The Thaumaturge must imagine their mana being released from their body and forming a barrier around them, in the shape they desire.\nChapter 3.\n\n   - Conjuration -\n    The Conjurer\n\nA highly complicated form of magic, Conjuration involves summoning organic beings from the Void.\n-Beings can only remain in existence so long as they have a mana source which would keep them alive in reality.\n-Conjurer’s need to understand the insides and outs, literally, of the creature they wish to summon lest they get something terribly wrong.\n-Primordial Conjurers can summon elemental beings, though they must have an understand of at least one evocation element.\n-Primordial Conjurers do not need to understand the biological structure of their being, only use their mana,  and an understanding of basic motor skills in creatures.\n-Conjurers must see where they wish to summon their creature.\nChapter 4.\n\n       - Illusion -\n      The Magician\n\nA dark form of magic which involves using mana to alter one’s senses.\n-Magicians can only alter the senses\n\n-Anything a magician does is not actually there, no matter how real it feels, looks, smells etc…\n\n-Magicians cannot read minds….or directly change one’s emotion.\n-This is often known as glamour magic.\n\n-A Magician must have experience with their illusion in real life. (ie. To make someone feel as if they are giving birth, the magician would have to understand what giving birth feels like)\n-Illusions only remain in existence so long as they have a source of mana.\n\n-Magician must be able to see where they wish to produce their illusion.\n-The Magician must (smell, taste, hear, touch etc…) the object before they can produce it, with accuracy.  This is why the Magician must have experienced the sense they wish to alter in reality.\nThe End\n         ~ ~ ~ ~", ""),
        StaticBook("The Arcane, V1", "Meldur the Blue", "______\n\n     Understanding\n       the Arcane\n          ______\n\n     Published 1472\n         Volume I\n\n\n\n\n  By Meldur the Blue\nChapter 1\n\n    Like in all fields of study, for one to adequately learn and retain knowledge of the Arcane arts, he or she must first understand the origin of what is widely known as magic. If a student were to attempt to do so without proper\nfoundation, there is a high chance that he or she would be unsuccessful, and, even if he or she was successful, there is a great risk that the knowledge gained would be used to inappropriate ends. Just as a politician would study history so as not to make\nthe same mistake twice, so must an Arcanist. \n    And so this is what the earliest parts of this book will aim to cover, namely where different forms of magic come from, how it has evolved over the many years, and important figures of study whos works and\ndiscoveries will prove beneficial to the process of understanding the Arcane.\nChapter 2\n\n    All forms of \"magic\" can be divided into two main categories, the first being Void Magic, and the second being Diety Magic, each named after their respective sources. The first of these two categories is the one which we will focus\non at the moment.\n    As mentioned previously, all Void Magic comes from the Void. The Void is the term for an endless expanse of nothingness, not physically of this world, that contains everything that has ever existed or been sensed in the material\nworld. In other words, nothing exists in the Void except for endless potential. At times, a being may attempt to make contact with the Void. If the attempt is successful, he or she may draw potential from the Void and use it in the material world. This\npotential can come in nearly any form. However, the being who attempts to draw it must first be very familiar with the form they wish to it to take. This difference in forms of potential gives us the different fields and sub-sections of Void Magic.\nNow because technically, any being has the ability to draw potential from the Void, any being also has the ability to practice Void Magic. This makes Void Magic the easier of the two main categories to learn. However, it can be difficult to establish\n╔══════════╗\n║\n║\n║   **************      ║  A diagram of the\n║    five elements\n║ is depicted on this\n║          page\n║   **************\n║\n║\n║\n╚══════════╝\n╔══════════╗\n║\n║\n║   **************\n║   A table of tips\n║ for connecting to\n║     the Void is\n║ shown on the page\n║   **************\n║\n║\n║\n╚══════════╝\na connection the the Void, and even more difficult to maintain that connection while drawing potential from it. First, the user must abandon his or her mental perception of reality, and instead open it up to the endless possibility of a limitless nothing.\nAs our minds are finite, it is impossible to grasp something infinite. Because of this, it is only possible to access certain parts of the Void, and not its entirety.\n    Connecting to the void is an exhausting and daunting task, and can take many\nattempts, especially on one's first experience. In order to establish and maintain contact with the Void, one must use the mystical energy which fills our bodies and world known as Mana. Just as performing physical tasks costs physical energy, so does\nperforming magical tasks cost magical energy. When Mana is depleted, or when the user is distracted, the connection to the Void is broken and any potential taken from the Void returns to it.\n    This brings us to the two governing laws of all Void Magic.\nThe first is called the Law of Conservation of Reality. It states that anything performed by magical means takes up the same amount of energy as if it were performed by non-magical means. In other words, there is no endless supply of energy for magic. The\nsecond law is called the Law of Creation, and states that something cannot be created from nothing. This is to say that all magical feats require an equal sacrifice to perform, usually in the form of Mana. It also states that all Void Magic comes from the\nVoid and must return to it. Now this second law is governed by what is called the Evaglno Effect, which says that just as seemingly super-physical feats can be performed at times through the help of adrenaline, so can super-magical feats be performed, but\nat an equally taxing and detimental cost.\n    Now there are four main Archetypes of Void Magic, used to more easily classify the fields themselves. They are Alteration, Mental Manipulation, Evocation, and the Dark Arts. This book will not go in depth on\neach individual field of Void Magic, but it will provide a brief description of each Archetype.\n    Alteration is the most difficult of the four Archetypes to learn. It involves using the Void to shape and alter objects that already exist in the physical\nworld. Subtypes include Transfiguration, Telekenesis, Translocation, Household Magic, and the Whimsical Magics.\n    Mental Manipulation involves using thoughts, experiences, feelings, and images from the Void in order to affect the user's\nmind or the mind of another. It is a very dangerous form of magic, although it cannot physically injure someone directly. The subtypes are Illusion, Mental Magic, and Cognitism.\n    Evocation is the broadest of all Void Magics, as well as the easiest to\nlearn. Through Evocation, a magic user can \"evoke\" potential from the Void and manifest it in a physical way as elements, objects, or living things. Subtypes of Evocation are the Five Elements, Arcane Evocation, Arcane Shielding, and Conjuration.\nThe last Archetype of Void Magic is without a doubt the most evil and dangerous. The Dark Arts are manifestations of pure evil, and serve no purpose other than the corruption of the world and the harm of others. They are outlawed by most Nations, and\ntheir use will yield dire consequences. Subtypes of the Dark Arts include Contract Magic and Okar, Necromancy, Shade Magic, Soul Puppetry, and Fi'hiiran'tanya, also known as Magic Blocking.\nChapter 3\n\n    As even the simplest of creatures is aware, our world is governed by beings of higher power known as Aenguls and Daemons. They are still ruled by the mightiest of all beings, the Creator, who brought everything into\nfruition out of nothing, including magic and the Void.\n    These Aenguls and Daemons are constantly seeking to interact with the physical world in some way, and because of this, many of them offer supernatural powers to otherwise mortal beings. We call\nthese powers Diety Magic. Diety Magic is more difficult to obtain than Void Magic, because one must be granted it by the Spiritual Entity itself, though control can be taught by other mortals with the same blessing. \n    All dieties expect something of\ntheir followers, and so not fulfilling these obligations results in what is known as an unattunement from the diety. This is known as the Law of Servitude, and states that a diety may at any time grant or take away magical abilities at a whim. This is not\nto say that it happens often though, and usually requires some sort of grave act of disobedience.\n    Now because of the first two laws of magic, we must assume that even Diety Magic requires some form of energy or sacrifice to perform. However, in this\nspecific instance, it does not only tax the user but the spirit as well. The magic used feeds off of the connection that the user has made with his or her mystical patron, and so when that connection is used up, no more magic can be performed for a period\nof time.\n    Due to their shared belief in the same or similar Dieties, many users of Diety Magic band together to form Guilds, Orders, and even Cults dedicated to the study and responsible use of their respective field of magic. Popular examples of such\ngroups include the Druidic Order, followers of the Aspects Ceridwen and Cernunos, the Ascended, servants of the Aengul Ariel, and those who practice Muun'Trivazja, Kharajyr Priests of the Daemon Metztli.\n    Now those Dieties which grant magical\nabilities often wish to establish a strong sense of contingency when it comes to their followers. As a result, Aenguls and Daemons alike have been known to strip magical abilities from their followers who attempt to practice other forms of magic. A common\nexample of this would be the Aspects \"unattuning\" a Druid if he or she were to establish a connection with the void. It is because of this nurtured sense of contingency that many practicioners of Diety Magic have harbored a mistrust and dislike of\npractitioners of Void Magic, and vice versa.      Now because there are a great deal of Aenguls and Daemons willing to bestow power upon mortal servants, the number of Archtypes used to categorize the many different fields of Diety Magic is far higher\nthan that of Void Magic. They include Druidism, Shamanism, Rune Smithing, Clerical magic, Monk magic, Ascended magic, Undead magic, Scourge magic, and Muun'Trivazja. Although there are more Archtypes, the Archtypes themselves are less internally diverse,\nin other words, they each hold far fewer fields.\nChapter 4\n\n    Now as we learned earlier, the use of magic still requires the expenditure of energy, whether it be through Mana or the connection with a Diety. However, most mortal bodies are not at all used to using these particular forms of\nenergy, and as such it can take a more costly toll on the user's physical body. This phenomenon is known as Corruption. The fourth law of magic, the Law of Corruption, therefore states that the more energy one puts into developing Arcane ability, the less\nis available for non-magical tasks. Therefore, in theory, an entirely magical being such as a spirit could not take on a real, physical form, except for through magical means.\nChapter 5\n\n    Just as with all things, magic was brought into existence by the Creator at the beginning of time. At first, magic was a very, very rare phenomenon. Not many people had access to it, and even fewer understood it. Only\na fraction of the currently known fields of magic were know during the first hundred or so years of recorded history, but great efforts were made to discover more.\n    Now as time progressed, mortals began to further explore this expanse of nothingness\nknown as the Void. Great wizards arose, and new fields of magic were found. The Arcane Arts were born as an object of study, and more and more people began to understand, study, and practice.\n    It was not at any specific time that the Aenguls and\nDaemons began to meddle with the affairs of Men, they did. As their involvement grew, so did the power and number of their followers, thus leading to the earliest Diety Magic Guilds being formed. These Guilds and their Void Magic counterparts yielded some\nof the mightiest, wisest, and maddest wizards the world has ever come to know.", ""),
