ind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind\nmind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind\nmind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind\nmind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind\nmind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind\nmind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind\nmind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind\nmind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind\nmind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind\nmind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind\nmind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind\nmind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind\nmind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind mind.", ""),
        StaticBook("To slay a Ghoul", "William Roswell", "Ghouls\n         =======\n\nBy Edward William Roswell\n*_*_*_*_*_*_*_*_*_*_*\n\nGhouls are strange creatures, the work of a deranged necromancer, they are deadly beasts, coming in many shapes and sizes, each-\n- a different set of skills, claws and teeth.\n\nGhouls are driven by their hunger, appearing to not feel any form of emotion, the only thing which can cause it to show a reaction is sudden and severe pain.\n\nGhouls often attack when you least expe-\n-ct it, often stalking it's prey from afar before striking.\n\nGhouls stalk their prey at a distance to mask their smell, it being a similar smell to rotting corpses.\n\nOften before the attack, the victim can smell the ghoul, but only when it is-\n- very close to the house, the smell of ghouls being easier to smell when the creature is outside.\n\nAs the ghouls attack when you least expect it, it is hard to combat and every ghoul comes with it's own et of deadly surprises.\nBut the one thing the ghouls have all shown to have incommon is their weakness to Aurum weaponry.\n\nAurum works effectively against the beasts, it being one of it's main weaknesses, it might be caused due to something similar to an allergic reaction to th-\n-e metal.\n\nAlthough aurum is effective against them, the one thing which can kill a ghoul instantly is a strike towards it's neck, sever the neck and the beast dies instantly.\n\nSome tend to explode after death, leaving-\n- a horrible smell and black muck covering the explosion radius.\n\nFerrum weapons are also effective against ghouls if, and only if you strike it's neck to kill it instantly, otherwise it is useless.\n\nFire is also highly effective against ghouls, either-\n- setting it on fire or using the fire to distract it works, as it causes the beast pain.\n\nAlways keep a broom handy to clean up the mess which a dead ghoul leaves, as slaying a ghoul is a truly messy ordeal.\n\n*_*_*_*_*_*_*_*_*_*_*", ""),
        StaticBook("To Speak To One", "A.N.C.", "To Speak To One\nby A.N.C.\n\"Bespeaketh,\" they say. \"Bespeaketh to we.\"\n\"No speaketh,\" says I. \"I speaketh, I die.\"\n\"Why ceaseth?\" asks they. \"Why ceaseth to we?\"\n\"No trusteth,\" I ply. I trusteth no fly.\n\"Do trusteth,\" says they. \"Do ceaseth no speaketh.\"\n\"No do,\" I reply, for \"trusteth be badeth.\"\n\"Cease trusteth be badeth,\" says they, me thinks sly.\nTo they I shake nay, \"For badeth be speaketh.\"\nBut when gone I comply, for badeth no speaketh.\nDo pain do we part, for this speaketh be goodeth.\nFor no speaketh be killeth, and alath, I died.", ""),
        StaticBook("To the stauntons", "Vincent Voronwë", "Dear Stauntons,\nI got in jail for the rest of my life and my brother has been killed. I NEED YOUR HELP! please try to free me i am the only one left that can keep the family alive.\ngreeting,\nVincent Voronwe", ""),
        StaticBook("Tobo", "Tobo", "~*~Living Simply~*~\n\nChewing on bamboo\n\nWithout a care in the world –\n\nThe Sun slowly sinks\n~*~ Daily life as...~*~\nRolling in the branches Enjoying all the fun Dancing about so proud\nPulling at the bamboo And defending his tree Never going to far from home\nDanger lurks everywhere.\nAll is well... for now\n~*~Cycling Wind~*~\n\nThe wind goes with it.\nSeemingly, always to fit.\nIt goes without a care.\nYet, it is always fair.\nNature is like wind.\nA cylce, never to end.\n~*~ Connected ~*~\n\nAll together, one.\n\nWe stand proud and just as we are bound.\n\nAll one, together.", ""),
        StaticBook("Tome of Ages", "Ebonsquire", "Tome of Ages\n\n((Originally written by 501warhead))\nAk'ligid feltis umden kitk'yl\n\nTeh'neh Asil Tremen Ashengaurd Denuso\n\nKIL'EH YETGH FESTILUS OG'ON'US\n\nTkehn Quen Genitus Op'kelful \nBeware you those who cannot read, for this is your bane.", ""),
        StaticBook("Tome: Air Evo", "Uradriethiel", "Air Evocation\nThis book was created by Master Air Evocatonist Alatar of the Mage Academy\n\nRecopied By\nUradriethiel\nTable of Conents\n\nChapter 1: The Void\n\nChapter 2: All About Wind\n\nChapter 3: Summoning\n\nChapter 4: Basic Spells\nChapter 1: \nAccording to legend and study, the void is an infinite plane of potential thought and energy surrounded by absolute darkness. Since complete darkness is rather hard to imagine, think about a blank canvas.\nHypothetically, anything can be drawn out of the void if it does not contradict nature. The first steps of becoming a mage is learning to understand the void and it's 'blank' \nconcept. Meditation is the most efficient and common way, as I have found.\nLearn how to blanket yourself in a blank state of mind and prepare to paint your canvas.\n\nChapter 2:\nWind and air are essences to all life around the realm of existance. You breathe it into your very self.\nWith the art of air evoction, you manipulate it. Wind is unpredictable and one\nof the best studied branches of arcane magics, You must experience the element in it's true form before you can even think about summoning it.\nIn order to achieve this, I suggest moving to a  region of high altitude and feel the wind rush over your very body. Experience how pressure seeps through the cracks of all substance. Taste it, feel it, smell it.\nAnother useful experiment is placing something in the air that can float. Watch how the wind carries the item and gently cradles it back down to the earth or\ncarries\nit away\nChapter 3:\nAfter you have learned all there is to know about your element, and your meditation is firm; you may begin your attempts in summoning your element from the void. Firstly, you must find a place of peace.\nNext, use your blank canvas to imagine air, allow the thought to fill you and the energy of yourself to course through your very blood. Through combining all of this. A small amount of wind may be conjured.\nWarning: You may pass out a few times uponfirst connection.\n\nChapter 4: After you have mastered basic connection, you can move onto some of the key spells in an air evocationist's arsenal.\nThe first and most obvious, is the force push. Essentially, a novice air evocationist musters all of his strength for one large\nand forceful push at the enemy. Secondly, and slightly more defensive, is the protective tornado.\nA skilled air evocationist can create a spiraling current of wind that could potentially keep most objects out of your sphere. That concludes this tome.", ""),
        StaticBook("Tome: Air Evo.", "The Scribe", "Air Evocation\nThis book was created by Master Air Evocationist Alatar of the Mage Academy.\n\nRecopied by The Scribe\nTable of Contents\n\nChapter 1: The Void\n\nChapter 2: All About Wind\n\nChapter 3: Summoning\n\nChapter 4: Basic Spells\nChapter 1:\nAccording to legend and study, the void is an infinite plane of potential thought and energy surrounded by complete darkness. Since complete darkness is rather hard to imagine, think about a blank canvas.\nHypothetically, anything can be drawn out of the void if it does not contradict nature. The first steps of becoming a mage is learning to understand the void and it’s ‘blank’ concept. Meditation is most efficient and common way, as I have found.\nLearn how to blanket yourself in a blank state of mind and prepare to create your canvas.\n\nChapter 2:\nWind and Air are essences to all life around the realm of existence. You breathe it into your very self.\nWith the art of air evocation, you manipulate it. Wind is unpredictable and one of the best studied branches of arcane magics. You must experience the element in it’s true from before you can even think about summoning it.\nIn order to achieve this, I suggest moving to a region of high altitude and feel the wind rush over your very body. Experience how pressure seeps through the cracks of all substance. Taste it, feel it, smell it.\nAnother useful experiment is placing something in the air that can float. Watch how tthe wind carries the item and gently cradles it back down to the earth or carries it away.\n\nChapter 3:\nAfter you have learned all there is to to know about your element, and your meditation is firm; You may begin your attempts in summoning your element from the void. Firstly, you must find a place of peace.\nNext, use your blank canvas to imagine air, allow the thought to fill you and the energy of yourself to course through your very blood. Though combining all of this. A small amount of wind may be conjured.\nWARNING: You may pass out a few times upon first connection.\n\nChapter 4: \nAfter you have mastered basic connection, you can move onto some of the key spells in an air evocationists arsenal.\nThe first and most obvious, is the force push. Essentially, a novice air evocationist musters all of his strength for one large and forceful push at an enemy. Secondly, and slightly more defensive, is the protective tornado.\nA skilled air evocationist can create a spiraling current of wind that could potentially keep most objects out of your sphere. That concludes this tome.\n\nFor any other questions, feel free to visit the Mages' Guild.", ""),
        StaticBook("Tome: Air Evo.", "Violetoin", "Air Evocation\nThis book was created\nby Master Air\nEvocationist Alatar of\nthe Mage Academy.\n\nRecopied By \nVioletoin\nTable of Contents\n\nChapter 1: The Void\n\nChapter 2: All About Wind\n\nChapter 3: Summoning\n\nChapter 4: Basic Spells\nChapter 1:\nAccording to legend\nand study, the void is\nan infinite plane of\npotential thought and\nenergy surrounded by\ncomplete darkness.\nSince complete\ndarkness is rather\nhard to imagine, think\nabout a blank canvas.\nHypothetically,\nanything can be drawn\nout of the void if it\ndoes not contradict\nnature. The first steps\nof becoming a mage is\nlearning to understand\nthe void and it's 'blank'\nconcept. Meditation is\nmost efficent and\ncommon way, as I\nhave found.\nLearn how to blanket\nyourself in a blank\nstate of mind and\nprepare to create\nyour canvas.\n\nChapter 2:\nWind and Air are\nessences to all life\naround the realm of\nexistance. You\nbreathe it into your\nvery self.\nWith the art of air\nevocation, you\nmanipulate it. Wind is\nunpredicatable and one\nof the best studied\nbranches of arcane\nmagics. You must\nexperience the\nelement in it's true\nform before you can\neven think about\nsummoning it.\nIn order to achieve\nthis, I suggest moving\nto a region of high\naltitude and feel the\nwind rush over your\nvery body. Experience\nhow pressure seeps\nthrough the cracks\nof all substance.Taste it,\nfeel it, smell it.\nAnother useful\nexperiment is placing\nsomething in the air\nthat can float. Watch\nhow the wind carries\nthe item and gently\ncradles it back down\nto the earth or carries\nit away.\n\nChapter 3:\nAfter you have\nlearned all there is\nto know about your\nelement, and your\nmeditation is firm; you\nmay begin your\nattempts in summoning\nyour element from the\nvoid. Firstly, you must\nfind a place of peace.\nNext, use your blank\ncanvas to imagine air,\nallow the thought to\nfill you and the\nenergy of yourself to\ncourse through your \nvery blood. Though\ncombining all of this.\nA small amount of\nwind may be conjured\nWarning: You may\npass out a few times\nupon first connection.\n\nChapter 4:\nAfter you have\nmastered basic\nconnection, you can\nmove onto some of the\nkey spells in an air\nevocationists arsenal.\nThe first and most\nobvious, is the force\npush. Essentially, a\nnovice air evocationist\nmusters all of his\nstrength for one large\nand forceful push at\nan enemy. Secondly,\nand slightly more\ndefensive, is the\nprotective tornado.\nA skilled air\nevocationist can\ncreate a spiraling\ncurrent of wind that\ncould potentially keep\nmost objects out of\nyour sphere. That\nconcludes this tome.\n\nFor any other\nquestions, feel free to\nvisit the Mages' Guild.", ""),
        StaticBook("Torn Journal", "Mosue", "...leads me to believe we are not safe here. Kenneth continues to ramble on and on about GOD knows what. I swear he’s a delusional man. Just last night, he looked like he was about to stab his wife- for what? A barely undercooked steak?\nNo, there is something else going on here. At night, I can hear the metal gates open and close… who could be leaving at such an odd hour?\n[!] The rest of the pages are ripped and torn out.", ""),
        StaticBook("Torn Page", "Ancient Dwarf", "[!] This page has been torn from it's respective tome.\nScratching on the walls. That's all I've heard for the last.. what? I can't count the days any longer. All of my kinsmen have gone missing. I'm all that's left. Khorvad preserve me, for I fear I shan't escape his place alive. Footsteps... I hear footsteps", ""),
        StaticBook("TOTALLY LEGIT AD", "Cery'lin", "[!] A detailed sketch of a woman with beautiful, smooth skin would be seen here.\nOTHER WOMEN ENVY HER!\nSHE IS 58!\nLOOKS 21!\nLOCAL FELSEN WOMAN FINALLY EXPOSES HER SHOCKING ANTI-AGING SECRET.\nLEARN THE 5 MINA TRICK TO HER RESULTS!", ""),
        StaticBook("Totus Annil'ker", "KawaiiAlice311", "Name: Totus Annil'ker\nRace:Mali'ker\nGender:Male \nHe lives in Ebonglade and is a gaurd there as well, he likes the color green and his favorite food is spaghetti with cream cheese and kalmon.", ""),
        StaticBook("Tower, v?", "Unknown", "When the world of Humans was still rather young, before Iblees wrought his ugly curse on the world, Horen, the great Father of all Humans, had comissioned the construction of a great watchtower to the north of the capital, in the\nwoods. Horen thought to use the tower to watch over any coming threat from the North, or to watch for bandits fleeing through the canopies of the trees. Moreso, perhaps he simply had it built because he could. He was always fond of\nsuch elegant structures and his people loved them as well. The tower was completed, spiralling high into the sky, always seeming as if the edge of the clouds was just within reach, yet never quite touching them. But the tower would be short lived, for\nshortly before its construction, the creature that came to be known as Iblees arrived in the land of Aegis. I scoff still at the accusations that this monstrosity's existence was a test from some god....for if it was, he is a cruel god indeed. But I\ndigress. Iblees came, and as the Ancient History states, tempted all of the brothers except for Krug with his ill-wishes. As we all were oft told as babes, Krug refused to give in to any temptation but when challenged to single-combat by\nIblees, he readily accepted. It was then that Iblees would show his true form, attacking all four brothers, all of their children , and all of Aegis. Countless lifes were lost as were many tomes of knowledge, and many architectural works.", ""),
        StaticBook("Translocators", "Archmage Haadi", "~*~\n      On the creation\n    of translocators\n                  -\n\n\n\n              \\o/\n              -O-\n              /o\\\n[!] Along the page you see many strange markings and drawings. They look to be rough copies, at best. There appears to be some sort of archway built of stone, below it, several odd circular schematics, or perhaps a map. Next to the map, the phases of the\nmoon trail along.\n\n[OOC] View in browser:\n\n tinyurl.com/slatrop\n-------------------\nAn odd name, indeed. Though, the actual device is far more mysterious and eldritch in nature. Translocators, Lori Oathcast refers to them as. Crumena took to calling them closets, as per their birthplace, the closets within Rasmot's magical porta-potty.\nRegardless, the process for creating these translocators is a fairly complex ritualistic one that involves both a skilled Void Translocationist with knowledge of the subject, and a fairly powerful enchanter capable of binding the spell to the locations\nin question.\n---\nCreation\n\nAn enchanter holds the magic to the spot. One who knows how to send matter to the Void and bring it back again must have learnt to create what is like a 'gateway' into the Void. More than just the item itself, but anything that touches it. Even\nair. Then again you do it on the other end and the mage attempts to pull the block back through the Void and the enchanter binds that to a doorway or pillar or something. In short, one must reach into the Void and pull out an open space.\nMaintenance\n\nAfter time, it will need a mana supply or one end will fizzle out before the other and create an endless hole. It's advised then that you have the enchanter available fairly often to refill whatever large mana gem or crystal you wish to power\nthe translocators' magic with. As far as i'm aware, this would be the only option, unless one would be able to get ahold of Kalameet's notes on the mana obelisk. For the ignorant, this is large pillar in which sums of mana are stored for use. The mana\ncannot be pulled out by any means by a magi to fuel their own spellcasting, but instead it exerts energy needed to power enchantments in the area. I'd elaborate further, though i'm only going off of what i've seen myself.", ""),
        StaticBook("Translocators p2", "_Elad_", "Translocators Mk.1:\n\nFirst Observations-\n\nThere appears to be a delay on the appearance of entities through a set of portals, but it otherwise works just as Rasmot's closets had. The creation of the portal at first results in a great\npulling force towards the opening. Such an effect may be troubling to observe, but otherwise seems harmless. Further tests may be required. Organic matter has yet to be tested for transportation, but normal objects as stated transport successfully.\nAn era of magic has dawned, and we are the Harbingers of the future.\n\nHuman Trials-\nThe first guinea pig passes through the gateway as I write. We are hopeful, but it is uncertain whether or not the elf quite literally walks into his own death.\nFootnote:\nIn waiting, we have coined this delay in translocator transportation with the name \"lag\". \n\nHuman Trials Cont.-\nGlacio has passed through the portal. We watched with anticipation for his return, and a few elven minutes later, there\nhe was. This new delay still brings concern, but i'm certain with some manner of tinkering, and perhaps more exotic and rare gems, it can be lessened. Organic and non-organic manner is officially clear for teleportation.\nNew developments in world events render the translocator useless. These 'tomes of power' have made my invention defunct, and now I am stuck with nothing more than a fancy arch. Shame.", ""),
        StaticBook("Traveler's Guide", "Illmori", "Nice and Bad Places\nGood=10\nNice=9\nPretty good=8\nPretty nice=7 \nOkay=6\nWell=5\nNot so Nice=4\nNot so good=3\nUgh=2\nEww=1\nWorse=0\nNerezza - 3\nPetrus - 8\nHaelun'or - 9\nCerulin - 9 \nDul'Sildure - 7\nAkovia - 5\nWerdenberg - 8 \nViridian Inn - 3\nDwarven City - 7\nCloud Temple - 7 \nUruk Lands - 6\nDwarven City with ships - 2\nConyon lands - 5\nBronze Elven Lands - 7\n\n-=-=-\n\nTourist's Guide : \n\nForest Walks : Cerulin, Haelun'or, Petrus, Nerezza, Akovia, Dwarven city with ships\nSwimming : Cerulin, Dul'Sildur, Dwarven city with ships, Bronze elven lands\nEarning Tan : \nUruk Lands, Dul'Sildur, Petrus\n\nParty and Events \n(One to Ten scale used) \nNerezza - 2\nPetrus - 10\nHaelun'or - 8\nCerulin - 4\nDul'Sildur - 7\nAkovia - 3\nWerdenberg - 5\nViridian Inn - 8\nDwarven City - 3\nCloud Temple - 7\nUruk Lands - 3\nDwarven City With Ships - 1\nConyon Lands - 3\nBronze Elven lands - 7\n\n-=-=-\n\nDescriptions : \nNerezza - Unpeaceful Cold Kingdom (Bad for elves) \nPetrus - Friendly Central\nKingdom. May have some problems there sometimes. \nHaelun'or - A beautiful, but spooky city, high racism rates\nCerulin - A Beautiful, but spooky city, high control rates\nDul'Sildur - Scary, but interesting castle. Good for walks or adventures. Have a lot\nof secrets\nAkovia - Small Kingdom, few citizens, not so fun\nWerdenberg - Small City, lots of citizens, pretty fun\nVirdinian inn - Inn with lots of things happening in it. Good for scandals or fights\nDwarven city - Absolutely boring place, but beautiful\nsomehow. \nCloud Temple - The. Center. Of. The. World. \nUruk Lands - Hot and dry places, bad peoples, lots of fights\nDwarven city with ships - What The Nether \nConyon lands - Pretty boring, but interesting too lands. \nbronze elven lands - ...", ""),
        StaticBook("Treasure", "Knwell Quill", "The strong treasure lies deep within felesen, Maybe even in a hospital...", ""),
        StaticBook("Treating Wounds", "Kristoff Herrick", "Wound healing\n\nMy time in the Order of Saint Amyas did teach me a few things, healing wounds is one of them. \nLet's start by steps\nStep 1- Cleaning the wound. \nAlthough that might sound simple, water will not do, for a gash or\ndeeper wounds you'll need something with alcohol in it, like beer or something of the sorts. (ofcourse you need to take the dirt off first if it has dirt in it). \nStep 2- Suttering the wound. \nFor that you'll need a needle and some thread.Sounds easy\nright? Well not really, you need a steady hand and you also need to do it by these...Let's called them sub steps.\nSub Step I Heat up the needle. You need to do that incase of the needle carring any illness.\nSub Step II Clean the thread.You need to do\nit beacause you don't want dirt on your wound.\n \nSun Step III-Suttering You start at well the start of the gash or deep wound (Warning this does NOT apply for small wounds.) \nYou'll then sutter like your sewing a rag, a messy and bloody rag\nthat screams like a little girl, non the less a rag. \n\nStep 3-Bandaging\nA bandage can be almost anything, a old rag (As long as it's clean) A bed sheet (As long as it's clean) etc. Then you just wrap it around the wound. Done\nNote: If there's any sign of infection after or  before you done this process you'll be better off cuting off the leg.", ""),
        StaticBook("Treating Wounds", "Dela Vaik", "My time in the Oder of Saint Amyas did teach me a few things, healing wounds is one of them. Lets start by steps.\n\nstep 1- Cleaning the wound\nAlthough that might sound simple, water will not do, for a gash or deeper wounds you'll need something\nwith alcohol in it, like beer or something of the sort. (Of course you need to take the dirt out if there is any in the wound first.) \n\nStep 2- Suttering the wound.\nFor that you will need a needle and some thread. sounds easy right? well not\nreally, you need a steady hand and you also so need to do it by theese... Lets call them sub types.\n Sub type 1\nheat up the needle incase it holds any illnesses.\nSub type 2 clean the thread\nYou need to do it because you do not want dirt in\nthe wound.\nSub type 3 shuttering\nYou startat well the start of the wound or gash.(warning this does not include small wounds.) you'll then shutter it like you are sewing a rag, a messy, bloody rag that screams like a little girl, a rag non of the\nless.\nStep 3- bandaging\nA bandage can be almost any thing, a old rag (as long as its clean.) A bed sheet (as long as its clean.) etc. then you just wrap it up. Done.\n\nBy: Kristoff Herrick", ""),
