        StaticBook("Triage: VolumeII", "Félix de Montauban", "Triage: Volume II\nBy Felix de Montauban\n\nADVANCED TRIAGE\n\nIn advanced triage, physicians and specially trained nurses may decide that some seriously injured people should not recieve advanced care because they are\nunlikely to survive. It is used to divert scarce resources away from patients with little chance of survival in order to increase the chances of survival of others who are more likley to recover from their wounds.\n\nThe use of advanced triage may become\nnecessary when the physicians and surgeons decide that the medical resources available are not sufficient to treat all the injured. The treatment being prioritized can include time spent on medical care, or bandages, suturing and other limited resources.\nThis practice is most common in battlefield surgeon's pavilions and refugee camps. In these cases some percentage of the injured will die regardless of medical care because of the severity of their wounds. Others would live if given immediate attention.\nIn these extreme situation, any resources devoted to those likely to die anyway can be considered resources denied to those who might have survived (or perhaps suffered less severe disability from their injuries) had they been treated instead. It becomes\nthe task of the surgeon and nurses to set aside some victims as hopeless, to avoid trying to save one life at the expense of several others.\n\nIf immediate treatment is successful, the patient may improve, and this improvement may mean that further\ntreatment of the patient can be delayed while other critical cases are dealt with. \nTriage should be a continous process and categories should be checked regularly to ensure that the priority remains correct.", ""),
        StaticBook("Triage:Volume I", "Félix de Montauban", "The term triage may have originated during the Asulon era from the works of Dominique Custos. The term was used further during the Imperial Unification War by Auvergnian doctors and chirurgeons treating the battlefield wounded at the medical pavilions of\nthe war camps behind the front. Those responsible for the removal of the wounded from a battlefield or their care afterwards divide the victims into four categories:\nI.) White: Those who are likely to live, regardless of what care they recieve;\nII.) Black: Those who are likely to die, regardless of what care they recieve;\nIII.) Red: Those for whom immediate care might make a positive difference in outcome;\nIv.)Those whose treatment might be delayed and still achieve a positive outcome.\nSIMPLE TRIAGE\n\nSimple triage is usually used on the scene of a catastrophe, in order to sort patients into those who need critical attention and immediate transport to the surgeon, and those with less serious injuries. This step is usually carried out\nwhile the logistics of moving the wounded are sorted out. \n\nUpon completion of the initial assessment (detailed in volume III) by stretcher bearers or nurses, each patient may be flagged with coloured linen denoting their status.", ""),
        StaticBook("Triage:VolumeIII", "Félix de Montauban", "Triage: Volume III\nbr\nFélix de Montauban\n\nTriage Assessment Guidelines:\n\nTriage assessment should follow these steps:\nI.) Is the wounded able to walk?\n\nIf yes, they are automatically categorized as WHITE (Likely to live regardless) and treatment may be delayed.\nII.) Are they breathing?\nIf no, do your best to ensure that their airway is clear and inobstructed. If they are still not breathing after these steps have been taken, classify them as BLACK (expectant to die). If yes, move along the process.\nIII.) Are they taking more than 30 breaths in a minute?\n\nIf no, classify them as RED (Immediate). \n\nIf yes, move along process.\nIV.) Check their pule at their wrist, and also press on their skin. If no pulse is felt at wrist, or it takes more than 2 seconds for their skin to return to normal color after being pressed, classify as RED (immediate). If good, move along.\nV.) Do they respond to simple commands given to them? Can they look at you when you ask, blink when you ask, or wiggle their fingers or toes if applicable?\n\nIf yes, classify as GREEN (Delayed) if no, classify them as RED (Immediate)\nQuick Sheet of Categories:\nWHITE: Likely to live regardless\nBLACK: Likely to die regardless\nRED: Immediate care may result in a life saved\nGREEN: Treatment can be delayed and still result in life saved\nTriage Chart:\nWalking (Y/N)\nBreathing (Y/N)\nResp. Rate >30 (Y/N)\nRadial Pulse/Cap Refill (Y/N)\nResponds to simple commands (Y/N)", ""),
        StaticBook("Trial 1 monster", "Roland Sparrow", "Stag headed beast, thin, moves quickly.\n\nAurum affects\n\nFire VERY effective", ""),
        StaticBook("Trial of Lyu E.", "Andria Ith'ael", "\n   Evidence for\n    the trial of\n  Lyu Elibar'acal\n\n          \n           By\n     Andria Ith'ael\nI, Andria Ith’ael, Tilruir’tir of the Sillumiran of the Haelun’or, promise that all evidence presented in this tome is accurate as per my observations he content in the following pages is intended for the trial of Lyu Elibar’acal and it is my goal\nthat this tome is archived in the library after such to ensure transparency.\n\n       Evidence\n  Upon finding the room of the recently banished Braxus Ni’leya on fire, I quickly moved to extinguish the flames which remained.\nI noted a ‘Sillumir line’ - a barrier made of dark coloured wood and stone brick, however I was foolish and decided to destroy said obstacle. The barrier was graced with a sign which greeted those who passed with a phrase similar to: \"Sillumir Line\"\nand \"DO NOT CROSS!!!!\" \n\nWhile little could be found on the inside as the room was burnt thoroughly, there appeared to be little motivation for anyone to want to burn a recently banished person’s room. Granted, Braxus Ni’leya may have enemies,\nit seems odd that anyone would risk the entire city to fulfil a vendetta.\n\nThus, my suspicions fell on Lyu Elibar’acal for many reasons:\n\nI Braxus Ni’leya and Lyu Elibar’acal came into frequent conflict, eventually leading\nup to Braxus’ banishment due to Braxus trying to strangle or harm him. It is highly probable that Lyu would attempt a revenge attack.\n\nII Lyu Elibar’acal has also been involved in prior conflicts where he had fought with another member of his own\nown family. Alas, no trial ever proceeded regarding such a manner.\n\nIII Shortly after saving Braxus’ home from the fire, another Evarir by the name of Zaniil Tinuvriel told me that he had left Lyu Elibar'acal to\ninvestigate the office of the Okarir’tanya recently. Upon my inspection of said area, I found a barrier made of dark coloured wood and with a sign saying: “Sillumir Line” and “DO NOT CROSS!!!!!!” in a similar manner to the scene found upstairs.\nWhile it seems that he could merely be investigating the fire, due to his prior conflicts with the old homeowner it appears to be more of a revenge attack.\n\n  Furthermore, I also have the account of the Evarir, which is found on the following pages:\nLyu and Braxus are well known to have had countless arguments. I have been a witness to many and have heard Lyu threaten Braxus on several occasions. I believe him to be the culprit behind the fire, and he is known to trade in cactus green. -Zaniil\nDue to the reasons presented, it is reasonable that Lyu Elibar’acal is a dangerous member of our society and must be dealt with by trial.\n\nThis investigation was performed between the 20th & the 24th of the Deep Cold, 1521.\n\n\nAymaehr’sae hiylun’ehya \nAy’Larihei\n\n~Andria Ith’ael, Tilruir’tir of the Silver Enclave of Haelun’or", ""),
        StaticBook("Trial of Respect", "OwynMorin", "Dedicant Ruari's book of his trial of Respect.\n\nTo be reviewed by Guide Toren once completed.\n\nNo deadline.\nKetiley of the Direwolf\nDruid\n\nModir joined the order because she wanted to help nature and seek knowledge, much like my own reason. Spurned by a desire to serve our gods, she chose this life to become more intimate with both them and nature itself.\n\nDirewolf's Task:\n\nTo meditate at the highest point of the Oasis grove, where I am most uncomfortable because of the heat, for an entire day.\nMythel of the Wind\nDruid\n\nSeemed to dislike me and said that my personality needed to be worked on. She spoke very little. Her reason for becoming a druid was that she was lost, had no direction in life, and sought them out at the behest of a friend.\nWind Druid's Task:\n\nCombine two different items, representing the aspects. Then I am to show it to Toren, explaining how it represents them.\nAnthron of the Acacia\nDruid\n\nDesired to become a druid before the Oasis Grove was created. He joined so as to give back to nature, and the Druids that had helped him in Athera.\n\nAcacia Druid's Task:\n\nHunt an animal and say prayer to Cernunnos -\nBefore slaying it. Once I have done so, I am to use every part of it's body. Bones, hide, and all.\n\n*A small note is scrawled.*\n\nI have used the remains of the animal for various things, including a few different tasks written on these pages.\nJeremiah of the Ram\nDruid\n\nHe joined the order because of a debt owed to another. His pet died to save his life, and Jeremiah felt the debt he owed to his pet, and nature as a whole, guided him toward the Druidic order.\n\nRam Druid's Task: Go out and find\nan animal friend, or do  something nice for the one I already have.\n\nI have decided to seek out an eagle. Since the death of Jalr, my last winged companion, I have yet to take another. The time is now.\nOliver of the Solace\nGuide\n\nHad no intention of becoming a druid at first, instead he ventured to the groves to prove to himself that not all Elfen were bad, as he had been brought up to believe. Upon entering his first grove, he felt something\nindescribable. Everything seemed to make sense. He had been raised a faithful Canonist, and fought the change at first, but what he felt in that grove outshone any other he had ever had.\n\nSolace Druid's Task:\n\nI am to gather soil from the Garden of the\nAspects and pack it into a pot given to me by Oliver.  Then I am to take a seed from within the garden and plant it within. Lastly, I must hunt for and find a sacred spring, from which Druids are attuned, to sate the seedlings thirst with its sacred water\nElswyen of the Rose\nGuide\n\nShe became a druid because she was raised to be one. Her parents, Ouity and Isabella Deathsbane were both druids, and all of her siblings became dedicants when old enough. The life was made for her.\n\nTask on the next page.\nRose Druid's Task:\n\nMake sweets or baked goods of my choice, but first I must meditate with the animals and plants used to make it.\nZa'Kabar of the Shark\nGuide\n\nHe lived amongst nature his whole life and felt that he could do more to protect it by joining the Order. They were his first choice, and the other Kha did not seemed pleased with this. Needless to say, his kin exiled him.\nShark Druid's Task:\n\nI am to craft a new fishing rod for Za'Kabar, one of high quality and make.\nCal of the River\nArchdruid\n\nAfter the death of his second wife, Cal felt lose and alone. He had grown tired of being a soldier, and the Druids took him in. From then on, he has dedicated himself to the Aspects without looking back.\nRiver Druid's Task:\n\nAfter learning of my previous task, to hunt a beast and use it's bounties, he gave me a lesson on bone charms and instructed me on their purpose, and how to contruct one, which I am to do and hang at my grove.\nTaynuel of the Hare\nArchdruid\n\nHis story starts like many others, he had no home, no family, and no belongings. Druidism gave him direction in life, filling those voids.\n\nHare Druid's Task:\n\nGather samples of mushrooms in the plagued\nlands of the Uzg. Seal them within glass jars to make sure no spores escape.\n\nNext, gather him a lot of sand, as the plague can not pass it.\n\nThirdly, a trip of silken cloth of the finest quality, and a pair of perfect, clear diamonds. \nMuildir of the Tortoise\nHeirophant\n\n\nWhen he was young, he was raised to respect nature. He did not know much about the druids at first. At some point, he set out on his own and spent some time in Larueh'lin. There he heard of the Druids, and decided-\nto go meet some, to sate his curiosity. That is when he met Archdruid Taynuel, who explained to him the nature of Druids. It was there he decided it was his path and so began his time with the Order.\n\nTortoise Druid's Task:\n\nMy weapons were taken from me-\nand in exchange I was given a small potato. I'm instructed to go out into the wild and survive with only the tools of nature to aid me. I must do so for a total of three days.", ""),
        StaticBook("TTOTWA: Book I", "SapphireShard", "The Tales of a Travelling Alchemist \n------------\nThe Fundamentals of Alchemy\n \nVolume I of V\nForeword\nI have heard many a man ask why one, such as myself, would take such interest in the art of alchemy when there are spells that could do just the same, if not better, a job than an apothecary’s potion.\na job than an apothecary’s potion.\nMy response to this statement is rather simple, whereas one must have a certain gift for spell-slinging, rune-craft, and spirit-speaking,\none mustn’t need these frivolous commodities to use a balm to mend a wound,\nshroud themselves in a nigh-invisible cloak with the swig from a bitter bottle, or set someone ablaze with bottled flame. Alchemy is not merely reproducing a wizard’s spell,\nbut it is shaping the spell in a way for the common man’s use. While magic still has its place in our world, Alchemy allows those without the gift to reap the same benefits as any mage might.\nChapter 1: The Fundamentals of Alchemy \n---------------\nThe Principle and Law of Equivalent Exchange:\nAs I was providing my services to a rather quaint hamlet, which also provided me information of the local flora and more of the same legends I always here of, I was approached by a man of common repute, and he produced a sarcastic, yet thought\nand he produced a sarcastic, yet thought provoking, question.\nThis question was, and I will attempt to reproduce the dialect as best as my phonetic ability will allow, “ ‘ow come, Mister, ‘hat ye’ can make all ‘dis ‘onderful stuff\nNow as shameful as his speech may be, the question remains the same. How is it, that alchemists are capable or reshaping the spells of sorcerers for the common folk out of, seemingly, nothing?\nThe answer to this question is simple to state, but fairly difficult to grasp, and this is the Principle of Equivalent Exchange. Much like the money one gives to the merchant for the apple or slab of meat, one cannot have something for nothing.\nAs the saying goes, “there is no such thing as a free brunch,” there is no such thing as a free potion. This fundamental rule applies to old alchemy, through the use of symbols and rituals, far more than what I call “Modern” alchemy.\nAlthough I will not get into ancient alchemy, I will state why this rule applies to Modern Alchemy. To craft a potion, for example a bottle of alchemist’s fire, one requires the materials. Much like a spell casted by a wizard, one cannot make something\nMuch like a spell casted by a wizard, one cannot make something out of nothing.\nKnow that to make a potion, and the possibilities are truly endless, one must take plants, objects, and other representations of what they wish to\nother representations of what they wish to make.\nFor example, alchemist’s fire could be best produced with an -inked out-, a mixture of -inked out-, -inked out-, and a dash of -inked out-. Why would one need these items?\nThe -inked out- provides the basic mixture of what we will make, and of course the better quality of the -inked out- the better quality of the Alchemist’s Fire, but of course we will get further into this in a later chapter.\n-inked out-, a -inked out- that grows most often in deserts, is often -inked out- and often associated with -inked out-. This provides an superb symbol for the element of -inked out-. -inked out-, another powerful symbol of -inked out-,\nmixes well with the crushed -inked out- to produce something truly strong. The-inked out-, an optional portion, is another symbol of -inked out-, albeit both weaker than -inked out- or\n-inked out- for both a -inked out- and -inked out-’s fire burns far greater than -inked out-.\nNow then, although you have these symbols of flame, rage, and warfare, one mustn’t simply toss objects into the metaphorical cauldron. Precision is necessary, as always.\nOne must measure their mixture carefully, or they will give too much. And giving too much will tip the scales of Equivalent Exchange, causing unpredictable results. Why is this you might ask?\nWhen balance is achieved through Equivalent Exchange, a mixture of the elements for the bottled spell of your desire, than all is well.\nBut when you disrupt this balance and push on the world, taking and forcing in too much of one or more element then the world will push back. Of course a word of caution is required,\nfor one may think taking less is the best option when balance is not a certainty. This is a common misconception for not providing enough of one element will result in the same conclusion, imbalance and chaos.\nThis can be easily associated with cooking, too much of one ingredient will spoil the recipe, not enough of an ingredient will produce similar results. Of course it could also produce blander results,\nand in that case a potion may not be spoiled but it will simply be weak. This is preferable to exploding, of course.\nPreparation and You:\nNovice Alchemists often question me, what advice would you provide the budding alchemist, what sip of wisdom could you provide one who desires to tap your fountain of knowledge? .\nThe answer to this is rather simple, preparation is key to any potion. What is meant by this is that to mix a potion that you have not mixed before, or do not mix often, you must have all the facts and supplies before you begin.\nThis is best provided in a short list of required procedures if one wishes to keep their eyebrows, fingers, and life.\n1. Have all the Facts\n2. Know the Recipe\n3. Clean and check all your instruments are in working order\n4. Ensure you have plenty of whatever ingredients you are using\n5. Keep a Calm Dispostion\nI believe the only topic that requires explanation is “Have all the Facts” and by this I mean that one should not mix a potion they know nothing about.\nEven having the recipe is not enough to craft a potion safely, but knowledge of its origins, ingredients, and uses are crucial. Although budding alchemists often overlook this step, it may save your life one day.\n((OOC Footnote: Due to the constraints of MC books, the Elememts of Alchemy must be made into a seperate book. It is titled \"The Tale of the Travelling Alchemist: Elements of Alchemy\"))", ""),
        StaticBook("Tu' Dear Mylas", "'Yogi'", "Yogi - Father of Thulsan, Slayer of Beasts, Breeder of steed. \n\n1544 of Harvest\n'Today I beheaded a great orc, It was almost too easy, I decided to mount his head on a plaque in my hideout - good luck finding that Thulsan!'\n8th of Sun's Smile\nToday Thulsan decided to create a couple pairs of chainma [!] The writing stops and appears hurried\n\n\"Mylas, this is  Yogi. I'm afriad my task as a Spymaster has finally called. you may come across me in the future\" ~Yogi <3", ""),
        StaticBook("Two Doors", "Dolan Velethen", "Two doors, both opaque in feature, stood in front of two staggly men. On the left stoff Veren, a human whose life was reached half. To his right stood Hijol, a wood elf younger than a fox out of season.\nIn between them was a sign that read \"Choose carfully\". Hijol inspected the sign and claimed that the sign was closer to the right door than the left one.\nAfter some long discussion the two decided that they must both choose the same door. They felt tired and rested.\nThe next marrow Hijol woke to fine two sleeping children. Both girls of unrecognizable race. He awoke one and quickly told her his tale. \"The right door is correct\" He notes that the sign has dissappeared. \"There was a sign that read 'There must be only\none right door'\".\n\nMeanwhile Veren awoke to find the other girl. Afyer he told her she must not trust Hijol, the two groups conjoined.\n\nFighting words came out. In one corner stood anger and spite.\nThe other stoff curriosity and fear. The winner, only the one brave enough to turn a knob,\n\nThey grew weary and slept another night. The next marrow Hijol's girl and Veren's girl awoke to find themselves alone and, adults.\nIn Hojol's girl's mind she had two facts, she must choose a door with Veren's girl, and it has to be  the right one. Veren's girl was to scared to speak to Hijol's girl.\n\nHijol's girl only had one option, in her mind, she must take out veren's girl to\ncontinue...\n\nHer fists hit her face. Her blood tained her tunic. Fist meet fist, and death was quick.\n\nHijol's girl turned the right knob slowly. On the other side was horrer. Both doors went to the same place. A small\nbedrock room with a sign that read \"Life isn't going to bend it'self to your perceptions.\"", ""),
        StaticBook("UFOs: A Study", "Spoduminister", "15th of Malin's Welcome, 1569. \nI saw a strange object appear floating above the sea east of the Cloud Temple. However, it disappeared as quickly as it appeared. It had an odd, amorphous shape, appeared jet black in color. I'm not sure if it\ntranslocated, or if it flew at speeds faster than I could observe. What I'm certain of is that I saw something.\n\nI waited around for some time, but it did not return.  Its location was, if looking from the central tree, up between the boat to the Mali'ame\nand Mali'aheral. I've decided to interview some of my acquantances, and maybe some citizens or nobles. \n\nThe first man I interviewed had no knowledge or sightings of odd, flying objects. The second man said he saw something mysterious flying\nobjects, however they were skywhales. I'm unsure if these creatures are what this unidentified flying object could've been, but I was told the whales were not moving as fast as I believe the object was. The third I interrogated mentioned some giant ravens\nthey had seen a few years back, though I doubt that is what I am looking for. \n\nAt the library I read a book about a Sky Terror known as Duump'auga. I suppose it's possible that I could've spotted a creature akin to this.\nWhile I was unable to come to a firm conclusion, I believe there is still much more to be learned about these Unknown Flying Objects, or UFOs. Could they be Skywhales? Some kind of undiscovered creature? A deity? I urge you, the reader, to keep an eye out\nin the sky for any strange objects you may spot. Van'ayla.", ""),
        StaticBook("UH Small Guide", "Siri", "Upper House \nSmall Home Payment Guide\n\nHello Household Owner of UH Small! This book will guide you through how to pay your taxes.\nTo pay the bank, you need to purchase Tax Receipt 100, and place the Tax Receipt 100 in your mailbox before the taxation year ends!\n\nAnd if you keep paying on time, the Guild will reward you!\nOnly the Stewards and the Household Owner can withdraw from the mailbox, but anyone else can put things in it.\nIf there are any difficulties regarding payment deadlines, do not hesitate to drop a letter to any Steward or myself, and we will work with you to improve your situation!\n\n-Siri, Guildmaster of Laureh'lin", ""),
        StaticBook("UH Special Guide", "Siri", "Hello Household Owner of UH Special! This book will guide you through how to pay your taxes. \n\nAs a owner of UH Special, your yearly/elven weekly tax rate is 150 minas.\nTo pay the bank, you need to purchase Tax Receipt amounting to 150, and place 1 Tax Receipt 100 and 1 Tax Receipt 50 in your mailbox before the taxation year ends!\n\nAnd if you keep paying on time, the Guild will reward you!\nOnly the Stewards and the Household Owner can withdraw from the mailbox, but anyone else can put things in it.\nIf there are any difficulties regarding payment deadlines, do not hesitate to drop a letter to any Steward or myself, and we will work with you to improve your situation!\n\n-Siri, Guildmaster of Laureh'lin", ""),
        StaticBook("Ultimate Yo Mama", "Isilynor", "This book is blank, like yo moma.\n\n\nC:", ""),
        StaticBook("Umbra Contract", "Fuze Yaander", "------------------      Legal Document\n------------------  \n\n\nThis document, once signed will bind the signed recipiant as an Enchanter of Sera and the Dreadlands. The signed recipiant can perform services for others, but may\nonly use resources provided by Sera and her people, for Sera and her people.\n\nIf the recipiant agrees with said terms sign on the \"X\"\n\n\n-Fuze Yaander\nCheiftess of Sera\nX: Umbra Ignis", ""),
        StaticBook("Undead City Trip", "Ebonsquire", "The Journey to the Undead City \n\n((Originally written by MinistryofGamess))\n\nToday, ((13-7-11)), I am embark on my journey to the undead city. I am accompanied by my dear friend Darfin. We leave the Valve and heaf off to the North! We followed the road to Oren, but we left the road to the North!\nI was in the middle of the dark, skeletons and zombies were around. We had to be careful! \n\nDay two.\n\nWe started the day with gathering some porkchops. We cooked them. They were so delicious!\nAfter breakfast, we continued our adventure to the city! We found some cobblestone on the way providing a bridge! People must have passed this already! I was certain, people have been here! We fund a little house with a chest and a workbench. We must\ncontinue! We found lava flowing out a mountain! We must be on the right track! We found another lava pit! We must be close! We found a pink sheep! We have no shears to get the wool, but we have a lovely experience! We haven't seen any sign of undead\nanymore for a long time, we are losing hope! It's getting night, we climbed up a tree and set out camp. We put some torches around us. We decided to rest, we shall continue our journey tomorrow!\nDay three. \n\nIt suddenly started raining! I felt the presence of the undead! at last we found a road. It was very long! I thought this was the way to the undead city. I saw another lava flow from a mountain, I knew we were very close!\nThe road ended up at a house with a sign saying 'Here is the home of the damned. We are coming'. Also, there was a bookshelf with a book saying that the first person to discover the city was Balorcim, the Ascended on the ((10th of July)).\nWhen I turned around, I saw it! The undead city! We reached our goal! Now we must set up a camp. We shall spy on them and come back with information! As we approached the city we noticed that we could not enter because it was too high. There must be a\nway! \n\nDay four.\n\nWe made ourselves a little home with a farm in a mountain to stay the nights and do research. We found a monster spawner near our house!\nDay six. \n\nWe went out again in the morning to check the undead city for undeads! We successfully met an undead, two even! They shall discuss our request of being an undead. We will probably need to face hard challenges.\nBut we are up for it! \n\nDay seven.\n\nI am going back to the city again to spy on them. We couldn't find any undead this day, we return home.", ""),
        StaticBook("Undead Magic", "Ebonsquire", "Observations on Undead magic \n\n((Originally written by Demolicious))\n\nby Licios\nUndead are mostly known as the bane of all life, the pure evil of Iblees and with many other names. But rarely is our interest focused more on the quickest way to behead and burn them.\n\nHow many have actually given thought to their\npowers over magic? Few, I would say. I Still, their might is something never before seen upon this world. That alone would be worth closer study, though many would disagree with me.\n\nMy own encounters with the servants of  Iblees have shown\nme strength of their magic, and I was lucky to leave those encounters alive, in one piece. Even close to death I was during those encounters, I cannot help but admire their effortless command overe raw forces of the universe.\nWith surprising ease, even weaker among Iblees' servants are able to conjure fire and lightning upon those mortals foolish enough to cross their path. One can only be fascinated by the raw power their weild. And this doesn't even yet give a thought to the\npower of the mightiest of Iblees' necromancers wield. I have seen the flying city of the undead with my own eyes. I cannot but watch in awe and respect the amount of power it must have taken to summon part of the nether itself and lock it\nhovering high above the ground.\n\nYet we have not given a thought to their powers of summoning. To bring force of life, that, according to our knowledge, is a gift from the Creator himself, so easily back to the rotting carcass it once inhabited.\nOne can only wonder, how are mere servants of Iblees, who was a servant of Creator, summon Creator's gift so easily to the masses of shambling servants they make use for. \n\nThis is indeed a fascinating subject, but we have not yet\ncovered the one mystery. The source of necromancers' power. \n\nFor would every necromancer draw his power directly from Iblees himself, surely would even Iblees weakened, even as powerful as he is, being able\nto grant such powers to his followers. \n\nIf his power does not come directly from Iblees, then where is it's source? I believe I'm able to answer that question. During my encounter with a necromancer, I saw him wielding a staff with a golden head.\nThe staff seemed slowly to fade away from this reality, as he harnessed more of his magical powers. I believe that necromancers use elemental power locked within gold as a source of power for their powers. channeling Iblees' will through the staff.\nMagical powers of Undead are a fascinating sight, for they are something few have ever seen, and far less have lived to tell about it. Many are drawn by the lure of power that Iblees promises. Humans are swayed even easier than most, due to our\nshort lifespan. No wonder many seek the way to immortality, through undeath.\n\nAuthor's Note: Contact author for any information regarding undead or their legendary Necronomicon. You shall not go unrewarded.", ""),
        StaticBook("Une Lettre", "Sullas the Scholar", "Iatrilemar,\n\nAs you are the only\nMali'aheral of any real worth that I know of, I require your aid in a difficult project.\n\nIf you  wish to discuss this further, please meet me within my wooden nightmare by the decrepit arena.\nCome at your convenience, but please--time is of the essence. The Citidel is beginning to show its age, and I am unsure how long I have before what I require shatters. Should that occur, I will be dreadfully inconvenienced.\nYour associate,\n\n   -L. Sullas\n\n\nP.S. Do not exert your energies too greatly on your trip. You will undoubtedly require them.", ""),
        StaticBook("Unknown Book", "Omar Grimmer'Lak", "((The book has no pages.))", ""),
        StaticBook("Unsigned Book", "Lorena Faeliel", "Chapter 1: The Start of a New Day\n\n   The sun was bright, the grass was soft. A group of men standed in a group by the glisening ocean. As we waited we told stories of times of olde. \n\n  Today was the day of new hope. We were finnaly blessed\nwith the pleasure of calling a castle our 'home'. We began with a firm plan of how to get the works in motion. I was to build a farm, they others where to gather resources.\n\n  Suddenly after some mass confusion arised, slowly eroding after a quick\ndiscussion. It became  more a worry about food then shelter, so we began to fish. Unforuntaly none of us were well trained fishers. catch after catch we began to fish No Man's treasure from the sea.\n\nNot so long, The sun was setting for it's work was\ndone for the day. And the tents were pitched. Letting all of us a soundless night among the stars.\n\n  It wasn't before long the men captured some animals to support their food problem. They would survive another day.\nAfter a fort night of smooth survivle, no building prgress had been done, however the brother of Markus de Sazkozy had be convicted of murder. Which was only to be a misunderstanding for Drevin de Sazkozy was only protecting his Duke.\nThe next events turned to be upsetting, After a fight at the Town of Felsen, Drevin fought for his people of Adria, Later conviced a traitor by his brother. Markus was merciful, Letting his brother live through guidence of his Steward. Drevin was\nsentanced to enprisonment. Later He brought him to Felsen to give him a trial.\n\n  After a long session of Drevin's defence, We held a fair trial with fair jury in the Cathedral.\n\n After the charges, Drevin was claimed a Traitor and\nsentanced to be locked up in the stocks and pay 1100 Minas to the King.\n \n Cast out of his Family and Nation Drevin was now a labeled enemy of the De Sarkozy Barony.\nChapter 2\n The Tide of War\n===================\n[!]\n\nThe book has dried blood stains on the rest of the pages, a large hole sinks through the corner of this page!", ""),
        StaticBook("Update", "Armin Ambertoe", "Update:\n\nThanhium dust has spread from the lab to the vault and so the vault has been sealed. DO NOT attempt to break said seal or enter the vault due to a risk of thanhium spreading into the library. Also, DO NOT swim in the water downstairs, it is also\ncontaminated with thanhium.\n\n-Armin", ""),
        StaticBook("Urgent Letter", "Unknown", "--------------------------------------\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n-------------------------------------\n===================The location of this said beast and phenomenon is unknown. This creature, is something of nightmares... It's limbs are quite long and it has claws which can turn the thickest hide into butter. Over the recent weeks, I have spotted this\n===================-creature a number of times. It is accompanied by some sort of phrase which lingers in the back of your mind.\nI do not know the origins, or even the behaviours of this creature but it does terrify myself. I have seen many a party go\n===================after it. Their bodies are normally found as unidentifiable mounds of flesh when I check the nest once again...\n\nI worry for the Vailorians...\n\nThough there have been rumors and small gestures that the creature\n=================== -is able to break swords with the strength of its hide. It's vacant eyes able to pierce the visage of the bravest men. Even those whom believe in gods and aspects think this creature was a mistake. A myth which has come to life, to\n===================-haunt all those whom try to find it.\n\nThe Cannibal roams, it hunts and it will kill.\n\nThe Vailorian Bounty Company will have to be contacted to try and defeat this beast, as my own company has fell prey to it for far too long.\n===================May the bounty company have mercy upon my troubles, knowledge will be found in the coming weeks.\n\n\n-Terryn Cairse\nVailorian Explorer.\n\n\n\n===================[!] The last page is covered in maddened illustrations of humanoid figures, with small suns lining the corners. Lines gesturing from the sun towards the darkened humanoid.\n[!]", ""),
        StaticBook("Urgent Missive", "Tarien Arvius", "Missive to the Spellcrafter Archon II - The Seal\nTo the Spellcrafter Archon,\nWe have completed our studies of the Forgotten Dwarven texts, and the revelations are astounding. The Forgotten Dwarves indeed date back\nto ancient times and the first war against the Great Ravager. It seems that the architects of the city devised a plan to deal with the Ravager: a machine capable of sealing him away in a plane of existence forged by the dwarves themselves. The\ncaveat was the power source of the machine: it had to run on souls. The Forgotten Dwarves suffered a schism over this: while most of the ruling caste were in agreement, one dwarf refused and led his followers to the surface to join the other races in\na coalition against the Ravager. The remainder, lacking confidence in the other races, prideful in their abilities and unwilling to depart the Hall of the Ancients and abandon the abundant veins of valuable materials it was built upon,\npressed onwards with the grand machine they called the Seal.\nWhile we understand not the intricacies of the device it seems to consist of three parts: four towers called soul capacitors hold the trapped souls of unfortunate\nmortals that power the machine. Two colossal engines known as Plane Forges create and sustain the “pocket plane” as we call it, and a huge disc known as the Ethereal Magnet somehow captures higher beings and draws them into the huge\naperture in the floor that acts as gateway to the pocket realm.\nIf such a machine exists, and the Forgotten Dwarves completed it, then this could be the path to victory. Obviously fuelling the machine would be problematic: it would be wrong to feed\nmembers of the Enlightened Caste to it and the Lesser Caste is vital to sustaining our way of life. However, if the Forgotten Dwarves did successfully fuel it then we need not.\nThe books reveal the location of the hall, and my fellow\nfollowers of the Radiant Man are keen to set off. I am actually beginning to worry about his influence over them: the powers the Radiant Man grants are easily the equal of our arcane abilities, but those who draw on them most heavily seem\nto be the most loyal to the Radiant Man by far. Either they see him as a more worthy governor than the Circles, or, as I fear is the case, the Radiant Man’s power comes at the price of one’s psychological independence from them. I advise arranging for\na few of my men to be detained in the city and studied. In the name of security, of course.\nWe will set out for the Hall immediately. I have but one cause for trepidation.\nThe Forgotten Dwarves commenced construction on this machine,\nostensibly completed it…\n...and then they went extinct.\n- Spellcrafter Tarien Arvius", ""),
        StaticBook("V Veritatis 6", "Jacob Chapel", "Verbo Veritatis\nSixth Edition\n23-3-1480\n\nIn this edition:\n*Knights Rebellion\n*City of Dwarves reclaimed\n*The Karovian Column\n*Words from the Emperor\nAnd much much more!\nReporters needed for the newspaper\nDear readers, as always we are in great need of reporters for our esteemed newspaper, especially now Ralph Riley has stepped down from his position because of his prolonged battle with illness. Since patronage has\nbeen secured, we are able to pay much better rates for articles. As a reporter you can expect up to 500 minas per article!\n\nThe work is freelance, and you are paid per article delivered. Please contact Jacob Chapel if you are interested.\n((Contact me (Pepernoot) ingame or on the forums!))\nThe Knights Rebellion\nIn one of the greatest moves of treachery in our time, Maric Varodyr, once Grand Knight of the realm, and part of the Order of the Red Dragon has taken up arms against the Empire. After word had reached the Emperor that Maric was\nconspiring against the integrity of the Empire, intending to break it up into pieces. Immediately the Emperor dismissed him from his position, but Maric fled to the Keep of Angren and barricaded himself in there, along with a few rebels.\nOur grand Emperor demanded Maric to leave the keep, but the traitor defied these orders and claimed the keep for his own. Hiding inside the keep he proclaimed himself King of Renatus, a claim so audacious and without any real claim to the title,\nhe must surely be absolutely mad drunk on power.\n\nAs of now the traitors are still hiding behind the walls of Angren, awaiting their certain and inevitable death. Quoting our Emperor: \"Do not recognise this traitor who has so aptly deemed fit to call\nhimself King without any form of legitimization from the Church or the faith. Fight back against the foreign tyrants who would dare infringe upon our sovereignty, despite their signing of various treaties intended to ensure such would not be so.\nYet do not fight because of who you are, Highlander, Heartlander or Farfolk. Fight because by the creator above, you will never allow such a spineless snake-tongued worm as Maric Varodyr to sit upon the throne of humanity”\n\nCarry on Carrion!\nThe City of the Dwarves reclaimed\nSince the reconquest of the dwarven capital, the dwarves have been very busy to rebuild and reclaim their old capital. Nearly all signs of the Undead occupation have been removed and a lot of work is being done to\nrebuild the homes of the dwarves, under the guidance of Lord Balek Ironguy and Thurland Grandaxe, the architects of the arts guild. The new city will be completely situated in the mountains, with farmland in the valley in front of the entrance to\nthe mountain home.\n\nThe new city has been named Kal’Karaad, and it should be ready for habitation in 2 saints days ((11 November)). We of the Verbo Veritatis wish the dwarves happiness and prosperity in their new capital, and hope that it may last\na thousand years. Sadly the dwarves have not returned our goodwill to help in the current rebellion of the knights, and have retracted their offer to come to our assistance.\nFormation of the House of Magnates\nThe Emperor has formed Oren's first legislative body! Known as the House of Magnates, it consists of representatives, or magnates, of all the nobles of the realm, along with the burghers of Petrus. The higher one's\ntitle is, the more representatives and votes, one gets. The body votes on appointing various ministers, akin to privy councillors, who make up the government. It is led by the Lord Chancellor who acts as virtually any under-king to the Emperor,\nworking on the day to day affairs of the realm. Currently the Chancellor is Joachim var Sarkoz, a learned barrister from the court of King Francis who has been appointed by the Emperor to help facilitate the establishment of the Magnates while the nobles\nand emperor focus on the military expenditures at hand. He shall rule for three years, before he is either voted in again by the various Magnates or a new Chancellor, or known more colloquially as the Prime Minister, is appointed by the Magnates.\nLet us hope Prime Minister Joachim can guide our country to glory!\nHoly ward over Petrus\nOn the 10th of The First Seed, mages have cast an undead weakening ward over our grand city of Petrus. The main effects of this is to dispel the effects of their magics. The mages creating the magic, one of them being the ancient\nwizard Blundermore, forged a magic crystal and lifted it up into the air, defying gravity. From the crystal lights and other magical effects originate, bringing a feeling of safety and calm over the city.\nThe populace can rejoice over this noble effort of the mages, and it is hoped that the ward will keep the undead away for a long time.\nThe Karovian Column\nA Tragic End\nOver to the news from Karovia this week, and it appears that her husband’s reluctance to give her a child has finally pushed poor Lynn Rovin over the edge. The other day, her body was found lying at the bottom of\na cliff face, her stomach punctured with knife wounds. Though it has not yet been confirmed, early evidence appears to suggest that the blows were self inflicted. What an unpleasant end to what could have surely been a most enduring marriage...\nThis newspaper would like to extend its deepest condolences to her widowed husband, the Imperial Prince, Aleksandr Carrion for his tragic loss.\n\nIn other news, yet more spook-filled sightings of the Imperial Prince’s late twin brother, Yakov Carrion!\nAs reported in Edition Five, little is known of Yakov’s decision to betray Oren to the Undead scourge, though recent events suggest he has returned to to seek vengeance against close members of his family. We can only hope that the guard remain viligant\nin these dark and dreary times. In brighter news however, Aleksandr is said to have found a new wife to replace his dearest beloved. Rumour has it that this is said to be Lynn’s very own sister! We suspect it shall not be long before we have another\nroyal wedding on our hands!\n\nSo dear readers, with his wife’s demise and his brother’s fall to the Undead, where will Aleksandr turn now? Find out next time in the Karovian Column of Verbo Veritatis, Edition Seven!\n-Our anonymous source in Karovia\nWords from the Emperor\nTo the loyal fellows of Orenia,\n\nThough some minor rebellion plagues our lands, a simple matter it will be to drive out the meager forces assembled under the traitor Maric Varodyr.\nSuch a petty uprising is of little consequence in the grand scheme of our Holy Empire, such writing on this day regards the welfare of our capital, Petrus.\n\nWhen I had first been elected by the Electorate to serve as Emperor,\nPetrus had been little more than a ghost town. Now I am happy to say Petrus is the single most populated city in not only our realm, but of the world itself. This cannot be attributed to I, but to the true heartlanders of Petrus who have graced this city\nwith life. Petrus stands as the jewel of our modern world, in not just the strong economy, but in terms of cultural regards, social mobility, and military might. \n\nNowhere else in Athera can one find a public library with such a vast array of knowledge\navailable to the public, nowhere else in Athera maintains our efficient system of free food, and nowhere else in Athera upholds a city guard as do our Wardens of the Imperial Watch, a Watch at current roster twenty-four men strong.\nThe average daily income to Petrus coffers is 5,000 minas. The average yearly budget for the Imperial Watch is 20,000 minas. Petrus maintains a budget of 44,000 minas for building improvements and public works. Average yearly tax income is 4,000 minas.\nPetrus holds 53 separate plots, of which only 8 are not filled at the time of this writing; 43 plots had paid taxes last tax cycle. Truly the economic structure of Petrus is unmatched by any city in the world, and this does not take into account the great\nndustrial capability of our Holy City. Within Petrus are produced the finest weapons and armor in the entire world, with the most efficient labor force in the world harvesting vast quantities of wheat, ores, stone, and wood everyday.\nUnlike the treasuries of many other nations, the majority of goods produced are not stashed away but sold as surplus to the citizens of Petrus, weapons and armor worthy of Kings can be found for open sale and in quantity, at a pittance compared to what\none may find at the cloud temple auctioneer.\n\nThough there is much more than just numbers to Petrus, I hope this has given some insight into our holy Capital and I am proud for all the fellows of Petrus who make our city the jewel of the world.\nCreator watch over us all,\nTuvya Sigismundovic Carrion\nReturn of the Order of Saint Amyas\nThe Order of Saint Amyas has been reformed, with Adrian de Bar as its new Grand Master. Shockingly though, Adrian quickly pledged his support to Maric’s traitorous cause and was convicted of treason.\nAfter persuasion by Oren, and a lot of mercy on the side of the Emperor, he was returned to the fold quickly though. Hopefully he is wise enough to not betray Oren again.\nWe hope that the Order will become a beacon of purity and righteousness, especially in these dark times, and that honest God-fearing men fight for its cause.\nPetrus tavern under new management\nAs the old Throdo has not been able to properly take care of the tavern at the central square in Petrus, the tavern has been granted to a new owner. The tavern has been renamed “the Drunken Dragon”,\nand it has been quite busy indeed.\nThe Verbo Veritatis wishes Sam good luck with running the tavern, and may many drinks be drunk!\n\nWe hope you enjoyed the newspaper, and remember: Athera’s greatest bookstore, “Books of Oren”, is the publisher of this great paper. There are shelves upon shelves of amazing books for incredibly low prices. Visit us at Abbey Road 13, Petrus!", ""),
        StaticBook("V. Veritatis 4", "Ralph Riley", "Verbo Veritatis\nFourth Edition\n\nIn this edition:\n\n*Ithelias Literature Award\n\n*Great Alrasian Purchase\n\n*Orc Attack in Karovia\n\n*New Orenian Anthem\nAnd more!\nIthelias Literature Award\nBooks of Oren, home of the Verbo Veritatis newspaper, is proud to announce the “Ithelias Literature Award” given to the most noted writers among Athera. The award is given to those that excel in the art of combining words to\nknit together an exceptional piece of literature, worthy of the honours of being recognized as a true masterpiece. A grand novel, a poem, every kind of literature is valid to be picked. The award will be given every Saint’s month and it will be funded\nby Books of Oren and his Highness, our emperor Tuvya Segismundovic Carrion to promote culture among our people. \n\nThe award will be a document stating the prize, and a sum of minas for the three best works of art.\nThe rewards are:\n\nFirst prize: 2000 minas\nSecond prize: 1000 minas\nThird prize: 500 minas\n\nTo join the competition send us a bird, with the book included, or just visit the store at Abbey Road 13a, Petrus.\nWinners will be announced in two Saint’s weeks!\nThe Great Alrasian Purchase\nWith the disappearance of the peoples of Alras in recent days, Emperor Tuvya made hasty moves to purchase the old lands of Alras from a local squatter. Recently annexed and renamed as the duchy of Karovia,\nPrince Aleksandr Carrion now sits as ruler of the new and burgeoning town, a proper homeland for the Raev in Oren being established.\nOrc Attack in Karovia\nIn Karovia, which recently joined the Empire of Oren after the Great Alrasian Purchase, orcs assaulted a defenseless young man. While this was happening Jacob Chapel walked in on them. He tried to hide, but was quickly\nfound by the savages, and captured and kicked to the ground. There were three, a large male orc, an apparently pregnant orc and a goblin. They went by the names of Malgroth, Shadback and Smelleh, respectively.\nAs they were busy extorting Jacob Chapel, the Emperor Himself arrived at the scene, covered in his Blessed Armor of St Augustus, wielding his Holy Axe of the Seven Skies. The male orc was quick to rush towards Tuvya, but his broadsword was no match\nfor the armour. After a short fight the Emperor’s sword was covered in orc blood. However, the goblin had just struck his pickaxe into the young lad’s chest, blood covering the ugly gobbo and the lush grass. The pregnant orc and the goblin quickly\nbolted off, Tuvya chasing after them, and killing at least one.\nThis was no help to the lad though, as he lay dying in the grass. He passed soon after Jacob Chapel took his confessions, and lies in an unnamed grave just outside Petrus. He died on the 3rd of the Deep Cold, 1474. May the Creator bless his soul.\nCaroline Hightower has composed the anthem of Oren\nThe new anthem of Oren, Thirty to One, has been composed by the great talent that is C. A. Hightower. Its great lyrics will surely inspire the peoples of the Empire and fill their hearts with pride.\nThe anthem is available for purchase at Books of Oren, Abbey Road 13a in Petrus. Contained within are the lyrics and instructions for the music.\nAmoss’ Crimes\nPart 1: Assault on the Uruguan Ambassador\nLegend tells of a white wizard, namely Amoss. Many say he is a psychopath, others call him a cannibal. I am here to confirm that both of these are true.\nTwas a cold and wet day. As I was heading to Petrus, a gentleman with a white beard approached me, asking me for help. I of course was obliged to help him find somewhere dry to stay. I offered my office and we began walking.\nAs we were walking I was kicked to the ground and bound in chains. He gagged me and I was dragged to the Orc capital. I believe this was an attempt to ignite the conflict between Orcs and the other races. Luckily a man witnessed this event and alerted\nEraborn Greyhame. He gathered an army of Humans and Dwarves to come to my rescue. I was rescued, but my captor still lurks out there. I feel that he is a direct threat to Oren and to his Majesty Tuvya himself.\nUpon returning to Petrus with many wounds from the attempts of Amoss to eat me, I was in a dire position. Luckily I was helped by a very kind gentleman, Jacob Chapel, who along with the city doctor saved me from the brink of death.\nAmoss’ attempts to kill me were not at an end though. He corrupted my own Co-Ambassador with a large sum of money, and had him try to assassinate me. Several shots were fired at me, and he also attempted to stab me.\nLuckily the he was captured by the guard forces of Petrus and justice was served.\n\nA report by Ambassador Grigor of Clan Grandaxe\nPart 2: Treason in Oren\nAmoss’ crimes didn’t stop with the attempted assassination of Grigor Grandaxe, soon after the last events Faramith Greyhame released information about a conspiracy, of which Amoss, Eraborn Greyhame, and the rangers were part of\nTheir plan was to assassinate Yukov Carrion, beloved son of the Emperor, and after that try and seize Petrus. Their plan was foolish and the mad man named Amoss was captured quickly.\nWords from the High Pontiff\nGreetings to the progeny of Horen,\n\nI bid you lot fair weathers and fair harvest. I write to address the peoples in a distinguished press, the Verbo Veritatis, in hopes the Pontificate can expand its listening base to those\nmore secular. And many have been, thanks to the ill-efforts of my predecessors, who've distanced many from the Church's goals.\n\nYet I hope to act with clemency and charity, and insure that the Church serves the denizens in Oren, enlightening them of the\nfaith, administering their poor and sickly, writing its histories, ministering a breadth of education, and establishing the epicenter of Oren's culture, intellectualism, and spirituality.\nAt hand, the firstmost goal of our Church is to train and induct new members of the priesthood, and finish the translations of the scripture. The former has been going great, and the Church number swells to new heights not seen in decades, though many\nstill are recruits and hence ill-fit to wholly minsiter. We hope to teach these Acolytes and Deaconry every two to three saint's days. Secondly, is the Canon; the Church has been working intensively on drafting a Common adaptation of its Holiest book,\nthe best of the Holy Scrolls, and the foundations of its Church, so that it may be spread to more of Oren's populace, who languish in uncertainty and apathy in regards to the Allfather and his worldly instruments. We have developed two identical\nmanuscripts of three of the seven books, a miracle in itself, yet we await a third to be sure that is His ordinance.\n\nUnfortunately, the feast day of St. Tobias had to have been cancelled due to a rogue guard locking the city off from the good parishioner\nduring the time of the ceremony. We hope to reschedule another feast day and fair soon enough, and we must apologize with this inconvenience. Whilst the Church has not made its presence wholly yet, this is due largely to an inexperienced budding\npriesthood and an undeveloped Canon. Once those are developed, we may engage in more active missions and more frequent rites and sermons.\n\nMay the Allfather protect!\nRoyal Marriage in Danger?\nThe proposed royal marriage between the Imperial Prince, Aleksandr Carrion, and the Waldian Princess, Lynn Rovin, may be in danger of breaking down! Harsh verbal arguments have broken out between Lynn and Aleksandr involving\nAleksandr’s twin brother Yakov. Yakov merely made a comment about the Waldian accent, which the princess took personal offense to. She then started yelling at Yakov and also took her anger out on poor Aleksandr, who made no such comment.\nLynn Rovin has also been heard slandering the Vanirs, calling them “Rude” and “Disrespectful”. The cruel woman also tried to deny little twelve year old Lorina Carrion her right to be the flower girl at the wedding, and instead tried to put a Waldian\ngirl, not even related to her, in the position instead. The Rovin has shown clear disrespect and disregard for the Prince, the Imperial Crown, and Oren itself. And yet Aleksandr Carrion has shown fantastic persistence in marrying her for the sake of\nuniting all of Humanity under one Empire. May the Creator have mercy on his life.\n\nA report by Jarrik Bloodstone", ""),
        StaticBook("V. Veritatis 5", "Ralph Riley", "Verbo Veritatis\nFifth Edition\n\nIn this edition:\n\n*Aesterwald united with Oren\n\n*Yakov’s Betrayal\n\n*A report from Kal’Agnar\n\n*And more!\nA deserved apology to our readers\nIt has been long enough since the previous edition was released, and I ask for your understanding. I, Ralph Riley, had caught a terrible illness, that medications could not really fight. Feverish and unable to work,\nJacob Chapel kept the bookstore working, but the paper could not continue without me. Now I have finally recovered from my illness and I am up to my feet, and this release marks the return of the paper.\nAesterwald united with Oren\nOn the 11th of Sun’s Smile, 1477, Aesterwald finally joined Oren, uniting humanity once more. Voron Rovin I, König of Aesterwald, and Tuvya, our dear Emperor, signed the Act of Unification bringing together the two realms.\nFrom now on Aesterwald and all its vassals are part of our nation, and house Rovin is a Duke Elector in the Empire.\n\nHumanity should rejoice at this act of peace, kindness, and unity in this time of great peril.\nLet the united Oren be a strong force against the undead blight.\n\nThe unification act is visible in the public library of Petrus, and for sale at Books of Oren.\nPrince Yakov's Betrayal\nA great tragedy has unfolded in our dear capital city of Petrus, after our beloved Imperial Prince, Yakov of the royal house Carrion, was last seen leaving Petrus riding a horse heading towards the north.\nIt is unknown what happened on his voyage, but one thing is certain, he is no longer the cheerful man we knew. He is now part of the undead menace and we hope that it was against his will to join them.\nBut all of this was yet unknown when he-who-was-Yakov entered Petrus and requested a meeting with Tuvya, accompanied by two staff wielding fiends and an armoured dwarf. Our dear Emperor was attacked, and in a valiant battle he was able to slay\nthe evil monsters. Quickly the Wardens, Sonn Ander, and Airthorn came to his help. The battle was won, and he-who-was-Yakov was captured, but at great cost. Our dear Emperor was struck by Iblees’ lightning and gravely injured. To add insult to this,\nthe being that once was Yakov was able to escape, after a great explosion.\n\nOur feelings of safety and pride have been tarnished, and the wounds of this betrayal will never heal. It will take time, but Oren will rebuild and get stronger,\nand defeat the undead menace and get vengeance for the injustice and savagery.\n\nCarry on Carrion, and Creator bless our Emperor and nation!\nA report from Kal'Agnar\n“Upon my entrance into the former capital of the Dwarves, I see much has changed. A gruesome disfigured cultist stands upon the wall of dark stone, waiting to be called upon by his fellow cultists to raise the darkened gates.\nThe once luscious and green rolling hills of this mountainland now bear the signs of darkness seeping into the soil and killing off all that lives. The city seems to be fighting against the evil that’s entangling it, and turning it into a hall of death.\nThe only witnesses of this being the cultists who slit the throats of the innocent, spilling blood upon the one life filled ground. A cavern to the left of the gates show signs of new inhabitants. The cavern called Asgol is watched over by the Doomforges.\nThey bear the marks of cultists, and I can only assume they live in this dark covered haven of death and despair. Along with death being strewn into the hills around the city, a substance of pure read laces through the hills, and a pillar stands near the\nentrance to the mountain hall, made of the same red substance. It is lit aflame and is perhaps a shrine to Iblees, or some deceptive pillar put up only to mock the once great capital. Fires burn in the hillsides, never wavering in the cold winds flowing\nthrough the mountains.\n\nIt seems as though the new residents of left large portions of the capital untouched, though. The mines seem to be sealed shut. As I wander about through the capital I come across a house built into the hillside.\nA steady stream of bloody water leaks out of a pipe heading out of the house. Limbs seem to be stuck in the drain, forcing spurts of bloody water out and onto the land. Dark ominous rain clouds float above, drearily drifting past,\nuntouched and unknowing of the evil and death that lays below. \n\nMy journey through the capital lead me to the gate once again, where a man stoop pleading for help to cross the deadly ravine between the mountains.\nWhile leaving a ghostly sight crossed before my eyes, a steed of pure bone, bearing no flesh, with a rider wearing the face of a pig and the body of a man. The city has been bearing the weight of this darkness for some time.\nIt is changing, growing in new ways that can define its path. These cultists prove to only kill viciously and with no defined reason and murder the innocent common folk who walk about.”\nReporter attacked by bandits\nAn eyewitness account from our reporter, Telperian Faroar\n“Just upon leaving Petrus my path seemed to be barred by two elven bandits. They seemed small, perhaps wood elves. Although I could not see much under their cover\nof darkness and the rusty chainmail that covered most of their bodies, leaving only hands and face exposed to the faint light of the moon through the rustling forest leaves. They held ferrum blades, with the lives of others before me stained upon the dull\nsurfaces of the blades. I was outnumbered and unarmed. I took my chance, seeing a narrow path, as I knew I could be killed if I’d stay and try to talk my way out of it.  I sprinted off through with fearless steps as my damp leather boots hit the ground.\nI hid behind a tree and waited. The two elves had been slow to notice my escape, but they pursued me nonetheless. From where I stood I could hear their heavy breaths. I took a risk and peered out from behind the trunk of the tree I was leaning against,\nnoticing the two men not far apart, one following a few paces behind the other. If only I had a weapon… Rummaging through my bag I located my bow. Removing it quietly, I notched a feathered arrow with ferrum tip. I drew the string up to my cheek,\nand all was silent except for the heavy breathing of the two elves. I leaned out from behind the tree, taking aim at the man in the back. As I released the bolt it whistled into the night, heading towards the man’s foot.  A scream pierced the darkness\nas the arrow struck the man into his calf. The next arrow found its target, and lodged itself into the elf’s chest. The screaming increased in volume, and his companion yelled out into the darkness “Show yourself and fight, you coward!”\nAt these words I began to whistle a merry tune. I released more arrows in their direction, and gave them a slight smile before running off, disappearing through the trees.”\nThe Karovian Column\nImperial marriage in peril?\nWhen first asked to write this week’s column on the quiet and peaceful town of Karovia, I of course asked myself… What would the people of Oren like to read?\nA thrilling piece on the construction of Prince Aleksandr’s newly erected fortress perhaps? A finely worded treatise on the governing institutions which keep Karovian society afloat? Or even a riveting article on how the good people of this town go about\nliving their daily lives? No. For we all know the real reason why the people of Oren choose to read this great newspaper over the rest. Why, to indulge themselves in the latest gossip and hearsay of course!\nWord on the street has it told that the Imperial Prince Aleksandr and his newly wedded wife Lynn Rovin’s marriage is in danger! Sources on the ground have told us that the pair were heard to be partaking in a bitter argument, late into the hours\nof the night. Supposedly the subject of such feud regarded Lynn’s desire to have a child, a suggestion which Aleksandr has adamantly ruled out. It has even been suggested that he may be considering offloading his significant other in favour of\na new mistress! In the past the pair have not been unknown for their turbulent marital status, having been forced into partnership in a bid to create peace between the Holy Empire of Oren and the Königreich of Aesterwald.\nThankfully unification has succeeded, though as we know, the marriage itself has left much to be desired. Unfortunately our sources have reported that Aleksandr has so far refused to answer any of our questions on the matter!\nSo dear readers, what is next for our Imperial Prince? Will he succumb to his wife’s wishes? Or will he emerge a fighter after all? Find out next time in the Karovian Column of Verbo Veritatis, Edition Six!\n -An anonymous source from Karovia", ""),
        StaticBook("V. Veritatis: 1", "Ralph Riley", "Verbo Veritatis\nFirst Edition         \nIn this edition:\n* A new emperor!\n\n* Fishing up relics\n\n* Greedy dwarves harras chef\n\n* “The Farmer”, by W. von Bran, reviewed.\nRecent News.\n\nA new emperor for our nation.\nOn the 11th of Sun’s Smile, 1471. A new emperor was elected to rule over the Empire, listen to people and keep the nation safe from threats.\nTuvya of House Carrion has sworn to our creator to represent our nation in good or bad times, keeping the innocent safe from harm.\nIn the ceremony, Emperor-Elect Tuvya kneeled before the Lorraine and the High Pontiff, Daniel I.  He recited his oaths to\nchurch and state. Afterwards, he received the onyx Crown of the Exalted and the Blade of St Amyas.\nThe new emperor will surely bring better times for the empire and we will prosper more than what any other nation could.\nFishing a relic.\nFishing on the river outside Furstenburg, Phillip Krasline, a skilled fisher and part-time guard of our Majesty the Emperor, found a strange rock after a tug on his line. The rock appears to be an old knife carved out of a stone.\nHe said \"You can see it for yourself, it's crealy older than even the ruins here in Athera\".\nFor now the carved stone knife will be kept by Phillip until the Imperial Museum is finished.\nPhillip will return in the next issue to tell us all about the capture of the Fishlord.\nReports.\nClan Irongut harasses Oren’s greatest chef.\nThe Bada Bing Inn just outside of Petrus, owned by Oren's greatest chef known as Vinnie, is in the process of being stolen by the greedy dwarves of the Irongut clan.\nThe clan seems to believe that the land the Bada Bing Inn is built on belongs to them, and not to Vinnie. To them, this means the inn belongs to them as well. Shortly after leaving, I was able to ask him what the Clan father of Irongut, Balek\nwas going to do next. He replied with, \"Don't worry about them, I will be back and have them handled...\" This seems to be very foreboding for Vinnie.\nI joined the group of men and women headed towards the Bada Bing Inn\nas they feverishly sprinted to Vinnie's aid.  Once we got there, clan father Balek Irongut refused to open the doors, and threatened those that were there. After a short period of arguing, the door was broken down, and people rushed in from the back\nAfter a brief moment of thick intensity, Balek agreed to leave.\n \nWhat will become of the Bada Bing Inn? Will Vinnie get to keep his restaurant, or will the Irongut clan be back to take him down? Find out more next week, when all of this gets resolved.\nA report by Cain Vir Miles.\nBooks & Reviews.\nThe Farmer, by Wilhelm von Bran.\n\n“The Farmer & The Witch, or The Defeat of the Conjurer”, a two part series, is a captivating and well written story about events in an unnamed village in the Waldenian forests.\nIt’s focussed on a man’s struggles and foes he has to deal with. Mainly in the second part is an elevating use of rhyme, making the books a joy to read.\nA must read!\n\n-Jacob Chapel.\nWords of the emperor.\n\nTo many of our fledgling state, the customs and traditions of my people of the Raev come off as foreign, queer, or outright disagreeable. Though predecessors of mine who shared\nmy blood desired to make Oren a Raevir state all-and-all, such outlandish desires my Reign shall bring an end to. I may be the eldest of living Crows and patriarch of my House, yet I was not born amongst the people of Raev. My childhood home was\nthe orphanage of Old Abresi, where I was warded until I was old enough to strike out on my own. Heartlander traditions and speech come more naturally  to I than my blood of Raev, and I simply wish to reassure all who remain loyal to our Realm that there\nshall be no pushing of Raev culture or language amongst our denizens, that Raev will largely be isolated from the affairs of our realm, and that my Reign is not that of Raev, but that of Man.\nCreator’s blessings to all subjects of Oren.\n\nTuvya Sigismundovic Carrion.\nLetters to the editor.\n\nIn future releases, you can send a letter to Ralph of Riley ((PM to nachotp, on forums)) to our offices on Abbey Road 13 next to the Cathedral of St. Sigismund, in Petrus.\nAll your letters will be published here for you to express your feelings about the current state of the society, economy or maybe denounce a villainous act. We expect your letters for the current releases, they can be anonymous if you wish to.\n-Ralph Riley\nEditor of the Verbo Veritatis.", ""),
        StaticBook("Vailor, A Report", "Fygmor Khen'aum", "The State of Vailor 1557, A journal.\n\nVailor is the place where the decendants live, The Orcs, The Dwarves, The Humans, The Halflings, The Elves, and the Kha. Amoung other magical constructs and creatures. It is a large island where most of\nthe cities sit upon plains or mountians, or even smaller minor islands. \n\nChapter 1 : The Monk Temple.\n\nThe main hub to all of vailor is the dock owned by the Monks.\nNo blood can be shed here, because it is under protection.\nIt is the hub to all of Vailor, like stated before, because of all of its carriages and boats that lead to the major cities of Vailor. It is a safe place for many and has its own shops and auction areas. \n\nChapter 2: Haelun'or\nThe large city of Haelun'or, home to the High Elves. A place with winding paths, towering buildings, and many many people. It is where most 'pure' high elven people live, but many high elves live elsewhere. The main area is home to a Tavern, a bank, and\nan auctioneer. \n\nAt the time of writing this, their stalls are empty or coming soon. I have yet to go into the walls of the main area of the city. \n\nThe houses in the outside area of the cities follow a road and wind up a hill, these houses at the\ntop of the hill have nicer doors, and nicer stone. The stone they use is a nice smooth one. Since I do not have sight I can not tell for certain what it is. \n\nChapter 3: Felsen\n\nPerhaps it self a mini-hub, felsen is home to the humans.\nIn it's center, after stepping of the cart. Are many other carts and a rough, coarse dirt ground, mixed in with gravel and some cobblestone. The Orenian people inhabit this land, they are the main leaders of humans, because no other human civilization\nis as strong in manpower as the Orenian one. Its rough path leads across a moat, if you could call it that, not of water but of trash. Once you cross this bridge you find yourself in an even rougher, winding, huge city. You would not be surprised to know\nthat most of the population is part of the army. So the people here are rough and though. Stalls surround the area along with some buildings. The place seems barren and void of any substance. It is a wonder to me why people live here. To each their own.\nBut on that note, it may have to do with loyalty. \n\n\nRacism in Oren:\n\nIt seems some of the people who live in felsen or the areas surronding it have a racial or cultural hate towards a clan of hillmen named the Raevirs. I can not tell\nwhy they hate these people, but it has something to do with them thinking they are tribal.\n\nChapter 4: The Caliphate\n\nSandy, desolate, uninteresting. This describes the caliphate. It is under construction, it seems\nbut the construction seems to have no workers. But the druid grove near it has people in it, and a nice, but small library. More on librarys next chapter.\n\nChapter 5: The Dragur Library\n\nThe Library of Dragur is one of the biggest\nlibrarys in all of vailor, to my knowledge at least. It is filled with books from magic, to fiction, and even history. It is a place where you can learn. But it is a public place so do not expect it to be too quiet. But do not expect it to be too exciting\nIt is a place I like to visit. It's 'scholars' are nice and the books are of good quality.\n\nThe library is the biggest in Vailor.\n\nChapter 6: Sutica\n\nSutica is a merchant island that sits upon the sea, to be more precise upon 10+ tiny\nislands. You can taste the salt on the air and the smell the scent of mushrooms on the west wind. It is a place where you can buy many things, for example: I recently bought myself a twisted staff. The island runs on trade and economy like this. It is a\nfairly peaceful area.\nSutica houses very valuable resources in a vault, inside the bank. About 100 people live in the city of Sutica. The city is said to have been around for a long time now, it had started in \"The Now Lost Salvus.\"\nSources: \nShynys\nItesti.\n\nChapter 7: The Iron Uzg.\n\nThe Iron Uzg is the place where the orcs reside. When you come upon the Iron Uzg's Capital, heading up the road will grant you access to a, what I assume is large from the echo, building.\nTo get to the Iron Uzg, you must either walk here, or take the boat from the dock. The front path is rough and rocky, but it is not hard to walk past it. When you come to the main path there are some smaller buildings leading up to the large one.\nThe Orcs are known for being quite rude to visitors, so I must go! \n\nChapter 8: Kal'Ishtul\n\nKal'Ishtul, home of the dwarves. The dwarven city of Kal'Ishtul is located quite deep in a cavern. If you would like to get into the city you through a short\ntunnel, then come out of it, and then you cross a bridge. It seems to currently be in construction but once it's done, it seems it will be huge.\n\nOn the dwarves, they are not seen around the capital nearly as much as you would think! Strange.\nChapter 9: Willow Hollow.\n\nWillow Hollow,\nHome of the halfings,\nThe smell of flowers and tea lingers in the air for all to smell. The grass is soft and fertile. Willow Hollow is as small as the halflings, and it does not seem to have such a high\npopulation. But I could be wrong, the halflings do usually share a home with their family, it would be selflish in their culture if they did not. The towns citizens are said to be nice. It is like any other town, but seems peaceful and smells wonderful, I\nwould wager that if I could see, it would be much better! It is a farming town, as the halflings are a farming people. For more on halflings I reccomend the history of halflings in the Monk Literature section.\n\nChapter 10: The Rebelious State of\nthe Dreadlands. \n\nA place where it is said, they have no laws and complete freedom. Currently in a war against the orenians, so by the time I am done writing this chapter they may already be gone... It is a dangerous place here, and an ugly looking\none too. I think it is a bad idea to have no rules at all, it makes it dangerous and attracts the wrong kind of people. They have rough orenian style buildings, which parallels their former liege. There is only one well in the city, and barely any other\nwater... Or anything for that matter. This place is strange, and dangerous.\n\n\nThe End.\n\nWritten By Fygmor Khen'aum.\nSources: Armin Ambertoe, Shynys Itesti, and others.", ""),
        StaticBook("Vengance", "Erik Stalfen", "A sorrowed day had struck, with the loss of a great friend. \n\n   I had been going to the Caliphate to check up on Iron prices hoping they would have dropped. To my dismay the prices lie the same, but as I began to leave the city a man stopped me.\nThis man had a corrupted look that lie upon his face. At first he began to question my ears and then my beard. He quickly realized I was a Half Elf and begin taunting and insulting me. Luckily for myself he could not come up with anything that I hadn't\nheard before. \n\n   As I was besting him in our game of wordplay some random Dwarf began to watch. This Dwarf had something strange about him, but what, I did not know. Then thankfully a true friend of mine was on her way to the library but saw my troubles\nand stopped to help. \n\n   To our dismay another man came and he was with the one who dared insult me. The two quickly stopped insulting myself for I had bested them in wordplay. Unfortunatly, they begun to insult Sahar and the Crimson Edict.\nWe were not going to stand by idly while they argued this but then the Dwarf joined in on bashing the Edict.. \n\n   I had turned to speak to the Dwarf and then the first man who was named Otto, attempted to stick a dagger in my stomach and in my armpit.\nThe dagger to the gut did nothing thanks to the iron armor I wear under my uniform, but the dagger to the armpit infiltrated the armor.. I began to bleed and it only got worse as he continued to turn the dagger.. At this same time I saw the other man,\nwho was named Bryson begin to attack Sahar.\n\n   As the battle had begun the Dwarf had made it clear what side he was on.. it was not ours.. So he began to create some sort of bright light that had blinded me and Sahar..\n\n   I could not see Sahar at this\npoint or anyone for that matter, but I knew Otto was right in front of me so I tacled him to the ground. I had began punching his face in with my spike gaunlets, but then, the Dwarf walked over..\n\n   This Dwarf put his staff close to my Gorget, I could\nfeel the heat arcing off his staff and then he told me to roll off of the man..\n\n   I did as he said or else I would have been electrocuted without a second thought. He told me to go into the tunnel that led to the residential district, and for Otto to\nhelp his friend that was attacking Sahar.. \n\n   We get into the tunnel and he told me to let him see my wound.. I began o tear the uniform where I had been stabbed and then he pulls out a potion and poors it on there healing my wound instantly..\nI asked him why he was saving me and he said due to a comment I made about the Coalition having their head.. He was part of the Dwarven Council of three.\n\n   I found out his name was Gauldrim and that he had high ties to Oren which is why he refused to\nhelp Sahar. He only allowed me to live since I made the comment about teh Coalition, he said he does not want conflict with them.. After we spoke Gauldrim nearly vanished and I was able to escape.. Sahar was not..\n\n   I vow vengance on Otto and Bryson for\nthat day. When I get a hold of them, they will never see the bright of light again.. Their bodies will be mangled.. Blood will poor out of them but I will cauderize the wounds so they live and feel more pain.. Those poor bastards will know what true\ntorture feels like.", ""),
        StaticBook("Very Unhappy", "Atturi Idate", "Unhappy\n       By Atturi Idate\n\n     Once there was a little boy by the name of george, this boy was quite the giddy fellow. He jumped and skipped throughout the streets until one day landing infront of a pair of black boots, Embrazened by-\ngold outlines. one came up instantly to kick poor George right in the crotch. He fell right down with no proper quarrel nor snide remarks. He lay there as tears stripen his face, being dragged away to some faraway place to be put to work...", ""),
        StaticBook("Vine A&P", "Daniel 'Vince' Ward", "This book has been written for the sake of study on the anatomy of your basic vine plant.\n   The various parts of the vine are the roots, the trunk, the head, the cordons, the buds, which grow into shoots, then finally into canes.\n*Upon flipping to this page, you find a very detailed sketching of the root system of a vine plant. Various branching out parts sprawl out in all directions.*\nThe Roots:\n   The roots exist for the purpose of extracting nutrients from the surrounding ground, including water.\n   This provides nourishment for the vines while they grow in their peculiar spinning fashion.\n*The next page shows a detailed image of what appears to be a trunk-like stem of a vine plant. Two pictures are placed next to each other, showing the insides and out. It also shows at the very top, something labeled as the 'head'.*\nThe Trunk and Head:\n   The trunk of the vine plant exists for the purpose of providing structure for the rest of the plant above ground as it grows outwards and finds extra support via the cordons. It also provides a method of transportation for the-\n-nutrients from the roots. \n   At the top of the plant exists something called the 'Head', which is the section of the plant which transitions into the cordons.\n*Finally, you find yourself on a page that shows a detailed sketching of what appears to be two horizontal branches that take off from the head of the trunk. From them several more branches appear to come out. They're labeled as cordons.*\nCordons:\n   The cordons are two branches that come off from the head of the trunk and begin to form buds. These buds will then grow into shoots, which are like extra little branches coming off the cordons. These shoots will have various leaves on them.\nAfter a certain amount of time, these shoots turn into canes, which are more wood-like in texture and tougher than their more pliable earlier form.\n   The cordons spin in a circular motion as the plant grows taller, wrapping around the nearest support.\nThe shoots wrap around this support as well as the plant continues to grow.\nSide Notes:\n-Grape vines are a specific species of this plant.\n-Many vines were killed in the making of this book. Sorry about that.\nList of Parts:\n-Roots\n-Trunk and Head\n-Cordons (Including buds/shoots/canes)\n*The final pages of this book appear to show all the previous sketches in miniature form. They show just as much incredible detail as they exist near each other for reference and comparison.*", ""),
        StaticBook("Void Connection", "Laureh'thill", "Notes on: Connecting to The Void\n\nWritten by: Laureh'thill\n\n-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-Chapter One: Basic Concepts\n\nThe Void is, as was explained to myself by Archmage Resia of the Mage's Guild, both everything and nothing. To properly learn arcane\nmagic, one must connect their mind to the void through demanding practice and study. This is usually done through what is formally referred to as \"meditation\" by Mages.\n\n\"Meditation\" while similar the non-Mage's understanding of the word, takes on a\ncompletely different meaning when mentioned in this regard. What Mages experience while meditating transcends what any non-Mage could achieve in the act without trying extensively to procure the same results. \n\nMeditation commonly has a focus of sorts,\nsomething for the subject to think exclusively about (or rather not think exclusively about) to focus their mind onto the subject matter that they are trying to understand or comprehend.\n\nSince the act of thinking about everything at once has so far been\nproven impossible by even the most accomplished scholars and intellectuals to date, Mages have taken to focusing on nothing at all to complete the ritual of voidal connection.\n\nChapter Two: The Process\n\nPurging a mind of all thought armed only with the\nwill of itself is no easy task. This fact is what deters most potential Mages, and the reason their kind is so spread out among the population.\n\nIt is, however, achieveable. With a mastery of will, one can train themselves to connect to the void easily\nand without even the slightest visible physical strain.\n\nThis does not mean, however, that the process of \"meditation\" becomes any easier. The mind is simply a muscle and, like any physical muscle, grows strong to compensate for stress placed upon it.\nA common misconception is that you should light candles or incense while going through the process of \"meditation\". These external distractions, however, prevent you from achieving the level of focus nessicary to clear your mind completely. Ignore those\nwho mention chimes and bells, those who urge you to listen to the sounds of the harp or flute; they are unknowing agents of your undoing.\n\nFind a small, quiet, room temperature area to rest and relax. Slowly begin to rid yourself of your five senses as in\nusual meditation. Sight, hearing, taste, smell, and feeling. And then turn your focus to your thoughts.\n\nThe most common mistake you can make is actively thinking about clearing your mind of thought. This paradox confuses you more than helps, and should\nbe avoided at any cost.\n\nEventually, you will find peace in your thoughts and senses; slowly coming to a state of complete rest (much like just before you fall asleep).\n\nYou will see nothingness, hear nothingness, taste nothingness, smell nothingness, and\nfeel nothingness. It is at this point that you reach your mind out, transposing your conciousness into this void of emptiness.\n\n-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-", ""),
        StaticBook("Void Fire Notes", "Daniel 'Vince' Ward", "*The following notebook seems to have various poorly drawn sketches and seems to be half-hazardly strewn together. The writing, however, seems to be quite legible and some what fanciful.*\nOn the Void:\n     I will note a few things about what I've learned about the void's characteristics and how people interact with them.\n    Firstly, the void seems to be an endless expanse overlapping our material plain. It is here, and yet it is\nnot. It is the plain of thought, emotion, energy, and more importantly, potential.\n     In a process I do not yet fully understand, the magi of our plain are capable of using a form of energy known as 'mana' to utilize the endless potential of the void.\nFirst off, the magi seems to be required in becoming accustomed to the way of the void. They need to meditate regularly and condition their minds into entering a state of nothingness. In this state of nothingness, given that a magi is studied enough\nin their craft, they should be able to expend mana in order to cast a spell.\n     As far as I have read, a spell must be thought of first within the void in its entirety, such as how a certain element will move once it is evoked before it is cast.\nThe magi is required to have ultimate focus on just this thought within the void, or the spell will fail. This means no form of emotion or cloudy thinking, and especially no overshadowing senses to break concentration, such as pain.\nIt should be obvious, but an evocationist is completely unable to bend existing natural elements. Only the thing that they evoked. Next to that, an evocationist must see where they are casting in order for a spell to take effect.\nLastly, an element is usually only evoked in its most raw form. This means liquid water, solid earth, etcetera.\nOn Natural Fire:\n     In order to perfect the evocation of a certain element through the void, one must have ultimate understanding of what they are trying to bring forth. With my understanding, I believe this to be because you cannot force something\ninto existence if it cannot obey the laws of our material plain. Of course, to a certain extent, as floating water isn't extremely natural either.\n     The most common way that I believe people learn about their elements is in studying how their\nelement exists to each of the five senses. They then continue the study by figuring out how the element reacts to each of the other elements.\n\nTouch.     Air.\nHearing.   Water.\nEyesight.  Earth.\nSmell.\nTaste.\nTouch:\n     Touching fire is not something you really want to do. The sensation of its heat slowly builds as you come closer to the source. If you are to have your own flesh in direct contact with a flame, it is likely to begin peeling and burning\nwith an intense stinging sensation. It is an excruciating pain and most people are smart enough to avoid this if it isn't necessary. \n     Without being in direct contact, a flame can give a warm and comfortable feeling.\nHearing:\n     A flame is usually giving off a type of sound that one would describe as 'cracking'. When it is moving and roaring, a flame will also have a 'flapping' sound.\nSight:\n     A fire can most usually be seen as a mixture of red, orange, yellow, and sometimes blue. The color combination goes inward from red, orange, yellow, then blue. The source of it being the most heat-intensive.\n     Above the flame\nthere is usually a cloud of smoke billowing upwards. The smoke either fades into the air or sticks around. When it does stick around, smoke can easily cause choking sensations and create ideal situations for suffocation.\n     The fire itself\nusually flickers and flies upwards in chaotic waves.\nSmell:\n     The fire itself does not usually have its own defined smell. Instead, it is usually the smoke that gives off a particular scent. If you have ever sat around a campfire with the smoke billowing, you may understand what this smell is like.\nTaste:\n     I felt that this should be covered, however irrelevant it might seem. The taste of a flame is non-existent. Putting a flame into your mouth is the same as touching it and is not advised. If you DO try this and DO taste something, it is usually\nthe fuel, and not the actual flame.\nAir:\n     Fire responds to air in a couple of distinct ways. Firstly, a flame can be manipulated and moved around by a particular breeze. It will sway and move based on the movements of the air around it, if the gust is noticable. If the flame is\nsmall enough in contrast to the power of the gust, the air is likely to completely put the flame out. \n     Secondly, a flame seems to be some what dependant on a constant supply of air. I have tested this by putting a small flame under a cup, and sure\nenough, the flame is ultimately snuffed out without its constant supply of air.\nWater:\n     The way that a flame will react to water varies based usually on amounts. If a flame is roaring and makes contact with a smaller source of water, it is likely the water will quickly evaporate into steam. The flame may be weakened in this\nsituation, based on the proportions of flame and water.\n     If there is a particularly larger amount of water compared to flame, the flame is almost certainly going to be extinguished and left with a dampened ground. Water may partially evaporate.\nEarth:\n     I believe that earth and fire have the least of interesting interactions of all the elements. A flame may be snuffed out if it is buried in earth, as it would cut out the constant need for air. Earth may be scorched and left a bit crisp if it\nmakes contact with a flame. Also seen within volcanoes and magma pits, an intense amount of heat is capable of causing earth to melt into a liquid state. This is normally not possible.\nOn Fire Evocation:\n     Fire evocation isn't entirely the same thing as natural fire, as it is something brought forth from the void in the form of mana. If it is not supplied with appropriate mana, it will fade from the material plain.\nI am not certain, but I believe the heat from evoked fire is able to create the circumstances for natural flame, which would prolong the effects of fire even after the evocation has faded. Of course, this natural fire is uncontrollable to the\nevocationist.\n     Cryomancy, the evocation of ice, which extends from hydromancy, can only be countered by the evocation of a magical flame. This is important to note if these two arcane elements ever come into contact.\n     An evoked flame is,\njust like hydromancy, capable of bringing forth a floating example of its element. This means you are capable of evoking floating flames and manipulate them, which normally isn't the case with natural fire.\n     Then, as previously discussed from the\n'On the Void' section of these notes, fire evocation is subject to all the same rules of evocation previously mentioned.\n\n\n-Current notes and understanding of Fire Evocation by Daniel 'Vince' Ward, 1512.", ""),
        StaticBook("Void Translo", "Sab'Aoth", "=--=--=--=--=--=--=                 Void\n         Translocation\n=--=--=--=--=--=--=            \n   \n        -<= (O)\nVoid translocation is the art of bringing and placing objects into and out of the Void. Mana is what fuels our world and is in all forms of objects and beings. Void translocationists use said mana to perform their art. By copying the mana that an object\nor person has exactly they are able to place and remove objects into and out of the Void. One must copy the pattern and amount of mana that is inside the object or being before they may perform said art. As per such they simply need to create a connection\nto the Void and form a portal of sorts to place and take objects into and out of the Void. However people are unable to be transported to to the Void due to the complex and large amounts of mana they have. The same is said for other large objects, As well\none must be careful when performing said art because if an object is placed into the void for too long, their bodies and very being itself would be torn asunder and transformed into mana itself. This process would take about one to two elven weeks to\nreach.\n\n\n[An astonishingly complex drawing of a portal of sorts is detailed along the page.]", ""),
        StaticBook("Void Transloca.", "Evelangéline", "About Void Translocation:\n\n- Void Translocation, the voidal art of storing objects in the void, is one of the provailing magical arts in the means of utility.\n\n- A Void Translocationist can place and retrive objects-\nthat they have placed in the void anytime and anywhere they desire. \n\n- Although it may seem fairly easy for the eye, it requires intensive studying and observing to be actually translocating even the smallest of objects.\nBasic Understanding:\n\n- First of all, although it may seem that the object is being transferred inside the void in one piece, it isn't. The object breaks down into tiny pieces, moving freely inside the void. To call for the object, a void transloca-\n-tionist has to remember the physical properties of the object.\n\n- The magic happens in a rather interesting way. First, a lavander speck of light appears at the desired location of the void translocationist, growing larger-\nto fit the size of the object. Then, the void translocationit simply pushes the object inside the lavander circle of light, the light swelling in side before dissapearing again. To recall an object, the translocationist does the same with the lavander-\ncircle of lightly. Only this time, the object starts to form at the enterence of the light, before simply dropping down. One can say this could be used to drop heavy objects on others' head. One would be correct. However, it'd be a lot slower and mana-\nwasting way of attacking if not mastered than the other evocation arts, which is the main reason this magic is used for utility, not offense.\nConditions for Translocation:\n\n- The translocationist must be knowledged of the object, having it in clear vision. One cannot translocate an object behind walls. To further explain, a translocationist needs to know the physical properties-\nof the object before even attempting to send the object to the void. During my studies, I have carried a notebook with me to note the properties of the objects I wanted to translocate. Like my doll, or the anvil I keep with me for any emergency smithing!\nFirst, make a list of the physical properties of the object.\n\nWhat does it look like?\nIs it soft/rough?\nWhat colour is it?\nIs it cold/hot to touch?\nIs its surface smooth or rough?\nHow does it react to-\nfire and pressure?\n\n- These are only examples, there may be more if the translocationist feels there is more properties to cover. For example, I'll be covering a simple silver knife's properties on the next page;\nSilver Knife,\n\n- Made of silver, is durable\n-Cold to touch\n-Reflects light\n-A small 'Eva' is carved into the handle part,\n- Shaped like a long rectangle but with a sharp edge,\n-Used for cooking-\npurposes,\n-Durable, doesn't bend with pressure;\n-When reacted with fire, keeps shape when reacted with the average heat of the fire.\nMore Things to Know:\n\n- As in all magical arts, one must not use more than their existing energy. Failure to do so will result in fatigue and maybe even death.\n\n- As the objects gets more complex, the mana required-\nto translocate it increases as well. Because of their extreme complex design, living beings cannot be translocated in any instance.\n\n- For being fairly complex and lacking solidity, liquids also cannot be trans-\n-located.\n\n- As the mass of the object increases, so does the mana required to translocate it.\n\n- For any questions, do not hesitate to contact me, Evelangéline for further information.", ""),
        StaticBook("Void-Origins V1", "Scholar of The Void", "Theory of Void-Based Origination - Volume I, Origination and Supporting Facts.\n\nGiven the nature of the following text, the author requestions anonymity.\nTable of Contents\n\nI - Statement of Theory, Page 3.\nII - Fallacies of the Creator, page 8.\nIII - Void Nodes, Page 15\nIV - Cast vs. Nature, Page 20\nV - Conclusion, page 25.\nI\nStatement of Theory and Support.\n\nPresented and supported in this text, and in following volumes, is a scientific theory as to the void's role in the creation of the world as we know it. An act commonly attributed to the\nomnipotent deity called \"The Creator\", it is the purpose of this text to provide evidence to an alternative means with fewer conflicts with the facts of reality.\n\nSupport for the theory, which will follow, can be initially comprised of three main points\nwhich will be detailed in their respective chapers. \n\nThe theory, as well as these three points, are as follows.\n\nThe Void is the origin of both our world and lives, brought forth by a means akin to a mage's spell.\nSupporting this:\n\nThe logical fallacies alongside a sentient 'creator'.\n\nThe recorded, known, effects of the Void on our world.\n\nThe nature of void-summoned elements and life, and\ntheir being capable of perfectly mimicking what is natural.\nII\nFallacies of the Creator.\n\nBy many names, a single, all-powerul being is the backbone of many modern idealisms. The idea that a sentient being existed before existence itself is popular.\nIt also, on a fundemental level, fails to make logical sense.\n\nThe core belief of these religions are that this creator was the one responsible for the world, the void, and all other planes of existence.\nThe flaw in this is the idea that sentience can exist without an environment for it to be housed in. \n\nThe abilities to see, hear, or feel are all useless when nothing exists to be seen, heard, or felt. And since these senses are the\nbases of thought, it can be inferred by extension that without these environmental stimuli, sentience cannot exist.\n\nWhile we can never know with complete certainty that this god does not transcend logic, the best solution is the most\nsimple. The Void, being both perpetual and infintesimal, has existed since before existence itself. It has been the constant throughout both reality and whatever lies outside.\n\nIt requires much less exceptions to accepted logic to assume\nthat the void is what created reality than a being did. When one sees a mountain, they do not assume the mountain was made by hand - merely that it has always been there.\n\nThe way by which the void has brought to life our world shall be\nexplained in the following pages.\nIII\nVoid Nodes.\n\nWhile in the vast majority of cases, the void cannot permanently change our world (For example, a mage's spell disappears when he releases it), there are recorded instances of such a thing happening.\nOne such instance was discovered in Anthos, where a void node under a southern Mage's hall had permanently altered a cavern with it's energy.\n\nHumming with energy, and the stone itself changed into all manner of material, the detonated void node\nbrought proof of two things - That the void can permanently sustain a physical change, and that it can enact itself on pre-existing material.\n\nThe first of these two facts is of most importance. The second will be addressed in a later volume.\nThe fact that the void can permanently alter the world is the basis of this theory.\n\nWhile what was created was random and without logic or pattern, it did create. In the node's aftermath existed material where none had existed before.\nIt did not simply change things, but created as well. The net sum of what was found after the node's change was greater than what had been there before.\nIV\nCast vs. Nature\n\nTwo types of magic interract with the void in the way closest to this theory's concluded way by which reality began. These are evocation and conjuration.\nEvocation, for the unaware, is the summoning of a chosen element from the void for temporary use within our realm. Usually weaponized, known forms are as follows: Air, Water, Fire, Earth, and Electrical.\n\nConjuration is similar, in some ways, but\ninstead of a simple object a conjurationist summons forth a living being. \n\nThe key point with both of these magics is that, in a skill mage's hands, the summoned element or creature is indistinguishable from a 'natural' piece, found in our world.\nIf a Earth Evocationist wished to form a stone exactly like one she had found, and she were skilled enough to do so, the summoned stone would be entirely identicle to the original. The same applies to all evocations as well as conjuration.\nThese facts in mind, in tandem with the void nodes' nature, provide evidence that our world could have been created as a result of the void, in a manner akin to evocation with a single key difference being that this is permanent.\nV\nConclusion\n\nFollowing volumes will include parts of the theory which answer possible conflicts with parts of the world. Volume II will cover the presence and divinty of aenguls and daemons.", ""),
        StaticBook("Vol 2 : Adunai", "Arnorian", "In the year 333 a massive populist rebellion led by Owyn Ulgaard, a common Highlander and the son of a miner succeeded in removing the Adunians from power. Starting in 332, one by one the Kingdom's settlements fell to the Rebels, their Adunian Earls\neither being put to the sword, or forced to flee to the Capital. By the following year only Idunia remained under King Arendil's control. Though Idunia did not stand for much longer, as it was the ancestors of the Highlanders who had built the cities\nwalls, and so it's secrets had been passed down to them. Rebels were able to penetrate the fortifications and open the gates for the Highlander hordes to rush in and sack the city. Owyn confronted Arendil, and cut him down in single combat ending the\nthree century long rule of Adunians over Fai O'Thuaid. \n\nDuring the sack of Idunia, rebels pulled Harren's remains from his mausoleum and cast them off the cliffs into the sea, never to be recovered. With Highlander victory\nsecured, Owyn Ulgaard had the remaining Adunians rounded up, and placed on ships at a small unnamed fishing town two days south walk of Idunia. Here, the newly crowned King proclaimed Adunians banished from Aegis as retribution for their unjust\nenslavement of the Highlander people, vowing to annihilate them completely should they ever return. Elendil, Son of Arendil and last of Harren's line took command of the Adunian fleet and lead them northeast across the sea, following in the wake of their\nMali'Dun ancestors in the hope of finding them. \n\nInstead, the Adunians found Al'Ildic, a large isle almost four months voyage across the ocean. Landing on cape Gelbor, the Adunian people settled the Island starting with the Western coast. Elendil\nproclaimed the settled lands 'The Adunian Realm of Al'Ildic' and himself as the 'Lord of the Adunian Realm and People., establishing a new capital at the foot of Maria's seven white marble peaks, a set of towering mountains named after his wife that stood\nat the centre of the Great Forest of Ar'Norian. The town was called Ildon, and grew to become an impressive city in the time that followed.\n\nOver the centuries, the Adunians began to spread east across the island, establishing new independant\nprovinces and realms. In the year 626, the Second Unification War drew to a close with Al'Ildic triumphing over the independant Realm of Eldion. From 334 to 1309, Adunian culture grew and developed on their isolated contintent, traditions evolving, clan's\nrising and falling, wars being lost and won. In the end, the Realm's end came and went in a day, taken by the undead scourge like the rest of Aegis.\n\nSince then, the Adunian people have failed to recover to a position anywhere near as grand, their\nhighest points being in the Duchy of Westfall under Godfrey Horen's H.O.E, and again as the Kingdom of Adunia in Kaedrin. In Modern times, Adunians are spread few and far between, wandering the world as Rangers, or integrating with various other societies\nand organizations. Through it all, most Adunians remain true to their ancestry and traditions, it's importance having been drilled into them over the generations since Al'Ildic.", ""),
        StaticBook("Vol. 1 : Adunai", "Arnorian", "~*~ Introduction ~*~\n\nFive years after the world was supposedly built by an almighty 'Creator', Horen, the first King of men was given a son by his wife Julia, whom they named Harren. The child grew up in a time of conflict, and before long was commanding\nsoldiers in his father's army. Through all this time however, Harren never felt entitled to his father's holdings and believed that he should instead forge his own kingdom to leave a legacy worthy of his Father. At thirty years of age, Harren was granted\nhis Father's permission to take his men and go north beyond the borders of his lands. The men trekked into the vast unknown wilderness for many weeks, eventually comming across a towering mountain range on the northeast coast of the Aegisean continent,\nthe Thuaid mountains. Here Harren decided he would build his kingdom, and set about conqeuring the Highland tribes that dwelled there. Little did he know that another tribe was already at work attempting to dominate the region; a tribe of elves who called\nthemselves the Mali'Dun, lead by a woman named Sarai. They were tall and elegant, had pale skin like snow, hair black as night, and hollow grey eyes, arming themselves with mainly bows and spears.\n\nAs they moved through the mountains from the North, the\nMali'Dun conquered the Highland tribes that lived there, enslaving them and forcing them to fight in their army. Eventually the Harrenites and the Mali'Dun clashed in the battle of Grumder Hill, with the frormer being vastly outnumbered. Harren was forced\nto retreat and avoid further confrontation with the elves for a time, focusing his efforts on the Highlander tribes once again. The Harrenites changed their tactics after the battle to mimic those of their enemy, forcing the survivors from each conqoured\ntribe to join their force, practically enslaving them as a lower class of soldier. The playing field was leveled, and the two armies clashed a numer of times in the following seven years during their individual conquest of the mountains. Sarai comming\nfrom the North, and Harren from the South. In the year 42 (Adunian Calendar) each side took heavy casualties in the second battle of Hrak's field, prompting the leaders of each army to meet and negotiate a conclusion to the war for the Thuaid Mountains.\nThe result of the meeting was a political marriage between Harren and Sarai, and the unification of their armies. Before long, Sarai was pregnant with Harren's child, and the son of Horen issued a command to his people; each Harrenite must marry a\nMali'dun, and that their children would be the first of a new, superior race. The Highlanders remained a lower class throughout this and were forbidden to lie with either of their overlord races. With the end of the 42nd year, Harren proclaimed the\nKingdom of Fai O'Thuaid, encompassing the entire mountain range with himself as King and his wife as Queen. What was formerly two armies became a single society and settled on the cliffs above the northeastern coast, naming their township 'Idunia'.\nIn the year 43 Harren's son 'Baren' was born, the first 'Adunian' with many more like him following. Gradually more settlements spring up throughout the kingdom, and in the year 104 Baren succeeded his father. Six years later, the remaining Mali'Dun\ndeparted for Mainland Aegis, the last of their human partners having died from one cause or another, leaving the Kingdom in the hands of their descendants, the new Half-breed race, Adunians. For three centuries the kingdom of Fai O'Thuaid\nflourished in the northern isolation, the Adunians living as an upper clase of noble Earls among the vast amount of Highlanders beneath them.", ""),
        StaticBook("Vol. 1 : Gy'Waka", "Arnorian", "Introduction\n\nA Gy'Waka bird is a large 'bird like' creature that the Kharajyr came to discover in Karakatua. Their uses are many, transportation, food, battle, and much more. To be removed from it's enviroment causes the bird rather severe depression.\nHabitat and Lifestyle.\n\nThe Gy'Waka are native to the island of Karakatua, they are very rare and found nowhere else in the world apart from there. They are carnivores and will consume almost anything that vaugely resembles meat, this includes humanoids\ntoo. The Gy'Waka birds will never hunt the more dangerous humanoid creatures unless they are so desperate that there is simply no other choice, however they will happily kill and eat children that wouldn't usually pose a threat, on a daily basis. Their\nlong, sharkp break is perfect for pecking large chunks of flesh off a creature's bones, and their large springy legs mean they can cover shocking distances in a very short time, running faster than a horse. They do not however, have the most sensitive\ntongue, leaving them quite happy to eat anything they can.\n\nThey are not stupid creatures, in fact they are very smart indeed. The Gy'waka are able to mentally adapt to problems that may occur. For example, if they hear of a race that commonly hunts\ntheir kind in the distance, they will have remembered this, and flee. This also means that the Gy'waka has learnt to tolerate the Kharajyr as they began to see them as a source of food and protection. Although they are feathered, their layer of feathers\nis very thin due to their adaptation to the warm jungle enviroment. The reason the Gy'Waka birds settled on the island years ago, was due to the fact it was a haven for them - There they found plenty of prey and no predators. This has resulted in a\nsomewhat cocky personality in the Gy'waka - they will never look up, as they are the highest of all predators. This means the Kha' are able to jump down onto them with moderate ease.\n\nFortunately, the Gy'waka bird's exterior senses are\nvery strong. They have fair hearing, and would most likely notice you approach or move about them unless very stealthily. Their sense of smell however, is nearly completely defunct and useless. To make up for this, they have extremely keen sight. Their\nsight strength is undoubtably more powerful then that of a Kharajyr's which allows them to pinpoint their preys location fairly well. Gy'Waka do however, have two blind spots.\n\nDo not be fooled by the Gy'waka bird's wings. It is in fact, a flightless\nbird. It has long since evolved past the need for it's wrings as everything it ever needed was found on the ground of the island where they lived. Using their wings proved to be a waste of energy, so as they went on, they were no longer able to use them,\nyet they still carry smaller, vestigal wings useful for jumping.\n\nUnity with the Kha'.\n\nUpon arriving at the new Island, it didn't take long for the Kharajyr to discover the marvelous beasts that they named the Gy'waka, which loosely translates into\n'wings of our Goddess'. The Kha' believe that finding these creatures was a blessing from Metzli herself, and that they should respect her gift and make good use of it. As they first approached the Gy'waka birds, they did so with caution. The Gy'waka fled\naway from the strange creatures, hiding themselves in the furthest corner of the island as they began to work out what to do.\n\nThe Kha' approached them with offerings of both fruit and meat, laying them down in the jungle floor and then\nretreating to observe them from a distance. Much to their surprise, the birds consumed every last shred of meat, yet didn't touch the fruit at all. After a few more of these visits, the Gy'waka began to accept the Kha' as friends, now understanding them\nto be no source of trobule. They spread back out over the island and continued their day to day lives amongst the Kha', often filling their stomaches on meaty treats left by the Kha'.\n\nIt then didn't take long for the Gy'waka to allow the Kha'\nto approach and touch them, creating a new bond of companionship. They didn't seem to mind the Kha' strapping saddles and ropes to them provided they were offered a large amount of food in exchange. And so with that, the Kha were able to make use of\nthese giant birds that they had been so lucky to stumble upon.\n\nWritten by Charles Napier, Dean of Naturalism and Kharajyr Studies.", ""),
        StaticBook("Vol. 1 : Origins", "Arnorian", "~*~ Origins ~*~\n\nDeities and Recognized Deities.\n\nIn the Old North faith, there are three circles are the domains of the Gods, and in each were both lesser, and demi gods. The three circles in order of least to greatest powerful\nare Nature, Life, and Astral. Within each rests one dominant God.\n\nWithin nature there is Gronn the Life Bringer, who is believed to be the aspects of the Druids in one form in the Old Faith's eyes. This means the old faith acknowledges the\nDruids as true and rightful worshippers of nature in their own religion. Gronn is said to be twelve feet tall with bark for skin and rocks for teeth. His eyes are two pools of water and his digits are branches. He has a full beard and head of leaves and\nvines which twist and turn wildly. It's said he is often seen, but never caught and has the ability to take on anything of living origin. This can be taken in two ways, as some hunters thank him for his sacrifices to let them eat, while others commune\nmore with animals, and forsake meat eating.\nHe is seen as a very benevolent God. His charge is to watch over all the planets, fish, animals and happenings of nature. He is what ties Astral to life. he is also credited for the creation of the elements as\ngifts to Morighaen.\n\n\nWithin Man there is Thronn the Impartial, who is believed to be the collection of all humanity's desires, virtues, morals and thoughts. He is a grey character, with no appearance albiet that of a silhouette. People who\npray to Thronn traditionally wait until the sun is half up or down, and wait in an open field. They turn to their own shadow and take to kneeling, nod their head and fold their hands. All the workings of men are held into the account of Thronn, which is\nwhy people depending on their lives will pray to Thronn. The people who are poorer, downtrodden and feel betrayed by life and tormented usually shun Thronn. The opposite crowd most often give thanks to him. Thronn is looked on positively by Mages and\nFathers for the gift of Heartfire to man.\n\nWithin the Astral planes beyond us, sits Morighaen, the Night Woman; a shoft looking lady covered in a veil of stars. She is larger than any planet or sun, and we are smaller then dust in her celestial eyes.\nShe is to large to see us, but she knows we are there - and she may assume a mortal form, or smaller size to medal with the cosmos as it pleases her. She is seen as sadistic by some due to her boredom. She was left alone, and to pass the time she often\nthrows planets, or galaxies together to watch the effects. She is easily the most fearsome of the Gods, and any mortal who claims to be Morighaen is usually killed out of fear, for if she has come down, the end is already upon us, and if she is lying, she\nmay anger Morighaen. People pray to her to appease her, and she is often seen as a Pillar of Strength for the female half of Northern society. She is the cause for moonblood and weather, time, space, and even magic. Her relationship to Thronn and Gronn is\nstrained by both the distance and nature of the figures.", ""),
        StaticBook("Vol. 1 : Tongues", "Arnorian", "~*~ Languages ~*~\n\nDead Languages.\n\nOld Northeron: After the Highlanders migrated to the North of Aegis, they remained as one united people. They still spoke in Flexio then, but having to survive the harsh winters of Northern Aegis\ngave them little time for religious reflection, and as pagan traditions began to evolve, Flexio became less practiced as a language. It was still spoken, but began to warp from it's original form. This langguage's evolution was sped up by the Mountain\nDwarf clans fleeing from the Empire of Khorvad to the south. As the Grandaxe clan began to trade with the tribal men of the North, their tongue's became merged, creating a gutteral language known as Old Northeron. This language eventually died out with\nthe splitting of the Raevir people and the Hanseatic Tribes.\n\nClassical Raev:\n\nAfter the Raevir folk split off from their Hanseatic brethern into the East Highlander grouping of indigenous people, their tougne began to warp from\nisolation. Few in the south ever ventured to the frozen tundra of the Northlands and the Hanseatic tribes only conducted raids over the mountains every so often, and fought other Hanseatic tribes more than they fought Raevir tribes. This formed Classical\nRaevir, which was the common language of the many Raevir tribes up until the Carrions united the tribes into one sivereignty. After being driven out of Aegis by the undead's invasion of the North, the Raevir migrated to the lands of Asulon, in between the\ncity-states of Alras and Seventis. Caught between two common-speaking nations who were also the Raevir's main trading partners, they were forced to Evolve. Classical Raev died out as a language as the Carrion lord of the time swore underneath the Empire\nof Oren. All that exists now of Classic Raev is a crude dialect known as Botch.\n\nOld Common.\n\nAlthough still found in some bard's tales, the language of Old Common is very much dead. It is assumed that Old Common formed from a split\noff of Marian, when the Hanseatic peoples were the largest powerhouse in Aegisian Oren. Due to their merchant ways, as well as the their ways of war, they became an integral part of society, and their Marian language quickly replaced Flexio as a common\nlanguage. Marian was easier to understand for the peasant rabble of Oren, and Flexio became the language of the upper class and the True Faith. Because most peasants couldn't read or write, the language changed rapidly, morphing into old Common. Due to\nthe mistrust between the races in those days, Old Common did not spread very far for the Elves were still speaking Elvish in those days. In those days, Old Common was known simply as 'Orenian'. Old Common died off when it evolved into Common.\nBrae Adunian.\n\nTthough many believe Brae Adunian to be an offshoot bastard child of Thuaidian, Brae Adunian is in fact a child of Old Common. It was spoken by the Brae Clan of Adunians who worshipped the Old Faith primarily. Because the Old Faith was\nformed when the Adunians spoke Old Common, the Brae clan stuck to it even after Thuaidian was formed. The Brae Clan was known for it's stubborness.\n\nThuaidian.\n\nThe True name of the now dead Adunian language, there\nare very few Thuaidian words surviving. Thuaidian was formed as a mix of Old Northeron and Elvish resulting from the union of the two peoples. Most clans adopted it as their main language, though the Brae Clan continued to speak Old Northeron which soon\nMorphed into Brae Adunian. Thuaidian eventually died out when the Adunians left their isle and integrated back into Aegisian society, speaking the new language, Common.\n\nMori'Quessir.\n\nThough it is not certain we can call\nthis a dead language, due to the fact that anyone is unsure if the Mori'Quessir are still living or not, nobody other than they have spoken the tongue in decades. Mori'Quessir formed as a result of Nemiisae's teachings. It is an entire manufactured tongue\nwhat with Nemiisae not wishing for her children to speak Elvish, a language which she considered inferior. Mori was the spoken language of the Mori'Quessir for many centuries, and some Mali'Ker learned it when arriving in Asulon. Mori'Quessir died out\nwhen Asulon was flooded and the Descendant races migrated to Anthos. It is assumed that all the Mori'Quessir died.\n\nSabunani.\n\nIt is entirely impossible to tell where this language came from. The best guess\nwagered is that it evolved from the Dwarven tongue brought by the Dervas to Anthos. Nonetheless, this was the language spoken by the Zil'Maktumi. We can identify two periods in the language, Sabunani and New Sabunani. Sabunani was\ncharacterized by little to no vowel usage, wheras New Sabunani added vowels on for easier use. The Language died out with the Zil'Maktumi people.\n\nWritten by 'Watyll.'\n\nAnnotated by Arnorian Elendil.", ""),
        StaticBook("Vol. 1 Naromism", "Arnorian", "Introduction\n\nWhen I became the Clerical Leader on the 19th of the Grand Harvest, 1547, I knew things had to change within the order I now led. The Clerics have always followed a traditional ideology that tends to result in putting oneself in grave\ndangers, including those above and beyond the line of duty. I myself have been a victim of this, I know of how it can affect the body and mind as well as other things. In the end, I decided to change how we do things to ensure the future of clerics and\nthat we are no longer what most of us expect to be. Thus, I created a New form of Clericalism to ensure this. I understand full well that there will be those agaisnt this belief, and if they wish to harm those who accept this belief, then I would like to\ninvite them to raise sword to me, instead of the followers.\n\nWhat exactly is Naromism?\n\nNaromism is a branch of clericalism which, while including the compulsory devotion and appreciation to Tahariae, additionally focuses on\nemotional control and the understanding of 'grey' beliefs, such as the idea that the world is not devided into Black and White, Good and Evil. The Purpose of this branch is to ensure that clerics do not get themselves into troubling situations that would\nlikely risk themselves getting harmed, killed or disconected. This is mostly through causing trouble amongst the Dark to suddenly breaking the Utterings by sudden emotional outbursts. For example, a cleric randomly killing Dark Mages that are doing\nno nefarious activities currently will likely ensure further and future conflict between the Dark and the Clerics.\n\nAdditionally, the concept of one controlling their emotions is put in place, as any sudden emotional outburst may distract a cleric from\ntheir duty, or will cause them to accidentally or purposely break an Uttering. For example, imagine a cleric is walking around and then insulted. The Cleric ignores someone at first, but as the person insults them more, their temper and Anger grows. Soon,\ntheir anger reaches a peak, and the Cleric has a high chance of loosing control of themselves. If the cleric loses control of themself, it could cause them to lash out at the other, brutally harming or killing them, thus breaking the uttering of killing\ninnocents out of cold blood. That is why I have included this in Naromism: To Prevent a cleric from being emotionally held back from their duties and ensure they do not break the Utterings. This Ideology also ensures that the clerics care about themselves\nand others, such as knowing their limits, and ensuring other clerics are not too strained.\n\nBackground to Naromism.\n\nThe background of Naromism came from my own background and understandings, as well as the beliefs of\nAshanaak, first Vailorian prophet of Tahariae, and understandings of Xionism. Being someone of a short temper, I realized that my own emotions and anger would and could lead me to losing control of myself, and potentially break one of the Utterings. I\ntaught myself how to control my emotions through immense meditations and comming to understand why these emotions came to me. Soon, I learnt how to keep myself calm, stop said emotions from overwhelming me. These traits greatly aided me in\nmy becoming a war cleric, as well as becoming the first Vailoring cleric to master the Flames of Reckoning. A War cleric relies on their emotions to use the spells, so mastering your own first is recommended before war spells.\nAdditionally, the beliefs involved with Naromism come from my early expierence before becoming a cleric, then later experiences with the Cult of Xion and The Covenant. Notmally, one would expect me to hold a grudge agaisnt the Cult and the Covenant for\nwhat they have done to me and my order throughout Vailor, but I do not. They are also people with their own goals and views, and different ways to achieve them. If anything, I believe that instead of constantly fighting each over what we believe, we\nshould learn about the other's beliefs and accept that they are there.\n\nDuring the Realm of Athera, I served as a bartender within the Drunken Monk tavern, located within the cloud temple, which was a rather ironic position considering\nmy capability for tolerating drink. Due to the Tavern being within the Cloud Temple grounds, no harm could come to me so I safely interacted with Dreak Knights and a few other Dark types, and even taught a Lich Alchemy, and learnt that not all Dark evil.\nAdditionally, I was trained in Alchemy by Daniel Nodal, a Master Alchemist and a father figure to me, who told me tales of times he had befriended a few of the Dark.\n\nSoon, Vailor came and I became a cleric. Why did I become a cleric? At first, it was to\nsimply aid others, and rid the dark. During that time, I was convinced that me and my fellow clerics had to purge all of the Dark, regardless of their intentions. However, due to a run in with a necromancer who tortured me for killing an innocent Dark, I\nbegan to wonder if I was truly right there, Had I done it out of choice, or forced duty? Was is necessary to kill an innocent Dark and bring this harm to myself for the necessity of Tahariae? As time went on, I began to question things more. It was not\nuntil I was captured by the group known as the Covenant, that I began deeply questioning the lessons the Itherals had taught to the Clerics. The Covenant had treated me like a guest, and showed no ill intentions. During my one year of captivity, I\nattempted a false disconnection to get out, and I was given a book regarding Xionism to read when they wondered if I was truly serious. There, I found that their ideologies were not truly as dark as first believed, and that some points were quite\nReasonable. \n\nWritten by Elvira Naromis, Edited by Arnorian Elendil.", ""),
        StaticBook("Vol. 2 'Adunai'", "Arnorian", "This blade became the official blade of house Elendil, and was passed down to the heir of the Adunian crown when he turned seventeen. No one is quite sure which Lord had the blade forged, but it is known that it happened around the reigns of the Lords\n'Dranan', 'Rymeul' and 'Anmas.' During Braen's (Dranan's father) reign, he commisioned a group of his finest archers and swordsmen to form an order of Elites, known as the Adunian Rangers - the sole military force of the Kingdom. This order would serve\nthe Kingdom for many years to come, under the command of several legendary Ranger Captains.\n\nDranan started another Adunian tradition, of burying each member of the Elendil family with a crown, each made out of a single Material with a gem on the\nfront. To have a steel crown meant that you were a brave and courageous warrior, whilst a gold one meant you were more diplomatically minded. A Silver crown would be a balance of both. A Diamond on the front of the crown would mean that you were an\nAdunian Lord, an emerald would mean you were a military man (Most commonly an Adunian Ranger), a ruby would mean you were a gifted Artisan, and a sapphire would mean you never found a calling, but were always willing to help others.\nWhen Rymeul came to the throne, he led an expedition back to the sea which his ancestor had crossed generations ago. He discovered a land bridge had connected the two realms, of Adunia and Aegis. Rymeul's grandson, Bethrol would then go on an expedition\nof his own across the land bridge and back into Aegis, where he discovered Idunia had long since been destroyed, and a new kingdom had arisen - The city, \"Al'Khazzar' and the Kingdom, 'Oren'. Bethrol came to arrange trade agreements with the Orenians\nwhilst on the expedition, and trade routes were formed. This was the first contact the Adunians had had with the outside world since the three hundred odd years of exile. \n\nOver the years, the Adunian settlement of Ildon turned from\nan Isolated village into a hefty city, then into a small Kingdom. By 'Rydel's' rule, the Adunian rangers were no longer sufficient to keep law and order in the Kingdom. He had his brother 'Thorenir' disband the Rangers, and start a larger force called the\nAdunian Legion. This decision, which was seen as foolish by some may have saved the entire Adunian race from destruction, as soon after this choice was made, Rydel's steward 'Icarian Delmar' turned agaisnt him, and attempted to ursurp the throne.\nThis marked the beginning of what would be reffered to in history books (such as this one) as the 'Great Adunian War'.\n\nWritten and annotated by Arnorian Elendil.", ""),
        StaticBook("Vol. 2 : Origins", "Arnorian", "~*~ Shrines ~*~\n\nGod's Gardens were the most natural and beautiful of worshipping places. These were seen as absolute holy grounds made by the aspects of nature themselves. There are believed to be three God's Garden's in existence - one for\neach of the three Aspects. The God's Garden that we have currently located and were near (In Anthos, Vaerhaven.) is the God's Garden of the Elements. It was a hot spring situated in the north, protected by a granite and stone wall. Spruce trees spring up\nwith flowers poking through the snow. People would come here to cleanse the blood off themselves, to pray in it's hot waters and to fast and meditate. No blood, on any condition could ever be shed in a God's Garden, but blood could be cleansed there.\nStone Circles.\n\nStone circles were a very signifigant part of the traditional culture and faith. They were built with massive flagstones and set in a ring, with a series of four gates, two pillars around the gates, and a central altar.\nHeartfire.\n\nHeartfire is the projection of the inner spirit of humanity. The Old Northerners believed that inside of everyone's body was a fire, that the most powerful could actually channel into physical use. The blood in your\nbody keeps the fire from eating you from the inside out. When you breath, you feed the fire. When you die, your fire goes out - Even if you were to cut someone open, you would not find their fire. A Father (Priest) must be able to channel their Heartfire\nto show their devotion and inner strength, be it for combat or for healing. Many Kings and Queens in the past have also shown the ability to use Heartfire as a testiment to their faith and Strength.\n\nIt is believed that deep in the heart of a cave\nfar from the light of the sun lived a man and his family. They had fires and torches, which burned all night and accumulated smoke began to choke his family and himself. They would soon need to leave the relative security of the cave into the unknown and\nthe man prayed to Thronn every night before his fires, wishing for an answer. Eventually, one came. In the crackling fire a voice said solemnly and dryly, \"Drink your fires and know peace.\" He took this as an omen, and swallowed a piece of charcoal.\nHe did not feel pain, instead finding warmth in himself. His eyes grew bright like pyres, and illumanted what was before him. His hands were engulfed by a smokeless fire, and he felt no cold pervading. His family was awestruck, the man was dumbfounded and\nthe first Father's of Thronn were founded in that smokey cave.\n\nFathers.\n\nThe Fathers are the holders of the knowledge and traditions of the old faith. They act of behalf of the Gods, and while all their actions are\nnot godly, but very mortal, and are considered to be more in tune with the Gods, or a particular Gods. Fathers have very informal training and usually learn most of what they know orally, with some few choosing to record and write their teachings to pass\ndown.", ""),
        StaticBook("Vol. 2 : Tongues", "Arnorian", "~*~ Living Languages ~*~\n\nFlexio.\n\nFlexio was the first language ever spoken by the descendant races, being that it was the language of the Aenguls and the Daemons. They taught it to the Four Brothers. However,\nafter the fall of Iblees and the great war between he and his followers, all but the Human race abandoned Flexio as a language. Elves, Orcs and Dwarves felt it was too corrupted by Iblees to continue speaking it. Flexio became a soley Human language.\nHumans felt it was a holy langyage that no evil being could ever corrupt. Flexio died out as a living language during the Aegisian period of history when it began to fracture apart. With the migration of the Farfolk people known as the Easterners to the\nEastern Isles, it slowly merged with their elven language to become Easterner. In the North of Aegis it morphed into the Highlander tongue known as Old Northeron. With the merging of Northeron and southeron peoples in Aegis, the language Illatian was\nformed. Aeldin Flexio was formed during the migration of people to the far islands in the verge, to the continent of Aeldin. Finally, Auvergnian was formed during the Old Kingdom of Auvergne in the Westlands of Orenian Aegis. The languages Illatian,\nAeldin Flexio and Auvergnian are all considered part of the Flexio Family of Languages. Flexio was reborn as a living language during the Holy Oren Empire under Emperor Godfrey Horen. Most nobles know it, and a few families speak it as their first tongue.\nIllatian.\n\nThe Illatian language formed from the commonly spoken Flexio in the midlands of Aegis, at the merging of Southeron and Northeron culture. It is still spoken today rarely.\n\nAeldin Flexio.\nThis language was formed by a group of human settlers who sailed through the Verge to the lands far, far to the East. This continent known as Aeldin, grew apart from much of the world and little is known about it. With the Great Orenian Exodus in Anthos,\nvast scores of the Empire's folk left to Aeldin and upon conqouring it, learned the language of their conquered people, just as the Aeldini learned Common. Upon the return of those who had left, Aeldin Flexio was brought back. It is most commonly spoken\nby the Chivay family.\n\nAuvergnian.\n\nThe Auvergnian language was born across the mountains from the Heartland of Oren, in the vast fertile lands of the West, in the Kingdom of Auvergne. Auvergnian society was one of beauty and honour,\nwhich their language reflects. As it evolved from Flexio, it changed to a flowing language that could be spoken without much effort. When Oren turned to adopting the Auvergnian culture, creating Knights instead of common soldiers, they adopted Auvergnian\nchivalry and other traditions. Kings began to dress in Royal Robes instead of Armor. Auvergnian is still a living language today, commonly spoken as a first language by the Valois and De Britagne families.\n\nWritten by 'Watyll', annotated by\nArnorian Elendil.", ""),
        StaticBook("Vol. 3 'Adunai'", "Arnorian", "Icarian and his followers started on the outskirts of the Adunian lands, taking villages and settlements one by one. They burnt houses, pillaged women, raped crops and took anyone left alive as prisoners. The Crops were sent to camps run by Icarians\nfollowers where they were treated like animals, while the men were tortured and brainwashed until they too followed Icarian. In a desperate attempt to find more soldiers, Rydel forced everyone between the ages of 17 and 110 into his brother's Adunian\nLegion. The war raged on for six years, until Thorenir was able to kill Icarian in a battle. At the begining of the War, the Adunians were at a large peak, with around 10,000 souls in Ildon, and around 8,000 in the countryside. The war ravaged the\ncountryside, leaving around 2000 left. Ildon was left a ghost town, with less then half it's population remaining.\n\nWith no longer the amounts of citizens that required the Legion, Thorenir disbanded the organization and\nrestarted the Adunian Rangers. The war had set back the race by centuries, but more than anything it had affected Rydel. Rydel was a diplomat, not a warrior, and seeing so many of the people he had known die before him. He eventually went mad.\nThorenir saw this, and decided to enact a peaceful coup'detat. Rymeul was lucid enough to call this an act of treason, banishing Thorenir and his wife Irene from his lands.\n\nLuckily, Rydel soon mellowed with age, and slowly became\nmore capable at his job once again. A few years before he was going to hand the throne to his youngest son, 'Aron', tragedgy struck. Aron, who was an Adunian ranger at the time was killed during a mission to clear out a cave full of orcs who had been\nraiding farms near Ildon. This left Rydel to rule for even longer, until his grandson Artorus Elendil the first came of age to rule.\n\nBefore Artorus's time came to rule, a fair greater tragedy struck. The undead threat that had\nbegun to sweep through the land from the north, and as they were further north than any other settlement, Ildon was the first place to be hit. Halboron Elendil, the eldest son of Rydel and the current leader of the Adunian Rangers, lead all the combat-\nworthy Adunians into battle agaisnt the undead, but it was not enough. Halboron was knocked unconcious during the battle, and all else were slaughtered.\n\nUpon waking, Halboron found the undead necromancers had left, but they had\nalready slaughtered all of the Adunians, and set Ildon alight. Halboron fought his way through the remaining undead minions to reach his house in Ildon. Upon entering, he found his nephew Artorus mourning over his mother's body. Halboron took the shards\nof Durendal from his lockbox, threw Artorus over his shoulder and fought his way back out of Ildon. The two travelled for many weeks, leaving the Adunian Kingdom and crossing back into Aegis. They reached the cloud temple at Aegis's center, and Halboron\nleft his nephew in the care of the monks. Before leaving, Halboron gave Artorus the shards of Durendal, in the hope that one day he might find a way to reforge the sword.\n\nFrom there, Halboron travelled back to Ildon to see what was left\nof the once grand city. Only smoking ruins remained. Halboron investigated the ashes of Al'Ildic where he found his father's charred body. He reluctantly prized the Circlet of Horen from the corpse before heading south to Oren, not to be reunited with\nanother Adunian for many years to come.\n\nWritten and annotated by Arnorian Elendil.", ""),
        StaticBook("Vol. 3 : Adunai", "Arnorian", "~*~ Appearance ~*~\n\nAdunians take traits from both their Harrenite and Mali'Dun ancestors. Brown or black hair are the only two colours found, though like other races it goes grey with age. It is not uncommon for an Adunian's hair to go grey at ages as\nyoung as fifteen, a trait inherited from the Mali'Dun. Their skin is pale, but generally no paler than the average Heartlander. An Adunian's eyes are either grey from the Mali'Dun, brown from the Harrenites (A rare trait), or green, which appears to have\ncome from a mix of both, the most common eye colour. Adunians generally grow to heights of about 5\"11', and have a lean build. Tradition has the Adunians keep their hair shoulder length, and grow beards that keep close to the face.\n~*~ Clothing ~*~\n\nThe general climate of Al'Ildic was quite cold. Though it's landscape was only coated in snow during the winter months, the chill of the northern wind was present year round. As a result of this climate, Adunians came to dress in a\nparticular way. Men would wear heavy coats and cloaks, lined with furs and warm skins, thick leather boots and warm wool breeches. Women wore long-sleeved dresses, often accompanied with furs draped over their shoulders, light cloaks and some form of\ntrousers to keep their legs warm. Over time it became considered 'rude' or 'Socially unacceptable' for more than the hands or head to be unclothed. This way of thinking has stuck over the ages, and Adunians true to tradition always cover themselves from\nthe neck down before presenting themselves publically.\n\n~*~ Weaponry ~*~\n\nDue to factors such as their slender build, Al'Ildic's snowy climate and the influence of their Mali'Dun ancestors, Adunians tend to avoid heavier or blunt weapons.\nArming swords, Bastard swords, Long swords, dirks and a variety of other smaller bladed weapons make up the majority of an Adunian army's weaponry. Unique to the Adunians however is the Bowie knife. Named after the Bowie nomads (named for their lifestyle\nreliance on the bow) who created it, the bowie knife was meant to bridge the gap between a tool and a weapon. A large blade with a clip point and sweeping belly, generally around nine inches in length. The wielders hand would be protected by a wide metal\ncrossguard that could be used to parry an incomming blow. Despite this connection to smaller arms like the bowie knives, an Adunian's favourite weapon is always his bow. The legacy of the Mali'Dun stayed strong with their descendants and bow making became\none of the most respectable trades of Al'Ildic. All sorts of bows were utilized by the ARC (Adunian Ranger Corp), the bowies, the Ildician order and the general common folk of Al'Ildic, from longbows to composite bows. The bow that left the greatest\nlegacy of all was the double recurve warbow.\n\nOnly the greatest Adunian bowyers could craft the weapon, using four prods rather than two for additional power, though not so much that it would break the bow. Generally made\nfrom the yew that grew in the forests of Ar'Norian, crafting a double-recurve warbow was more about finding the balance of power than anything else, maximising it's ability to shoot with strength, but not so much as to splinter the wood. An accomplished\nbowyer might only ever craft two or three in his lifetime, and it is unknown if any today possess the skill to even make one.\n\nThe last of the three weaponry innovations unique to Al'Ildic is a material known as 'Eldar steel.' Called Eldion steel by some,\nthe material was forged in a grand fortified cavern to the south of Ekvol in Al'Ildic's Irthgard mountain range. Smiths from around the small continent would go there to study the art of crafting blades, and over the centuries they were able to create\ntheir own unique style of blacksmithing. In a lengthy process the smiths would craft weapons by layering steel for a sharper edge and a more ornate appearance.\n\nAt first, the blades were brittle and could shatter if used extensively, but the Eldar smiths\nhoned their ability over many generations, and eventually their weapons were as strong as any other. The time it took to craft such a weapon meant they were rarely made and far more expensive than a regular steel sword which could do the same job just as\nwell. As a result of this, only the richest and most influential clans of Al'Ildic ever owned an Eldar steel weapon, and they were usually passed down through the generations as a family heirloom. In modern times, weapons of Eldar steel are in fewer\nnumbers than the endangered Adunians whose ancestors made them, and like the double-recurve bow, it is unknown if anyone yet has the skill to recreate it.", ""),
        StaticBook("Vol. 3 : Tongues", "Arnorian", "~*~ Languages Cont. ~*~\n\nMarian.\n\nMarian is a language that split off from Old Northeron. It's brother, Classical Raev is quite different. Marian evolved from the spoken tongue of the Westfolk, who lived in the mountains\nof the North, as opposed to the Raevir who lived on the flat frozen Tundra. The Westfolk became known as the Hanseatic peoples, and remained a harsh tribal people for a good long while after most other Humans had become civilized, they still raided the\nRaevir and the Southeron Lands. About half of the Hanseatic tribes left by the grace of the Wandering Wizard, and migrated to Asulon in search of new lands, but some stayed behind. Many of the remaining tribesmen moved into the Teutonic order city of\nNuremburg, and the language was absorbed by the Order members that lived there. Upon migration to Asulon, the Teutonic order found the ruins of the Kingdom established by the Hanseastic tribes who had left Aegis and migrated there, and named it Hanseti in\ntheir honour. Though the Teutonic Order is now disbanded and defunct, Marian is still spoken in Hanseti in Athera. The Marian language evolved into Old Common when the Northerners in Aegis migrated South and built a large trade league along the borders\nbetween Heartland and North. Though they were still what we could consider barbarians, they were also skilled traders, and their trade spread through the Heartland, and Flexio became replaced with Marian which evolved into Old Common.\nEasterner.\n\nThe Eastern tongue of the Eastern peoples of the Farfolk subrace of Humans evolved in the Eastern Isles of Aegis. It is a bastard tongue of Elvish which formed with the mixing of the two peoples. It is still spoken by all Easterners,\nmost prominently the Ishikawa family and the White Tiger Yakuza.\n\nOld Blah.\n\nOld Blah was taught to the Shamans of Aegisian Orcs by the Spirits. The Shamans, who were looking for a tongue to teach the Orcs besides Flexio which they\nconsidered corrupted began teaching them Old Blah. The language fit well with the Uruks, who could speak it well even with tusks. As Common began to rise to prominence however, it warped into the dialect simply known as Blah. Old Blah is now only spoken\nby Shamans.\n\nBlah.\n\nBlah is a warped and ugly dialect made by the merging of Common and the Orcish Old Blah. It was very few 'S' sounds, which are replaced with 'Z's. The grammer is nearly non-existant, and it is barely\nunderstandable by the other races. To add to this confusion, it still contains a great many Old Blah words, such as Bub'hosh, Nub'hosh, Rulg, Throm'ka, and others.\n\nElvish.\n\nElvish was first spoken in the Elvish lands in Aegis many\nyears ago. For reasons unknown, it was outlawed thousands of years ago and died out. The Elves switched to Common in favour of Elvish. It was the High Elves of Asulon who brought it back. It began to rise to prominence in Malinor (Anthos) as the High\nElves gained more power, and now in Athera, almost every Elf knows a bit of Elvish. Some High Elves speak it exclusively, though most of the words are lost to History. Perhaps someday the mysteries of Elvish will be uncovered. The Elvish language has\nthree child-groups. Thuaidian, which evolved by merging with the Old Common tongue of the Adunians, Easterner which evolved by merging with the Flexio tongue of the Easterners, and Mori, which merged with Proto-Asulonian.\nKharajyr.\n\nKharajyr is a language formed by the evolution of Proto-Asulonian into it's present form. It split off from the other Asulonian tongue, Mori'Quessir. Kharajyr is still spoken by the Kharajyr peoples today.\nBotch.\n\nA dialect spoken in the Highlands of Oren, it is a bastard child of the mostly lost Classical Raevir and Common. It rose to prominence during the Crow's age of Oren, when the Carrion family took the throne of the Empire. Raevir culture spread\nthroughout hte land, and Botch became commonly spoken.\n\nBlack Langguage.\n\nA language that can be naturally spoken by any being using, or formed by Dark Magic. According to Ikuras cultists, it was created by their Patron Ikuras, Greater\nSpirit of Fear and Insanity. In addition to being a common language spoken by the servants of Evil, Black language is used for spells in Phobism. Black language is most commonly spoken by the Ikuras Cult, the Necromancers, the Dread-Knights, and Shades.\nWritten by 'Watyll', annottated by Arnorian Elendil.", ""),
        StaticBook("Vol. 4 'Adunai'", "Arnorian", "Artorus eventually left the care of the monks and slowly made his way in the world as a military man. By the time Aegis was destroyed and it's citizens had voyaged to Asulon, Artorus was an esteemed military captain of the Phoenix Guard, which was the\nprimary military force of the Kingdom of Renatus. By chance, Halboron saw a notice in the cloud temple that had been placed by Artorus, claiming he was looking for his long lost uncle. Halboron traveled as fast as he could to Renatus where he was reunited\nwith his nephew. Together, they planned to reunite their race and rebuild what they once had.\n\nAfter many years of tireless work, Artorus and Halboron completed building a new Ildon in Western Renatus, by which time their family had grown\nimmensely. (Authors note: I was born at this time.) Many years after the time when it was acknowledged officially complete, Halboron met the end of his days due to an infected wound he had sustained serving Westfall as Lord Commander of the Westwatch.\nThe years pressed on, and Ildon continued to grow, eventually becoming one of the largest cities in Oren. As with all good things, they must come to an end - The Order of the Mongoose came to three figures - Artorus Elendil, Jon Norsem and Eze'kiel Tarus.\nThere, they convinced the three of a plot that in the House of Horen, lay the blood of Demons and the next undead uprising. Sold by the stout and trustworthy wizarding order, the three immidiatly rose in revolt in what they believed was for the greater\ngood, and were swiftly put down in a three day siege and battle in Ildon. Artorus and Eze'kiel were executed by Lancel Hightower (Who then forced marriage upon Eze'kiels Widow), and Norsem fell in combat. \nIn pity of Artorus's son Torrhen (My brother) the\nEmperor gifted him with the dukedom of Westfall in a successful attempt to sway Torrhen from wanting to rebel agaisnt Oren Himself. The Elendil family and the Adunian race continued to live in Ildon under Torrhen's rule, until the final days of Asulon.\nFrom there, the Adunians moved to the Isle of Elysium, where complex politics and a severe lack of land led to a terribly ugly bout of infighting amoungst the three young Elendils (I was a child at the time, too young to be concerned with said politics.)\nIsabella Elendil (My sister, age 17), Torrhen Elendil, aged 25, and Harrion Elendil, aged 35. It was during this time a near civil conflict broke out, and the two sides remained seperate well into the time of Anthos.\nWritten and Annotated by Arnorian Elendil.", ""),
        StaticBook("Vol. 4 : Adunai", "Arnorian", "~*~ Religion ~*~\n\nWhen Fai O'Thuaid was founded, Orthodox Creationism was the official faith enforced by Harren and his descendants. Though they were isolated from the church and took no efforts to maintain contact, they were still devout Creationists who\nkept true to their beliefs and traditions. After landing in Al'Ildic however, many Adunians lost their faith in the Creator, pondering why he allowed the brutal Ulgaard Rebellion to occur, and the Adunians to be cast out of Aegis. In the early 400's,\nBraen Elin'sehn, son of Elendil began preaching a new belief; that the Creator had abandonded them, leaving the spirits of their ancestors behind to take on the role as their guide. The belief was quick to catch on in Al'Ildic society, giving rise to\nAdunian Creationism.\n\nAdunian Creationism centers around the belief that a single Creator build the world, but then abandoned his people after they disapointed him. This called for the spirits of the dead to step in and take up the Creator's role as\nthe guiding hand for the living. A Century later, the religion had evolved and built itself upon three main figures - Elendi the father, Maria the mother, and Braen, the son and chosen prophet of\nCreator. Followrs of the religion would pray to these three figures, as well as their own personal ancestors.\n\nAs the centuries went on, more patrons were added to the original three. Cirimas Elendil became the patron of protection and justice, Garth\nHaryn the patron of war and triumph, Falcryn Delmar, patron of politics and negotiation, Reman Elendil patron for vengance and loyalty, Rymeul Elendil, patron of exploration, and so on and so on. Other lesser patrons also existed, though they were not as\nwidely worshipped and were generally confined to provinces in Al'Ildic.\n\nVarious shrines were erected across Al'Ildic, most notably a shrine to both Elendil and Maria in Ildon, a shrine to Cirimas in the north of Ar'Norian, a shrine to Braen high in\nMaria's peaks, and a shrine to Garth Haryn in Del Kaev. The shrines took the form of large stone statues representing their chosen figure, and a stone altar at the feet where offerings of gold, food and other valubles would be left. Pilgrims would often\nleave relevant offerings to their patricular patron at each shrine. Another important part of Adunian Creationism is the connection between the people and the Sea. This importance was derived from the sea having been where the Mali'Dun disappeared, where\nHarren's remains were cast by Owyn Ulgaard's rebels, and the place where Elendil had chosen to take his last voyage.\n\nThis method of worship went undisturbed for around six hundred years before the Canonist faith was brought to Al'Ildic.\nWhen Lord Rymeul Elendil reopened trade with mainland Aegis in 1001, the faith of Oren was soon to follow and began spreading it's influence on the smaller continent. This was not very effective, as for three centuries the Canonist church struggled to\ngain a foothold, only having only converted around five percent of the population by the Realm's fall in 1309.\n\nWhen the Adunians eventually resettled in new Ildon under Lord Artorus Elendil the future of the Adunian religion was reshaped again.\nArtorus had been raised for the most part in Renatus, and had been brought up with the Canonist faith from a young age. When his time to lead came, he made sure to spread his beliefs amoung his followers as to assure they were fully accepted as members of\nGodfrey's Holy Oren Empire. Artorus's son and heir, Harrion was himself extremely devout, to the point of founding a holy organization known as the 'Monastic Order', making him another driving force behind the Canonization of the Adunians.\nDuring Artorus's reign there was a massive shift away from Adunian Creationism, almost wiping out it's existence. The majourity of Adunians converted to Canonism, while the vast remainder merged Adunian Creationism and Canonism together, forming what is\nknown as New Adunian Creationism.", ""),
        StaticBook("Vol. 5 : Adunai", "Arnorian", "~*~ Customs ~*~\n\nThere are an array of traditions and customs that have shaped Adunian society over the ages and many continue to practice to this day. Many clans have their down traditions specific to themselves, some provinces have a similiar system\nsuch as the Nivinese connection to bowie culture, while the most important customs are those that apply to the realm and race as a whole.\n\nSuccession laws in Adunian society differ from those of most other races. While a man in Oren will leave his lands\nand titles to his eldest son, an Adunian would leave his simply to the youngest. This system was put into place to assure a landowner could spend the longest time possible in his position, and quell the threat of political instability from provinces\nchanging hands. It also took away the issue of sons killing their fathers to take their positions ahead of time, as most heirs were to young to even consider such a cource of action. In spite of this, the law did cause unnecessary hostility within\nfamilies as a young boy could grow up thinking he was his father's her, only for said father to bear another child and force the first to relinquish their claim. It was a testing dilemma for many young Adunians, but also a fitting method for them to prove\ntheir honour and dedication to their family above all else. The Adunian succession laws have been in place since Braen Elin'sehn, who left his position to his youngest son Farwil rather then his eldest Cirimas, who had been absent for many years living as\na ranger.\n\nAdulthood in Adunian culture begins for a man when he has lived through seventeen winters. At this point, a large celebration of drinking, dancing and music will be held in honour of the man, the extent of the festivities varying\ndepending on his clan's wealth. The celebration can last from one day, to an entire month. When the festivities and hangovers have cleared, the now adult man sets out on a trek. A man from a common clan may only explore his native province, when a member\nof a greater clan might also visit his neighboring regions and important sites around the realm. An Elendil however, is expected to visit each of the realm's provinces, their main towns and cities, along with the major shrines to the patron saints.\nWhen the man returns, he can take on political or military roles in society, or elevate his position in whatever sect of the workforce he was being trained in beforehand. Adunian women have a very different experience in thier transition to adulthood.\nThe largest discrepancy, is the age at which they are considered adult, which is twenty winters past. Festivities are held, although not public; more closer family affairs. When she turns twenty, she gains the ability to marry.\nWomen have a strange place in Adunian culture. They don't face the same extreme control they are subjected to in societies like Oren, but it is frowned upon if they seek certain careers. When they reach adulthood, they gain the same freedoms as men; they\ncan enter the workforce, choose their own husband without the interference of other family members (though they were often threatened or pressured into heeding the judgement of their father or clan Elder), travel, or in rare cases, enter the Military.\nWhile the 'ARC' (Adunian Ranger Company) and Ildician order were male dominated, occasionally a woman could rise to command a squad or company. For the most part, women were known to stay home, tend to the household, raise children and contribute to the\nwellbeing of their town or city. Like men, they were expected to uphold dress standards, covering themselves from the head down, only exposing their hands and head.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 1: Adunai", "Arnorian", "In the years of ancient Aegis, there were no Kingdoms or cities, there were tribes and clans of Men, Elves, Dwarves, and Orcs. One of mankind's greatest tribes were the Harenites, a group of strong hearted warriors led by Harren, the First born of Horen,\nand one of his favourites. Soon the Harenites clashed with a tribe of Elves led by Princess Sarai of Malinor. The Two people fought for fifty years, until one day they finally made peace with one another. Weapons were thrown down, and soon the peoples of\neach tribe began to marry each other. The Blood from the Elves and the Human's mixed, and soon the first of a new race were born. \nThe descendants of the two tribes called themselves 'Adunian' and as one, they created the kingdom of 'Idunia' - one of\nthe first civilized settlements in Aegis, where they would rule over normal men for many years. During the rule of an Adunian called 'Arendil', the grandson of Haren, one of the pure-blooded humans living in Idunia called 'Owyn Godwinson' began a\nrebellion agaisnt the Adunians, sick of them ruling over his race. Sparing the gritty and long details, he succeeded and took Idunia for himself. Arendil was killed, and all Adunians were henceforth banished.\n\nArendil's son, 'Elendil' (Later to be\nknown as 'Elendil the Exiled') led the remaining Adunians to the far north of Aegis to get away from Godwinson and his forces, whom he feared would decide to chase after them and exterminate the scattered Adunians. The Adunians went as far north as\npossible, until they came to a large sea, (creatively named the Adunian Sea, by Lord 'Rymuel Elendil'.) Stretched out far before them. Elendil has his people build two boats, Salvation and Haren's Voyager. After months of labour, the Adunians completed\nthe boats and set sail across the sea, hoping to find a Haven for them to settle in peace on the other side.\n\nAfter a two month journey, the Adunians anchored their ships on the coast of a then foriegn land, and set off to find a fine place to settle. For\nanother week Elendil led the Adunians across an area of large open plains, and then into a thick forest of pine and spruce. It was within this area Elendil decided to settle, and put his people to work on building the town that would eventually be named\n'Ildon', meaning 'Haven.' The castle that sat on the hill nearby would be called 'Al'Ildic', meaning 'The Invincible.'\n\nThe Adunian race flourished in it's new Home, and under the rule of Elendil's descendants who\nbecame known as the House of Elendil. Elendil's only son, Braen Elendil became leader of the Adunian race, taking the title of 'Adunian Lord', and started the tradition of passing his title down to the youngest son, to give himself the longest reign\npossible. The Adunian Lord also wore a circlet called 'The circlet of Horen' which the Adunians believed to have belonged to Horen himself, before it was given to his son Haren, and passed down the line. Somewhere in the comming years, one of the Adunian\nLords had a blade forged, and specially enchanted, dubbing it 'Durendal.'\n\nWritten and annotated by Arnorian Elendil.", ""),
        StaticBook("Voucher", "Dominik Oafdin", "Voucher:\n16 stacks of Spruce\n\n100minas", ""),
        StaticBook("Vow Anaylises", "Damai Torena", "I will walk the forests with my new kin shows that I should follow and band together with my new Druid and dedicant brothers and sisters.\n\nI shall protect and keep the balance with them, they shall help me and I shall help them.\nI will swim the river of truth wherever it takes me\n\nShows that I should follow my destiny and to now go against the truth, like swimming against a current, it is hard and stupid. It shows that I should tell the truth no matter what the consequences\nAs the trees watch over me I will watch over others shows that I should help everyone like I am helped myself.\n\nThat I should always think about others before I and help them who need help. That the trees watch me as I go about my life.\nI will seek to preserve the balance, even as dusk approaches\nStates that I will always strive to keep the balance no matter the weather or time of day.\n\nThe balance is the most important thing about being a druid/dedicant.", ""),
        StaticBook("Voyage", "Griffith Du Mont", "Voyage\nby Griffith du Mont, Grand Maestro (seffx)              transcribed by Elorna Avern\nUnfurl the sails and off we go,\nDivine wind will gently blow,\nStirring waves t'rock us to and fro,\nGuiding us the stars that glow.\nShining brightly with the moon in tow,\nTowards the horizon, they'll come to show,\nA land where times ever slow,\nThat eases your heart and quells your woes,\nBut for now we rest in the deck below,\nWe'll reach paradise before you know!\nWritten by Griffith du Mont, Grand Maestro.\n\nThis book was found in the Bard's College Library on the Isle of ArmaLuna in Asulon. It was brought over and transcribed by Elorna Avern.", ""),
        StaticBook("Vydra: Vol I", "Andrik Vydra", "7th day of Malin's Welcome, 1491\n\nThe war is over.\nI sit here, in this empty hall, upon the seat of Humanity. The Lord's seat in Petrus, once grand capital of the Empire. \n\nI cannot help but feel utterl unaccomplished and empty as I write.\nOur once proud people are splintered into pieces, this farce of unity nothing but that - a facade. Petty squabbles and ambitious Lords litter my new Kingdom, but it was their swords and banners which placed me upon this throne.\n\nI have won a crown,\nbut now I find myself shackled to it, unable to act as a true monarch of men.\n\nWhat should happen when I pass? Who will follow? And if they succeed in keeping the Kingdom intact, what then shall come after? Our destiny as a people lies in the hands of -\n- indivduals who rise through whatever methods possible, no matter how incompetend, or cruel, or undeserving they may be. The reigns of Varon Kovachev in Akovia and Aleksandr who sat where I do now is testament to this. \n\nI fight now not just\nfor my homeland Akovia, but for all of Humanity. I must ensure that there is always someone to take up the mantle, even when I am but ash and memory.\n\nThere is much to do.\n12th day of The First Seed, 1491\n\nI have heard whisperings of discontent in my realm. In every corner, I see enemies. If only there were some way to purge these blinded fools in one fell sweep.\n\nDo they not understand?\nI AM Humanity. When I sleep, I see the destiny of our flock. Our people, MY people, shall reign supreme over all the lands from sea to sea. And then I wake to see the despicable state of things, the idiocy that has permeated itself throughout the very air\nthat we breath. The shortsightedness of my court is enough to make me sick. \n\nThe other races, even my own subjects - are not fit to rule themselves, squabbling as children do over toys.\n\nTo even attempt such a feat is monumental\nin itself, impossible in the current state that once proud Orenia finds itself. Personal armies and retinues dominate the nature of our marital strength. We must consolidate so that we may conquer.\n\nOne King. One Kingdom. One iron fist to smite down my -\n- my enemies in the form of the unwavering ranks of those who will see my vision into reality. My children, my Sons of Vydra - the Zealots of Man.\n\nMy legacy.\n20th day of The Grand Harvest, 1491\n\nUnfaltering strength is the only way to make others see reason, I have decided.\n\nThe weak are the easiest to manipulate, and any man can be made weak through force - A man cannot make a stand if\nhe has no legs with which to raise himself. \n\nMen mistake my silence for those very things I despise. Lethargy, indecision, weakness. How I pity the fools. Wisdom is patience. I sit and listen to them prattle and say nothing but simply nod. I watch on -\n- as they sneer and scheme, wringing their filthy hands with malice as they conspire against what I have build - what is yet to be built.\n\nBut one day, I will strike. \n\nWithout mercy and without remorse.\nI will gut them from head to toe, boil their children before their very eyes, let them watch on in terror as I skin their women alive. Their screams and pleas for death will do nothing for them.\n\nWho then, will have the will to oppose me? My vision?\nBut until that day - I sit and wait, and not like the buffoon they take me for.\n3rd day of Malin's Welcome, 1493\n\nObstacles do not exist to be surrendered to, but only to be broken.\n\nToday I walked past a great pasture field, full of grazing livestock. The grass within the field was dry - a field of nothingness.\nBut without...beyond the pitiful fences, that constituted the barrier between these feeding cows and freedom - was lush greenery, far as the eye could see.\n\nAnd yet they continued to roam aimlessly, picking at the dirt and dry patches of weeds.\nWhen the greater good is at hand, there is no length one should not go, no number of taboos taken up, traditions and expectations trod upon. I take the lesson those mindless creatures taught me to heart.\n\nOnly when every single possible -\n- option is exhausted, every terrible - deplorable - path explored, will I consider conceding. But only for the time being, for there is always another opportunity. Another man to kill. Another idiot to fool and bend like the branches of a tree in summer\nor if need be - break, like those in times of winter.\n17th day of The First Seed, 1493\n\nI am ashamed by my countrymen. They war seems to have given every human able to draw breath a bloodlust equal to that of even the savage Orcs to the west. There is no reason to it, no logic.\nThey cry for more and more with each battle, victory or defeat. What has happened to the versatility of Humanity? We are not weapons forged for war, a people with a purpose so singular and barbaric as simple slaughter.\nWar is raged for a purpose, and the day that purpose is for the simple slaughter of others and razing of homes is the day I set down my crown and relinquish any notion that Humanity is truly superior.\n\nYou cannot rule the dead.", ""),
        StaticBook("W'aflu's Diary", "W'aflu", "Diry\n\nMy nayme is W'aflu B'ranguffji\nMy pairents wos kill by nasty, stinkie green Orcke. I use to live in toun caled witerun and peeple use to say, this is wot they sey: I use to be adventererer licke you but then i took an aroww to the nee.\nOrckes and raders smashed up my home and i fled, and became a theif steeling from rich peeple. \n\nafter many yeers of traning I became a master at the bow and was the best in Felson. \nEvryone loved me. I became king of Oren and its true eeven ask sumeone.", ""),
        StaticBook("Wandering Shoes", "Naz Atatis", "Wandering Shoes\n    \nIt feels like yesterday, \nI heard my parents say: Son you were born to stray, will you ever settle down?\n\nMaybe there'll come a\ntime,\n\nI might just tow the line,\nRight now I'm doing fine \nRolling from town to town.\n\nI don't care where I ride, I'll let my feet decide.\n\nDo anything but don't ask that I\n\nHang up my wandering shoes\nHang up my wandering shoes.\n\nGuitars and ringing tones\n\nIn my blood and in my bones \n\nShow me a bridge and I'll call it home.\n\n'Cause I don't wanna change.\nI don't care where I ride\n\nI'll let my feet decide\n\nDo anything but don't ask that I\n\nHang up my wandering shoes\n\nHang up my wandering shoes\nOn a lonely day\n\nWhen they put me in my grave\n\nAin't a worry you need to save\n\nJust hang up my wandering shoes.\n\nSo let that bard run\nHeaded out, towards the sun.\n\nI'm in the mood for moving on\n\nBut I'll be back some day.\n\nI don't care where I ride\n\nI'll let my feet decide\nDo anything but don't ask that I\n\nHang up my wandering shoes\n\nHang up my wandering shoes", ""),
        StaticBook("Wanted Poster", "Athir al-Hashim", "WANTED\n \n       Dejal Aestil\n\n** Descriptive sketch of the man is centered upon the document. **\n\n   Oathbreaker Murderer & Arsonist\n\nSeize immediately for trial by Caliph or Amir", ""),
        StaticBook("Warhawkesong", "echang4", "Venturing through the snowy mountains that\n\nshe once resided at,\n\nshe spotted a beige, brown-striped\n\nfeather and concluded it was of a hawk, which piped\n\nin the distance. The feather seemed to ha\nve fallen from the sky,\n\nswaying down through the air as a light breeze carried it to where she stood by.\n\nWhen it fell to the ground, the feather got flecks\n\nof the delicate snow on it and seemed exquisite in many aspects.\nAs soon as she picked it up by the quill,\n\nshe heard another loud shrill.\n\nShe moved towards the noise\n\nwithout losing her poise,\n\nand stumbled upon a\nhawk\n\nthat did not seem to be able to get up, fly, or walk.\n\nAround the bird's leg wrapped a band\n\nwith a description of the location engraved and\n\na rolled up note lay\nbeside its body\n\nwith writing that was surprisingly bawdy.\n\nThe lady trekked across the mountains of Urguan\n\nto get there. The hawk laid upon\n\nher arms the whole way until\nthey reached a fortress upon an icy hill.\n\nShe yelled at the gate\n\nand there was not a long wait.\n\nA dusky skinned night elf let her in,\n\nthough for a moment\nhe was hesitant.\n\nThe elves thanked her for returning\n\ntheir messenger hawk. Discerning\n\nof the trouble she had to go through for them,\n\nshe was bestowed a small lapis gem.", ""),
        StaticBook("Warmth Within", "Lucien 'Worm' Fournier", "Let our loving, my dear touch more then the skin.\n\nFeel the warmth\nThe growing warmth\nThe love and the warmth within.\n                    ~Lucien", ""),
