        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "3rd of The Amber Cold, 1551.§0\n§0\n§0We've gotten chickens. I cleaned the entire palace alone\n\n1st of Snow's Maiden, 1552§0\n§0\n§0I nearly killed Beelzebub with my shovel. I caught him with Catherine. His Majesty knighted him and complimented my strength. I went shopping with Jaromir. There's a new maid. Jaromir gave me flowers. Dead ones.\n\n13th of Malin's welcome, 1552§0\n§0\n§0 Bertrand continues to mention the fact that his  Majesty holds feelings towards myself. He brings forth somewhat relevant proof. This bothers me. Perhaps I will ask his Majesty when court is done.\n\n19th of The First Seed, 1552§0\n§0\n§0The answer was unexpected. I won't write more. I whipped the royal children seventy times. I nearly killed a man with my shovel. Beelzebub has been given his quest. My waist has thickened just slightly, perhaps I must stop\n\nbaking my emotions.§0\n\n21st of The Grand Harvest, 1552§0\n§0\n§0He looked at my eyes and froze. No more staring. No more pies. No more questioning. I believe I truly am dull at this point. I am not chubby anymore, however. Do not offer to make an egg for him.\n\nWe kissed.\n\n24th of The Grand Harvest, 1553§0\n§0\n§0I attacked Gwyn. I hate her. She's a whore. People should stop speaking to her. She called me a lowly maid.§0\n§0 I cried into  him, it felt nice. I think I am too skinny.\n\nJ§0\n§0o§0\n§0h§0\n§0n§0\n§0h§0\n§0a§0\n§0s§0\n§0a§0\n§0t§0\n§0h§0\n§0i§0\n§0c§0\n§0k      ass.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "A series of short poems.§0\n§0\n§0Frozen Beauty:§0\n§0\n§0Eyes glistening of Sapphire,§0\n§0That allure a passion stronger than fire.§0\n§0Your skin as pure as a bright jewel,§0\n§0Let our love become the fuel. . For our happiness together.\n\n My Love:§0\n§0\n§0My love burns brighter than the sun,§0\n§0Knowing that you are the one.§0\n§0A one in which I can share,§0\n§0A life full of love and care.§0\n§0I assure you that these last words are true,§0\n§0I love you.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "I hate you with a necessary hate.§0\n§0First,§0\n§0I sought patience; passionate was she.§0\n§0My patience turned in very scorn of me.§0\n§0That I should dare forgive a sin so great.§0\n§0As this , trough which§0\n§0I sit disconsolate;\n\nMourning for that live soul§0\n§0\n§0\n§0I used to see:§0\n§0Soul of a saint, whose friend§0\n§0I used to be:§0\n§0Til you came by! A cold corrupting fate.§0\n§0Why come now? You whom I cannot cease,\n\nWith pure and perfect hate to hate? Go ring§0\n§0The death-bell with a deep, thriumphant toll!§0\n§0\n§0Say you my friend sits by me still? Ah peace!§0\n§0Call you this my friend? This nameless thing?§0\n§0This living body hiding it's dead soul?\n\nThat was named The Destroyer Of A Soul.§0\n§0\n\nThis one is named The Horrors Of Sleep§0\n§0\n§0Sleep brings no joy to me §0\n§0Rememberance never dies§0\n§0My soul is given to my misery§0\n§0And lives in sighs§0\n§0Sleep brings no rest to me;\n\nThe Shadowes of the dead§0\n§0My wakening eyes may never see§0\n§0\n§0Surround my bed§0\n§0Sleep bring no hope to me§0\n§0In the soundest sleep they come §0\n§0And with they'r doeful imag'ry deepen the gloom\n\nSleep brings no streanght to me§0\n§0No power renewed to brave§0\n§0\n§0I only sail a wilder sea§0\n§0A darker wave§0\n§0Sleep brings no friend to me§0\n§0To sooth and aid to bear;§0\n§0They all gave on,\n\nhow scornfully§0\n§0And I despair§0\n§0Sleep brings no with to fret§0\n§0My harrased heart beneath;§0\n§0My only wish is to forget in endless sleep of death\n\nAnd this one is named§0\n§0The Changeling§0\n§0\n§0\n§0The Gallows tree is straight and tall§0\n§0Save for every jutting limb...§0\n§0And from a spot across the road§0\n§0I watched the tortured legs of him\n\nWho dangled there...§0\n§0The hangman laughed§0\n§0So merry was the sight withal§0\n§0\n§0The hangman's daughter standing near§0\n§0Was lovley as a waterfall;§0\n§0Her yellow hair streamed over her§0\n§0Her symmetry was starkly limned...\n\nI loathed and loved her, and it seemed§0\n§0Her scarlet roses glowed and dimmed§0\n§0\n§0As my wild eyes upon her fed§0\n§0Her glance was free and bold, I tought§0\n§0Our tryst was secret when the dark§0\n§0Had fallen, where the corpse hang taut\n\nIn the red moon...§0\n§0The cursed babe§0\n§0Was hideous as hell, and we§0\n§0Shrieked as we knew the twisted face§0\n§0Of him who decked the gallows tree.\n\nThe poems were written is Aegis shorly after the rise of the undead written by The Prophet Rott, first prophet of Iblees and the undead", ""),
