9123456789123456789123456789123456789123456789123456789123456789123456789123456789123456789123456789123456789123456789123456789v\n\nabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzabcdefghijklmnopqrstu\n\nzyxwvutsrqponmlkjihgfedcbazyxwvutsrqponmlkjihgfedcbazyxwvutsrqponmlkjihgfedcbazyxwvutsrqponmlkjihgfedcbaYouAreGoingToDieHerezyxwvutsrqponmlkjihgfedcbazyxwvutsrqponmlkjihgfedcbazyxwvutsrqponmlkjihgfedcbazyxwvutsrqponmlkjihgfedcbazyxwvutsrqponmlkjihgfedcba\n\n假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字假借字\n\n•?((¯°·._.• ČĤĮČЌẸŇ  •._.·°¯))؟••?((¯°·._.• ČĤĮČЌẸŇ  •._.·°¯))؟••?((¯°·._.• ČĤĮČЌẸŇ  •._.·°¯))؟••?((¯°·._.• ČĤĮČЌẸŇ  •._.·°¯))؟••?((¯°·._.• ČĤĮČЌẸŇ  •._.·°¯))؟••?((¯°·._.• ČĤĮČЌẸŇ  •._.·°¯))؟••?((¯°·._.• ČĤĮČЌẸŇ  •._.·°¯))؟•", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Notes to trapping beasts~§0\n§0Large beasts may require elixirs. Bear traps commonly used with specific oil lathered on, depending on the beast. Oils slow down the beast, and cause harm. ONce the screams of it are heard, it's good to come and finish it off\n\nBoat rope commonly used for snares.§0\n§0Place snare where beast has left a trail, by nest or home. Depending on size. Rope inches from the ground, snare tied to tree size depending on the beast. Bait placed in snare so beast places head through loop.\n\nContinued on snare.§0\n§0The beast moves forth and is then choked. Tree bent over, rope tied to a tree and stick or log. Stick or log is placed under another stick or log on the ground. Stick or log is attached to loop. When beast tries to wrech free it is\n\nContinued on snares.§0\n§0It is then lifted into the air.§0\n§0Nets in traps.§0\n§0Nets have weights on the edges. Used on flying beasts normally. Two mounted men carry each side of the net, then toss it onto beast. Also can be tied between trees or dropped.\n\nNotes on pitfalls.§0\n§0Done to catch stray vodniks, slyzards, or reavers. Simple hole, deep and wide enough for creature. Disguise is key, need more surprise. Which means more injury. Weighted nets covered in leaves can disguise, thin leather with dirt-\n\nPitfalls continued.§0\n§0-With dirt will work. Beast steps ontop and just falls right down.§0\n§0Rope fun with a tree or three.§0\n§0Neck length to choke a man down. If beast is big enough, it can bring trees down on it.§0\n\nDeadfall notes.§0\n§0Three logs, and a large object. One log gets pulled, dropping the large object. Hydra known to understand the trap.\n\nFinding mages is easy, their fingers may twitch, they mumble, raise their arm, or tense up in some way. Eyes can even glow sometimes.\n\nHydra was defeated by putting blood on it's back. It devoured itself in a frenzy. Hydras can only be killed by their own doing.\n\nDetermining a monster. Descendant race with magic is generally not a monster. Only monsters who are inherently magical ornon-physical are fought with aurum. Trolls are a no. Undead are a yes, same with spectres. During possesions, aurum works wonders.\n\nWhen dealing with sentient beasts, it is easy to intimidate as long as you do not back down and eye the creature. Keep your sword producing violent moves and yelling could work. Yell only when you know it is safe.\n\nFatigue and lucidity. Keep reality tracks, as some monsters can alter reality. Count digits and that sort. Keep an item nobody knows of. In a dream state casted onto you, it should not be there. Always have eight hours sleep and don't work drunk.§0\n§0~~~~~~~~\n\nIce witch names.§0\n§0Liriel Rivers.§0\n§0Desc- Thin, blonde hair, and light blue eyes.§0\n§0Arelin.§0\n§0Desc-Thin, black hair, and brown eyes.§0\n§0Brunhydle§0\n§0Gabrielle- Supposed mother.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Name: Ariselle§0\n§0Age: 13§0\n§0Race: Pure Mali'Aheral§0\n§0Gender: Female§0\n§0Appearance: Blue eyes, blonde hair, light beige skinned, standing at 4'7 (11th of The Grand Harvest, 1533).§0\n§0Interests: Literature and Nature.§0\n§0Aspirations: To write and travel", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "-Social Etiquette-§0\n§0  As Mali'aheral, we intend on regarding ourselves as superior and liked minded while others remain less. It must be found in the ability of communication and 'respect' that we appear mutual with them. §0\n§0\n§0 ~By: Ciri'lla Riannon~\n\n  -Table of Contents-§0\n§0*Gestures 3-9§0\n§0*Verbal Intro 10-14\n\n       -Gestures-§0\n§0    It is usually seen by Mali'aheral within their own stone settlement to exchange waves, the more polite ones bows. When attempting to start conversation, resort to these two methods if sealing one's lips. Woman may cursty at the\n\nleast only if their dress will not reveal too much skin; it'd be detrimental to carry around the status of a harlot. Hand shakes, performed by many of the unwashed groups, are to be taken with caution. Predators may even lurk around and plagues spread\n\nthrough these minuscule exchanges. When offered one and wanting to decline, simply either:§0\n§0*Put on a glove§0\n§0*Simply back up casually, dimissing the whole notion naturally§0\n§0*Suggest you recently have been sick\n\nA big note to know is that at all times refrain from pointing. It isnt taken lightly most places of being extremely direct and the pointing gesture usually has a negative connotation to it whether rude or embarrasing. Our present Tilruir'mali, Riondil\n\nSeni, has even been at fault for this. Within Laureh'lin boundaries, he focused his attention on an uruk by elevating and spanning his staff towards him to question his origin. It ended up in a heated debate with the grotesque beast almost\n\nattempting to harm one of our kind. This example will be referenced more than once; it exhibits how those even highly of order are at fault. Lastly, when walking do not swing ones arms out like the midget dwarves attempting to compensate for their lacking\n\nspeed due to height and weight. It is better to hold them at your waist, either front or back, and keep them clasped together loosely to appear comfortable too.\n\n ~Verbal Exchanges~§0\n§0     Conversation is usually the most important component that determines one's impression to others. There are usually two approaches that can be taken whether it be culturally enriched or either quaint. There are multiple ways\n\nto say similar phrases in various languages. To greet someone, it can be said:§0\n§0*Elves: §0\n§0   Karin'ayla (morning and afternoon)§0\n§0   Ker'ayla (evening and night)§0\n§0*Uruks:§0\n§0   Ug§0\n§0*Kha:§0\n§0  Sa'vi\n\n*Others:§0\n§0   Greetings§0\n§0   Hello§0\n§0 §0\n§0Try to not say anything too casual such as 'Hi' or 'Aye' since it would only reflect badly upon oneself. Also refrain from any foul language at any point\n\nin time even if it appears it would make you fit in. These distasteful words get people dont make people look intelligent. And also if a child immediately asks what one of those select words mean, try to skip the subject onto something more appealing\n\nlike a flower or nearby butterfly.\n\n       -Demeanor-§0\n§0     Many people use words to communicate which is easiest. In some cases however, a simple sulking of the lips or spread of a red hue on someone's cheeks can signal a reaction from the other person. An expression of a person\n\nintuitively guides conversation. It allows people to gauge the emotional state and personality of others. In many cases, people dont want it to be obvious of what they feel. There are moments in time where it is justified to hide true feelings in order to\n\ncarry on the exchange or keep peace. It is important to remember when wanting to act as if pleasant to:§0\n§0*Prevent lips from sulking§0\n§0*Keep brows aligned horizontally§0\n§0*A cheery tone§0\n§0*Hold back facial tics§0\n\n*Withdraw sarcasm or any relative phrases that uncover your inner feelings§0\n§0\n§0People are also able to interpret a person due to their body positioning. Uruks tend to slump their shoulders and", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "5th of the Deep Cold,§0\n§01561§0\n§0I went to see my Uncles gravestone in Calradia, and i met a man which invited me to join a army just like my fathers fighting for the same cause so i joined to follow is foot steps. §0\n§0I got assigned a room with someone\n\nelse. He told me he was training to be a medic.§0\n§0which isnpired me to try and be has usefull. Oh and is name is Benkt Hungil.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Dedicancy started:§0\n§012th of The Deep Cold.§0\n§0\n§0Dedicant: Grimthae§0\n§0\n§0Teacher: §0\n§0Aeras Ibar'ker§0\n§0\n\nFirst task: §0\n§0The Aspect Cycle§0\n§0\n§0Find three items that each represent one of the aspects.§0\n§0\n§0*Aeras's signature*\n\nSecond task:§0\n§0The Hat Task§0\n§0\n§0Find things in nature and make a hat with them.§0\n§0\n§0*Aeras's signature*\n\n- Laureh'lin, Wood Elves-§0\n§0\n§0*Mithras Aegor Ardere-Sylvari's signature*§0\n§0\n§0Referred to my lecture as biased propaganda, most likely a voidslinger.§0\n§0\n§0*Maev Sira'yuln's signature*\n\nSeemed to not know how to answer, socially awkward but did listen to the entire thing.§0\n§0\n§0*Amras Yeager's signature*§0\n§0\n§0Agreed with the lecture, also lectured him about the Aspects and their purpose/goals.\n\n-Oren, Humans-§0\n§0\n§0*William Smith's signature*§0\n§0\n§0Said he felt neutral on the topic yet my lecture might have swayed his view.§0\n§0\n§0*Lerald Reno's signature*\n\nAgreed for the most part, seemed to be a man of strong faith.§0\n§0 §0\n§0*Xander Volaire's signature*§0\n§0\n§0Didn't seem to care or want to share his opinion but he did listen to the entire lecture.§0\n§0\n§0*Jon Ponder's signature*\n\nAn Amyas man yet he listened, I did not share why I lectured him because of the fear of being attacked, Amyas men tend to do that.§0\n§0\n§0-Uruguan, Dwarves-§0\n§0\n§0*Daivd Goldhand's signature*\n\nDidn't share his opinion, saw a customer and zipped off after the lecture had come to a close.§0\n§0\n§0*Bartlin Irongut's signature*§0\n§0\n§0Simply replied with \"eh\" at the end of the lecture and walked off, he did listen though.\n\n*Pilgrim Starbreaker's signature*§0\n§0\n§0Disagreed yet did listen to the entire lecture, was kind of plain and walked off right after signing and sharing his opinion.§0\n§0\n§0Ten signatures and their respective responses to the lecture have been\n\nrecorded above. §0\n§0\n§0*Aeras's signature*\n\nFourth task:§0\n§0The Staff Rite§0\n§0\n§0Plant a sapling, and from that after having followed the instructions make your staff.§0\n§0\n§0*Barry's Signature*", ""),
        StaticBook("Task From Verden", "Jallentime", "((X -425 / Z -331))\n\n*A simple map has been drawn over the page, leading to a specific point of interest somewhere on the continent*\n\nI would like for you to take Tony, the lakefish, back to his home at Highland lake. The lake has been having troubles recently with an invasive parasite, and Tony here is our solution. Also, please don't eat him.", ""),
        StaticBook("Enchanting", "§b[Ivae'Fenn Guard] Lady Nessa Anarion", "((I'm a tad bit too lazy to actually write anything in here, but this is a link to the \"book\" just type it in))\n\nhttp://tinyurl.com/oz9bvn8\n\nOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOO", ""),
        StaticBook("Fear and Misund.", "§bLillith Winterleaf", "Fear and Misunderstanding;\nThe Wihuns from a survivor's perspective.\n\n\nBy: Lillith Winterleaf, the Sequoia Druid\n\nI was taken from my home.\n\n\nIt was the middle of a perfectly normal day on Asulon, there was a light rain in the early hours that left the air clean and fresh, and the birds were singing happily outside my Inn. I ran an Inn about a '\n\nday's horse ride from Skravia. My husband had built a large Fortress there and I built my Inn within the upper courtyard next to the stables,  overhanging the cliffs above our farmlands. It was a popular stopover for travelers from\n\nSkravia heading out into the wilds to hunt, gather lumber, fish, or simply travelling. Some, including a few of my fellow Druids, and members of Skravia's royal family, enjoyed coming to my Inn for liesure, to get away and relax, so we were accustomed to\n\nhaving people stop by frequently.\n\nOn this day, the Inn was empty, which was unusual, but I was taking the opportunity to sweep and clean the inn, and as I began wiping down the bar,\n\na man came in holding a piece of paper and wearing a friendly smile. He came over and sat on a stool at the bar, ordering a mug of ale and placing a gold coin on the counter.  I gave him his ale and we began talking.\n\nHe asked about the Inn, if there was anyone else there, then if I knew a Druid named Lillith Whisperblade, me, and I was beginning to grow wary from the rigid way he was speaking, so I said she was not around. But he looked back at his paper,\n\nthen slid it over to me, it had my name, a physical description, and the word DRUID written on it, and he told me there would be 'no more games' and that I was coming with him.\n\nI backed away but I was trapped behind the counter, and he stood and came around, pulling out his blade. As I spoke to try and talk reason with him, he backhanded me, telling me to be silent and come with him,\n\nor else.  I refused, and he slashed at me suddenly, cutting off half my left ear.  I jumped over the counter and reached the door as one of the guards from Skravia came in for his regular drink.\n\nHe saw my ear and tried to defend me, but my attacker yelled after me as I ran, calling me a witch and stabbing my would-be rescuer, leaving him to bleed out as he caught me.\n\nHe knocked me out and I awoke in a cage, built on a raised platform in a room designed like a chapel, pews facing the cage. My attacker was next to me, laying out tools of torture on a small table. He introduced himself as Tyret Wolfgank Farfor,\n\nand asked if I knew why I was taken.  I knew the name, my friend and teacher, Jena, had been tortured by the same man before escaping him. The discussion was calling me a heretic and a witch because I wielded 'magic' as he called,\n\nand would not listen when I tried to explain the difference between nature's gift and void magics. He and his followers all cursed me and any other wielder of ant sort of powers, even the temple monks,\n\nof being the cause of Aegis' destruction, and that they would 'cleanse' us.\n\nThe torture began by slicing and peeling away small portions of skin on my arms and legs, then using a red hot poker to burn the wounds and cauterize them.\n\nHe took a small pocket blade and had one of his men grab my head and force me to look upwards as he pushed the knife through my lower left eyelid and behind my eye, seeking to pry it out intact, but his hand slipped\n\nand I saw a black line spread through my vision as his blade sliced through my eye, blinding it.\n\nHis hand slipped because of a commotion upstairs, he dropped the knife and left one of his younger followers to watch me while he\n\ndealt with it, and I later found out that it was my sister and the King and High King of Skravia with a few if their men. The guard at my inn had survived and reported what happened and me being called a witch,\n\nso they had little doubt who had taken me. The Wihuns had more men, but apparently Tyret said he had not taken their Druid, and they had never mentioned exactly who was taken, so they departed to get more men.\n\nWhile this all happened without my knowledge, the young man I had been left with had come beside me, telling me that all the pain could be over if I renounced my druidic path and joined their cause, that I wad too beautiful to suffer like this,\n\nand I should be with him, he would make them stop and protect me if I would only turn away from my mistakes. I looked him in the eyes and held my kindest smile as I told him my husband would come for me,\n\nand long after the Wihuns were made to answer for their crimes, I would live happily with my husband and our children as a Druid.\n\nThe young man frowned deeply at my response and walked over to the brazier,\n\ntaking a poker right from the heart of the coals, the tip white hot. He came back and held it in front of my face, I could feel the heat and I was terrified, but not nearly expecting what came next.\n\nHe lowered it towards my waist, telling me \"No, there will be no children for you, witch.\" then stabbing me just above the thigh, pressing it through my womb and moving it in circles, I screamed.\n\nI could feel the fire burning me from inside, and he poured a healing potion down my mouth and held his hand over it, making me choke it down through my screams to keep it from killing me,\n\nholding the hot iron within me for what seemed an eternity of pain, but must have been only a minute, then removed it right as the healing potion's effects wore off.\n\nI screamed and cried as Tyret returned down the stairs, asking what was wrong, the young man saying he had simply continued.  They continued on as before, my body going numb on me, my mind crying out for my husband.\n\nAfter another few hours of physical and mental torture, the base shook from a violent explosion, men screaming the front wall had been breached.  They closed their inner gates and grabbed their weapons as a second explosion shook us,\n\nmuch closer, sending rubble down the stairs, and I heard my husband shouting commands to his men as they rushed down the stairs. The fight held at the final gate for many minutes, but my husband and his men broke through\n\nand slew many, though Tyret escaped out a tunnel with many others.  I was released into my husband's arms, only managing a weak smile before finally succumbing to my agony, passing out.\n\nThe rest is history, I awoke at home, a temple monk beside my bed trying to mend me, but he could not, their powers were at the time greatly weakened, and he could not save my womb.\n\nI believe the Wihuns could have been a force of good, but they allowed fear, prejudice, and misunderstanding to rule their actions and became a force of great evil in the world, harming many innocents and few of the 'dark mages' they claimed to target.\n\nI did recently however meet the son of Tyret, and learned the group had disbanded upon his father's death, and he did not agree with the targeting of druids, among others.  I have lived with the injuries and mental\n\ntorment of that dark, horrible day for over a century, never able to bear another child, but blessed with my only son before that terrible day. I devoted myself to the druids, turning my past into my reason, my drive.\n\nI want to bring love and peace and balance back to the world, and let others see that any pain can be endured and still work towards a better tomorrow for everyone. Prejudice only divides and weakens\n\nus against the darkness that follows us from land to land, and now, more divided than ever, we stand prepared to fall for the last time if anything threatens our world with peril once more.\n\nDo not let anger and pain from the past ruin our chances to make this a better world for our children, our grandchildren. We must unite and set our differences aside so we may stand against the darkness and prevail,\n\nlest Iblees the Betrayer prevail over the descendants.", ""),
        StaticBook("House Greyhame", "§bJacob Chapel", "The History of House Greyhame\nInformation about House Greyhame\nHouse Greyhame is a newly formed family under Lord Edmund Horen. Their leader, Faramith Greyhame is the patriarch of the house and commander of the Fort Dunaf,\n\nsituated close to Petrus, the Capital of the Empire. With the two great architects, Eraborn Greyhame and Yahya Kuad'je, Fort Dunaf rose above a mountain. After construction finished the Greyhame family settled in their fort, together with Yahya.\n\nHistory of House Greyhame\nThe history of House Greyhame starts with Hawk Whitestorm. The Famous Ranger-Commander who lived around the time of the Phoenix Revolution. He defended the South of the Kingdom of Renatus together with House Lycia.\n\nLater he became Aede of King Ezekiel's realm. After the war Hawk settled in Renatus and married a woman named Helga Greyhame.\n\nTogether Hawk and Helga had one male child named Ulfric Greyhame. Ulfric took his mother’s name  due to his father’s orders. Hawk did not want to draw attention to himself and his son. He wanted to be a peaceful life for his son,\n\nalthough life of Ulfric Greyhame turned out to not be a peaceful one.\n\nAfter a few years, crisis struck Aegis. Iblees and his forces marched through the realm and exterminated nearly everything. The people of Aegis, however, managed to escape to other\n\nrealm named Asulon, Ulfric and Hawk being two of them.\n\nUlfric Greyhame was raised by his father and became a superb Ranger, knowing almost the whole continent of Asulon. He mastered the bow and the sword. His Ranger Regiments helped Godfrey Horen to\n\nbecome the King of Renatus. The most famous act of Ulfric Greyhame is saving a regiment that was surrounded by enemy forces in the woods near to the borders of the realm. Ulfric divided his Rangers into two equal groups, took the command of the first\n\nRanger battalion, and ordered the second battalion to climb up the trees. He also ordered them to give the signal by mocking jays. After that, the two separated ranger groups reached the battlefield where the allied regiment and enemy forces fought.\n\nUlfric's battalion started to rain fire upon the enemy forces, and meanwhile the second battalion attacked the enemy from the height of the trees. This act gave the allies time to retreat to a safe area. After that, Ulfric's forces surrounded the enemy,\n\nbut Ulfric's irregular Rangers could not handle a regular force. When Ulfric realized that, he commanded his forces to withdraw to the outside of the forest. Because of their knowledge of the terrain the rangers were able to quickly traverse the forest\n\nand reach the clearing. At that moment Ulfric gave his infamous order, \"Burn the woods\". Although Ulfric regretted his act, dozens of enemy forces burned in the forest and died screaming. Ulfric's reputation was harmed because of this decision.\n\nAfter the Unification of Humanity and the extermination of Asulon by floods and plagues, Ulfric Greyhame was too old to be a ranger. He married Carla Greyhame and Carla provided him with four healthy sons: Edgar, Ragnar, Aelle and Ecbert.\n\nThey moved into Anthos and established a town named Berdersberg which was located north of Tempum, the new Capital of Holy Oren Empire.\n\nThey lived happily in their peaceful village. Ragnar had a son named Eraborn, Aelle had a son named Harkat,\n\nEdgar had a son named Thorondir, and the last brother, Ecbert, had a son named Faramith. The four cousins were very close. Harkat was known by his combat skills, Eraborn was master of building and architecture, Thorondir was skilled at archery and was\n\nsincere and kind to the people of Oren, and Faramith was known by his knowledge of history and his utmost loyalty.\n\nFor a time they were split up, but after the chaos following the destruction of Anthos they were reunited in Athera and built a Fort\n\nnamed \"Dunaf\". House Greyhame was declared a Gentry House by Lord Edmund \"Blackadder\" Horen, and the cousins carry the legacy of Hawk Whitestorm and name “Greyhame” with pride.", ""),
        StaticBook("Not Poetry", "§bOtaehryn", "Death. Death is a very under appreciated part of nature, which some seem to fear. However, death is necessary for the cycle, for life blossoms each time a life is naturally taken. Let's take the deer for example. A deer may be felled by a wolf and on the\n\nsurface this may look like only the wolf is benefiting, but there's more to it than that. The wolf may drag the carcass to her pups, providing sustenance  to a family. The leftovers may be swarmed by ants or other small bugs in the coming days and will\n\nprovide nutrition to the earth. This isn't the end though, for everything that was aided by the death of that deer will go on to serve its own purpose before eventually dying as well. Thus the cycle continues.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Farmer", "§bWilhelm von Bran", "The Farmer\n            &\n              The Witch\n            OR\nThe Defeat of\n            The \n         Conjurer\n\nBy, \n   \n   Wilhem von Bran\n\nIndeed at once, a long time ago, there stood a village. Indeed, this village would be ordinary had it not been for the events that took place there, and for the people that witnessed.\n\nThis village was a small hamlet located deep and surrounded by the Waldenian forest in northern Athera.\n\nIn the bitter cold, the people survived on the food that they harvested, occasionally sending out parties to fish and trade, but in all, this hamlet was sleepy and still.\n\nUnaffected by the corrupting notions of dwarven industry, or manly battles. Truly, this town only had a handful of peasantry, a few houses a church, and it’s square.\n\nThough this tale concerns itself with none of that. For this tale is one of the forests, and the corrupting force that it brings.\n\nIn the village – named Ansbluch – lived a family named Vurteger. Though, this family was broken. The mother having died years prior from pneumonia, and the father set to rise their only daughter alone.\n\nThe father was a good friend of the Churchman; Father Alphonse was a finely educated man who was ordained in the short reign of Pius II and spent a number of months at the Monastery of Saint Tobias during the plague.\n\nReverend Alphonse and Herr Vurteger were good friends, and were crutches for each other at many times.\n\nAfter the death of Frau Vurteger, Erika Vurteger, the young daughter became slothful to her chores, spending her time in the forests. Rumored to be cursed, she reveled in the adventure.\n\nHer father tended the chickens and the potatoes of his farm, while carrying the burden of not knowing where his daughter had been.\n\nOne day, Erika did not return for supper, indeed this was most peculiar as Erika had just turned sixteen the day before. Her father had made a potato stew: her favorite.\n\nHerr Vurteger was worried, but as he prepared to go out and search, a terrible storm released itself upon the forest, and the hamlet. Unable to make his bearings, the farmer trudged to the church rectory and called upon Father Alphonse.\n\nSurprised to see his friend in such distress, the priest welcomed him in and sat him by the fire. In a short while, Herr Vurteger awoke, the sun was in the sky, and the storm subsided.\n\nHe had awoken by several knocks on the rectory door, and opened it to Frau Heiden; Frau Heiden had become worried when Father was absent from mass and had not seen him all morning.\n\nSeeing that his cloak and staff were absent from the vestibule, Herr Vurteger had come to the horrific conclusion that Father Alphonse had gone out to search for Erika.\n\nAlarmed by this, Frau Heiden rang the bells and the men of the town prepared a party to search the forest.\n\nFor the whole day and the whole night they searched, but alas, found neither priest nor girl.\n\nBut the morning after, a sick scene was found. Father Alphonse; slumped against the trunk of an elder spruce tree, dead. He was found nearly a whole nights travel from the village and at the foot of a hill.\n\nAs the posse prayed over the corpse, they heard the sound of foreign music and vain cheering. Herr Vurteger ran to the top of the hill at once and saw a small caravan of gypsy. The small wagon chain was camping by the river, it seemed.\n\nFilled with rage, Herr Vurteger ordered the men to abandon the body of his friend and to investigate the gypsy.\nAs the armed band of town folk approached the camp, a jolly brown skinned man emerged from a wagon.\n\n“A hello there my friends!” bellowed the man, a long pipe in his hand, smoke billowing from its stem and bowl. The man stood quite tall with a black goatee. A domineering figure to the farmers of Ansbluch.\n\n“Guten tag, herr gypsy! Ve come to qvestion you on your vhereabouts zese passed few days und your actions. Vhat is your name so zat I may know who I interrogate?” responded Herr Vurteger\n\n“Well I am-a Faiz Kharadeen-a! And who-a might you-a be? Why must I-a be interrogated?” retorted the gypsy, as his fellows began to gather at his side.\n\n“Herr Kharadeen, we found our priest, Fazher Alphonse, a gut und holy man, dead und murdered in ze vood over yonder hill. Vhile ve vould not vish to be so bold, ve feel ve must!”\n\nUpon hearing of death, the gypsy band gasped and dropped to their knees, facing east, and began speaking a foreign tongue. Alarmed by this, the farmers braced their pitchforks and farm weapons. The gypsy continued,\n\n“We certainly-a never killed any-a man! We are a peaceful people, you see? We are the gypsy’s who dance under the-a crescent moon! The ones who travel-a to bring cheer-a not death!”\n\nConvinced, Herr Vertuger turned “But did you see a girl, herr Kharadeen? Around 16? Blue eyed und blonde?”\nThe gypsy shook his head and returned to his camp as the farmers returned, to bring their priest home.\n\nUpon closer inspection, the priest’s body was branded with a fresh crescent, and died from what seemed to be pneumonia. The branded crescent was most odd, and led the townfolk to suspect foul play.\n\nA few days after, the funeral was held. Erika had yet to be found, and Herr Vurteger was feared to have gotten the same fate as Father Alphonse.\n\nDepressed and overcome with grief, Herr Vurteger made the night’s journey back to the gypsy camp to question them again. Fearing they might have gone, he was relieved to hear the same songs and joyful dancing he had heard prior.\n\nUpon seeing the approaching farmer, the same gypsy went out again to greet him \n“Welcome-a back my friend! I hope we-a meet under better-a cir-“\n\nThe gypsy was swiftly interrupted as Herr Vurteger grabbed the collar of his tunic and backed him up against the outside of a wagon, dropping his pipe in the process.\n\n“Vhere ist mein daughter und vhat did you do to mein freund, Fazher Alphonse!” He cried out, pressing the dark man roughly against the wagon.\n\n“My friend! Aha. I assure you, We did not know anything, it was not us!” The gypsy felt pained, and was shocked when he felt a sharp jab against his stomach.\n\nThe farmer had procured a butchers knife and pressed it against his torso before raising it to the man’s chin. “Speak! Or I vill cut off your precious beard vith zis dagger und open your skin so ze air can taste your blood!”\n\n“It…It was the queen! The Lady of the forest! She came to us months ago beckoning us to be her subjects! She branded us with her banner-a symbol! The crescent! Alas, that horrid figure!”\n\nThe gypsy lifted his tunic to reveal the same marking as was found on the priest, only older. At this confession, the farmer released the gypsy and sheathed his knife.\n\n“Explain zis qveen or lady.”\n\n“In truth my friend, she is no lady she is a phantom. Perhaps a queen from some by gone age, but now just a terror. She comes to us with tasks, like delivering dirt and rock, and water. We were too scared to refuse!\"\n\nThe gypsy wagon tread slowly, though it was morning, it might as well have been night, and the horses were easily spooked.\nHerr Vertuger and several armed gypsies sat in the back and continued to plan.\n\n“So she resides in a tower, ja? Und it is never her zat comes down but vone of us goes up? Vell zis is easy! I vill go in to carry ze bundles, surprise her und you come in vhile I keep her busy und support me!”\n\nThe gypsy nodded “This-a sounds like it may work. We can only-a pray.” When the time came, the wagon grinded to a halt and the farmer adorned a thick hooded cloak and descended from the wagon with a few bundles.\n\nUnbeknownst to the Lady, the farmer had two scimitars belonging to the gypsy in his bundles. As he peered out from under his hood, he saw a crumbling tower, darkened and aged with time.\n\nNo one greeting him, the gypsy beckoned him forward and the farmer went up the ancient stairs and into the tower.\n\nHaving seen cracks of light at the top of the tower, the farmer trudged up the spiral staircase until he finally arrived at the door of the hall.\n\nAfter he had stood there for a short while, the thick oaken door swung open to reveal a brightly lit chamber, and an avanite throne on the far side.\n\n“Ah, so you have arrived, set se bun-“ the phantom voice spoke from the throne, but was interrupted by the farmer removing his cloak and brandishing his swords.\n\nHowever, when he looked up, he was shocked to see his daughter standing with not a phantom, but her mother, and his wife, Frau Vertuger.\n\nThe lady, dressed all in white, and looking much younger than she ought to stood from the throne and watched as the small band of gypsies scrambled through the doorway with arms of their own. Herr Vertuger, however, was too shocked for words.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Great War", "Saviordude", "The Myth of the Great War\n-------------------Thousands of years ago a terrible war broke out between two mighty guilds of mages - one good and one evil - that controlled great empires. Mages on both sides vowed to fight until they were utterly triumphant,\n\nseeking to purge their rivals from the earth. With fearsome magic and drake armies they battled for centuries, neither side winning a final victory.\nIn the proces of their warfare. the mages wrought vast devastation on the world. Forests caught fire and\n\nblazed till fone, islands sank into the sea, and entire races became extinct. Eventually the Aspects awakened from their slumber to witness the savage confict. Shocked by the destrucion, the Godess sent a vision to a single elf; the woman who would become\n\nthe first Arch Druid, Through the vision, this chosen figure saw that she must found a Druidic Order to preserver the fragile remains of her worlds ecology. With the guidance of the Aspects, the order grew in strength until it finally had the power to\n\nintervene in the mages's war. The force of young Druids pooled their powers and together vanquished the members of both battling guilds, transforming the combatants into innocent wild beasts.\nOnce the former mages - no longer caring about good\n\nversus evil - slither, bounded, loped and crwaled off into their ruined habitats the Order began to heal the world. Since then, the Druidic Order continually works to prevent such destruction from ever occuring again. Druids pledge to make sure the wars\n\nof good and evil no longer mar the precious earth. But the order also has bitter enemies in the ancient remnants of the guilds of warring mages; those good and evil mages who luckily escaped the fate of their fellows. Each guild claims it had been on the\n\nverge of victory and would have won, had the Druids not interfered.\n\n-Transcribed by Ol' Treehugger", ""),
        StaticBook("Journel Part 1", "Blundermore", "*The Journel is Rustic and worn, it smells foul with many stains that are both known and... unknown. The pages are torn and ruined somewhat and the picture of a skull dots the front of the book.*\n\n-A faded and worn date can be seen here-\n\n\"FOOLISH MORTAL. YOU HAVE RECOVERED THE JOURNEL OF BERTDILE! MASTER MAGNUS OF THE NINE NETHERS! COMMANDER OF THE DEAD (and other things) SORCERER SUPREME AND FEARED BY ALL WHO KNOWS MY NAME. But for you mortal. You, can call me Bert.\n\nFear what you may discover in these pages! I'm warning you! Dark evil things that will toy with your very mind lie within! Don't turn that page. I'm warning you. No, don't do it, I'm an evil maniac! ...\n\nY'know what, fine, be that way, I'm going to spare you the torment of reading my journel by ripping it off from the rest of the book. Bwhaha! *You can see the last \"Bwhaha!\" was scribbled out* Sorry, that sometimes happens... But know this mortal! Dare\n\nnot find the rest of my journel! For your mind will go mad indeed for what you may uncover! This is your one and final warning!\n\nFEAR ME\n\\/\\/\\/\\/\\/\\/\\/\\/\\/\\*You can see that the rest of the Journel has been torn from here*", ""),
        StaticBook("w", "§bEoin Andraphel", "stephensj is a fag", ""),
        StaticBook("Intro To Aspects", "§bTristanm1", "Introduction to the Aspects\n\nBy Sunshine Druid Verden\n\nThe Mother of the Harvest, the Goddess of Change, the Green Lady. Cerridwen has many names, but all of them stem from her warm and caring demeanor toward the world. She is the matron Aspect of farmers and gardeners everywhere, and it is\n\nher divine radiance that blesses the crops and flora upon the world. The fragile, but beautiful flowers and the delicious fruits of the land are her domain. The seasons are gifts from her.\n\nThe Father of the Hunt, the King of Forests, the Green Man. Cernunnos also has many names, but all of them reflect upon who this Aspect truly is; a hunter. It was he who brought about the existence of wolves and bears -- of tigers and hawks. The predators\n\nof the land are his apprentices, and the thorny plants his traps. He is the patron Aspect of huntsmen and warriors everywhere, and it is his divine strength that blesses the worthy. The steadfastness of nature comes from him.\n\nThrough their grace, the world and all who dwell upon it came into existence. What we refer to as \"Nature\" is the combined efforts of these two infinitely powerful beings, and it was them that saved the sentient mortal races from utter destruction during\n\nthe Great War. It is our duty as their children to respect and aid Nature whenever possible, and by doing so give praise to their greatness.", ""),
        StaticBook("Serpentions Note", "§bSerpention", "Your father, Luthar, has said we should not be together. I find it not to be so for you are the beautiful intellegent aengul of my eye but yet it seems not to be. I wish that we could be in a romance but your father will kill me. I might just go to him\n\nanyway...but thank you for leading me astray from a dark path.\n\n~Serpention, the necromancer expertiment to-be\n\nP.S. I still love you.", ""),
        StaticBook("Game of Minds C1", "§bSofetios Jayamen", "A Game of Minds\n\n             ~By Sofetios\n                  Jayamen\n\n      Chapter One\n        Awakening\n\nPulsing. That is all I felt as I started to see again. Everything was a hazy black, even though my eyes were open. Another pulse happened inside my head. Things were beginning to become clearer.\n\nI could make out that I was in a small stone room… and there was something else in here with me. \n\n“Finally awake are we?” said a gruff voice.\n\n I could now see moderately well and saw a person sitting across from me looking straight at me. I coughed and said slowly, \n\n“Where am I?” My head still throbbing though getting better. The person then said,\n\n“You’re in the holding chamber, along with me.”\n\n“Holding chamber?” I said. “Holding chamber for what?”\n\nAt that moment there was a slight creak to the right, as a part of the wall opened up like a door.\n\nThe person got up and came to me, holding out a hand. He said,\n\n“We had better get going. They don’t like it when we stall.”\n\n I grasped his hand and he hoisted me to my feet. I nearly fell over but he helped me catch myself.\n\n I looked upwards and far above saw there was an opening to the sky.   I said,\n\n“Who are they?” The Person helped walk me toward the door.\n\n“They call themselves…The Followers of Truth.” He said.\n\nWe got to the door and opened it. A burst of cold air came in.  In the room was only a table that had frost on it, A fireplace to the right that had a metal pan on the fire with what appeared to be a steak on it,\n\na small vase with a small top for an opening was on the floor with a lid next to it, a poufy cushioned chair was next to the fireplace, and there was also another  fireplace at the far back of the room.\n\n“Great…”  Said the person. “ A Puzzle.” He walked with me into the room. As we got in the door slammed behind us. “Can you stand now?” \n\nI nodded slightly as I took my own weight back.\n\n “Good. My name is Jed. And you are?”\n\n“James.” I said.  Suddenly  I felt an overwhelming hunger as I smelled the smell of cooked steak.\n\nI looked to the steak and started to walk over, but as I did Jed caught my arm. “What?” I said.\n\n“The thing you should know about this place, is that it’s a death trap.” Said Jed with a serious face.\n\nHe lifted his sleeve and there was a severe gash about a foot long. “That.” He said pointing to it, “Is what happens when you aren’t careful.”  I nodded, swallowing. “We had best get to solving this. Before the cold kills us.”\n\n I hadn’t noticed but my toes were starting to get numb. \n\n“What should we be looking for?” I said. \n\n“A key to get back into the room we came in.” Said Jed.\n\n“What!?” I said, “We were just in there?” \n\n“Trust me James.” Said the man. “We are better off in here for the moment. What you need to understand is that The Followers of Truth are ruthless.\n\nIf you don’t do what they want you to, they will make you do it, one way or another. Put your ear to the door.”\n\nI went to the door and put my ear on it like he said to, There were clicking sounds in the room. Like a giant insect walking in there.\n\n“Hear that?” Said Jed. “That’s the Grimmer.”  I got my head off the door and said. “The Grimmer?”\n\n“Yes.” Said Jed. “A fearsome thing, they release it through the ceiling in the room to make sure we are out.” Then a thought occurred to me. I said,\n\n“How do they know we are still alive?” Jed Shrugged the said,\n\n“Well If the Grimmer doesn’t come back early I guess that means we are still alive. Either way we better find the key to the door. Before they push us to find it. Now where is that key…”\n\n“Maybe it’s in the chair?” I said. “Seems poufy enough to hide it.”\n\n“Or to hide something else.” Said Jed. “Always test things.”  Jed took a glove off and went to the couch. I followed but at a distance.\n\n Jed, a few feet from the couch tossed it on the Seat. \n\nSuddenly there was a click. The cushion rose at an incredible speed to the ceiling, crushing the glove, then lowered back to position.\n\n The cushion didn’t even flatten, like it was rock hard.  The glove fell on the floor.  Jed looked to me, nodded, and said.\n\n“See?”  As he picked up and put his glove back on. “Never touch things directly.”\n\nI took off my own glove went to the jar on the floor. \n\n I kind of whacked it with my glove. It just knocked over… and something clanked inside it. Jed looked to me and said “Be careful.”\n\nI put my glove back on, picked up the jar and looked in the opening. The key was inside it. \n\n“The key is in here!” I said. I turned the jar upside-down and the key came out… a little. It was stuck.\n\nJed let out a small grunt. “They never make it that easy.”   Suddenly there was a trickling and hissing sound coming from the right fireplace.  There was a small amount of water coming above the fireplace.  Jed cursed under his breath.\n\n I then panicked. I tried to smash the vase against the floor.  “Stop!” Said Jed. The vase landed on the ground in my hand with a large thump, but didn’t break.\n\n“Careful you idiot!” That could have been filled with glass as well as the key.\n\n “Well what are we supposed to do?” I said in a panicked voice. Jed then said, “Not panic. That’s what they want us to do, we need to think.”\n\nI forced myself to relax.  Jed then said after a moment,\n\n“I’ve got it! Put it on the cushion.”  I rushed over and tossed it on the cushion. The Cushion rose again with great speed and there was a loud clunk.\n\n The cushion went back down and the vase, still whole, rolled off it. Jed cursed again. “So it’s unbreakable.” Jed then angrily threw his glove at the frosted table. As it hit the side, it stuck and turned frosted too.  I came to a sudden realization.\n\n“The water!” I said.\n\n“Yes I know.” Said Jed looking down at the ground, which had water rising a half inch above it.\n\n“No I mean-“ I didn’t explain. I ran to the jar, picked it up and the lid.\n\n I ran to the fireplace that was now extinguished, and filled it with water, then screwed on the lid. I turned to Jed who looked at me and looked like he just realized what I was doing and nodded. I Hurried to the table and tossed the jar at the side.\n\n As it made impact it stuck, frosted, then in a few short seconds the jar exploded. Shards went everywhere. Startled, I yelled and was knocked back on my backside.  A few cut me on my arms and legs from the jar shards.   “Jed!” are you alright?”\n\n I heard a few curses from the back of the room.\n\n“I’m fine.” Said Jed. ”I should have seen that coming.”  I saw that there was a solid bit of ice in the shape of a vase on the floor near the table, and inside was the key.\n\n I picked it up and headed to the extinguished fireplace, picking up the pan.  I didn’t head to the chair to smash the ice because I didn’t want to risk breaking the key.\n\nBy now the water was an inch and a half deep and I couldn’t feel from my ankle down. I ran carefully to the other fireplace, which was luckily a bit raised off the ground, but not much.\n\n I put the pan over the fire and the ice on top of it. The pan was about 2 inches deep. Jed came behind me. After about a minute, the pan was full of water, but the ice wasn’t melted all the way.\n\nI took the ice in one hand and tossed the water in the pan on the ground with the other, then put the ice back on the pan and started melting it again.\n\nAfter another minute the key was free. I took it and went to the door where Jed was now with his ear on the door. He took his head off the door and said,\n\n“The Grimmer isn’t there anymore.”\n\n  I unlocked it and opened it. The Grimmer wasn’t in there. I went into the room and Jed followed me, but suddenly the door closed, there was a hissing sound, like air through a tight space. I started coughing, as if the air was bad. So did Jed.\n\nThen everything went black.\n\nEnd of Chapter One\n\nThank you for taking the time to read this book! I'll be writing more in the future!\n\nIf you like my writing, please send me a bird or talk to me in person! \n\n~Sofetios Jayamen\n((Sofetios))", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "I have some bad news as well. I talked with Illern about us three meeting to discuss the surrogacy, and she does not like the idea of you being the one.", ""),
