        StaticBook("Tristan The Bard", "§bAepholis", "The soft breeze touches Tristan's skin, as he wakes up in his lower class bed, small windowcrack open. Tristan gets swiftly up on his feet, as he'd grab his favorite turquise blue shirt, buttoning up, as he fetches his black pair of trousers.\n\nHe'd grab his colorful royal blue coat from a hanging rack, along with his feather trilby, heading to the local inn, flute slinged over his right shoulder.  As he heads to the local inn of Aldersberg, whistling a small tune, a nice smirk on his face.\n\nAs he enters, the inn seems crowdy, the brightness moody and candles lighting the place. He nods firmly to Ciera, aswell as two other lovely ladies. He orders a glass of red wine from Ciera, handing her the sufficient minas.\n\nHe slowly gulps it down, as he brings the flute up to his puffy lips, a soft and gentle melody starting to play. Three burly mates arch a brow at Tristan, shaking their heads disapprovingly.\nOne raises, walking forward to Tristan.\n\nTristan nods to the lad, as his flute is suddenly pulled away from his lips, drool connecting between. Tristan frowns, as he'd hear a crack, the man breaking the pipe in half. A elegant man, puffing on his pipe raises, heading to the scene.\n\nHe'd nod to the burly man, of the name Tor, as he'd put a hand gently on Tristan's shoulders, shaking his head, grip on his gold-tipped cane tightening slightly, as he'd give Tor a hateful and mean look, before walking away with Tristan.\n\nTor grumbles, sending a kick for the back of the elegant man's knee, causing him to fall facefront, making quite the ruckus. The man chuckles, as he'd raise, dusting dust off his fine clothing, tilting his head at Tor, smirking widely.\n\nTristan gulps worriedly, as he'd step a bit back, glancing between the two. The elegant man turns again, as Tor sends a punch for the back of his head, grabbing a bottle from a table, breaking it on the edge of the table itself.\n\nThe elegant man is hit in the back of the head with a fist, as he'd swiftly turn on his heel and send the gold-part of the cane into Tor's jaw, a loud crack being heard. Tor cringes exremely, falling to the ground, as the elegant man turns yet again.\n\nThe two burly chaps raise from their seats, one unsheathing an axe, while the other draws a dagger. The elegant man frowns slightly, as this will be a challenge. He notes that a few of the customers have left the establishment.\n\nHe glances at Ciera, that remains behind the counter, a slight frown on her small face. He'd take a step back, as the axe wielding mate charges at him, putting a foot infront of him, causing him to fall onto a table.\n\nThe lad holding the dagger approaches slowly, sending a slash for the elegant man's chest, as the man would send his cane onto the dagger wielder's temple. The dagger chap falls down to the ground, unconsious, as the elegant man turns.\n\nThe axe wielding chap charges, as he'd send a punch for the elegant man's nose. The elegant chap is punched, stumbling back, before sending the tip of his cane into the attacker's adam's apple, causing him repeated coughing, before he'd fall down, dead.\n\nThe elegant man turns to Tristan, before he'd glance at all the three defeated men, darting his eyes at Ciera, letting out a simple \"Apologies.\" as he'd grasp onto the collar of each one of them, throwing them out. Tristan grins happily at the elegant lad\n\nThe elegant man nods to Tristan, glancing down at the flute, frowning slightly, before he'd nod to Ciera and Tristan, heading out by himself, shutting the door, of course.\n\n-This novel is dedicated to the innkeeper herself, Ciera Lefevre. May she enjoy the reading of this fiction, and give me an honest opinion.\n\n-Aepholis Celadithel.\n\n*An image of a lurky, slender and tall blonde-haired elf, with orange eyes, covers this page, the inn being his surroundings.*", ""),
        StaticBook("Sunshine Letter", "§bGrand Druid Verden", "Dear Arch Druid Leyun,\n\n   Judging by the large knot on the side of my head and the missing leg of my traveling companion; the Black Hand has returned. They're stalking the roads within Human borders and I have reason to believe they may be getting near\n\nRespiren's grove. Be wary along the roads and spread awareness among the Malinor grove so that our brothers might remain safe.\n\n\n\n        Aspects be with you all,\n                                        Verden", ""),
        StaticBook("Misfortune Hunt", "§bThe Misfortune Teller", "Giant Flying Sheep\n\n\nMisfortune Hunt 2\n\nDown the road, towards the holy white Stag that rides off into the grand forest of all.\n\nRun into the unknown ruins in front of the tree waterfalls.\n\nClimb, Climb up the tall earth, straight forward into the forest of mossy stone hills.\n\nStraight you go.\n\nFollow the path that does not lead to Oakhearth but into the opposite direction.\n\nTwo mossy hills you meet together with one torch on the road, move through the hills and down to the rivers of tall green plants.\n\nStraight, straight you go.\n\nThe Holy White Tree with the colorfull crystals meets your curious eyes.\n\nIgnore the Tree but look towards the Tower of old.\n\nThe Stick into the sky.\n\n\n\n\n\nGiant Flying Sheep!", ""),
        StaticBook("Thirty to One", "§b\"Caroline Hightower\"", "C. Hightower:\n\"Thirty to One\"\n\nKey of A-dur | A maj\n\nSharp notes:\n\nF, C, G.\n\nTonic: A\nSub-Dominant: D\nDominant: E\n\nC#     E         D    C#\nBap- tised  in    batt-\n\nD      |   D         E      D\nle         thir-  ty     to\n\nF#- - -\none- - -\n\nRepeat F# / D / A / E\nuntil otherwise instructed.\n\n| - So silent before the storm - -\n\n| - Awaiting command\n\n| - A few has been\n| chosen to stand - -\nas | one outnumbered\nby | far - - -\n\n| - The orders from high command - -\n\n| - \"Fight back hold your | ground\" - - - \n\n| In early Snow's Mai | den it came - - a | war\nunkown to the | world - - -\n\n| - No army may | enter that land - - - that | is protected by\nD      C#      B\nO-    ren's hand\n\n| - Unless you are\n| thirty to one - your\n| force - will soon be un | done - - -\n\n        F\nun-  done - - -\n\n| Bap-tised in bat-tle\n| Thir-ty to one - \n| Victorious Oren\n| Just like al-ways\n\n| Sol-diers from Or-en\n| Se-cond to none -\n| Wrath of the Teutons\n| Brought - to a ha - - - lt.\n\n# Continue the chord sequence with new lyrics.\n\nThe third of Snow's Maiden it starts. Attacks Aesterwald.\n\nA barrage of arrows and fire. Stand fast. The city will hold.\n\nThe emperor has pledged his life. \"I'll face my fate here.\"\n\nThe sound of barbarians so fierce like thunder up high.\n\nSo come bring on all that you've got.\n\nUndead, magic and ba-lli-stas.\n\nUnless you are thirty to one, your lives will soon be undone. Undone.\n\n*** CHORUS. ALL SHALL SING. ***\n\nBap-tised in battle\nThir-ty to one\nVictorious Oren\nJust like always\n\nSoldiers from Oren\nSecond to none\nWrath of the Teutons\nBrought to a halt\n\n*** SING ONCE MORE, PLAYING OCTAVES. ***\n\n*** CODA: ***\n\nCadential 6-4\nChord V 2nd inversion\nChord I (sustain)", ""),
        StaticBook("You are", "§bArthur Caulfield", "You are a tumour, taking me out of a perfect life. \n\nYou are a fire, forcing me out of my home.\n\nYou are a thief, stealing all of my fun and joy. \n\nYou are a killer, killing the immersion.\n\nYou are a fly, around my food. \n\nYou are a parasite, leeching onto my head.\n \nYou are my only clothes, disgusting and ripped.\n\nYou are a sword through my still beating heart.\n\nYou are an ass, dirty and unwiped. \n\nYou are a spider, driving everyone away.\n\nYou are a current, desperate to pull everyone down to your level.\n\nYou are a guard, closing down my party. \nYou are an aggressive orc, capable of only one emotion.\n\nYou are porridge, bland and dull.\n\nYou are the crayon nobody uses.\n\nYou are a made up language or fictional history. \n\nYou are a lump in a comfortable pillow.\n\nYou are an obscenity shouted at a deaf man.\n\nYou are a greedy beggar at my door all day.\n\nYou are the corpse of a king who demands a temple for a resting place. \n\nYou are a pot with no soil that demands a flower.\n\nYou are the loose string that undoes a shirt.\n\nYou are the thunder I hear while trying to sleep. \n\nYou are the crack in a large expensive window.\n\nYou are the loose screw in a banks vault door.\n\nYou are a spot on a fresh piece of paper. \n\nYou are a creaky step in a silent house.\n\nYou are a moth among butterflies.\n\nYou are water extinguishing a torch.\n\nYou are dried up ink.\n\nYou are a chicken without a head.\n\nYou are wilted soil in a draught.\n\nYou are a church without a religion.\n\nYou are.", ""),
        StaticBook("Tales of Alchemy", "§bKardel the Elder", "We will begin with one of\nthe most rudimentary and used element, Fire. Fire can represent many a feeling, action, and state because of this, it is often one of the most useful elements of the Alchemist's Arsenal. Fire represents rage, anger, §§hatred\n\n*The word Hatred is circled in blood* and gusto *The word gusto is also circled in blood, the rest of the pages are torn out, and only a note lies on a surviving page over the text. djhsbgkdjskdgjhskjfghksjdhgksjhgksjfhgksjfhgksjfhgksjfhgksj\n\nakdjhsdkgjhskfjghskfjghskjfhgksjfhgksjfhgksfjhgksjFire is passion; it is hatred and gusto. . .these are the emotions i feel torwards you\n\n-Your /son/ \nskjdghlajfdhgldkfjhgkdjfhgalkdjfhglkjdfhglskjdglksjdflhgksjdfhglksjdlgksjdglkjsdhflgkjshfdglkj", ""),
        StaticBook("Jayas Journey C1", "§bSofetios Jayamen", "Jaya's Journey\n\n             ~By Sofetios\n                  Jayamen\n\n((This is a book I'm actually writing in real life. I really need motivation to continue, so please let me know what you think.))\n\n     Chapter One\n\n   A Walk in the Woods\n\n“Mom!” I yelled as I went out the back door. “I’m going out to the woods!”\n\n“Fine Honey!” My mom yelled back. “Just be back before dusk ok?”\n\n“Sure Mom!” I answered. I put on my sword, closed the back door, and headed out. Knowing exactly where I was going as I ventured more into the woods. As I walked I couldn’t help but notice how nice everything was today.\n\nFrom the glistening emerald green leaves leaving a slight greenish tint on the ground, to the random scuttles and noises coming from the woods, it just seemed like it was going to be a good day. I approached the usual spot I hung out at near dusk.\n\nA small opening with a flat rock and a small clear pool in the middle. It wasn’t much but I always thought better here. I laid down on the rock looking up into the trees and begin to wander off in my head.\n\nI was about to dose off when I heard something. I sat up quickly. It wasn’t a normal sound. It sounded like the song that Linink played on the piano at the Festival, only it was on a flute. Cautiously, I got up and went in the direction of the sound.\n\nIt got louder and louder as I went along through the trees and bushes until I reached another larger clearing. I looked amazingly at what I saw. There was a Statue in the middle of the clearing with multiple statues around the clearing.\n\nThey all looked just like the one in the middle of town, except in different poses. The one in the middle had his arms outstretched in front of him.\n\nI went closer and saw that in it’s his hands outstretched was a Bow, Arrows, A sword, a dagger, and a scroll. Cautiously I went up closer to the statue and was about to take the scroll when suddenly the music stopped and a voice said,\n\n“Hey! Boy! What are you doing here” Startled I jumped back and turned to face an old man.\n\n“I’m sorry!” I said shocked. “I was just going to look at it! I swear!” The Old man had a frown on but then smiled and said.\n\n“Well why didn’t you say so? Go ahead and take a look.”\n\n“Wait… what?” I said surprised.\n\n“Go on, take a look.” Said the old man again still smiling.\n\n“Um… ok…” I said confused, turning to face the statue. I slowly took the scroll and opened it. Written on it was… nothing.  I frowned and looked back but the old man was gone. Only in his place was a flute.\n\nI scratched my head, went to it, and picked it up. As I touched it I heard the music start again but this time, it was in my head. Shocked again I dropped the flute, the scroll, and the music stopped.\n\nCuriously I picked up the flute and tried to play the notes I had heard. I had played the flute many times before but this time felt different somehow. As I finished playing the notes nothing seemed to happen.\n\nI wondered what I was expecting to happen. Once I waited about a minute I bent down and picked up the scroll. Only this time there was something written on it. Curiously I examined the scroll.\n\n It had a few lines of music notes on it and the title of it was called “Song of the Rain”. The music notes where different from the ones I had just played, so I began to play these ones.\n\nAs I played it sounded like a stormy night at sea. When I completed it, nothing seemed to happen. After a minute I walked to the statue, wondering if it would be a good idea to take the sword. Suddenly a Voice behind me said,\n\n“Would you look at that! You’re a natural at the flute.”\n\nShocked, I turned to see the old man again, this time he had a bird on his shoulder. He had the same smile on his face. \n“Where do you keep coming from?” I said.\n\nSeeming to ignore the question the old man looked at the weapons the statue had.\n\n“I see you like those weapons?” Said the old man. \n\n“Yea. They look amazing.” I said turning to the statue.\n\n Then looked at the face and said,” But… where they his?”\n\n“What do you mean?” Said the old man. “You’re the one wearing them.”\n\n“What do you mean?” I said turning to the old man again.\n\nBut he was gone. I turned back to the statue that had the weapons but they were gone. Suddenly I saw something on myself. I looked down and there was a sword on my belt, but it wasn’t my sword, it was the statues.\n\nI looked to the other side of my belt and there was the dagger. I slowly reached up and touched my shoulder. As I did I felt the feathers of an arrow and the string of a bow.\n\n I then realized it was dusk, my mom would be annoyed if I wasn’t home soon. I took off the weapons, put them back on the statues hands, and began to walk home.\n\n As I walked home I noticed the air began to feel denser and the wind began to pick up a little bit. Suddenly. I felt a raindrop. I looked up and one hit me on my forehead. I started running as fast as I could toward the house.\n\nAs I got in the back door my mom was standing there with her arms crossed. \n\n“Where have you been?” She said sternly. “You’re late and your soaked head to toe!”  All I could say was,\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\n“Jaya.” Said my mom in a warning voice. \n\nI sighed and recounted the whole story about the old man, the weapons, the flute and the scroll. As I finished my mom looked pale.\n\n“Are you alright?” I said. She walked slowly to a kitchen chair and sat down.\n\n“Jaya.” My mom began. “I’m going to have you see Linink.”\n\nLinink was the best doctor around and also the leader of the adventurer’s guild, a guild that helps people and goes on quests. My friends Sofetios and Sofia go there.\n\n“Wait, why?” I said concerned. “Do you think I’m sick?”\n\n“No Jaya.” My mom said. “It’s about something else. I think it’s time you went to bed.” Confused and concerned, I went to bed.\n\nThank you for taking the time to read this book! I will be writing more in the future!\n\nIf you like my writing, please let me know by bird or in person!\n\n~Sofetios Jayamen\n((Sofetios))\n\nIf you were not sold this book by Sofetios Jayamen, it was done without his permission.", ""),
        StaticBook("Feyko's Journal", "§bFeyko Drei", "Day 1.\nI have left the city. Leaving uniform and friends behind. I will venture towards Alras, as I have heard that it is there were I may find the information I need to find the creature which has stolen all I have worked for.\n\nDay 2.\nI am currently seated outside of the walls of Alras, looking up to the sky. I have gained no information from Alras, but have from myself. Today I remembered seeing the creature enter the city from the direction of Norithel.\n\nDay 3. \nI have traveled past Haelun'or and to Norithel, stopped by the water which meets its southern walls. Tomorrow I will find a way around the cliffs. I remember on my first scouting mission, I found a path that lead to swamp lands. This may work.\n\nDay 4.\nI have traveled back from Norithel and then through the path. I am writting this while sitting on top of a broken cart on the edge of the swamp. I will make preparations tonight, for my travel tomorrow.\n\n[!] What is written is damp and smudged.\n\nDay 5.\nI hate the swamp.\n\nDay 6.\nI have found the exit to the swamp, with no luck on my quest. I have found a human settlement. I traveled briefly through it, with little issue. I am writting this from inside of a large tower, to shield myself from the rain.\n\nDay 7.\nI have reached water yet again. At our land's edge I have found a monastary and a large, glowing tree. Near me is a dense forest, with what appears to be a lot of spider activity.\nI am preparing to venture there tomorrow.\n\nDay 8.\nToday I found a tree.\nThis tree was not like any tree I have seen before. It was made completely out of metal, with a black something oozing out of the sides. It made the area around it metallic as well. I spent most of my day investigating this.\n\nDay 9.\nI am writing this from on top of a tomb. I have found the ruins of a gravesite. Bodies have been dug up and disturbed. I could here noises and clawing from some coffins. This place smells of death, and it feels as though life is not welcome here.\n\nDay 10. I have begun venturing back towards the city, to report my findings. On my way back, I found a small cave, with skeletons littered around, and a pile of leaves appearing to serve as a bed. I left before whatever made this their home returned.\n\nDay 11. \nI have returned to the city to give the Div'cruan this journal. I am leaving back to further investigate the gravesite and dwelling. I will not return, until I am sure that I have found, 'it'.\n\nI am alright thus far,\n-Feyko", ""),
        StaticBook("Poetry ((2))", "Sofetios", "An Uncommon Sight\n            and Courage\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n                            -By Sofetios\n\n        An Uncommon Sight\n\nI go about my day,\nDoing the same things as before,\nThe same old road,\nFrom my humble abode,\nNot realizing it is a bore.\n\nI walk ever onward,\nKnowing all my tasks by heart,\nA straight narrow trail,\nWith nothing new to avail,\nAll I do is my part.\n\nThen one day as I'm going past,\nA park once again,\nI see a boy,\nwithout even a toy,\nwho couldn't be past ten.\n\nThe boy was doing something abnormal,\nsomething I hadn't seen before,\nHe was looking in the sky,\nNot even blinking an eye,\nthey didn't even seem sore.\n\nI wondered if there was a hawk,\nOr something in the sky,\nI didn't hear a thing,\nOnly the birds that always sing,\nSo I did wonder why.\n\nI looked over,\nto the boy sitting by,\nHe was being bullied by his peers,\nAnd was starting to go in tears,\nJust for looking in the sky.\n\nI was about to go over,\nTo try to help if I could,\nbut the parents went first,\nBefore I feared the worst,\nTo help as they should.\n\nI walked on my way home,\nWondering why what I saw came to pass,\nI had a thought why,\nso I looked to the sky,\nand saw it in it's great mass,\n\nAs I looked to the sky,\nI was transfixed,\nI hadn't done this in a while,\nAnd it did make me smile,\nAs past and current memories mixed.\n\nI saw the vast expanse,\nIn all its mystery,\nI watched the clouds in the sky,\nAs they changed shape and passed by,\nAs they had for all of history.\n\nSuddenly someone laughed to my left,\nEven thought it was mild,\nSomeone looking at me,\nLaughing at what I appeared to be,\nJust as the others had done to the child.\n\n                Courage\n\nIt is a thing you use\nWhen you can't go to the end,\nWhen all seems lost\nAnd you can't defend.\n\nYou use it as\nIt combines with your will,\nAll you need to do is\nLet it give its fill.\n\nThe choice is yours,\nGive in or go,\nThough in the end\nOnly you will know.\n\nSee with your spirit\nAnd not with fear,\nfor then you will know\nThat courage is here.\n\nThe End", ""),
        StaticBook("Moonlight Dance", "§bLucius Tython", "The sun submerged itself beneath the dimming salmon mountaintop to the sight of the harmonic moonlight begining its climb from the darkened earth. The air was amongst a thinning moisture that tingled the auromic smell of lingering fern. Perfectly placed\n\nfingers were present amongst the warmth of her grass pressed palm. Her teals eyes pierced the skin to which she glanced, enticing my very being. The charming aura surrounding such a very being pulled me closer to her ever so slightly. Returning the gaze I\n\npulled my lips closer to hers, watching the dimming sight of her illuminated lips under the moonlight as I found my eyes closing furthering my body towards hers. The warming sensation of her lips became that of a snug hearth under a wood burning fire.\n\nMy mind soon became detained in cadence of her lips on mine. A joyful ache overtook me as her arms found home upon my shoulders wrapping themselves around my neck. The slight tug of her arms took me onto the ground alongside her torso. The swing of her\n\naffection brought me amongst a trance of cherishment. The trance was broken by an unevitable pulling from my lips. My adorning eyes opened in a graceful haste to look upon a warming smile within her eyes. Almost as if it was a pleasant virus, I too felt a\n\nsmile manifesting at my mouth. The beating of our breath was only so slightly shadowed by the delicate rushing water of the creek. I could only take a moment of admiration towards her before I felt the forward pulling of her weakened hands among my shirt.\n\nThe scene faded into an Aengulic light as I was pulled against her chest.\n\n-Published on 17th of the Amber Cold, 1472 by Lucius Tython ((Kyle4924))", ""),
        StaticBook("The Heist", "§bElrach", "Well, well well. It seems as though you have once again, despite your best efforts, become a pawn in one of my rather elaborate ruses, and it seems as though the end result of said rusehas left your state being of a lesser quality than you had dealt\n\nthe card hidden up my sleeve while being none the wiser. And yes, while it is true that you will eventually recover from this recent turn of events, it remains unclear whether your social status on this city will remain at its current level,\n\nor if it will take a turn for the worse. After all is said is done, at the end of the day, you will have to accept the fact that you just got the short end of the deal. I hold no remorse or regret for I am and always shall be a master ruseman.", ""),
        StaticBook("Game Of Thrones", "§bShariana Lock", "- Game Of Thrones -\n\nAnd then everybody died...\n\nThe End.", ""),
