        StaticBook("CMask Guidebook", "§b[Ghost] The Eternal Librarian", "Anti-Contagion\n  Filtration Mask\n     Guidebook\n\n       by Mirtok\n\nThank you for accepting your very own anti-contagion filtration mask, through this handbook you will learn all the important functions and procedures for operating and effectively using your mask. By the order of the Hochmeister in hopes to combat the\n\nrising infectious ailments, every Hanseti citizen is issued their own filtration mask for safety.\n\nSafety Tips\n1.) Never misplace or lose your mask, though the Hochmeister cares deeply for your safety, he will not be able to give out spare masks to careless people. \n\n2.) Never share your mask, to prevent the spread of the illness\n\nthe Hochmeister urges all mask users to stay with their own mask no matter the circumstances.\n\n3.) Maintain your mask, keeping your mask in proper order at full upkeep will prevent incidences of break or breaches in your mask's special\n\nprotective filtration and sealant systems.\n\n4.) Use filters to their fullest, each few years the Hochmeister will distribute new filters for your masks but until then you must ration off what you have, this will not only test your ability to be\n\nresponsible for your life but to prove you have what it takes to survive in a hazardous world.\n\nPreparing your Mask\nUpon receiving your mask, it is a good idea to become acquainted with how it works and how to properly use it to fend off infective agents of death.\n\nFirstly, it is a good idea to make sure your filters are in place\n\nand secure along both sides of the mask.\n\nUnder the eye sockets of the masks should be two metal containers with 3 clip secure locks located on the circumference of each capsule. Inside you can see the several layers of filters and spinning mechanism\n\nthat make up the filtration system. Do refrain from tinkering with the delicate parts and only manipulate the filters. Once assured that the filters fit snuggly in their respective areas we can make to making sure there are no cracks in the mask. We are\n\nunsure as to how the infection is spread and therefore it is important to count for all possible biological entries into the body. First examine the two large eye sockets and lenses on the front of the mask. Also can along the main folds and seams of the\n\nmask to make sure no loose strings or openings are present. Finally, make sure the center piece where each tube leading from the two filter capsules is not loose or emitting a rattling noise when shook. Be sure to be extra careful when checking for\n\nbreaches as a single hole can mean the difference between life or death.\n\nReplacing Filters\nTo replace a filter, you must first unlatch the lid to the two filtration capsules. Once the interior is exposed, carefully turn the filter pads until a hard click is heard above the smaller clicking during turning. When heard, the\n\nthe pads should slightly elevate themselves into a popping motion and then should be ready for removal. Replacement works in the opposite fashion, place the new ones in the direction of the painted arrows and turn when the resistance is felt.\n\nSeveral clicks should be heard until a final snap indicates its in place. Close the capsule and move to the next one.\n\nUsing your Mask\nWhen your situation calls for you to use your mask, you must follow a few simple steps to ensure your maximum safety in the face of an infective area. First loosen all of the straps along the back and front of the mask. Then place the\n\nloose mask over your head and position the eye sockets for your preferred viewing.\n\nWhen ready, slowly tighten each strap until the strapped area feels tight and snug over your head. It is very important to maintain a tight fit but also to keep the mask\n\nfrom cutting off blood flow to your mask and face as such will prompt discomfort and compel you to adjust the mask. When all straps have been secured, turn the small dial located on the front of your mask where the capsule tubes meet until you hear a\n\nsmall rush of air., When all these are done, you should have achieved maximum anti-contagion safety.\n\nBreaching/Cracks\nIn the event of a breach or crack in your mask, it is advised by the Hochmeister to quickly remove yourself from the jeopardizing area and seek cover and solitude from any other contact with others. The Hochmeister then\n\nurges you to remove the filter pads from your filtration capsules and attempt to apply them over your nose and mouth if no other repair methods are successful on the damaged mask.\n\nGood luck out there and may Celestia keep this tragedy from our frozen lands.", ""),
        StaticBook("Sunshine Letter", "§bGrand Druid Verden", "Dear Arch Druid Leyun,\n\nI'm sending this as a confirmation of my mailing address in the event that anything needs brought to my attention. My mail is kept confidential and locked tight, so feel free to let me know of any developments.\n\n                 ->\n\nPO Box 24 Equinox Island Rd.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 156", "§bLily", "Diary of Katina\n\nVolume 156\n\nThe Watch of the Okar\n\nEntry one, Tulatha has just\nbonded her Okar, Lan, this Diary will recant what they did, effects will be in the next Diary, so I can keep all of these in order.\n\nSo what Tulatha did from what I can tell is she skinned a live wolf, they tried a dead wolf skin, she said it failed.  Something about it doesn't have as much power, and its useless.\n\nNext she and Lan mixed 3 bowls of blood, one of hers, one of his, and\n\none of their blood mixed together, in which case she drew a huge symbol which is drawn below \n*The symbol is drawn here*\n\nInside they wrote their names in their own blood and then made the joint symbol.  \n\nThe bond of friendship,\n\n*A symbol is shown*\n\nA bond of lovers\n*A symbol is shown below*\n\nA bond of Criminals\n*A symbol is shown below*\n\nA bond of loyalties\n*A symbol is shown below*\n\nApparently the benifites of doing this are increased speed, increased hearing smell and sight but decreased touch and taste...\n\nWe'll see what happens with those two later...", ""),
        StaticBook("Marked Magic", "§bAdeon of Rhoswen", "\"Though they are not wizards that employ  powerful curses, most Witchmen can cast simple spells that can prove effective when used properly. Witchmen usually use them to fight monsters, though they also have non combat applications.\"\n-An Observer\n\nMarked Men are encouraged to use weaponry light enough to allow them to more easily cast simple spells with their free hand yet heavy enough so that they may deliver considerable damage to the thick hides of monsters.\n\nIt is suggested that a Marked Man refrain from using magic if any of these three illnesses affect him:\n\n-Injury\n-Fatigue\n-Loss of lucidity.\n\nIf a Marked Man is injured it is unlikely he will be able to evoke anything more than a few harmless sparks, this entirely depends on the magical skill of the Marked Man, however, most Marked Men sacrifice some of their magical ability for skill with a\n\nblade. Using magic while injured is risky as it may cause further injury or worsen bleeding due to increased blood flow when casting.\n\nLoss of lucidity will impact a Marked Man's judgement, intelligence and concentration- the nature of a Marked Man's job will always contain potential threats to his lucidity such as illusions or traumatising events.\n\nIt is suggested that if a Marked Man senses he is no longer fully lucid that he immediately ceases the casting of any spells due to the risk of causing harm to himself or another Marked Man.\n\nMagic causes the caster to become fatigued, no matter how powerful the caster is, eventually fatigue will catch up with them.\n\nA Marked Man should always maintain a high level of stamina while on the job as being fatigued can cause\n\nboth other illnesses.\n\nFatigue is the leading cause of death amongst monster hunters.\n\nA Marked Man's spell is most effective when cast with an element of surprise and proximity followed by the strike of a blade. It is always encouraged that a Marked Man keeps close watch on their sense of fatigue and do not spend all of their mana in one\n\nspell unless absolutely necessary.", ""),
        StaticBook("How2KillMonsters", "§b[Ghost] The Eternal Librarian", "Monsters\n        and\n   How to Defeat\n        Them\n\n    by Seth Calith\n\nMonsters roam our lands, and a good fighter has to know how to repel them as well as their strengths and weaknesses. In this book, the reader will find a guide on how to defeat each beast using either a bow or a sword.\n\nZombies\nZombies are creatures that, wanting to attack, follow you around. With that knowledge, a bow is a useful tool to defeat these monsters. The key to this fight is charging a shot in your bow while running. Once it is charged, shoot the arrow to\n\nhit it while still charging another arrow.\nIf it is of your preference to use a sword, you can take advantage of the Zombie’s low terrain-calculation properties. Try to stand higher than its head so that it is unable to strike you.\n\nSkeletons\nThe average skeleton is not as strong as the zombie, yet deadlier because of his fine use of the art of archery and bowmastery. It is recommended to use a sword against such creatures since they are bad in close combat and can only\n\nshoot at a determined rate.\nTo do such, circle around it fast enough so you can avoid its arrows, which is not recommended unless you are wearing good armor. When it fires, sprint towards it and slash him as hard as you can. With an average iron sword\n\nand a good reaction, you can defeat it while only taking one arrow, probably to the knee, due to its position upon death. If you can’t dispose of these requirements, you will have to study its rapidness of shot or withstand some more arrows, which is not\n\nrecommended unless you are wearing good armor.\nFor profficient bow-wielders, a duel can be had against one of these creatures. One should find a place to cover oneself and do so. When it shoots, wait for the sound of the arrow to crash against your\n\nhiding place and leave it. Shoot it once with a good aim and hide again. It will surrender sooner or later.\n\nBlazes\nThe blazes’s combat is not very different of the skeleton's, and with the blaze shooting fireballs instead of arrows, three at a time. If using a sword, follow the steps for the skeleton with a slight change. Wait for three shots to be fired,\n\nnot one, and make sure you don’t step on the fires set ablaze by the blaze itself. Do a similar thing with the bow. You can know when he is about to shoot because it fires up.\n\nSlimes\nSlimes come in various sizes, and upon death they divide into smaller ones. Use a bow to defeat all but the smallest, that are inoffensive and can be easily defeated with a sword.\n\nEnderbeings\nThese are strange beasts. They can move from a place to another in a blink, known as teleporting. When fighting one, the only tip I can give you is to jump in some water. Oddly, these beasts are hurt by it, so it will die in its chase.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Farmer Pt. 2", "§bWilhelm von Bran", "The Farmer\n            &\n             The Witch\n            OR\nThe Defeat of\n             The\n        Conjuror\nBy,\n   \n   Wilhelm von Bran\n\nPart II - End\n\n“Papa! Papa, I found Mama!” cried Erika in innocent charm, her beauty quite similar to that of her mother’s. An odd silence fell upon the group, a stillness. The gypsies stepped back and simply watched the interaction closely.\n\n“Amelie, how could you! I loved you!” cried out Herr Vertuger in a pitiable rage.\n\n“Ah dear for vhich mein heart did bleed, you are now vhere ze moon is seen! You say you love me vith ze sveetest vords, but come to hug me vith zose swords?”\n\nThe lady responded stepping ever closer, her hands moving as if cursing, and her words forming sick rhymes as if spells.\n\nWorried, the farmer beckoned Erika back to him but she stood still, as if frozen. “Please, Amelie, just let her go! You can come back vith me!”\n\n“Sveet Anders, how I vish I could! But my home is now amidst ze vood! Amidst ze crow, ze goat, ze bitch, for indeed you know I am a witch!” The gypsies, filled with fear dropped their weapons and stood in fright, the witch moving closer every step.\n\nAnders Vertuger stood, confused and faint. “But Amelie, how could you be a vitch? You are my vife, come back to me!”\n“Nein, zis cannot be, my love, come vith me und ascend above. Vasting your life is so tragic. Come vith me und accept ze magic!”\n\nThe farmer turned to his daughter “Come mein dear, your mother ist not ze same,” he then turned to the gypsies “To arms, mein gallant heroes! Zis curse can be qvelled by ze grace auf Gott! Rise you Creationist spirits! Be vith me, Alphonse!”\n\n“You speak auf ze Creator here? Be assured ze lies you bring strike no fear. Indeed, magic is opposed to zis notion, so come to me und stop ze commotion!”\n\nWith this new found inspiration, the gypsies leapt at once before she could cast any spells or react, they subdued her and threw her back to the throne. “Release ze spell Amelie, give mein daughter back to me!”\n\n“Why, oh Anders, do you demand so loud? Do you zhink ve fight above ze clouds? Indeed zhere is no need to yell, you have not yet reached ze gates auf hell!”\n\nWith those words, the gypsies released their hold and turned menacingly back to Anders, their brands lighting beneath their tunics.\n\n“Tell me, my sweet what will break this spell?” He shouted to his wife as the gypsies approached\nInstead of responding, the witch simply remained sitting\n\n“But how, mein vife, did zese men come under your spell, but gut Fazher Alphonse did not?” Anders retreated away, his sabers held high, ready to attack.\n\n“The good father was pure of heart, and these men have done things that he would not. Albeit, you can have a good defense, if you make remove of every sense!”\n\nWith this riddle, the farmer cast one of his sabers out the window and charged through the gypsy zombies to the lady and swung violently, in one swipe her nose was removed.\n\nThe Queen screamed a horrible scream and began to turn homely and plain. The gypsies shook violently and quaked.\n\n“I am sorry, mein love, I know not why, but I am cursed, I cannot fly! I’ll tell you vhat your Gott behooves, zhat each sense be cut; removed!” Wanting only for this tale to end, the farmer plucked the eyes from his fallen wife.\n\n“Vithout mein eyes I cannot see, Oh Erika remember your mother, Amelie! Dear Anders, make zhis curse end qvick, lest our daughter become so sick!”\nCrying, now, the farmer took both her hands and severed them at the wrists.\n\n“Alas, mein hands are cut! Und soon ze door auf life slammed shut, at last mein cursed soul must die. Oh Anders, I bid you: never cry!”\n\nAnders, reluctant reached his hand into the witch’s mouth and pulled her tongue sharply bringing his sword closer, ending her last sense; taste.\n\nIndeed with this shriek, the fog of the valley lifted at once and the Sun showed brightly through the stained glass, the pitiable image of the Lady was even more seen as an ugly and plain, pale and unattractive.\n\nAt once, he held his wife close, but one second more, he was only clutching her white dress. The gypsies laid unconscious and Erika wept in the corner.\n\nAnders however, thrust his sword in the air and spoke with eloquence, before unknown to him, “Ah dear vife I find you gone, but vith me und Erika, you still live on. Und vhile I vanquished, und made you tot, I kneel here before you und pray to Gott.”\n\nTogether, the Waldenian family returned to normalcy in that little town of Ansbluch.\n\nIf you, reader, wonder what the moral of this story might be. Take heed and read more carefully; Take heed and do not take it light, lest you find the Lady White.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n--The End", ""),
        StaticBook("A Note...", "§bVladimir Tahorran", "Dear friends,\n\nWith the departure of my dear husband-to-be, I feel as though I can no longer remain in this city. The area holds too many memories for me.\n\nAs such, I have chosen to depart in search of a new home.\n\nI take only a small handful of food and a tent under which to sleep to aid in my journey.\n\nI bid you all farewell.\n\nSincerely,\nVladimir Tahorran", ""),
        StaticBook("Verden's Summary", "Jallentime", "I sent Bartley to Glowshroom cave to collect spores from the rare breed of mushroom that grows inside. His small and quiet stature made short work of sneaking past the spider nest located inside.\n\nHe did well.\n\nThe point of this lesson was how we, as druids, must understand nature before we can make any attempt to aid it. This was coupled by also learning the proper technique used to observe spiders in their natural habitat without interfering more than needed.", ""),
        StaticBook("Ode to Nature", "§bPine Druid Gi'Garun", "An Ode to Nature\n    by The Pine Druid\n\nWe walk upon her soil,\nWe gaze upon her trees.\n\nWe're surrounded by all kinds of things, \nThat sprouted from her seeds.\n\nWhen one steps out of one's front door,\nOne will see her work.\n\nHer prowess great and beautiful,\nNo dark or evil mirk.\n\nWhat is it that I speak about?\n\nWhat is it that I praise?\n\nThe lands we roam,\nThe streams that foam.\n\nThe trees so tall,\nThe rain that falls.\n\nAll of these are of her make, for they are her and her alone. The make of two of love and great.\n\nThat be Nature.", ""),
        StaticBook("CH Handbook", "§bSaithor", "Introduction\n-------------------The Crimson Hearts are a guild of Spellswords, Sellswords, Mages, and Scholars all alike, all seeking the same goal, knowledge, power, wealth, and fame.\nThe Hearts of this guild are brothers and sisters, bound by oath.\n\nPaths\n-------------------The Crimson Hearts are divided into three categories. Knights, Mages, and Mechanists. No matter what path you pick, your brothers and sisters will protect you. And no matter what they pick, you will too.\n\nPeople of Honor\n-------------------Sonoren Klaren: He is the Ace of Hearts, our leader\nTalimeracahe: He is The Red Joker, second in command\nEdwyn Lynderly: He is the maker of the Crimson Hearth, the guild hall of the Crimson Hearts.\n\nTrainers o' the Paths\n-------------------Olympus: Trainer of the sword\nLelien: Trainer of the magic\nVirgo: Trainer of the Mechanists\nSaithor: Trainer of the bow and of the Realm\n\nRules\n-------------------1) Ye will protect your brothers and sisters\n2) You will obey all orders\n3) Knights will remain loyal\n4) Mages will heed to heal\n5) Mechanists will procede to prosper\n\nPath o' Knight\n-------------------The path of the Knight consists of the noble warriors of the Heart. They are the men and women who seek to be armed with a sword, those who know how to use one, and those who wish to know. They are loyal to the Hearts\n\nPath o' Mage\n-------------------The path of the Mage consists of the arcanic people of the Hearts. They are the men and women of the Hearts who seek the arts of magic. They heed to heal us, and will continue to do so.\n\nPath o' Mechanist\n-------------------The path of the Mechanist consists of the artisans and merchants of the Hearts. They are the men and women of the Hearts who seek the most money and fame they can get. They will procede to prosper.", ""),
        StaticBook("Da", "§bMitz", "Tha buuk uv da Goboz:\n\n1. Elweys lomp sumwun iv dey klomp latz.\n2. Elweys bi guud frendz tu utter Orcz en Goboz.\n3. Elweyz travul en paks, et iz nub guud tu fite alown\n4. Elweys flat t'e twiggehs!\n\n5. Tri en bi neis tu dah drewidz ur dey wil klomp latz.\n6. Unli mugg peeple en groopz.\n7. Stek tugithur az Goboz.\n8. Nevur bi neis tu enywun whu latz nub knuw. But unli iv latz haz tuu.\n\nDat iz ebery t'ing abowt beyiing a Gobo.\n\n  Latz frend Gobo's,\n           Mitz en Fitz.\n\nP.S Mey duh Gobo bi within!", ""),
        StaticBook("Old Man's Dream", "§bMerekM", "'There once was a dream,\nIn this man's heart,\nThat life was never sullied,\nInstead was cherished,\nWould not be perished,\nAnd brought to eternal flame.'\n\nA keep they built,\nSo it was felt,\nA burden they all could carry,\nBut darkness fell,\nIt did compel,\nA burden for none to bury.\n\n\n'New burden made,\nSo lightness fade,\nAnd the man was who to blame.'\n\n\n\n\n-Poem rewritten by Merek Moonstrike. Original creator: Gladius Moonstrike.", ""),
        StaticBook("Insane", "§bIsengar Brockfall", "A stor'eh yer want ey?\nWel' believ' me'h a've got te'h stor'eh fer ye!\n\nSom' 'f te'h pages are Extracts from ma'h real diary.\n\nOnc' upon a'h toime...\nWai'! was t'at too cleche?\n\nHmm lemme'h t'ink 'f somethin' mor' original.\n\nI wok' up?\nNae t'ats stup'ed\n\n\"Yer can go fock yerself\", she sai' when a'h asked ma'h gir' friend ter marry me'h.\n\nA'h look 'n shoc' as a'h was sayin' t'is tu 'er twin sister.\n\nMa'h gir' friend was be'ind ma'h.\nTe'h bitc' slaps ma'h in te'h face 'nd calls me'h a'h cheatin' bastard.\nA'h watched t'em dissapear 'n te'h distance as a'h was holdin' ma'h face.\n\n\"Yer feckin' bitc'\" a'h called out.\nT'en a'h felt a'h han' grip ma'h arm tight'leh, A'h turned me'h head 'round 'nd a'h saw te'h Menacin' face 'f 'n Orc, wel' Olog ter be'h exact.\nHe'h was 'bout 10 ft tall, 'nd a'h was 'bout 3 ft tall.\n\nLucki'leh a'h new Ologs wer' prett'eh dumb.\n\n\"Miz eatz latz\" he'h growled.\nA'h troied ter squirm ou' but 'e 'ad me gripped toight.\n\n\"WAI'\" a'h screamed.\n\"Halflin' 'ave diseases\"\n\n\"Dizeazez?\" he mumble'.\n\"Err, ye' we'h all bor' wit' Spidtrocious diseas'\" I 'ad a'h worried look 'n me'h face.\n\n\"Wutz tatz?\" he'h grumbled wit' slop comin' from 'is mouth.\n\n\"We' born wit' spiders insoide ah'll bell'eh, 'nd 'f yer eat me'h yer will get em!\" \n\nHe'h dropped me'h 'n me bac', but a'h got up straight awa'.\n\n\"Latz dizguztin'\"\nHe'h shouted.\n\n\"No' wil' yer eat me'h?\" \n\"Nub, latz weirdz.\"\n\nA'h 'ad a'h sloight smirk 'n me'h fac', 'nd watched 'm stomp of'.\n\nA'h read t'is book calle' err Into the nature a'h t'ink et was called.\nEt was 'bout t'is man 'o lived 'n te'h wild 'n went crazy afte' 'bout 16 days.\n\nA'h coul' du bette' a'h thoug' ter meself.\n\nBut ofcours' a'h ain't goin' ter bloddy wast' ma'h toime 'n goin' inter a'h jungle.\n\nIf a'h remembe' correct'leh a'h wante' ter go 'n a'h hike.\nBut nae on' woul' com' wit' me'h\n\nA'h ended up gatherin' 3 friends!\nOn' was Arthur Caulfield 'nd on' was Jessie Boatwrite.\n\nA'h new t'ese two fer quoite a'h while but neve' spok' tu em.\n\nA'h told em ter brin' food 'nd campin' equipmen'.\n\nAs we'h set ou' a'h glanced bac' at te'h temple on' las' toime.\n\nWe'h walked throug' thic' sludge 'nd swam across a'h murky river.\n\nWe'h settled down 'n 'n Extrordinar'eh fores'.\nTe'h sounds 'f birds tweetin' mad' et seem loike 'ome.\n\nJess'eh pitched the larg' tent 'nd Arthur ate his Caramel Cookies.\n\n\"Seems rather dull doesn't it?\" Arthur calle' out.\n\"T'ats te'h fores' fer yer\" a'h laughed.\nJessi'eh smiled but didn't sa' 'nything from t'at day on.\n\nWow lon' toime sinc' a'h wrote 'n ye.\nBee' sixteen days now, w'ere bloddy starvin'!\n\nDay 33\n\nAges sinc' a'h wrote 'n yer, te'h pages ar' turnin' tu a'h mossy yellow colour.\n\nDay 34\n\nWe'h need meat lads, pleas' we los' weight every singl' day.\n\nDay 47\n\n\nA'h feel t'em gettin' closer!\n\nDay 52\n\nWe'h ate jessi'eh, te'h poor lad 'ad te'h mos' meat 'n 'm.\n\nDay 71\n\nSo tired ma'h bodies shuttin' down, ah've been livin' off ma'h own shite ter stay aloive.\n\nDay 100\n\nTe'h voices get closer ter ma'h everyda', sayin'\n\"Kil' him\"\nA'HM NO' KILLIN' ARTHUR!!!\n\nDay 123\n\nMa'h pee tastes sweete' ever'eh day\n\nDay 131\n\n\nLoo' at Arthur, 'e looks so Juic'eh, so sweet so meeeaaattttyyy.\n\nDay 149\n\n\nJ'ost 'ad te'h best meal 'n me'h whole loife toime!\n\nDay 000000000000\n\n\n... Woy ar' t'ese dots so small?", ""),
        StaticBook("Amoss The Wizard", "§bArthur Caulfield", "Amoss the wizard spoke of death to the prince. \n  Yakov, the target, had not been seen since.\n  Hold up in a castle full of armed guards. \n  Amoss' mind was smashed into shards. \n  Believing he could take on all of the men.\n  Amoss the wizard\n\nrushed into Oren.\n  Then rushing back out, chased by an angry mob.\n  Amoss the wizard looked quite the knob.\n  Surrounded by metal, which could deliver such pain.\n  Amoss the wizard gave up and put in chain.\n\nGrigor, the lead man in a mob of patriotic Orenians. \n  A determined mind that put him above millions. \n  Organised the mob into a killing machine. \n  Each section took the orders ever so keen.\n  To shed blood, for the emperor who runs this place.\n\nAnd oh how they moved, at such a high pace it would seem to be a race to rip off the enemies face.\n  Eraborn. No one saw him, sneak on the rooftop. \n  Only when his eyes fell on to Grigor did he stop.\n  Withdrew a bow, and an arrow made from\n\nthe finest of steel. \n  To make sure that where the head struck, the wound would not heal.\n  I kid you not, it struck his head, but he shrugged it away. \n  Many may tell you different, but I was there on that day.\n\nA day when the guards seized the man on the roof.\n  But using his magic, he escaped their clutches with a poof. \n  While that man is being hunted Amoss is on trial.\n  The treachorous things he shouted were ever so vile.\n\n\"Off\" shouted the crowd, \"Off with his head!\"\n  And after the trial, I am sure Amoss will be dead.", ""),
        StaticBook("The guard", "§bArthur Caulfield", "A guard on duty\npulled out his sword\nthreatened an innocent man\n\nThe man cried \"what have I done\" but reason with the guard no one can. \n\nThe guard searched his clothes, took his food and hit him on\n\nthe head. \n\nThe guard shouted \"you quiet now, I kill you and you be dead!\"\n\nThis is when the man realised who he was dealing with. \n\nA guard as dumb as this one could only be a myth.\n\n\"How did you pass training? How have you survived?\" \n\nThe guard simply awnsered \"I stole armour, when the soldiers arrived.\"\n\nThe man slapped the sword away, landing on the floor.\n\nLooking to the guard he asked \"what the hell is all this for?\" \n\nThe guard shrugged his shoulders, drool falling from his chin. \n\nAfter a moment the guard tried to pick his nose, his gigantic fingers not going in.\n\nThen he began to sob, tears falling down his face. \n\nBut to the man, everything seemed to be out of place. \n\nHow could such a bufoon have so much strength?\n\nAnd in order to avoid this people we must go to such great lengths.\n\nTo take away their swords. \n\nAnd point them towards..\n\nA place run by a mayor like that\n\nwhere? You would like to know is such a place as that.\n\nA town not far from petrus known as Fustenberg.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Book of a Tale", "§bCecil Halcourt", "In a land far away, once called Maledictus, lived in a tent a man named Sindri. Sindri was of a long lost race, the Avarii.\n\n     One day, the bastard of the bastard named Eddard Griffon followed her to her tent, and they struck a friendship.\n\nTheir\n\nfriendship grew into playful and then eventually serious flirting, and then it was discovered Sindri was not male at all, but female, though they kept this secret.\n\nThey did many amazing adventures together, such as the time they kidnapped The Khan Hamago\n\nfrom behind the eyes of their own people.\n\nSindri was made Duke of the City of the Pirates, and ruled well. Justice was enforced, and the realm proposered. A deeper calling cam to them though, so Sindri abdicated, and with Eddard, founded the legendary\n\nGrey Citadel.\n\nThey protected the land from the Fear, the Great Darkness, with friends like Autumn the Holy Priestess, Marin the loser of valuable artefacts, Natsu the Miner, and Sindri the bold. \n\nWell, the rest you know", ""),
        StaticBook("Alch. Notebook 2", "§bilikefooddude", "The Alchemist's Notebook\nPart Two\n\nThe Great Anthos Forests\n\nTippin's Root\nA purple flower found growing beneath rotting trees and on the sides of hills, Tippin’s Root is one of the more common alchemical regents in the great forests of  Anthos.\n\nCharacterised by its thick, deep digging roots and yellow spotted skin, this plant is the favourite of ‘healing’ alchemists everywhere, due to its almost uncanny blood coagulating properties. When crushed and made into an\n\noil or balm, the root of the Tippin’s plant is capable of stopping the bleeding of most any wound (Within practicality, of course) in only a few moments. Though limited to only stopping bleeding, not actually healing the wound or cleaning it, as is a top\n\npriority in healing, its balm is a must to carry around in case of emergencies. Due to its natural endurance and blood-stopping abilities, it’s a brilliant symbol of Earth to use in Potions of Mending, and can mostly be used in other concoctions needing\n\n strength or balance. Another interesting property of Tippin’s Root in its crushed form is an awful, putrid smell; though this can be found useful in situations where someone sleeping or unconscious needs to be woken- unhappily,\n\nindeed, but woken all the same.\n\nNight Sap\nA golden sap found seeping out of growths of birch trees; Night Sap is regarded as the most deceptive alchemical regent in any of the four base biomes. Upon consumption, the thick, honey like substance proves to be a\n\npowerful sleeping agent- lulling you into a warm slumber only minutes after drinking it. Its taste, though sometimes over exaggerated, is perhaps the most pleasant effect this ingredient will give you; it’s soothing to the throat and calming to the mind,\n\nand slows your breathing and heart rate as if you’re now instantly lying in a soft featherbed, ready to sleep. Upon waking, however- Generally two to four hours later- you will soon come to realise that aching pains now plague your body. On most occasions\n\nafter awaking from Sap Sleep, the unfortunate victim will find themselves too groggy or in too much pain to move- and will be racked with headaches and muscle pains for days after the experience. Apart from the natural effects of Night Sap,\n\nan alchemist will find it useful as a symbol of Earth, endurance, and strength, and is commonly used in poisons to give them a good, addictive taste. Consumption is not advised, and harvesting is as easy as collecting\n\nthe weeping sap in a vial from an affected tree.\n\nAlabaster Leaf\nA plant appropriately named for the colour of its foliage, Alabaster Leaf is a fairly common alchemical ingredient found growing around felled trees, in the drier regions of forests. It’s difficult to spot and identify\n\nsave for its alabaster hue, but is easy enough to grow in a grove once a herbalist gets a sample. If eaten, the plant can be found to be slightly poisonous; having the effect of nausea and throbbing headaches on those unknowing foragers who consume it.\n\nIn brewing potions, Alabaster Leaf is useful as a symbol of Earth and fatigue. Simply cut off the leaves and store them dried or fresh in your satchel to harvest the regent, and later crush them to add to your potions, oils, or balms.\n\nSerpent's Stalk\nAn emerald cluster of reeds usually found thriving among small ponds or beside slow flowing riverbanks, Serpent’s Stalk is an average symbol of Water, and is best used in potions revolving around calmness and relief.\n\nBrews such as the Potion of Mending would indeed find the mild herb as a key ingredient with a more placid representation.  When crushed and mixed with water, the plant’s stalks have a somewhat similar effect as the Frost Vine\n\nplant; soothing the pain of recent, minor burns when applied directly to the skin. The best way to harvest the plant is by cutting as close to the base as possible, and leaving it to regrow. Crushing the plant and infusing it into a potion's\n\nbase is the best way to get the most concentrated effects of Serpent’s Stalk; and though eating it is not advised, it’s not poisonous in a sense that you would think of with the name it holds.\n\nFlame Tongue Root\nFound in the more dense parts of forests and groves, this regent is more easily defined by its deep crimson rose like blossom that is most commonly completely covered in a plethora of spines and brambles. Upon touch, Flame Tongue\n\nis physically hot, and when administered to wounds it coagulates the blood much like Tippin’s Root does- Though with an almost unbearable burning sensation in addition. Spores are released from the plant’s formidable spikes which cause\n\ncontinuing pain and irritation long after touch, and simply refuse to be washed off- though a mixture of Serpent’s Stalk or Frost Vine will relieve your affected skin and wash off the spores quite easily. The most potent parts of the herb would be\n\nthe roots; being known as a somewhat competent symbol of Fire for potions involving power, pain, or torment. Harvesting the plant must be done with gloves and heavy clothing, as many would-be-alchemists find out the hard way.\n\nUprooting the whole plant and keeping it in a leather pouch is the best way to transport it back to storage, as even though the spines aren’t as potent as the roots, they are sometimes useful in the more mild types of poisons.\n\nAdditional Entries\n\nMiner's Helmet\nA cave dwelling mushroom found all around the continent of Anthos, Miner’s Helmet is a large, abundant fungi preferring to live down deeper in cave systems than herbalists\n\nwould wish to go. Somewhat weak as a symbol of Earth, this subterranean growth is still useful in brewing for its use in potions needing resilience, strength, and poison. The plant is poisonous if consumed without proper processing;\n\nundetectable save for in the first few minutes in which the victim will be plagued by a series of violent twitches, the mushroom will stop your nervous system in only a matter of hours. The top of the mushroom is covered in a fine, but deadly toxic slime,\n\nwhich must be used in poisons only. Harvesting this plant should be done with upmost care; with gloves, a mouth covering, and a safe, sealed container. When preparing the fungi for brewing, make sure to properly wash and boil the\n\nmeat of the plant for about fifteen minutes, as this should remove any of the more deadly parts of the plant while keeping the head safe and pure for potion making. Crush and infuse the pulp into your base, and filter\n\nfilter it out later, as is common procedure for mushrooms and roots.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Diary & Story", "§bLyra", "This is Violetoins Diary!\n  \n\nIt is my real diary.\n\nYou just bought this book!\n\n\nMany copies will follow if this book sells well. Then i shall start writing useful books!\n\nDear Diary, Lyra is trying to kill me! If you find this i'm probably already dead! \n\nSomeone help! Anyone  just dont let Lyra find me!\n\nOh No, she's coming...\nShe's going to k...\n\nHello, this is Lyra. I like following Violetoin all around. This book might be useless. Indeed it is. Still i shall write a story at the next page. Thanks for shopping!\n\n-=-=-=-=--=-=-=-=-= The Evil Bird\n    \n        &\n          \n          The Alchemist\n------------------- Written by Lyra Moonstone\n-------------------Copyright is probably owned by Lyra.\n\nOnce upon a time, in a little, little town a bird was sitting on a tree next a big wheat field. The wheat field was laying next to a few houses and a blacksmith building. Many people were strolling around carrying logs and stone. The people of Astruagan\n\nwhere building the town since they started it last month.\nThe bird sat in the tree on a twig. It was one of the rare species. The Mad Chicken. Its so rare you can only see it in special trees! So a few days later, someone new joined the town.\n\nHis name is Joseph. He was an Alchemist and made potions for everyone in town. Everybody was healthy until the Alchemist went chopping trees to get more logs for houses. When he started chopping the special tree of the bird Magma\n\nMagma jumped out of the tree, pecking on the man's head. It started to bleed heavily and he screamed for help. Everyone ran to him and helped him. He died later. R.I.P. Joseph\nA message to Magma. Bad bird.\n\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=  \n\n\n\n\n         THE END\n\n\n\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-", ""),
        StaticBook("Incident Report", "§bDamai Torena", "The city was full of people and a cloacked women walked through the gate. Mean while a guard was standing near a large tree which had ladders on it. The guard asked her to take her hood off. She kicked the guard and then scrambled up the ladders and up\n\ninto the leaves.§0\nThe guard then climbed up the ladder but she kicked it down and the guard fell in a bush. He then pulled the ladder back up and then climbed up it. He found her in the leaves and she then climbed across a banner and pulled a knife out\n\nand started cutting through the banner. The guard scrambled back to the tree, he only just made it.§0\n§0\n§0The girl then fell onto another tree. The guard then climbed up that tree towards her. He asked her to take the hood off and get down out of the\n\ntree. She then drew her knife but quickly put it back in her pocket as the guard pulled his sword out.§0\n§0\n§0She then tried to grab his ankle to pull him off but she then slipped off and the guard fell backwards. §0\n\nThe guard then climbed down the tree while the girl's fall was broken by a mage and cleric of some sort. §0\n§0\n§0The guard approached her and said, take you hood off. She then ran and she fell down the stairs into a burrow. The guard was then filled with\n\nanger and then cut her arm with his sword. People ran towards her and he was told to walk away.", ""),
        StaticBook("Basic Ethics", "§bGlacio Nereus", "Motivism, Deontological, and Consequence theories of ethics.\n\nFirst, the consequence theory dictates that an action is either right or wrong based on the consequences of any given action. An example is if a man kills another man.\n\nman. The action would be right if the consequences were good, such as saving the lives of five good people, or bad, such as if the man who was killed were to have saved the world. Utilitarianism is based off of consequentialism,\n\nand the basic point of utilitarianism is an action is good if it brings about the greatest amount of pleasure, and the least amount of pain, for the largest number of people. Issues with this type of utilitarianism though are that one can not see the\n\nthe consequences of the future until they have occured, and one can commit heinous evils if others were to be pleasured by the action. An example of the first argument would be if a man is in a comatose state, and others work to keep him alive,\n\nalive, killing him would be what a utilitarian \nof this type would argue to be the good action. However, the effort that is no longer keeping him alive \ncan be used to aid others, but this condones murder of of the comatose person. The other argument\n\nis the \nsoldier analogy. A soldier fighting in a war decides to spare a fleeing enemy since the enemy is \nunarmed, wounded, and in no state to fight back. However, this soldier later kills fifty innocent men. \nAlthough the motive for sparing\n\nsparing the man was a good motive, the consequence in the long run was ill, \ndespite being unable to foretell such a result. This would make the soldier who spared the enemy guilty of what he could not foretell.  Another type of utilitarianism is rule\n\nRule utilitarianism. Rule utilitarianism is different in how it attempts to reconcile consequentialism and deontology. This formsays an action is morally justified if an act conforms to a rule, and leads to the greatest good.\n\nFor example, if a man follows a rule of to not murder, then his act is good, even if one he may have murdered will do so himself. So, two forms of consequentialism are utilitarianism and rule utilitarianism.\n\nContinuing the discussion of ethical theories is deontology. The deontological theory states an action's is based not on the consequences or the motives of an action, but rather the action in it of itself. An example would be if a man were to fight\n\ncriminals. criminals. This man's actions may be fighting for good, but he is bad due to the act of fighting being morally wrong. An example of a good action would be being charitable. charitable. If a man were to donate money to a man who uses money for\n\nhedonistic acts, it would still be a good action, and the man a good man. This is because deontology states that the act, \ncharity, is good, so the man doing the act is good. There are more nuances within this theory however, but they will not be\n\ndiscussed in this book. Finally, motivism is the theory that an action is either morally permissable or morally wrong based on the motives of an action. An example would be if the previously mentioned soldier spared his foe. The action would be considered\n\nconsidered morally permissable since the act of not killing him, and the reason for it, is good. A good. A more direct way to follow motivism is the idea to universalize a given maxim, and see if it can be ordered as a universal law. An example would be\n\nif the soldier spared the enemy once more. The action would be considered morally permissable since the act of not killing him, and the reason for it, is good. A more direct way to follow motivism is the idea to universalize a given maxim, and see if it\n\ncan be universalized as a universal law. law. An example would be the action of stealing. The maxim would be 'I should steal.' If one universalized the law, then everybody should steal. If everyone were to steal, the idea of stealing itself collapses,\n\nfor stealing is the act of taking something that is not rightfully yours. Now, there is no object or thing that is rightfully anyone's since all is being stolen, and all is gained by stealing. Therefore, this view of motivism states\n\nstealing is never morally permissable. Other actions with this include murder, lieing, suicide, and many more. An example of a morally permissable act by this view of motivism would be any action that does not defeat itself when universalized.\n\nFor instance, the soldier that spared his foe is doing the right thing since the idea of not killing does not defeat itself, and it can be universalized. However, a flaw in this argument would be conflicting duties.\n\nFor example, if a general ordered the soldier to kill the surrendered foe, the man's two duties would be conflicting. He has the moral obligation to not kill the man, and the moral obligation to obey his superiors due to the idea that\n\nboth maxims can be universalized. This argument is against this type of motivism.\n\nIn conclusion, the three types of moral theories include consequentialism, deontology, and motivism. All\n\nthree have their independent benefits, and their independent flaws.", ""),
        StaticBook("Poetry", "§bSofetios Jayamen", "Poetry\n\n             ~By Sofetios\n                  Jayamen\n\n        The Lake\n\nI wander upon a path by a lake,\nWondering where it’s all gone.\nEvery day I used to come here,\nFrom the morning to the evening's yawn.\n\nEvery now and again I wonder,\nWhy the days have passed.\nAnd think very deeply,\nWhy they don't seem to last.\n\nDay by day, night by night,\nThe Lake becomes smaller still.\nThe thoughts of it become thinner,\nEven to my strongest will.\n\nIt has become ten times smaller and ten times shallower,\nEven thought it has not changed.\nTo other people it might be normal,\nbut to me it is considered strange.\n\n      The Dreamer\n\nThe Dreamer,\nThe one who dares not be the same,\nFor in dreams mysteries unravel,\nAnd endless space you do travel,\nAs your dreams you try to tame.\n\nAs the Dreamer,\nDreams above the sky,\nAnd looks down below,\nTo the people that show,\nWho know not what lies that high.\n\nUnawakened, the Dreamer,\nIn a maze of thoughts,\nWandering the mists,\nWith endless twists,\nAs the path on minds paper he jots.\n\nThough be wary dreamer,\nAs thoughts tend to run away,\nOnly the ones you really need,\nAnd the ones you often feed,\nAre the ones that often stay.\n\n      The Cobbler\n           and\n        The Crow\n\nI was working in the night,\nAs I bent over a shoe,\nI was working very hard,\nto finish before the morning dew.\n\nI was just about finished,\nWhen what was this I hear?\nA tapping, slight tapping,\nAnd it seemed to be so near.\n\nI turned to the window,\nAnd what did I see?\nA crow tapping at my glass,\nStaring at me.\n\n\"Go away.\" I said,\nSternly as I did stand.\n\"Don't you know I have work to do?\"\nThough I know it didn't understand.\n\nBut the crow kept tapping at the glass,\nAnd I began to get mad,\nThis crow just would not stop,\nNot even a tad.\n\nI turned to my unfinished shoe,\nTo work as long as I could bear,\nBut then I noticed something missing,\nThat would stop me from completing the pair.\n\nThe golden lace that I needed,\nFor this order to be complete,\nI couldn't find it anywhere,\nAs the glass the crow did beat.\n\nI checked my table, checked my pockets,\nAnd then to my surprise,\nThere was a hole in one,\nThat was just the right size.\n\nI would never finish this shoe,\nBy the morning light,\nNow things were dire,\nand my situation was quite tight.\n\n\"I must have dropped it,\nOn the way back from the store!\"\nBut it was too far I thought,\nAs my confidence tore.\n\nBut then I realized \"What's this?\"\n\"Where was the tapping I couldn't bare?\"\nI looked to the window,\nAnd the crow wasn't there.\n\nI went to the window,\nAnd what was in its place?\nIt was simple and divine,\nThe golden lace.\n\nI opened the window,\nAnd took it with care,\nBecause of who gave this gift,\nI was certainly aware.\n\nI went to my table,\nAnd finished the shoe.\nBut then thought of the crow,\nIf I only knew!\n\nI then went to bed,\nWith not much I could say,\nI only did hope,\nThe crow would come back some day.\n\n         Imprint\n\nI stand in a forest,\nOne that I know quite well,\nWhere the trees are tall,\nAnd they never do fall,\nAt least that's what I like to tell.\n\nI came here,\nWhen I was a boy,\nUsually in the day,\nBut any time I would say,\nThe forest was my favorite toy.\n\nI came here again,\nA few years ago,\nIt was the same place I knew well,\nAs far as I could tell,\nThere was nothing different to show.\n\nI open my eyes now,\nTo see the trees,\nMy eyes see an empty place,\nAs if leaving no trace,\nThough this is not what my mind sees.\n\n         Friend\n            or\n          Enemy?\n\nEverywhere I am\nI hide from your sight,\nIf it's in the day,\nAnd especially at night.\n\nI am one of sleep's worst enemies,\nAnd one of fear's best friends,\nI grow ever more larger,\nAs your will begins to bend.\n\nIn the day I am more friendly,\nEspecially when its hot and I'm in sight,\nBut when it turns to night time,\nI'm sure to give you a fright.\n\nI am a mimic of sorts,\nI try to copy you while you walk,\nI even mimic other people,\nAlthough I cannot talk.\n\nEven when you go about the day,\nI skulk and kind of stalk,\nFear or fear not, I am always here,\nI am the shadow on which you walk.\n\n   An Uncommon Sight\n\nI go about my day,\nDoing the same things as before,\nThe same old road,\nFrom my humble abode,\nNot realizing it is a bore.\n\nI walk ever onward,\nKnowing all my tasks by heart,\nA straight narrow trail,\nWith nothing new to avail,\nAll I do is my part.\n\nThen one day as I'm going past,\nA park once again,\nI see a boy,\nwithout even a toy,\nwho couldn't be past ten.\n\nThe boy was doing something abnormal,\nsomething I hadn't seen before,\nHe was looking in the sky,\nNot even blinking an eye,\nthey didn't even seem sore.\n\nI wondered if there was a hawk,\nOr something in the sky,\nI didn't hear a thing,\nOnly the birds that always sing,\nSo I did wonder why.\n\nI looked over,\nto the boy sitting by,\nHe was being bullied by his peers,\nAnd was starting to go in tears,\nJust for looking in the sky.\n\nI was about to go over,\nTo try to help if I could,\nbut the parents went first,\nBefore I feared the worst,\nTo help as they should.\n\nI walked on my way home,\nWondering why what I saw came to pass,\nI had a thought why,\nso I looked to the sky,\nand saw it in it's great mass,\n\nAs I looked to the sky,\nI was transfixed,\nI hadn't done this in a while,\nAnd it did make me smile,\nAs past and current memories mixed.\n\nI saw the vast expanse,\nIn all its mystery,\nI watched the clouds in the sky,\nAs they changed shape and passed by,\nAs they had for all of history.\n\nSuddenly someone laughed to my left,\nEven thought it was mild,\nSomeone looking at me,\nLaughing at what I appeared to be,\nJust as the others had done to the child.\n\n         Courage\n\nIt is a thing you use\nWhen you can't go to the end,\nWhen all seems lost\nAnd you can't defend.\n\nYou use it as\nIt combines with your will,\nAll you need to do is\nLet it give its fill.\n\nThe choice is yours,\nGive in or go,\nThough in the end\nOnly you will know.\n\nSee with your spirit\nAnd not with fear,\nfor then you will know\nThat courage is here.\n\nThe End\n\nThank you for taking the time to read this book! I will be writing more in the future!\n\nIf you like my writing, please send me a bird or tell me in person!\n\n~Sofetios Jayamen", ""),
        StaticBook("Application", "§bJentos Of Blackwood", "Application\n---------+---------\nName: Jentos Blackwood\n((Jentos))\nMagics you wish to study: Wind Evocation, Lightning Evocation\n\nGuild you wish to be associated to: The Sanctuary", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Solaris the Pyromancer:§0\n§0\n§0Solaris cups his hands together in front of himself, exhaling slowly as he stares at the space above his palm, his ears folding back slightly. There seems to be a light spark, a flicker, before a small flame bursts to life\n\nabove his hand, the orange fire dancing in the cold air. The scent of apple pie fills the area, along with burning planks. He seems to shiver lightly, his eyebrows furrowing in focus.  The orb of fire seems to rotate in his hand clockwise, orbitting the\n\nthe space it formed, and gaining slightly in size and brightness until it looks to be the size of a tennis ball. Solaris seems to have beads of sweat on his forehead, his breathing becomes shaky. The fire stops orbiting as he brings back his hand, the\n\nball follows his hand. Solaris narrows his eyes onto the horizen and throws the ball of fire, as it lurches foward. Solaris shivers, his cheeks slightly flushed after the cast, as he watches it for a few moments then snaps his fingers. At the snap of his\n\nfingers the fire ball seems to implode in a firey heat. Some of the ice around the explosion seemed to melt quite quickly and sliding into a glacier. Solaris takes a deep breath and uses an arm to wipe away the sweat from his forehead. The cast has ended.\n\nTip: You can only cast with a given element that you have experienced.", ""),
