        StaticBook("Whitestorm Ck.Bk", "§bRosso", "Baking with the\n      Whitestorms\n  Some of\nThe family’s\n    Favorite recipes.\n             James\n\n       Whitestorm\n\n\n\nVolume One:\n               Cakes\n\nBasic Cake\n----------\nServes an entire party. Prepare long in advance, and douse in brandy to prevent mold.\n\nIngredients\n----------\n1 lb Oat Flour\n1 lb Egg\n1 lb Sugar\n1 lb butter\n½ cup Candied OrangePeel\n½ cup Candied Lemon Peel\n½ cup Candied Citron\n1/3 Cup Currants\n\nIngredients Contd.\n----------\n½ Cup Almond Flour\n1 Tsp Nutmeg\n1 Tsp Mace\n1 ½ Tsp Cinnamon\n1 Tsp Ginger\n1 Tsp Cloves\n¼ Cup Sherry\n¼ Cup Brandy\n\nDirections\n----------\n1. Heat stove to adequate temperature. Grease a 10 inch pan in preparation.\n\nDirections Contd.\n----------\n2. Beat the butter in a bowl until creamy. Add sugar and beat until fluffy. Stir at a medium speed while gradually adding in egg, until thoroughly combined.\n\nDirections Contd.\n----------\n3. Sift the Oat Flour in a separate bowl. Add the spices and almond flour. Add the mixture gradually to the butter, sugar, and eggs, while stirring. Continue stirring until thoroughly mixed.\n\nDirections Contd.\n----------\n4. Add the brandy and sherry to the batter and mix.\n5. Pour the batter into the pan, and spread it evenly. Bake until a knife can come out clean. Allow cooling for half an hour.\n\nDirections Contd.\n----------\n6- Invert the pan onto a plate, then onto a cooling rack. Allow even more cooling.\n\nWhitestorm cook book\n\nWritten by James Whitestorm\nCoppied by Rosso\nVaerHaven", ""),
        StaticBook("Notes III", "§bArchmage Crumena", "Eighth of the Sun's Smile, 1497\nCrumena Ilwindior \nI took the liberty today to carry out an experiment for I could not quell my curiosity. It was early in the morning as I did not want to disturb anyone nor do I want Lotheriel\n\n\ncoming across my studies. I traversed the halls of this tower with my Shade trailing behind. The damned mist expanded its reaches outward and clutched any it could. Finally at the garden between Ceruberr’s Arch-Mage quarters and the meeting\n\n\nhall I had discovered something. Something pulsated above. For one second I was certain it was just above the ceiling and then without worry it circling about. Like a roach skidding away for dear life. Quickly its height altered with every moment.\n\n\nHigher and higher and then it dropped. Right before my Shade lost its grasp of the anomaly it seemed like an ocean away. I truly wonder if we should further dwell in Rasmot’s domain.§§", ""),
        StaticBook("Art of War-P1", "§bNapoleon Fournier d'Avenese", "*The book is written in Relourian ((French))\n*\n\nTHE ART OF WAR\n\nBy Napoleon Fournier D'Avenese\n\nSquire to his LORDSHIP THE COUNT OF ALDERSBURG.\n\nNoble gentry and nobility of the many realms loyal to the true faith, I present to you, my military treatise.\n\nIt is particularly intended for the house of De Savoie, however, others may, at their own will, use such a treatise.\n\nI.Plans\n\nThe art of war is of great importance to the state. Hence it being a subject of inquiry which no noble lord can ignore. \n\nThe art of war is governed by 5 factors, each of great importance.\n\nThese are: \n\n(I)-The Moral Law.\n\n(II)-Heaven\n\n(III)-Earth\n\n(IV)-The Commander\n\n(V)-Method And Discipline.\n\n(I)The moral law is the popularity of the ruler and the soveriegn, and of their immeediate liege, and weather they, undismayed by danger, will follow them into the fray.\n\n(II)Heaven signifies night and day, cold and heat, times and seasons. \n\n(III)Earth comprises distances, great and small; danger and security; open ground and narrow passes; the chances of life and death.\n\n(IV)-The Commander stands for the virtues of wisdom, sincerely, benevolence, courage and strictness.\n\n(V)-By method and discipline are to be understood the marshaling of the army in its proper subdivisions,e maintenance of roads by which supplies may reach the army, and the control of military expenditure.\n\nThese are the five heads of any commander. He that knows them will be victorius, he that knows them not will fail. \n\nAll warfare is based on deception.\n\nHence, when able to attack, we must seem unable; when using our forces, we must seem inactive; when we are near, we must make the enemy believe we are far away; when far away, we must make him believe we are near.\n\nHold out baits to entice the enemy, feign disorder and crush him. If he is secure at all points, be prepared for him. If he is in superior strength, evade him. If he is taking his ease, give him no rest. If his forces are united, separate them.\n\n If your opponent is of choleric temper, seek to irritate him. Pretend to be weak, that he may grow arrogant. \n\nThese military devices, leading to victory, must not be divulged beforehand.\n\nMany calculations lead to victory and few to defeat-how much more no calculation at all? \nBy these deliberations one should be able to forcast who will win, or lose.\n\n(II) Waging War\n\nA standing army is expensive. For the mail of the sergeants, for the gambesons of the crossbowmen, for the helmets of them all-one can expect a cost of 1000 ounces of silver per day. Such is the cost of raising a standing sortie.\n\nWhen you engage in actual fighting, if victory is long in coming, then men's weapons will grow dull and their ardor will be damped. If you lay siege to a town, you will exhaust your strength.\n\nTherefore, one must keep only a small\n\nstanding force, and rely mainly on levies, but use the standing force to win a quick victory. \n\nAgain, if the campaign is protracted, the resources of the State will not be equal to the strain.\n\nNow, when your weapons are dulled, your ardor damped, your strength exhausted and your treasure spent, other counts and dukes will come and take advantage of your exetremity. A skillful soldier need not raise a second levy,\n\nnor are his supply wagons loaded more than twice. Hence a wise commander makes a point of foraging from the enemy.\n\nBring war material with you from home, but forage on the enemy. Thus the army will have food enough for its needs.\n\nNow in order to kill the enemy, our men must be roused to anger; that there may be advantage from defeating the enemy, they must have their rewards.\n\nEstates to the officers, subdivisions of the county to knights.\n\nWealth to the miltias, wine to the sergeants, etc. When a regiment of troops kills or captures a noble, they should be rewarded. They should be given half the ransom price.\n\n(III) Attack by Strategum.\n\nIn the practical art of war, the best thing of all is to take the enemy's country whole and intact; to shatter and destroy it is not so good.\n\nSo, too, it is better to recapture an army entire than to destroy it, to capture a regiment, a detachment or a company entire than to destroy them.\n\nHence to fight and conquer in all your battles is not supreme excellence; supreme excellence consists in breaking the enemy's resistance without fighting.\n\nThus the highest form of generalship is to balk the enemy's plans; the next best is to prevent the junction of the enemy's forces;the next in order is to attack the enemy's army in the field; the worse of all is to besiege walled cities.\n\nThe rule is, not to besiege walled cities if it can possibly be avoided.\n\nThe preparation of mantlets, movable shelters, and various implements of war, will take up three whole months;\n\nand the piling up of mounds over against the walls will take three months more.\n\nhe general, unable to control his irritation, will launch his men to the assault like swarming ants, with the result that one-third of his men are slain, while the town still remains untaken. Such are the disastrous effects of a siege.\n\nTherefore the skillful leader subdues the enemy's troops without any fighting; he captures their cities without laying siege to them; he overthrows their kingdom without lengthy operations in the field.\n\nWith his forces intact he will dispute the mastery of the Empire, and thus, without losing a man, his triumph will be complete. This is the method of attacking by stratagem.\n\nIt is the rule in war, if our forces are ten to the enemy's one, to surround him; if five to one, to attack him; if twice as numerous, to divide our army into two.\n\nIf equally matched, we can offer battle; if slightly inferior in numbers, we can avoid the enemy; if quite unequal in every way, we can flee from him.\n\nTherefore, there are five esstentials for victory.\n\n(I)-He will win who knows when to fight and when not to fight.\n\n(II)-He will win who, prepared, waits to take the enemy unprepared.\n\n(III)He will win who has the same sense of duty throughout all his ranks.\n\n(IV)He will win who can handle both supierior and inferior forces.\n\n(V)He will win who has military capacity and is not interferred with by the emperor.\n\n(IV)Tactical Dispositions\n\nThe good fighters of old first put themselves beyond the possibility of defeat, and then waited for an opportunity of defeating the enemy.\n\nTo secure ourselves against defeat lies in our own hands, but the opportunity of defeating the enemy is provided by the enemy himself.\n\nThus the good fighter is able to secure himself against defeat, but cannot make certain of defeating the enemy.\n\nHence the saying: One may know how to conquer without being able to do it.\n\nSecurity against defeat implies defensive tactics; ability to defeat the enemy means taking the offensive.\n\n Standing on the defensive indicates insufficient strength; attacking, a superabundance of strength.\n\nThe general who is skilled in defense hides in the most secret recesses of the earth; he who is skilled in attack flashes forth from the topmost heights of heaven.\n\nThus on the one hand we have ability to protect ourselves; on the other, a victory that is complete. \n\nTo see victory only when it is within the ken of the common herd is not the acme of excellence.\n\nNeither is it the acme of excellence if you fight and conquer and the whole Empire says, \"Well done!\"\n\nTo lift an autumn hair is no sign of great strength; to see the sun and moon is no sign of sharp sight; to hear the noise of thunder is no sign of a quick ear.\n\nWhat the ancients called a clever fighter is one who not only wins, but excels in winning with ease.\n\nHe wins his battles by making no mistakes. Making no mistakes is what establishes the certainty of victory, for it means conquering an enemy that is already defeated.\n\nHence the skillful fighter puts himself into a position which makes defeat impossible, and does not miss the moment for defeating the enemy.\n\nThus it is that in war the victorious strategist only seeks battle after the victory has been won, whereas he who is destined to defeat first fights and afterwards looks for victory.\n\nhe consummate leader cultivates the moral law, and strictly adheres to method and discipline; thus it is in his power to control success.\n\nMeasurement owes its existence to Earth; Estimation of quantity to Measurement; Calculation to Estimation of quantity; Balancing of chances to Calculation; and Victory to Balancing of chances.\n\n victorious army opposed to a routed one, is as a pound's weight placed in the scale against a single grain.\n\nThe onrush of a conquering force is like the bursting of pent-up waters into a chasm a thousand fathoms deep.", ""),
        StaticBook("House Stafyr", "§bTheoren", "=         =\n      House Stafyr\n        --------\nLaw, Honor, & Loyalty\n            -+-\n\nLiving Family Members:\n-------------------Count Symon Stafyr:\n - Descendant of Galtor I. Stafyr \n\n(continued on p.2)\n\n(cont.) \nLady Milena Stafyr:\n - Born Carrion\n\nArchduchess Cecilia Winter:  \n - Descendant of the Hanethor I. Stafyr\n\nMoris Stafyr:\n - Descendant of Hanethor I. Stafyr\n(continued on p.3)\n\n(cont.)\nLady Dowager Evelyn\nStafyr: \n - Descendant of Hanethor I. Stafyr\n\nJakob Stafyr: \n - Descendant of Galtor I. Stafyr\n \n-------------------\n\n   Important Family\n-------------------\nBeorn Stafyr:\n - First known Stafyr\n\nHanethor I. Stafyr:\n - Great Great Grandfather of Godwein Stafyr\n\nGaltor I. Stafyr: \n - Brother of Hanethor.\n\nImportant family cont.\n-------------------Thondorus Stafyr:\n - Founder of Rivacheg.\n\nGraveth I. Stafyr\"\n - Former Head of House Stafyr\n\nGodwein I. Stafyr:\n - Reunited House Stafyr\n\nImportant Family cont.\n-------------------Edmund Stafyr:\n - Former head of House Stafyr. \n=================== \n     Family Tree\n             []\nhttp://goo.gl/acjJ77\n\n         History\n            -+-\n\nIt is unknown from where Beorn, as the first Stafyr originally comes from, due to the fact that there don't exist any written records anymore or there were never any.\n\n- Only the name has been passed on by speech to the descendants of his family. Godwein presumed that they might origin from earlier Hansetians, as the language his House and he himself speak do have obvious similarities and do share the same words\n\n - Likewise it is possible that Beorn has been one of them, but left due to uncertain\nreasons, establishing his first family seat back in Aegis.\n His sons Hanethor and Galtor founded the two lines of Stafyr, respectively named after them.\n\n- Godwein knows he is a direct descendant of the line of Hanethor and knows of most of the descendants, although he doesn't\nfield any further records about them. The line of Galtor might still exist, although Godwein doesn't know of any descendants of it.\n\n- Never the less, it can be assumed that Hanethor the First and Galtor fought in Aegis in order to protect their families and to let them move on to the new world.\n\n     So it was told by Graveth, son of Thondorus and grandson of Hanethor I.\nIt is known that the town of Rivacheg was founded by the remaining survivors of House Stafyr at the borders of Asulon, led by Thondorus Stafyr.\n\n- After a couple of years, Thondorus died from age or weakness, which was unknown of, letting Graveth take the seat of the head of House Stafyr.\nGraveth's reign continued the peace in Rivacheg and let the family grow again.\n\n- His three sons, of which the last one was named Hanethor II., brought new hope to the House Stafyr, as the brother of Graveth, Uron, only had a daughter, so the line of Stafyr ends there.\n\n    Godwein was born as the first son of the first son of Graveth, Theor.\nGraveth has seen that Godwein might become the next head of House Stafyr and personally started to teach him the ways of a Stafyr.\n\n   Soon after Marian's birth, Ravenna, mother of Godwein, fell under a disease, followed by her husband soon after, who had a broken heart and griefed for the remaining time. Nevertheless Godwein and his uncles uldorzil and -\n\n - Hanethor II. stood strong and kept House Stafyr up together with the now getting elderly Graveth.\n\nUnfortunately, an incident occured and a band of marrauding knights destroyed Rivacheg and killed all remaining members of House Stafyr, leaving out Godwein and Graveth. Whilst Godwein returned and saw the destruction of his home and family, Graveth -\n\n - watched him leave again. Soon though a distant cousin of Godwein visited the ruins of Rivacheg, finding Graveth, who\nthen left into the forests to die alone, taking a last letter from him. This letter reached Godwein, containing Graveth's last wish.\n\n    Godwein led the house for a long time and had two sons with his wife Alesira, Graveth II. and Theor II., During his leadership the family grew in size as Stafyrs from all of Oren started to come back, the house established its first hold in Elysium -\n\n - in the small fort  village of Huntshill, where the life of House Stafyr flourished and the house gained more political influence in the Holy Oren Empire as Godwein was named Lord Privy Seal by the emperor. When the island group of Kalos was discovered\n\n- House Stafyr together with House Valois built a large manor on top of a mountain, overlooking the island claimed by Oren.\n\n   It was on this island Godwein's two sons, Graveth II. who was supposed to be heir but had been disinherited after violating the family words and Theor II. Who had been named heir instead dissapeared.\n\n   It was on this island Godwein's two sons, Graveth II. who was supposed to be heir but had been disinherited after violating the family words and Theor II. Stafyr continued to prosper, Godwein was named Lord Treasurer and Grand Justice -\n\n- by the emperor but he would later succumb to the stress of it all and the simple lumberjack who had managed to become one of the most powerful men in Oren had now died, his heart failing him. Many years later the church would canonize Godwein,\n\n - naming him Saint Godwein of Huntshill\n\n  His great nephew Ser Edmund was now made to rule House Stafyr.\nEdmund being a very content man in life had no aim to seek glory or power, his main wish was to keep the legacy of Godwein and House Stafyr safe. He had for some years been a knight in the -\n\n - Chapter of the Lion and his main duty was to that, fighting the enemies of Oren and protecting the empire and the imperial family. Edmund managed to keep House Stafyr out of most internal conflicts, mainly the Teuton - Flay war, where they were neutral\n\n  The everyday life of Stafyr would soon be shattered as Edmund and his younger brother Ser Farley I. got into a fight, swords were drawn and shortly afterwards Farley was lying in his own bloodkilled by his own brother and Lord Daniel Baelish\n\nEdmund had loved his brother and it shattered him, he would lock himself into his study for days, injuring himself for no apparent reason.\nHis wounds would shortly afterwards get infected, sending him to bed with a high fever -\n\n - but in this fever he had a dream and once he felt better he suddenly left, naming his sister Leana and cousin Alistier lord regents in his absence.\n\n   Edmund had departed to the holy lands, to fight in the crusades to redeem himself in the eyes of the creator, leaving his wife, his daughter and his entire family at home whilst he fought for the creator and Oren. A year later he would return home -\n\n -  to his family, resuming lordship of the house.\nThe house would remain stable for a few years and a son would be born to Lord Edmund and Lady Angelika, they named him Farley II.\n\n  Shortly after the great exodus of Oren, Edmund was named lord privy seal of the faltering empire and made Knight Commander of Renatus in the Holy Order of the Red Dragon, but only a few months after Edmund died, infected with TB.\n\n  His son Farley II., who was only four years old inherited the lordship and his aunt Leana was named lady regent.\nOren was in chaos, war ravaged the lands as different noble houses grasped for power and land. -\n\n- To protect House Stafyr from the madness the house vanished from the face of Anthos, barricaded into the Shadowcastle.\n\n  At sixteen years old Farley took over House Stafyr and the house returned from it's hiding, once again ready to return into the political game that is Oren.\n\n-------------------\n\n - Transcript published and copied by Imperial Scribe, \nTheoren Siege", ""),
        StaticBook("Anon. Battle Acc", "§bGrandmaster Jack Rovin", "The Battle Of ShadowCastle -=-=-=--==-=-=-=--=With many on both sides, we prepare for the rage of war.\n\nMany will be lost in the waking hours of morn as the battle begins.\n\nWe marched to the Keep, assembling our\n\ntroops. I see an Orc, he is armored as we are, he will fight for us. I grin, tis good to see the races banding together to take down a greater foe. \n\nDay 2\n=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=I shake in anticipation for the final march to begin. Weapons are pa\n\n-ssed out in preparation of the coming bloodshed. I clutch a diomond axehead and munch n bread; My uncle is nowhere to be seen. I look to the Silverblade prince, Cedric. I wonder how he will fare in such a dark war.\nBrome Underhill, our o\n\n-nly Halfling on our side, clutches his minature blade, a sadness in his eyes;\nHe does not wish for the death of anyone, but in recent times, his people have been forced to take aggressive measures.\nOi am one of the few dwarves to be foiten with the Teila\n\n-arian Legion, the otehrs have allready left.\n\n14th of the Amber cold, 1447\n=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=My two uncles, Loric And Elmore, have yet to appear for battle, Oi am worried fer there sake...\n\n18th of the Amber Cold-1447\n=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=\nWe marched through the snowy moutains, finding refuge in our keep. I am manning a trebuchet with Cedric SilverBlade, he is a good youngin, and we prepare for the battle. Mister Underhill has found himself a\n\nbow, and searches for arrows in the many chests. As I load the treb, I hear Lord Silverblade barking orders. I see the other teams on the trebuchets, cranking it into postion. The enemies castle is viewable from our perch on the castle wall...\n\n19th of the Amber cold-1447\n=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=We have waited an entire day and night, I do not know what the enemy is planning, but they have yet to attack. The men grow restless. Suddenly, A young guard shouts out \"The elves are here, they have arriv\n\nied!\" Oi sprung into action, grabbing hold of my axe. The captain orders us to the front line, but Cedric tells me to stay put. He is a bit snobby for a prince, but oi listen to em, and stay by the trebuchet. I here the battle begin. Mister Underhill char\n\n-ges over the wall and runs to the battle field. He fights bravely, but stumbles into a trap. With nothing else to do, he prays to a wizard, and is teleported back to his home. Me and Cedric jump the wall and charge in....", ""),
        StaticBook("Canons of Honour", "§bBerilac Goldworthy", "\"Canons of Honour\"\n\nAs written, the following cards explain the meaning of Aesculian honour - Bastokarla - as based upon the Resol'nare, six, last commandments of St. Elam left to the Descendants and upheld by followers of Fjordin Sylver.\n\nBa'jur - Education.\n\nSome believe that children are a blank card. Some others find faith in the belief that we are born, and die, with certain knowledge encrypted in our heads like incantations upon a blade. Education means many things. Sometimes, it is\n\nteaching skills, abilities. How to plow a field, write and read, hammer the red hot ferrum. We learn, and so we forge ourselves. But as we are young, we've usually little to no true ability to discern evil from good - and to choose the right path for us\n\nto follow. Children are aenguls, but aenguls easily misguided and even more so easy to destroy. It is up to us as parents and Askalians whole to uphold the traditions and pass them onto the new generations, meaning that so long as at least a single\n\nchild of Sylver lives, true honour will never perish. \nRaise your child to be strong and resistant, capable of looking after itself and it's fellow generation, teach it kindness for any good hearts and despisal for the signs of vileness - of weakness and\n\nyou shall know that you are following the right path. \n\nBeskar'gam - Armor\n\nA warrior is more then his armor, and that is to be remembered. But for the Sylva'ada, armor is a sign of solidarity and honour, it represents everything that we are or want to be\n\namong the Descendants - it is meant to be pure and strong. It is a sign of companionship, for in the armor, we all are the same, men and women, Descendant races all alike - so that we can always tell ourselves from aruetii.\n\nAra'nov - Defense\n\nDefense means many things for our people. It means standing up for our ideals, people and things we love. \n\nIt also means our ability to defend ourselves and each other that we pride ourselves in - for just as our bodies are shielded\n\nby our armor and training, our souls remain pure and strong, burning bright whereas those of the degenerate are but fickle lights above the swamp. \nIt means something we all should remember - that a true warrior isn't an aggressor. A true warrior is a\n\nprotector, a defensor. We must value the lives bestowed upon the oyu'baat, the multiverse as much as we value the ability to take it, lest we all become degenerates and bootlicks to Iblees, and forsake our ideals and the one, true honour.\n\nAliit - Tribe\n\nBack, long before the colony was settled, each of us had a different past and a different story to tell. Misfits, deserters, outcasts, united together by a hope for prosperity, led to the first of our fatherlands by the vision of our\n\nbeloved leader - and soon, did we realize, that what lives we had before didn't matter greatly - we were free from our sins and from our past. From our secrets and our downfalls. We weren't who we used to be. We were children of Sylver, and we were born\n\nanew to work for bettering of ourselves, each other, and oya'baat itself. We fought, we bled, and we grew accustomed to each other. Whomever we were before, we were one people - if perhaps a people missing identity. Resol'nare provided us with that, and\n\nwe proudly upheld the name of Sylva'ada, swearing to protect each other and our homeland, our business and families.\n\nSylalat - Language\n\nA grandchild to the speech of Bastura, Sylalat was developped for the simple mean of creating a personal, private form of communication to strenghten our identity. Simplistic, gender-neutral and highly dependant\n\non context, it remains in our grasp as something to wield with just as much pride as we wield our weapons, as we don our armor. Sylalat gives us the ability to speak without aruetii intrusion on our privacy, leaving any Sylva'ada truly\n\nfree in speech and manners, while also providing us with a single language we can use, for regardless who we used to be, where we came from, what tongue do we speak, one can always know that there is at least one common ground for us to speak in - one\n\nthat we all know and remember for days that were, and those to come.\nWield it with pride, and you shall do honour to each other and the legacy you leave for your children to carry.\n\nSylva'alor - Our Leader\n\nDerriving from the tradition of Basta'alor, the Sole Ruler of Honour, men and women of Sylvaestina who became Aesculians call their single leader Sylva'alor, in honour of the ambitious governor who brought them all together\n\n- Fjordin Sylver, a wood elf born and a Orenian bred, who, with the wise king Olivier de Savoie's blessing began settling magnitude of individuals in the area of mesa that'd later be known as Provincia Sylvaestina.\n\nSylva'alor isn't a king, and he isn't a lordling, in spite of the habit of Orenians calling the ruler of clans \"Lord Sylvalore\" or just \"Lord Sylver\" - the man or woman assuming that rank isn't just a blueblood. He or she is the first among equals, the\n\nparamount example of ideals that tied us together, burning with the same spirit that made first of us follow our beloved leader - he or she has to be a warrior, general and patriarch or matriarch at once. While the family may be staying\n\nautonomous in many a case, governing itself whenever it be necessary, it was and always is our duty to rally to our leader's call - to answer the call of the one we deemed worthy to be the living avatar of our honour, our faith and our indomitable\n\ndetermination to survive and combat Iblees and his bootlicks at any chance possible. We are Sylva'ada, and we shall carry the title of children of Sylver with same pride an aruetii may call himself a Vladov or de Sola. We are one people, even if many.\n\nThree Daemonic Warnings:\n\nOne - Never hurt children, for they are the greatest gift Descendants were given above all else.\n\nTwo - Always repay in kind. For kindness, kindness, for joy, joy, for sorrow, sorrow, and for blood, blood.\n\nThree - Remember that regardless of who you are, and how unremarkable you may be, you are always one of many - and together, you can achieve so much more.\n\nFor those seeking guidance: On traditions.\n\n\"Sha'kajir\" - \"Over a meal\" - as a people that rarely have a good chance to feast together, it should always be perceived as a celebration. Some of us do truly view it as the most extravagant and noble way\n\nof debating over matters, or deciding for a business contract, usually over meals usually taking longer to eat, such as snacks and soothing wine. \n\n\"Darasuum\" - Eternity.\nLove and marriage.\n\nLove is a quirky little thing. Can be infatuation. Can be\n\nsome charms being cast down like sunlit sprinkles of dust. But sometimes, that feeling is true and overcoming - and when the two feel like it, they may wish to take another promise for each other. Some of the romantic traditions among our folks include\n\nthe heads of respective families blessing the two and aiding them into an exchange of gift, after which comes the exchange of words:\n\"Mhi solus tome, mhi solus dar'tome, mhi me'dinui an, mhi ba'juri verde.\"\n\n\"We are one when together, we are one when parted, we will share all, we will raise warriors.\"\n\nFamily and adoption:\n\n\"Aliit ori'shya tal'din\" or \"family is more then blood\" pretty much sums up the sincere belief that blood ties may have worth, but in the long run, don't mean as much as those we choose to establish and uphold.\n\nThat is also why among us it is possible for children to denounce parents unworthy of their love - and as such, carry out a parental divorce, shedding any allegiance they owed to them - sometimes so that they can ensure the cin vhetin, pure start, not\n\nunlike the \"first turn of the screw\" rule, of their life among Sylva'ada - where they might be adopted. Traditional ritual includes sharing sangu'gal, bloodwine, with following words: \n\"Ni kar'tayl gai sa'ad.\" \"I know your name as my child.\"\n\nFor bloodlines and lineage mean little to those that never rest in their war with Iblees. Gar taldin ni jaonyc; gar sa buir, ori'wadaas'la, as the saying goes, ranging back to the first days of Sylvaestian rebirth:\n\n\"Bloodline is not important, but you as a parent, are most important of things.\" or as some interpret it, nobody cares who your parents were, only the parent you will be - emphasizing the importance of our role in raising the future generations, and\n\nstating clearly that an individual is judged by more then his ancestors.\n\nRemembrance - no man or woman has truly passed away while they are remembered - and while the joys of paradise may be at their reach, we, who sweat for the divine work, rarely remember that - knowing only the bitter emptyness that was left in our heart at\n\nloved one's passing. Some find comfort in aay'han, meditation of balance - joy of solidarity and companionship of fellow Sylva'ada filling their hearts, while still tearing those with memory of the brothers and sisters lost, a sensation at the verge of\n\npain. Bittersweet it is, and some of us simply find ourselves in remembering - every day, once, at the passing of hours, do we kneel and praying, do we carry out the remembrance of ours, contemplating on our future in heaven.\n\n\"Ni su'cuyi, gar kyr'adyc, ni partayli, gar darasuum\" - \"I am still alive, but you are dead. I remember you, so that you are eternal.\", do we say, speaking out all those we wish to remember, whom we held dear, respected or loved.\n\nRet'lini - while the canons are your guide, you must remember, that the only constant in the multiverse is change - and that over, even if our honour shall still apply, many things may change - and you shouldn't struggle. For the beauty of shereshoy, our\n\njoy and thirst for life and combat, lies in countless means to interpret it - and our ability to cull down the poor interpretations, and uphold those worthy of passing onto new generations. I, for one, Berilac of clan Nightgrove, wish you, children of\n\nSylver and God, good luck. K'oyacyi. Oya Sylva!", ""),
        StaticBook("Blood", "§bCorvo", "Blood, Such a liquid appears red and rests inside a living being’s body. In some occasions it has two different types of sensations to it, For some sensations of heat and for others sensation of cold.\n\n Blood can appear in the real world when a human or creature’s body is slashed or cut with a sharp knife or even an arrow. It appears in the color red sometimes signaling war. However the color red as well has to different meanings,\n\nWar and love. This love meaning originates from that of the rose, Such wonderful and majestic petals cover this plant and usual is brought to those by others who have intentions of love.\n\nThis is how the rose and the color red is able to symbol that of Love. As well the symbol of war comes from blood as during such a fantastic slaughter in war the floors and everything around the battle ground and exchanging of blades is coated in such\n\npure… Red… Liquid. As such people have used the color red to symbolise that of war. Blood seems to be flowed out all through our bodies, Its origins from that of the heart. A strange part of our body that creates such gorgeous\n\nliquid and uses it to give us energy. It seems that when we are in trouble or in times of tiredness the heart creates blood faster and faster in order to replace that of the energy we lost.\n\nThe taste of such a thing tells us more about it, It seems to be a bitter taste with a strange sensation to that of metal or iron. Using such it seems that our bodies are a giant magnet for that of electricity and that of an electromancer.\n\nAs such blood could be used as a magnet in order to redirect or direct a mage’s electricity to another. As well on the matter of consumption of such blood it seems that our energy is not even replenished when we are to drink it.\n\nPerhaps there is something more.. Perhaps that there is a special system which this blood flows around in our bodies not actually piling up in one spot. Perhaps some sort of tube which the blood flows  through in order to be brought to all\n\nsections of our own body, Which would explain the reason blood on consumption does not restore that of our own energy. Now let us continue. When one is to breath in air from the sky I would believe it heads straight into our own heart and into our blood.\n\nI think such as when we become tired a strange sensation in our heart comes as well it seems that we breath heavily in order to supply our own hearts with more air. As it seems to command our own brains into making us do so.\n\nNow for the flow off our blood. It seems that these tubes are everywhere in our bodies, However they seem to have the appearance of the color green inside of our bodies, As I am able to see from the top of our hand.\n\nNow if I think about it our bodies are full of many objects, That these tubes do not have space for, Perhaps these tubes wrap themselves around such objects in our body in order to supply their fuel to them,\n\n As well I would like to think that we have smaller tubes inside our bodies, Tubes that are all different sizes and supply the flow of our blood all over inside of us. This also explains why when a part of our body is cut,\n\nWhatever it may be it begins to overflow the area with the strange red liquid which is our blood.  I am not sure but it seems some animals have a different color of blood inside their own body then us, Such as the insects and birds that have blue\n\nblood inside them, I would think that this symbolises the sensations of cold inside their own blood. As well that their blood is much different than our own. It would seem that we are also able to control our own blood by controlling the sensation of heat\n\nand cold inside it as well as controlling the flow off it. From what I feel, I was able to control the blood inside my body and to focus a large sum of blood to flow to one area will the rest focuses on heading towards the other.\n\nThis has occurred from when I have thought about what I wished to do with said blood, I thus forced that command into my brain and caused it to do what I wished. That is all I have to say for now, The rest I leave to you,\n\nThe future generations of this world.", ""),
        StaticBook("Politica", "§bAmbros", "Politica\n\nBy Ambros\n\nNOT exalting worth keeps the people from rivalry. Not prizing what is hard to procure keeps the people from theft. Not to show them what they may covet is the way to keep their minds from disorder.\n\nTherefore the Sage, when he governs, empties their minds and fills their bellies, weakens their inclinations and strengthens their bones. His constant object is to keep the people without knowledge and without desire, or to prevent those who have\n\nknowledge from daring to act. He practises inaction, and nothing remains ungoverned.\n\nHe who respects the State as his own person is fit to govern it. He who loves the State as his own body is fit to be entrusted with it.\n\nIn the highest antiquity, the people did not know that they had rulers. In the next age they loved and praised them. In the next, they feared them. In the next, they despised them.\nHow cautious is the Sage, how sparing of his words! When his task is\n\naccomplished and affairs are prosperous, the people all say: \"We have come to be as we are, naturally and of ourselves.\"\n\nIf any one desires to take the Empire in hand and govern it, I see that he will not succeed. The Empire is a divine utensil which may not be roughly handled. He who meddles, mars. He who holds it by force, loses it.\n\nFishes must not be taken from the water: the methods of government must not be exhibited to the people.\n\nUse uprightness in ruling a State; employ stratagems in waging war; practise non-interference in order to win the Empire. Now this is how I know what I lay down:--\n\nAs restrictions and prohibitions are multiplied in the Empire, the people grow poorer and poorer. When the people are subjected to overmuch government, the land is thrown into confusion. When the people are skilled in many cunning arts, strange are the\n\nobjects of luxury that appear.  The greater the number of laws and enactments, the more thieves and robbers there will be. Therefore the Sage says: \"So long as I do nothing, the people will work out their own reformation. So long as I love calm, the\n\npeople will right themselves. If only I keep from meddling, the people will grow rich. If only I am free from desire, the people will come naturally back to simplicity.\"\n\nIf the government is sluggish and tolerant, the people will be honest and free from guile. If the government is prying and meddling, there will be constant infraction of the law. Is the government corrupt? Then uprightness becomes rare, and goodness\n\nbecomes strange. Verily, mankind have been under delusion for many a day!\n\nGovern a great nation as you would cook a small fish.\n\nIf the Empire is governed according to virtue, disembodied spirits will not manifest supernatural powers. It is not that they lack supernatural power, but they will not use it to hurt mankind. Again, it is not that they are unable to hurt mankind, but\n\nthey see that the Sage also does not hurt mankind. If then neither Sage nor spirits work harm, their virtue converges to one beneficent end.  In ancient times those who knew how to practise Tao did not use it to enlighten the people, but rather to keep\n\nthem ignorant. The difficulty of governing the people arises from their having too much knowledge.\n\nIf the people do not fear the majesty of government, a reign of terror will ensue.\n\nDo not confine them within too narrow bounds; do not make their lives too weary. For if you do not weary them of life, then they will not grow weary of you.\n\nIf the people do not fear death, what good is there in using death as a deterrent? But if the people are brought up in fear of death, and we can take and execute any man who has committed a monstrous crime, who will dare to follow his example?\n\nNow, there is always one who presides over the infliction of death. He who would take the place of the magistrate and himself inflict death, is like one who should try to do the work of a master-carpenter. And of those who try the work\n\nof a master-carpenter there are few who do not cut their own hands.\n\nThe people starve because those in authority over them devour too many taxes; that is why they starve. The people are difficult to govern because those placed over them are meddlesome; that is why they are difficult to govern. The people despise death\n\nbecause of their excessive labour in seeking the means of life; that is why they despise death.\n\nA Sage has said: \"He who can take upon himself the nation's shame is fit to be lord of the land. He who can take upon himself the nation's calamities is fit to be ruler over the Empire.\"\nWere I ruler of a little State with a small population, and only ten\n\nor a hundred men available as soldiers, I would not use them. I would have the people look on death as a grievous thing, and they should not travel to distant countries. Though they might possess boats and carriages, they should have no occasion to ride\n\nin them. Though they might own weapons and armour, they should have no need to use them. I would make the people return to the use of knotted cords. They should find their plain food sweet, their rough garments fine. They should be content with their\n\nhomes, and happy in their simple ways. If a neighbouring State was within sight of mine--nay, if we were close enough to hear the crowing of each other's cocks and the barking of each other's dogs--the two peoples should grow old and die without there\n\never having been any mutual intercourse.", ""),
        StaticBook("Notes on Scourge", "§bShady_Tales", "Scourge Research\n--------------------\nBy Dresmorlin Natura, Leader of the Psijic Circle\n\nTable of Contents\n1. Figures of Interest\n2. Harbingers\n3. Magic\n4. Staffs\n5. Prophet\n6. Blood Shards\n7. Beasts\n\nAruzond\nHarbingers\nProphet\nWither\nSkeleton King\nJaime Loken\n\nHarbingers:\nBlue- The original members of the Golden Lance\n\nRed- The new harbingers who proved themselves.\n\nMagic:\nFrost fire: A deadly attack that acts like frostbite.\nSummoning: Summoning of creatures\n\nStaffs: \nMade of Iron, but contain massive taint, overwhelming to the mind and soul, do not cleanse, do not use, just destroy them with in a pit of lava!\n\nProphet:\nThe beast they use to decide who is worthy to be corrupted. Extremely dangerous, do not approach, just run. It has multiple ways of attacking, the first is translocating upon you and hitting you with unnatural force, and the second is it's claws.\n\nBlood Shards:\nRed Chrystals of death and corruption. If one is placed inside you the corruption spreads through your body and controls you, the master of the scourge takes control of your will and you fight for him. Be cautious and do NOT touch or go near\n\nBeasts:\n\n*The rest of the book appears to be unfinished, the last few pages being splattered with blood. There is a note on the next page.*\n\n~I found this notebook off the corpse of an elf during a battle in Lenfarthing to push the scourge back out. The Elf was impaled and hung over the wall. Although the work he was carrying is unfinished, perhaps what is here can be put to good use. I pray\n\nthat we find the solution to the Scourge problem soon.\n\nDeus Magnus, Deus Vult.         -Ser Kato", ""),
        StaticBook("On Dragonkin", "§bAruzond, Warden of the North", "On Dragonkin\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWritten and recorded by Aruzond, Warden of the North\n\nThere are two species of dragonkin known to have descended from Dragur, though corrupted and defiled forms of both have formerly existed. Dragur, the Daemon of Knowledge, oftentimes appears as a dragon in his true form.\n\nDragur’s chief descendants, the Dragaar, are sentient “greater dragons” that seek to collect and share knowledge with mortals. They can conceal themselves under the guise of mortals and other creatures, though it is a rare occasion to meet a Dragaar\n\nin person. Dragons, on the other hand, are more common -- though proportionately rare compared to other living beings -- than greater dragons. They are generally referred to as dragons, though the proper classification is “lesser dragon.\"\n\n They are non sentient, and are commonly viewed as natural predators that roam our world. However, like all living beings, they are susceptible to taint and corruption. Though a rare occasion, dragons have been known to alter their very nature\n\ninto a twisted, foul portrayal consumed by hatred and rage for the mortal races. The outcome of this calamity is referred to as a drake. Little is known about the purpose of the dragonkin as a whole, except that each species' intentions vary greatly.\n\nDragon\n \n    The dragon (or lesser dragon) species is beastlike in nature, and generally accepted to be non sentient. Though they cannot process thought or comprehend concepts, they are highly intelligent in regards\n\nto instinct. The lesser dragon was once a rare sight to mortals, but they have appeared to exist in greater numbers in Anthos. Particularly, dragons roam the frozen wastelands of the North. Though they live in other regions of Anthos,\n\nthe frigid North is home to many deep caves and caverns, which they prefer for nesting purposes. The region is cold, secluded, and tribesmen allow the food supply to remain steady. Dragons may roam the land of Anthos in search of cattle or mortals if\n\nthey grow tired of pork flesh, and they continue to hunt until they are satisfied. Although lesser dragons have been frequently encountered in recent times, their numbers are slowly dwindling as they have begun to retreat to the deep, cold North.\n\nDrake\n \n    “Do not underestimate the allure of darkness, mortal. Your kind has yet to discover that even the purest of souls are drawn to it.” -Aruzond\n\nThrough the malevolent desires of twisted corruption, the dragon has the potential to unwillingly alter its very existence. When a dragon degrades into a drake, its body decays into a shadowy skeleton of its former self. The scales of a drake turn black\n\nas midnight, and their fangs cloak themselves in putrefying poison. While a drake does not develop sentience, its desires can be manipulated to follow any malignant alignment. Drakes forfeit any connection to their former selves,\n\nand their lust for eternal damnation of life causes them to instill fear into those who would oppose it. The drake is slightly smaller in size compared to a dragon, but what it lacks in size and physical strength is compensated by maneuverability\n\nand lethality. It is rare to encounter an evil powerful enough to twist and taint the mind of the mighty dragon, but these influences communicate through our world more readily than we may often realize.\n\nDragaar\n\nBestowed with the gift of immortality and sentience, the Dragaar exist chiefly to seek and gather knowledge. Each Dragaar is assigned the role of gathering a distinct aspect of knowledge, and this role defines their characteristics\n\nand demeanor. However, they do not solely desire knowledge and wisdom. The greater dragons, or Dragaar, act as ever watching wardens over the mortal races. However, most prefer to live in seclusion in deep thought and meditation, only to emerge when\n\n the races call upon them in their time of need. The Dragaar can and often disguise themselves under the guise of a mortal or creature. This is their preferred method of watching over mortals, as it is both subtle and inconspicuous. There are only two\n\nDragaar that have revealed themselves to the mortal races, and the decision to do so was not lightly concluded. Though the Dragaar seek to protect the mortal races, they often decide to do so by living in secrecy. Many discern that if they chose to\n\n associate themselves with mortal affairs too frequently, dire consequences would arise. The primary concern held by Dragaar, in regards to involvement in the matters of mortals, is that the races would eventually resort to relying on them to protect\n\n mortals from any form of evil. However, the greater dragons aspire to teach the mortal races self-preservation, without them depending on the intervention of immortals.\n\nDrakaar\n\nTo attempt to bend the will of a Dragaar would be all for naught, and thus what can cause a Dragaar to devolve itself into a twisted abomination of hatred is unknown. The ancient ancestors of the four mortal races once believed\n\nthat the choice for this horrendous transformation to occur was their own, though the aftermath of such a decision would contradict their entrusted duty. The greater dragon itself is a terrifying opponent, but a Dragaar that feels no remorse and\n\nfears no consequence? That is the true fear of corruption; the perversion of the mind and distortion of one’s morality is a dreadful circumstance. In past times, the outcome of such an event would often be referred to as a “greater drake.”\n\nIt is the combination of a Dragaar’s sentience and a drake’s all-consuming hatred. The likelihood of a Dragaar devolving into a being tormented by rage is next to none, as most would, in their right mind, turn themselves into stone to prevent the\n\nconversion. The only verifiable event recorded of a Dragaar turning to stone is that of Velketzar. The Guardian Velketzar, consumed by self-condemnation and despair due to his failure, dramatically transformed into a stone statue\n\nto prevent the inevitable calamity. If a Dragaar were to fall under the hand of corruption, it would hold the power to politically corrupt nations with ease. Additionally, detecting and pursuing such a foe would be toilsome, all due to its shape shifting\n\nability. Fortunately, deep meditation and concentration can inhibit this rare occurrence, along with immersing themselves in their aspect of knowledge.", ""),
        StaticBook("Vourukh V. I", "§bProfessor Franklin Gavin", "Brief description of the Vourukh\n\n\nThe Vourukh (Voo-rook) would be classified as birds of prey, to those who would call them birds. ((In OOC contemporary terms, they would be described as theropod-esque as\n\nwell as raptorial)). A normal venturer of Athera would mistake it for a dragon, however. Their thick, leathery skin, which very much resemble scales, are partially the cause of this misidentification.\n\nThe hide is on average two centimeters thick, a quarter thicker around the underside and the talons, and a half thinner at the spot atop the head, where the skull joins to the spine. This thick skin protects the Vourukh from many perils,\n\nincluding the attacks of other animals.The beak is short and hooked, like many other raptors and the neck of the Vourukh is short and immensely powerful. This is beneficial as it allows the Vourukh to apply\n\ntremendous force, used for cracking and shattering bones.The beak of the Vourukh differs from other predatory birds though in that it is serrated. Towards the front end of the hooked beaksharp and small serrations are spaced between larger\n\npointed ones, which are pointed back, so as to savagely tear the flesh of prey attempting to escape. These front serrations are used primarily for predation, and are frightfully effective.\n\nThe serrations towards the base of the beak, however, are smaller and less pointed. Similar to mammalian molars, these back serrations are used for grinding and crushing. In the Vourukh’s case, it is the grinding and crushing of bones.\n\nOn the beak are four nares, two larger ones closer to the eyes, and two smaller ones further down the beak. It is believed that the larger ones are solely for breathing while the smaller ones are solely for smelling.\n\nThe eyes of the Vourukh are red, like their feathers, and the irises range from a deep amber to a golden yellow. The bones of the Vourukh are solid, very strong, and reinforced, comparable to that of sturdy mammals.\n\nThis is not beneficial to the flight of the Vourukh, as it limits them from large migratory patterns. Otherwise, it allows the Vourukh to absorb heavier blows.\n\nAs well, stronger and more lean muscles (compared to other birds) can develop without risk to the integrity of the bones. The Vourukh’s feet are anisodactyl, adapted to grip prey, but are also similar to basal theropods\n\nin structure in that the base of the feet are lifted from the ground, allowing for limited walking and running.The talons are unique in the fast rate that they grow. If left alone, the talons would grow too large and become cumbersome to the Vourukh.\n\nBecause of this, the Vourukh whittle their talons down on rocks, which sharpens the talons as well.\n\nWhen outstretched, the wingspan of the Vourukh averages around five meters.\n\nThe wings are very large and muscular, to keep the heavy body of the beasts afloat. To power these wings, the Vourukh have pectoral muscles comparable to that of an Uruk or an Olog.\n\nThe wings themselves are separated into two parts that can fold in and downwards slightly. This allows the Vourukh to catch currents in the air with more ease and more efficiently ride them.\n\nAs well, there are two long and curled talons near the first joint of the wings. These talons can bend in, and allow very limited grasping, and aid in walking on land.\n\nThe Vourukh have nearly all of their body covered by feathers, except for their feet and their wing-talons. The plumage of the top of their wings tends to be dark colored, with white to gray intermediating in streaks, whileas the plumage of\n\ntheir bodies are white. It is exceptionally rare to find a white Vourukh though, as they stain themselves red in the blood of their prey. From below, the wings are near to invisible, while the body stands out.\n\nThe tail feathers of the Vourukh are long, outspread, and rounded. Differences between make and female are few. The furcula of the female protrudes further and is thicker than the male's, and the female's tail feathers are narrower and more\n\npointed.", ""),
        StaticBook("Northal, Hanseti", "Aetosion", "The Lands of Northal in Anthos, A Compendium of Environmental and Historical Information\n~~~~~~~+~~~~~~~\nby The Rt. Hon. Edward, second of his name, of the noble House Winter of the lands herein writ.\n\nNorthal --- A History\n~~~~~~~+~~~~~~~\n       Chapter I\n\nNorthal, previously within the demesne of the Kingdom of Hanseti, was never properly settled. In fact, the past human habitation of Northal is so entirely unremarkable that the writer isn't even sure the purpose of this passage. Previously unsettled, and\n\nonly recognized as arctic wasteland \"Wall-Adjacent\", it was unused by its lords, left as ice and stone. It was not until the modern lords and ladies of House Winter, and their hardy bannermen and vassals approached the steep and snowy cliffs, and said,\n\n\"Yes, this is land. It can be tamed like any other,\" and so it was. The tundras remained just as glacial, but the indomitable human spirit prevailed against the elements. The County of Northal was chartered by his grace, the Duke Codrik Green, for his new\n\nfound vassal, the Rt. Hon. Lord Robert Winter, son of Harold.\n\nNorthal --- A Land\n~~~~~~~+~~~~~~~\n       Chapter II\n\nThe lands of Northal stretch from the old Teuton bridge due east of the Castle Greywyn, to the open seas of Valence off the eastern coast of the Kingdom of Oren. Defining the Southward border is the sharp shift in flora and fauna notable where snow begins\n\nto fall south of the wall. The northern border is, of course, the North Icewall. Built by whatever force for whatever reason, no human who has dared summit it has come back entirely intact. These borders, effective by edict of his grace, the Duke Codrik\n\nGreen, determine the bounds of the land.\n\nNorthal --- A Climate\n~~~~~~~+~~~~~~~\n      Chapter III\n\nThe climate of Northal is the most entirely inhospitable south of the Wall. Any man, elf, or dwarf idiotic enough to build their home within these lands comes out a member of a hardened folk. The lands are infertile and barren, a layer of permafrost rests\n\njust beneath the soil, requiring intensive labour to render what sparse deposits of nutrient poor soil there are in Northal, usable for planting. However, it is still somehow managed, but what magics of a massed peasantry it cannot be fathomed. The\n\navailable land to be built upon is rocky and irregular. To traverse it is difficult, and to establish is even moreso. Excluding the pockets of flatland within Northal (namely, Ice Lake, Snowy Pass, and Bridge Gully), all other locales must be mined into\n\nwith a strong iron pick to break through the frozen stone and ice. It is postulated that these rare pockets are the result of glacial movements centuries past, which carved out basins into the terrain, and later melted to water the fields of Ager. The\n\nhigh temperatures of the land are rivaled only by the peaks of the Silver Mountains of Furnestock, and at only at one month during the year in the height of summer does the temperature rise above freezing, enough so for the ice of Northal to sweat and run\n\nthin streams down the cliffs into Ager, the Greymarsh, the Raev, and Norfolk. Lower down the mountains these streams gather into stillwater pools, which will continue to provide irrigation for southward farmlands, even when the water in the high up lands\n\nof Northal remains frozen. It is said that if you piss towards the Castle Greywyn in the dead of night, from any peak in Northal, that the ghost of the Hochmeister Mirtok will freeze your piss into a spear and the winds will blow it back into you, running\n\nyou through with your own piss. A nastier death a man could never fathom. The mountains of Northal are home to the frozen corpses of hermits, and the bundled men of the Winter, and none other.\n\nAn Author's Note.\n~~~~~~~+~~~~~~~~Father, why didn't you move us to Salvus. There is a complete lack of immoral Southeron noblewomen. Send help.\n-The Rt. Hon. Edward Winter II\n\n*The Snowflake Sigil\nof House Winter*", ""),
        StaticBook("Age Wisdom", "§bAmbros", "Wisdom of the Ages\n\nBy Ambros\n\nHe spake unto the multitude and said: Inasmuch as ye are led by the desire to gratify selfish propensities are ye excluded from the higher light which is the natural birthright of every soul.  Be ye seekers after the higher truths of the spirit,\n\nnot content with the vain babblings of men who are puffed up with their own self-conceits.  He that overcomes the flesh, not by crucifixion and mortification, but by sublimation, that leadeth to the complete purification of this house in which spirit\n\ndwells, shall become a leader among men, and shall know all things in heaven and earth.  Knowledge is not always gained of books,for oft-times these are misleading and unsatisfactory. Spirit must speak to spirit, and soul must vibrate responsive to the\n\ninner harmonies of the universe of the creator.  He who seeks of the spirit shall find the royal road that leadeth to the great garner house where is stored the rich fruitage of the ages.  Be not deceived of men who occupy the chief seats in the church\n\nand universities of the outer world, for these are puffed up with their own self-conceits.  They conceive of theories, then search the universe that they may find facts that seem to prove their verity.  They go no deeper than the outer husk of the\n\nexternal universe. Like children, they play with these cast-off shells. Out of these things are builded the sciences and religions of the world.  The teachings of the great masters have been misunderstood and misapplied. The world has been too engrossed\n\nwith material things to read aright the lessons given.  Only the things of the spirit are permanent. All outward things are transitory and fleeting. Vain pomp and glory of the world without life, ye flaunt your gaudy rags before eyes whose spiritual\n\nvision is sealed. Ye have no dominion over him who is baptized of the spirit.\n\nHe rises glorified and exalted into the atmosphere of gods.\n\nHe reflects no light of sun or star, but glows and shines with the inexhaustible light of spirit.\n\nHe acknowledges no leadership, either of book car man, but follows the star of his own destiny.\n\nTo, him is given the broadest liberty, for the wings of his spirit, at last, have been unfettered,\nand now they cleave the ethers of infinite space.\n\nI that speak unto ye am as deathless as the Unspeakable One. I assist at the birth of worlds and universes. I am my own star of destiny.\n\nWhat I am ye also may become. Through the gateway of suffering and poverty ye must be led until the spirit asserts itself.\n\nKnow then your oneness with the Infinite, and claim the royal birthright that is thine inheritance.", ""),
        StaticBook("Of Wood", "§bAmbros", "The Philosophy of Wood\n\nBy Ambros\n\nThe table\nno time for its\nexistentialism\nand absurd\nchair leaning against\nthe table’s futile stance.\n\nI’m a pragmatic man\nso I have no use for knowing\nmyself.\n\n\nThe table\nstudies its own nature\nby looking at its askew shade.\nChair, somberly\ncontemplating suicide\nbecause it wants to remove\nits painfully ingrown nails.\nParadoxically they keep it alive,\nin form, in function.\n\n\n\n\n\nI have only one reality and the clarity of purpose.\n\nMy furniture’s\nintrospection\nis a trifling problem\nin my busy condition.\n\nThe table has begun questioning things. \nIt likes it when I leave philosophy\non its surface. \nI hear the creaky whisper, quoting:\n‘the human wooden heart has a tiresome tendency\nto label as fate only what crushes it.’\n\nAbsurdly, the chair stares at the modernity\nof my modus operandi.\n\nI cannot be stopped to wonder. \nProgress is my mission.\n\nThe table is a stranger to itself.\nThe chair competes \n for my attention.\n\nI have appetites that the world\ncannot satisfy.\n\nTable is dissatisfied with its lucidity,\nthrough logic the chair has\narrived at the conclusion that\nknowledge is a form of chaos.\n\nI’m a man of the world in spite of everything.\n\nIn spite of poverty, war, injustice or\nmy furniture’s uncertainty and their long\nepisodes of incoherent silence.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Principle", "§bAmbros", "The Eternal Principle\n\nBy Ambros\n\nThe struggle between light and darkness, between good and evil, is as old as the world, and yet there is no principle of evil; whatever degrades is evil, whatever elevates is good.  Evil or so-called sin is simply undeveloped good.  Good and evil are\n\nthe light and shadow of the one eternal principle of life, and each is necessary for the existence of the other.  The struggle between light and shadow is life, and there can be no life without a struggle.  Matter transmits force, but does not originate\n\nforce. It is for a time the receptacle of power, but not the power. All essence of power belongs to Spirit. All pure force is invisible.  Matter and Spirit may be one to the Absolute and Infinite Being, but that one is Spirit. In human speech, matter is\n\nonly the name of an effect whose cause is wrapped in mystery.  It remains for the human to penetrate the veil and solve the mystery. And the solution of this problem of mind and matter discovers man unto himself, and binds him in loving union forever with\n\nthe Infinite \"I am,\" the Spirit of all.  Absence of feeling and of suffering only shows that the process of death has begun, or that the animal has taken full possession. Pain is not an element to be most dreaded; for, as gold is refined by fire, the\n\nSoul is refined by pain, and only through the death of suffering does the human Soul rise into eternal life.  If selfishness and the animal instinct have full sway, the Soul shrivels toward decay, while all its noble powers are congealed, its\n\nsensibilities benumbed, its vision blinded, its intellect dimmed, and from the once clear mirror the reflection of a noble Soul shines no more where the innermost temple might have been radiant with truth and virtue.\n\nDEATH—Is death more to be feared because it is an enigma the mysteries of which western dull minds, because obscured by theology, cannot fathom, or their weak fancy cannot comprehend?\n\nThe human Soul is the anchorage or place of ideas. The Astral body or star magno is the mirror that reflects and records them, human thoughts being simply the clothing of these ideas. The spirit is the self-acting energy that produces the Idea.", ""),
        StaticBook("Found By Raigeki", "§bShady_Tales", "A Song for the Forgotten\n\nFrom the shadows, you watched us leave\nTo the shadows, the dawning's eve\nOne of three, though two are known\nLast of three, the seeds were sown\nThe Lover, not you, but the one of Old\n*marked out*\n\nYour lover, not him, but the unconsoled\nThe Mother, not her, but truly you instead\nThrough stone and death, the rivers of red\nYou led, you changed, you worked two-fold\nThey followed your steps, but they no longer know.\n*the name is illegible*\n\n===================This was found by Holy Ser Raigeki Kato at the old cloud temple.", ""),
        StaticBook("Request of Bears", "§bRilkor Niwarsay", "§4A Solemnly Simple Request§0\n§0\n§7Addressed to the §2Holy Caliph, Faiz Kharadeen§0\n§0\n§7Writen by §2Herr Rilkor von Niwarsay\n\nAs in the days of the Konigreich of Aesterwald, I Rilkor Niwarsay served as the Lord Kastellan.§0\n§0I assisted in the restructuring of the city of Vanderfell and aided the Konigreich as apart of the military.\n\nI asked for little during those times and was labeled as Recognized Nobility within the rest of Athera.§0\n§0Not only this, but furthermore I had provided you with your grand city,\n\n which I still provide structure for and the structure of the Mubarizun which has been uselessly changed time again.§0\n§0If there is but anything that I ask for it is these requests be met as my loyalty is undying.\n\nFirstly, I request the title of Builder, as contributing many works to the world, I get little recognition.§0\n\nSecondly, I have provided you with a statue to be built on the east mountain of mining. Which is to be built on the donning of the expansion eastward.§0\n\nThirdly, I request my family to be seen as more than just recognized nobility but an actual noble family as I do not have accommodation of land yet. I have several sons, one of which is dead.§0\n\nFourthly, I request the underwrite of land in the future. Whether this is, preferably not now, or later. This is where I wish to build a community reminiscent of Vanderfell when I acquire a following, this will include a castle.§0\n\n§2That is all, thank you your grace.§0 May your reign be long, and your might stronger. Be carefull of your lineage as I hope your heir is as great as you are.", ""),
        StaticBook("Forest at Night", "§bLeithlan", "Walk softly through th forest at night\n Pay homage to the spirit that's there\n Kind men look at the shade and do right\n\n Good men lay down their arms and respite\n Lesser men grow scared and their eyes bright\n Wild men brandish\n\ntheir weapons this night\n\n But she is not of the dark, just light\n A remnant of a mother with care\n Whose passing came not from lack of sight\n\n But from he that slew her in the night", ""),
        StaticBook("Mysterious note", "Terafir", "I have put your redstone in a safe place. Talk to Poet if you wish to have it back. Be sure to lock your chest next time.\n\n-A", ""),
        StaticBook("The Dark One", "§bErik Stalfen", "The black Kha.. It hunts.. It shows no weakness.. Its claws tear through flesh.. \n\nThe Kha Joto acts with anger and shows no mercy. This Kha runs with great speed. He fears no one. Yet this Kha trembles at the thought of alcohol..", ""),
        StaticBook("Inforum Guide", "§bcometking123", "\"There are many things which constitute the world, including the secrets behind the masks, the meetings behind closed doors, and the unfolding of events and subterfuge. We are the light in the dark which strives to solve these puzzles.\n\nINFORUM POPULI CODE\n\nLetter will use ciphers. \nWe will be using these ciphers to encrypt our messages to keep them away from prying eyes.\n\nDO NOT IN ANY CIRCUMSTANCES LOSE THIS BOOK.\n\nKeyed Ciphers.\n\nTo encrypt a message simply move the letters down by a certain amount within a certain message. \n\nFor instance, take this phrase\n\nWords are golden.\n\nIf I were to move this phrase down one letter it would turn into:\n\nXpset bsf hpmefo\n\nTo decrypt this message, take each letter and move it up by one. For instance, X is the letter after W, so change it into W.\n\nTo signify how much to move the letters by, we will be using code. \n\nThere are two components into encrypting a message. One is the direction to move the letters (up or down) and a number to signify how far to move the letters in that direction. (1-24)\n\nTo say this, we will be using dates.\n\nUp = The Amber Cold\nDown = Malin's Welcome \nThe degree of which you will change the letters will be dependent on the day. \n\nFor example, 2nd of The Amber Cold will\n\ntell you to move the letters up 2 to figure out the message.\n\nThis would be a example letter \n---------------\n3rd of Malin's Welcome \n(3 Down to solve)\n\nPbka ebim. Zlsbo yiltk. \n----------------\nSend help. Coverblown\n\nmake it longer, but that is the idea when writing a message. \n\nDO KEEP IN MIND THAT CIPHERS ARE FOR IMPORTANT MESSAGES ONLY. For regular reports, ciphers are not nessecary, but some phrases may need to be encrypt with passcodes, which\n\nwe'll cover next. \n\nCode: To identify certain nations, informants, or volatile information, we will be using code. The glossary will be here.\n\nOrcs = Green Beans\nHumans = Pinkies\nElves = Bowmen\nDwarves = Beards\n\ninformant = Merchants secrets = goods\n\ntarget (people to spy on or to look for information) = friend\n\nInforum Populi = union \n\nmeeting = party \n\ndead-drop = Giveaway\n\nUndead = Skeletons\n\nMagicians = Wanderers \ninformation = wares\n\n*More to be added*\n\nIf you wish to use the literal meaning of a password, use a asterisk (*) next to the word.\n\nExample:\n\nA merchant has some wares for me regarding a friend. I will be at a party*, a ball really. I will be at the party for the union tomorrow as well. \nTranslation: A informant has some information regarding\n\na target. I will be at a party, a ball really. I will be at the meeting for the Inforum Populi tomorrow as well. \n---------------\nThere are also other methods of encryption, but they will be disclosed at a later time. Consider this as a beginner's guide.\n\nPlease ask me for any addendums regarding passwords and ciphers. Do not share this, and keep this amongst yourself...\n\nI hope that we'll have fun. \n\nSincerly, the Secretkeeper.", ""),
        StaticBook("Annulment Letter", "§bCaliph Arlen Kharadeen", "Faskh (Annulment) of the marriage of Malik Kharadeen and Sahárfajhari Zafeera-dai Ajaháli el-Abdulrashid\n\nBy permission of the Caliph and with blessing from Allah, this marriage which was performed lawfully in the eyes of Allah and other earthly\n\nwitnesses is henceforth desolved on the grounds of insanity, blasphemy and practice of the dark and nefarious magics by Sahárfajhari Zafeera-dai Ajaháli el-Abdulrashid.\n\nSahárfajhari Zafeera-dai Ajaháli el-Abdulrashid being the offending party,\n\nhas as witnessed by various trust worthy sources dabbled in magics deemed unlawful in the lands of Khalestine and Vandoria as a whole.Sahárfajhari Zafeera-dai Ajaháli el-Abdulrashid is also, as has been discovered recently, been found to be in league with\n\nknown traitor Nafis Yar, who in his own insanity has made attempts on both former Caliph and Iman Faiz Kharadeen as well as the Emir Alai Kharadeen. Treachary, degeneracy and practice of dark magic are deemed more than cause enough by the Caliph and in\n\nthe eyes of Allah as Valid reasons to annul to this marriage. \n\nTherefore by the authority of the Caliph Arlen Kharadeen and with divine sanction from Allah himself, the marriage of Malik Kharadeen and Sahárfajhari Zafeera-dai Ajaháli el-Abdulrashid is\n\nlawfully disolved.", ""),
        StaticBook("Two Kingdoms", "§bJorg Arramurten", "The\n    Two Kingdoms\n            A\nDramaticized Account\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nJorg Arramurten, 125\n\n..’tis only right that you take what is yours, Oron,” said the maiden, glistening with the morning light. Oron stared at the ceiling, pondering his next move. “My love, fair maid from the sky, I shall conquer this land as you wish,\n\nshould you be willing to seal our matrimony in th’after,” he said. The girl smiled. “I shall bear you many children, my lord. Strong young boys, and the most beautiful girls of the land…\n\nTogether we can rule.” And in a flash of brilliant light, the girl departed…..\n……….looked at the men in front of him in confusion and disbelief. “What treason is this?” he yelled. “Who has sent you?” Three of the darkly clad men remained silent\n\nas one of them replied: “I apologize, Lord Brador.” The knife had pierced his heart before Brador knew what had transpi………………..\n\n…..The skulduggery of the seductress changed the fate of the Two Kingdoms forever. United through cor………………ough bound by the love.", ""),
        StaticBook("Archgriffin head", "§bNafis", "The Archgriffin's head is kept cool in the ice chest.", ""),
        StaticBook("Official Format", "§bHarald Vanir II", "Official\n   Galahar Bank Note  \n-------------------\n\nThis notorized document, verified by the Lord Treasurer & Bank of Galahar, hereby authorizes the following:\n\n(CLIENTS NAME) Shall recieve (OWED AMOUNT) for the (SERVICE/PRODUCT) they have provided. This shall be paid in full no later than the elven date of (MONTH/DAY/YEAR).\n\n- The Seal of Galahar is stamped -", ""),
        StaticBook("Letter", "§bSnipes9990", "Steward,\n  I hope this letter reaches you. I seek a house in Abresi and hope you may help me. If you could, i am hanging around Abresi. Just look for a white haired elf with a red cape.\n   -Spencer H. Cross", ""),
        StaticBook("The Way of War", "§bCelcian Colin", "Chapter One: Good Leadership.\n\n      One must first take into consideration how to treat their soldiers and how to keep their moral high.\n      A great leader inspires his men and is willing to endanger himself for them. A general who stays in\n\nthe back of the battle will be safer, but his men will not have a good sense of command. The general who leads from the front can easily inspire his men and they will follow any order and die for their leader.\n\nChapter Two: Proper Strategy.\n\n      Most generals find it sufficient to allow their soliders to randomly charge the enemy with swords and axes. I call this tactic, organised foolishness. Soldiers are not simply pawns that can be thrown at the enemy,\n\nthey are useful living tools who are to be taken care of and well treated.\n       A general should properly use a diverse army consisting of archers, infintry, and a small amount of cavalry.\n       Archers should be used to pick off and slow down the\n\nenemy. The infintry should be used as a shock force, they would cover the archers and fight off the remaining enemy force. The cavalry would be used to run down the routing enemy and to charge remaining enemies and break them.\n\nChapter Three: Proper Tactics.\n\n         A general should know how to use his soldiers' moral and willingness to fight to his advantage. He should put his men in a place of no return, that is when they will fight to the end. A great general also\n\nmust be near his men at all times, he must do this to inspire his men and to build trust. The average soldier must be promised cover and support from others in order to hold his position and to end disertion and routing.\n\n        Conclusion\n\n     In conclusion a successful general must follow all these rules with no exceptions to other rules.", ""),
        StaticBook("Halfling Sword", "§b[Child] Aldoras", "Please send the Sword to the Tavern called The Lions Hearth, its on the main road near The Crossing, ask them to give the sword to Aldoras, if no one is inn send a bird", ""),
        StaticBook("ID. CARD-AL", "§bonm224", "##@################@##      IDENTIFICATION\n           CARD\n\n\n    Amras Lúinwë-VIP\n          \n          OWNER\n\nSignature:       Signature:\n[Amras L.]       {@AL@}\n\ndskdoskad31/01/14dksldksdl", ""),
        StaticBook("Sketchbook 1", "§bDaycia Polyian", "*This page has a drawing of a cup. The shadows drawon look like the light is coming from behind teh right side of the cup.*\n\n*this page has a drawing of two shoes laying in the sand*\n\n*this page has small doodles of a whale and a fish making kissy faces*\n\n*this page has a drawing of a very large druid tree. It appears to have a stain on it.*\n\n*this page has a drawing of houseing plans of aesterwald\n\n*This page has very details blueprints of buildings in Aesterwald*\n\n*this page has a drawing of a door on it.*\n\n*The page has a few sketch lines of what appears to be some sort of animal head but has been unfinished.*\n\n*this page has been scribbled on in ager*\n\n*this page has been torn out*\n\n*this page has drawings of a man and a young girl that resembles Daycia. The mann appears to be her father, and he's holding  a smithing hammer over his shoulder, and his little girl in the other hand.\n\n*this page has drawings of the people's she's met in Aesterwald. Guildenstern, Rosencrantz, Konrad, Father Rags, Miss Charlie, Quinlan, Hoss, and a few others.*\n\n*This page has detailed ideas of weapons like swords, greataxes, hammer,s daggers, clubs, spears, spiked balls, and morning stars*\n\n*this page has a very detailed and realistic drawing of a wolf*\n\n*this page is half missing.*\n\n*This page has various drawings and ideas of fletching. It has detailed drawings of arrows in all different angles, and different styles of arrows as well.*\n\n*On this pagei s a drawing of Rosencrantz and Quinland praying in a prayer circle around a ritual pillar that looks like the eye of iblees. Light glows from the town as they pray, getting rid of the taint in the circle.*\n\n*Sketches of eagle heads are drawn here, each better than the previous one.*\n\n*A picture of Guildenstern raising a hammer high above his head is drawn here. In the background is the forge he works in.*\n\n*feathers are drawn in great detail all over the page. Some are scribbled out.*\n\n*A sketch of a giant eagle perched on the top of the Eagle's Nest is here. Dacyia Captured the eagle and the keep perfectly in this picture, surrounded and bathed in sunlight.*\n\n*This is a crude drawing of an ocean. A boat is in the middle of it, being tossed around by the waves*\n\n*A few words are printed here...\n\n\"Talk to Miss Charlie about Ser Morvan's Ale\nFind out how to supply the tavern with the proper food\nFish more?\"\n\nThe handwriting is simple and neat*\n\nThere are rough sketches of a human in different poses, shapes, and sizes*\n\n*the human pose sketches continue on*\n\n*And again, human poses*\n\n*A close up of facial structues is drawn all over this page. Noses, eyes, mouth, ears. Different point of views, different directions.*\n\n*A drawing of Quinlan is here. The background appears to be in the tavern. He sits with a mug of ale in his hand, and a potato in the other.*\n\n*This drawing takes on a different tone. In it, a man is on the floor, seemingly dead. His face is bloated and purple. Soup is spilled all over the floor. The man appears to have choked on the food and lays unconcious on the tavern floor. The tone is sad*\n\n*A drawing of a man in a mage's robe and a mask is here. He stands in a grassy area with a red and purple shimmering butterfly resting on his finger. The drawing seems whimsical and magical.*\n\n*This draw has a picture of two  figures sitting on the docks. One is fishing and has a bucket beside her. It's Daycia. The other is watchiing quietly. It's Quinlan. They both appear to be talking. The sun is setting. The tone is happy with heavy shadows.\n\n*This drawing again features Quinlan and Daycia. The drawing is blurred, as in they were tipsy or moving. They are dancing together in the mead hall. The picture has bright tones and bubbles, signifiying bliss and alcohol. Morvan and Keitley stand behind*\n\n*Morvan stands next to a bird's nest, with a hatchling beak sticking out of it. His arm has 3 beak sized holes and he appears strained. Keitley stands off to the side, holding bandages and remaining calm.*\n\n*A drawing of a rotten squishy potato on the floor, in front of what appears to be Daycia's front door. Perhaps someone threw it in her house?*\n\n*A golem, seeimginly part of the rock around him and overgrown bu vines, is drawn here. He looks peaceful, like he's finally free of his rocky life.*\n\n\"I killed a man.\"\n\n*It is the only thing written here. It is written in red.*", ""),
        StaticBook("The Agustus Raid", "§bFinel Cole", "I am keeping track of what things I see, hear, feel, and touch. What I also eat, too. I will record what the people are like, what they think of the destination, their occupation. There is a long journey ahead of me, and I hope this is a success.\n\n So far I am in Oren. The people around me have this wierd accent. I will classify these things in my way. I will attempt to make my own language for it to spread around. I will call these people Orenians. The architecture around here is very complex.\n\n This job that I am doing right now is a Menel. A Menal is someone who records the information around them and makes new things. Now, there is something called Mountain Agustus. Who lives in there are fighters. Protecters of Oren.\n\n Dwarves are attacking. There will be a battle. Todays date is the 30th of The Grand harvest, 1456. This battle will be called The Battle of Oren. The humans are currently in a world war with everyone. I will call the world war, The War of All.\n\n The humans fought the dwarves over, but it isn't over yet.\nThe dwarves have taken over the fort. We have lost control. We are contesting over Fort Vekaro, which is the fort that we lost. The battle is not over yet. The dwarves are almost in.\n\n  I am not calling it a battle anymore. It is no longer The Battle of Oren. It is now The Agustus Raid. Orenians took back Fort Vekaro, no one has won the The Agustus Raid. The dwarves will come back.\n\n They have come back. We fought them back, but they WILL come back. It turns out they were a mix of races in the group. Elves, orcs, and dwarves. They went back to their city, Kal'Alras. Orenians are strong. But are they strong enough?", ""),
        StaticBook("Roots of Wisdom", "§bJack", "In handling affairs, take yielding as supreme, retreating a step is the preparations for advancing a step;\n\nIn dealing with others, generosity is prosperity, benefitting others is the foundation for benefitting oneself\n\nRetreating is advancing, giving is receiving. Someone who doesn't contend with others often benefits greatly; someone who retreats a step often advances a hundred steps.", ""),
        StaticBook("Roots of Wisdom", "§bJack", "One with pigweed in the mouth and amaranth in the gut, is as pure as jade, as clear as ice.\n\nOne who wears dragon robes and eats jade, will kneel like a servant, and grin like a slave.\n\nBecause aspirations are realized through simplicity, while integrity is compromised by the rich and sweet.\n\nPeople who dont seek favours from othrs are naturally upright. Someone who prefers to eat coarse food, like pigweed and amaranth, isn't moved by temptation", ""),
        StaticBook("Roots of Wisdom", "§bJack", "Industry is a fair virtue, but sheer drudgery lends no joy to the spirit;\n\nSimplicity is of high merit, but stark indifference helps no one and nothing.\n\nIf in conducting all affairs we can maintain a balanced and unbiased attitude, our happiness and joy will be never-ending.", ""),
        StaticBook("1 Drunkards Pass", "§bOrlik", "This document is proof that Maddok has purchased the tavern at 1 Drunkards Pass.\nHe understands that if he does not pay tax (Inactive for more than one month) His building and items may be forfit,\n\nSigned Orlik Ironcrow", ""),
        StaticBook("request an item", "§bcraigr341", "How to request an item from the vault:\n\nThe first thing you must do to request an item from the vault is that you must pick up an unwritten book from the chest. The next step is that you must write your name in the book, then write where you reside in the\n\n keep, then write what item you require from the vault and lastly, what item you shall put in the quota chest as payment.\nThis is to stop people from relying on the  vault for items and to help motivate the Brothers to help the vault out. What the\n\nGrand Conservator will accept is ores from the earth, wood, food, items that can be used to decorate the keep or strategic items such as feathers or leather.\n\nThere are items that the Grand Conservator will not accept as they can be\n\nconsidered junk such as dead shrubbery and sometimes cobblestone.\n\nThe Grand Conservator will not give you the item you seek because it can be considered junk and he will ban you from requesting an item for one elven day so\n\nyou can learn a lesson for wasting the Conservators time.\n\nAnother reason is that the vault may not have the item you seek and the Conservator will talk to you the moment he has a chance.\n\nIf you do succeed and the Conservator will\n\nstart to process your order and will deliver the item straight to your room in a package with your name on it if the item is missing, then someone has pilfered your item and you must report this incident to the Grandmaster or the Grand Conservator\n\nimmiediatly so the order can find out who the thief is and punish him correctly.\n\nThat is all you need to know about how to request an item from the Vault, Deus Magnus Brothers, may the Creator guide us all.\n\n\n\nwritten by - Craig Rovin the Grand Conservator", ""),
        StaticBook("Imperial Seers", "§bTuvya", "By signing this offical document, you hereby accept the following.\n\nThe Duties of a Seer\n-To serve and protect the King as a bodygaurd or other when you are off duty and or not currently completing assigned missions.\n-Follow orders from\n\nany and all superiors.\n-To Keep watch over foreign and local lands and ensure them of no wrong doings.\n-To scout out and record enemy terrain, data and plans, and to report them to the Capital Seer, King or watch capitan, whomever it applies to.\n\n-To deliver capital punishiment if the court deems it necessary and just.\n-To help plan out raids, and major battles.\n\nRANKS (In order)\nKing\nQueen\nPrince\nPrincess\nMear\n\nWatch Capitan\nCapital Seer\nSenior Wardens\nSeers\nWardens\nInductees\nCommon Wealth\n\nThose who are permitted to know are Senior Wardens and above, but only if they ask prior.\n\nBy signing this, you will agree to the following.\n-Seers may kidnapp forieign or local citizens and interrogate them as long as it does not violate common the common decency of our citizens and allies.\n-Seers may protect thier identity in or outside of\n\nthe walls.\n-Seers may roam any part of the city freely.\n-The Mage's Tower will become the head quarters of the seers, and will belong to them. It is subject to construction.\n-The Seers may accompany the King on foreign expaditions if they deem it unsafe.\n\n-The Capital Seer will be invited to ALL battle plannings if it involves the Human nation.\n-A Seer may only be appointed by the Capital Seer or the King.\n-Seers are allowed to keep confidental information away from all eyes except for the King.\n\n-Missions can only be assigned by a Watch Capitan, Capital Seer or the King himself.\n-Seers may break into any foreign facility without permission from the King.\nIn closing, I'd like to note that no order will be ridiculus or without good reason and\n\nintention. You can trust us as honorable men. We would never hurt or punish an innocent person.\n\nPlease Sign Below\n*Alexander Bedevere*\n*Bruce Wailer*\n*Bartholomeo Carindal Zorgi.*\n*Thomas Whitley.*\n*Airthron Domnall*", ""),
        StaticBook("Fire evocation.", "§bTerra", "Fire Evocation:\n   The Basics I\n\n   Written by\n             'The Exile'\n\n[!] The page is written in a weird but graceful handwriting, different from the original book.\n\nFire Evocation, a very common and simple form of Arcane Magic is deadly when mastered. This book shall teach you about the basics of fire evocation and Arcane magic. Fire Evocation,\nas stated, is a form of Arcane that allows you to conjure fire, make fire\n\nballs, and eventually some more complex spells. If you understand enough about the fire and the void, you will be able to conjure it.\n\nChapter I: The Void\n\nThe void is a strange thing. It is where we draw all of our\n\nenergy from, especially for magic.\nAs stated, if you understand enough about fire, you can use the \"void\" as a middle man for evoking your own fire. \nConnecting to the Void is an extremely difficult process, albeit some have gotten it on their first try.\n\nIn order to connect to the Void, you need to clear your thoughts COMPLETELY. As if you were thinking of nothing.\n___________________\n[!] The person who copied the book has stopped here, as the book does not go on,\nas rest of the pages have been ripped off", ""),
        StaticBook("AoD (Manga) #1", "§bRaigeki Kato", "~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~THE END!\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~KONBONWA! \nSilly Gaijin, you read book backwards! Easterner race is master race! See next issue soon! To be continued…\n\n*A drawing is on this page of a derpy looking drunk dwarf at heights of over fifty meters. It lacks skin, showing the muscles on the behemoth.*\n｡゜(｀Д´)゜｡\nドワーフ異端は、私たちすべてを殺す！\n\nMankind had softened up, believing they were simply a myth. But when Young, charming Heinrik Carrion looked up to the top of the fifty meter wall, A dwarf’s head popped up. And mankind remembered who they were ruled by.\n\n*A picture of orcs being eaten by Giant Dwarves is here. Most grotesque!*\n(ノಠ益ಠ)ノ\nオークは私の親友だった！\n\nWall Malin fell in just a few days and the Abresi Civilization became further encroached by the Giant Dwarves. Then there was a 107 years of peace with no sight of the Giant Dwarves.\n\nThe orcs enthusiastically went to Wall Malin but little did the ruling Dark Elves know that the orcs would open up the gates of Wall Malin to fight the Giant Dwarves, which lead to the demise of their race.\n\n*A drawing of the Abresian Civilization is here. The historical city is at the middle surrounded by Wall Horen. The other walls surround the historical city, creating layers.*\n\nファンシー！\n＼(☆o◎)／\n\nThese walls circled around their respective civilizations. Up until the Dark elves took control of Wall Horen. They pushed the humans into Wall Krug and told the orcs that there was more “glomp” with the giant dwarves in Wall Malin.\n\nThe people of Abresi created three walls: Wall Malin was the outer wall, Wall Krug was in the middle, and at the center, there was Wall Horen.\n\nNo one knows how they were created. This city was and still is known as Abresi, though now it was much bigger to encompass all of Anthos’ survivors.\n\nHe sent forth the giant dwarves, wiping out the lands of the elves, orcs, and humans. Only one city stood firm. Having had time before the giant dwarves came, they created walls that were 50 meters in height over night.\n\nDuring Inadgo’s shock, Iblees stabbed Inadgo in the heart, ending the corrupt king’s life.\n*A drawing of Inadgo being stabbed brutally is here. It looks as though the artist put a little bit too much detail into it.* \nщ (*ㅇ△ Φ☆)ノ\n私は何を見たのですか？\n\nThey even lost their digestive systems. They were only driven by three things: gold, booze, and the extermination of all other races, especially humans. Most ranged in height at around fifteen meters.\n\nAll dwarves: Male and female, old and young, short and not as short, began to grow in epic proportions. But at a cost. They lost all of their reproductive organs and lost their ability to think freely.\n\n*A drawing of Iblees with big Kawaii eyes is talking to a large fat dwarf, supposedly Inadgo.*\nＯ(≧∇≦)Ｏ\nDESU DESU DESU DESU DESU!\n\nIn exchange, Inadgo had to give up his soul to Iblees when he died. Inadgo, being the greedy heretic dwarf he was, accepted without hesitation. So Iblees conjured a curse.\n\nThe Grand King Inadgo Stormhammer of the Dwarves made a pact with Iblees, the Fallen One. As part of their heretical plan, Iblees would give the Kingdom of Urugan an unstoppable army that would destroy his enemies (all of the other nations).\n\nThe dread that was a life under their rule…The humiliation of being caged like birds. On that day, Humanity remembered the terror of being ruled by them.\n\nIt was 107 years ago in the year 1450 when they first gave rise. \n｡゜(｀Д´)゜｡\n\n[OOC DISCLAIMER: This is based off the Manga/Anime Attack on Titan. Any likeness to IRL people is completely coincidental. This is for IC propaganda only. Spuller does NOT really make deals with Iblees.]\n\n*A picture of a young Heinrick Carrion is seen slaying many Giant Dwarves on the cover of the book. Heinrick appears very kawaii desu with big bright eyes. These eyes appear strong though, showing he is major badassuru!*\n\n“Attack on Dwarfu!” \nThe Manga!\n Issue #1", ""),
        StaticBook("contract", "§bIthor Grandaxe", "The Ownership of illicia\n\nI Fimlin Grandaxe take ownership of Illica for 7 Elven days to modify the town, All changes within the town in the week must be verified by Ledros or Arina and Once the week has ended i will hand ownership and all rights to the\n\ntown back to Ledros Nindel\n\nSigned\nLedros Nindel\nArina Valornis\nSigned \nFimlin Grandaxe\nElsa BloodBorn\nIthor Grandaxe", ""),
        StaticBook("Sketchbook 2", "§bDaycia Polyian", "*The drawings in this book are darker. Darker in the sense that the lines are heavier and the shading is black. There's very little contrast between the shadows and the non-shaded areas, and there's no color in the book at all.*\n\nI've decided to begin drawing again. I will begin with the events that have consumed my thoughts.\n\n*A sketch is here. Upon first glance it seem to be a scribble, but after focusing a bit you notice it is the darkness under a cauldron, with the words \"lies\" hidden in the shadows.*\n\n*A picture of a large man with boils all over his body and greasy skin is on this page. The POV appears to be on the floor in front of this man. In one hand he holds a rope, the other, a knife. He is grinning with a sociopathic smile, evil behind his  -\n\n* -eyes, which are scribbled into dark circles.  The background appears to the be the cellar of the tavern*\n\n*This page has a drawing of a large castle looking out of the darkness. The lines are heavy and there is almost no white on the page from all the lines used to create this terrifying sight.*\n\n*A cage is drawn here, a girl is sitting inside. Her head has dark tendrils coming frmo it. The man from earlier is in the cage, leaning close to the girl and drooling. The words \"Only he will care for you\" are written.*\n\n*The man stands with a look of shock on his face. He appears to be looking down, where a knife is shoved through his fat gut. Blood drips out in heavy lines.*\n\n*Daycia is drawn here, except there is no happiness in her eyes. In fact, her eyes are blank. She holds a knife close to her, and her head is scribbled on, to express the turmoil within.  Her hands and legs are bound by rope, and blood on her clothes*\n\n*A drawing of outside is here. It appears overcast although the sun is shining brightly. A man with yellow hair stand with a goofy smile saying \"let me help\" As daycia is curled in a ball on the ground, shaking her head, which is scribbled for turmoil.*\n\n*An ocean is drawn here. It is crazy and wild, but in the middle it's calm. It appears to be slowling down, the storm appears to be growing calmer*\n\n*The whole page is black, except for a large white spot in the middle.\n\n\"Light at the end of a tunnel.\"\nShe's almost free is written there.*\n\n~Thank you~ Is all that's written here.\n\n\nat the bottom of the page, birds are being released from a cage and flying up to the side.", ""),
        StaticBook("Coppied Book", "§b-", "*Coppied book, the paper seems dirty but you can see it was written not long ago*\n\nPanacea, one of the Four great Myths of Alchemy. A concoction which is said to cure all, be it a cut, a broken bone, an illness or a curse. No matter what ails the user will be cured. I wonder if this is a good thing.\n\nThe only thing said that Panacea is not able to heal is death. Such is said to be a myth due to the nature of cure everything. It is said to cure even illnesses of the mind, have one thought what that is?\n\nAn illness of the mind might be as extreme as hearing voices that aren’t there, or phobias of all kinds, so those would be healed. Yet what of those which might be simple and might or not be an illness?\n\nThink of depression, would that be an illness of the mind, or simply a state of mind? Would one that drank it reach a state of full perfection of body and mind, perfect immunity of new illnesses, how would it be?\n\nThe matter that an alchemist can never know the full outcome of the myth is one of the parts that make it a myth. I spoke of immunity of illnesses and perhaps even poisons. That is one theory that Panacea can also bring.\n\nOther parts of the Panacea that are left undecided are of the speed it acts. Some say it’s instantaneous, others say it reacts in seconds, others say it takes hours or days depending on the severity.\n\nOne thing all say about it, it may heal all cuts, broken bones, physical ailments, and all types of poisons. Most of these things can already be done with powerful or greater potions of mending and healing.\n\nWhat makes Panacea so special is that it is said to cure all curses. Alchemy reaching into the realm of magic healing and taint healing is unheard of being successful. Such is the greatness of Panacea.\n\nThe greatest curse to heal is of course, the curse of Iblees. This separates it from the Elixir of Life, the myth of the creation of immortality, which is said makes one unable to die, but does not remove him of harm or curses.\n\nOne can imagine the outcome of a human taking the Elixir of Life without taking Panacea as well, as one that can not die having a curse of old age would not have a good result. Sometimes curing everything is better than being immortal.\n\nThoughts of Alchemy Myths - Panacea\n\nby Luteran Barkin", ""),
        StaticBook("Contract", "§bViktor Carrion", "The contracting parties being the Holy Oren Empire and the dwarven Clan Irongut, agree to the following terms.\n\nThe Holy Oren Empire:\nShall pay Clan Irongut 850 minas per soldier.\n\nThe Irongut Clan:\nShall participate in the fight against the rebellion.\n\nSignatories:\n\nTuvya Carrion\nHogarth Irongut", ""),
        StaticBook("AoD (Manga) #1", "§bRaigeki Kato", "~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~The End!\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~KONBONWA! \nSilly Gaijin, you read book backwards! Easterner race is master race! See next issue soon! To be continued…\n\n*A drawing is on this page of a derpy looking drunk dwarf at heights of over fifty meters. It lacks skin, showing the muscles on the behemoth.*\n｡゜(｀Д´)゜｡\nドワーフ異端は、私たちすべてを殺す！\n\nMankind had softened up, believing they were simply a myth. But when Young, charming Heinrik Carrion looked up to the top of the fifty meter wall, A dwarf’s head popped up. And mankind remembered who they were ruled by.\n\n*A picture of orcs being eaten by Giant Dwarves is here. Most grotesque!*\n(ノಠ益ಠ)ノ\nオークは私の親友だった！\n\nWall Malin fell in just a few days and the Abresi Civilization became further encroached by the Giant Dwarves. Then there was a 107 years of peace with no sight of the Giant Dwarves.\n\nThe orcs enthusiastically went to Wall Malin but little did the ruling Dark Elves know that the orcs would open up the gates of Wall Malin to fight the Giant Dwarves, which lead to the demise of their race.\n\n*A drawing of the Abresian Civilization is here. The historical city is at the middle surrounded by Wall Horen. The other walls surround the historical city, creating layers.*\n\nファンシー！\n＼(☆o◎)／\n\nThese walls circled around their respective civilizations. Up until the Dark elves took control of Wall Horen. They pushed the humans into Wall Krug and told the orcs that there was more “glomp” with the giant dwarves in Wall Malin.\n\nThe people of Abresi created three walls: Wall Malin was the outer wall, Wall Krug was in the middle, and at the center, there was Wall Horen.\n\nNo one knows how they were created. This city was and still is known as Abresi, though now it was much bigger to encompass all of Anthos’ survivors.\n\nHe sent forth the giant dwarves, wiping out the lands of the elves, orcs, and humans. Only one city stood firm. Having had time before the giant dwarves came, they created walls that were 50 meters in height over night.\n\nDuring Inadgo’s shock, Iblees stabbed Inadgo in the heart, ending the corrupt king’s life.\n*A drawing of Inadgo being stabbed brutally is here. It looks as though the artist put a little bit too much detail into it.* \nщ (*ㅇ△ Φ☆)ノ\n私は何を見たのですか？\n\nThey even lost their digestive systems. They were only driven by three things: gold, booze, and the extermination of all other races, especially humans. Most ranged in height at around fifteen meters.\n\nAll dwarves: Male and female, old and young, short and not as short, began to grow in epic proportions. But at a cost. They lost all of their reproductive organs and lost their ability to think freely.\n\n*A drawing of Iblees with big Kawaii eyes is talking to a large fat dwarf, supposedly Inadgo.*\nＯ(≧∇≦)Ｏ\nDESU DESU DESU DESU DESU!\n\nIn exchange, Inadgo had to give up his soul to Iblees when he died. Inadgo, being the greedy heretic dwarf he was, accepted without hesitation. So Iblees conjured a curse.\n\nThe Grand King Inadgo Stormhammer of the Dwarves made a pact with Iblees, the Fallen One. As part of their heretical plan, Iblees would give the Kingdom of Urugan an unstoppable army that would destroy his enemies (all of the other nations).\n\nThe dread that was a life under their rule…The humiliation of being caged like birds. On that day, Humanity remembered the terror of being ruled by them.\n\nIt was 107 years ago in the year 1450 when they first gave rise. \n｡゜(｀Д´)゜｡\n\n[OOC DISCLAIMER: This is based off the Manga/Anime Attack on Titan. Any likeness to IRL people is completely coincidental. This is for IC propaganda only. Spuller does NOT really make deals with Iblees.]\n\n*A picture of a young Heinrik Carrion is seen slaying many Giant Dwarves on the cover of the book. Heinrik appears very kawaii desu with big bright eyes. These eyes appear strong though, showing he is major badassuru!*\n\n“Attack on Dwarfu!” \nThe Manga!\n Issue #1\nBy Raigeki Kato", ""),
        StaticBook("Application", "§bMoro Boffin", "Name: Morro Boffin\nAge: 23\nRace: Halfling.\n\nGreetings! I have acknowledged the need for a writer on the outer wall of your Inn. I'd be very pleased to work at your residence. Thanks.\n\n~ Morro Boffin.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Meeting", "§bworldiswar28", "I would like to scheduel a meeting with the Jarl of Fellbor. ((when you see this message goto Kal'Ithrun for a meeting anythime between 4-7 EST/ 9-11 GMT)) Ye may bring who ye like but excpect not to bring weapons\n~Lord Fimlin Grandaxe", ""),
        StaticBook("Roots of Wisdom", "§bJack", "Strong, rich, spicy and sweet arenot genuine flavours;\n\nGenuine flavour is only plain.\n\nPeople with wondrous skills are not perfect people;\n\nThe most perfect people are common\n\nExciting and novel things dont last very long. Only plain and common things endure over time. Anything that is too contrieved has lost its original face. Only the natural is genuine.", ""),
        StaticBook("Edicts", "§bFaiz Kharadeen", "I, Faiz Kharadeen, swear on the Gods of your beliefs, that I will make Annie Kato my Queen.\n\nNo matter what circumstances arise, when and if I am King, Annie Kato shall be my Queen.\n\nQueen of Oren.\nQueen of I, Faiz Kharadeen.\n\n\n~Faiz Kharadeen", ""),
        StaticBook("To any Cleric", "§b[Medium] Jack Lawrence", "Notes on the Headless Horseman\n\n-First appeared at the Ghastly Tree, by CT.\n-Tree was lit with the hot variety of ghost fire. Green.\n-Composed of said green fire.\n*skip to last page for letter\n\n-Seems to take on a skeletal form.\n-Is affected by physical attacks, not just gold.\n-Seems to go for decapitations.\n-Affected by normal magic.\n-Heads around tree appeared to be cut by a blunt instrument.\n-Has the ability to fly\n\n-Likely to be extremely strong physically.\n-Appears to be able to fly by shooting away in a flash of green lightning.\n-May or may not have power over said lightning as well.\n-Mixed reports over form of weapon. Some say a scythe, others\n\nan executioner's axe, and another says a sword.\n-Appears to go only for killing blows, namely the chest, and the neck.\n-Was able to withstand a magical lightning bolt which should have killed a full grown orc.\n\nWe need help at Kralta, now. Monsters attacking everywhere, some injured, some dead.\n\n-Jack Lawrence", ""),
        StaticBook("Letter to Khel", "§bHan", "\"Dear Khel,\nOr Prince Khel, I never did congratulate you on your... Princness... though who would expect a Flay to do that anyhow.... Anyways, I need a boat, old man Emberhard told me you might know someone that sells.\" -Old friend Han.", ""),
