        StaticBook("Torn Page", "§bAncient Dwarf", "[!] This page has been torn out from it's respective  tome.\n\nThe Magi have begun dissapearing. I pray to Khorvad that we find them, else we may be stuck in this accursed place. Their leader, Dwov, is still about. I fear for him, though. His mind has begun to deteriorate for reasons unkown. We've enough supplies to\n\nlast another three weeks.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "LIST OF BANNED NAMES:§0\n§0\n§0- Ornias is Unbanned\n\n-Zahrer Irongrinder§0\n§0\n§0-Dizzy Irongrinder§0\n§0\n§0-Grungron§0\n§0\n§0-Patrick Windraker§0\n§0\n§0-Thatch (attempted to kill/capture Armin)§0\n§0\n§0-Ceto(Frost Witch, attempted to kill Armin)\n\n-(Patrick Windraker is exempt from this list)§0\n§0\n§0-Dram§0\n§0\n§0-Bitch women who wear armor and act like men§0\n§0\n§0-Eliza (sicked a Golem on Lyeth)§0\n§0\n§0-Elwyn (tried to manipulate and kill Revion in the Library)", ""),
        StaticBook("test", "§bArmin Ambertoe", "{\"text\":\"\"}", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Dirk Martijn I Novokain vouches for Bell Sano.§0\n§0I vouch for Bell Sano seeing as he is a most capable scholar while also being trustworthy and loyal, an example of his loyalty is carrying out fine guard work in Felsen.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Xionism For Dummies!§0\n§0       By: Faenor§0\n§0     Introduction§0\n§0This book is for those of you who are too lazy to read the /many/ scriptures of Xion. §0\n§0\n§0 Xion is a religion, or lack of religion based on removing the AengulDaemonic influence\n\nfrom this world. Xionists believe that the world would have been much better off had the Aenguls and Daemons never came. §0\n§0While mortals may believe that the world is better off this way, but if the Aenguls and Daemons left this world. The people of this\n\nworld rely too heavily on the Aenguls that heal them. Rather then healing naturally they would go to a cleric and not suffer the consequences of their actions and just have their wounds healed instantly.\n\nThey come for me, I fear I cannot finish this scripture.§0\n§0            [!]§0\n§0The book ends here, blood droplets covering the page", ""),
        StaticBook("Sister's Curse", "§bCal \"Bee\" Fiorne", "The curse of the two sisters§0\n§0\n§0Once, a long time ago…§0\n§0Well, not very long ago. §0\n§0When your Grandmother was a child, there lived two sisters.§0\n§0Their names were Nettle and Thistle.\n\nNettle and Thistle lived with their mother and father in a cottage in the woods. §0\n§0They were very poor, but they were also very happy.§0\n§0Nettle was very strong and brave.§0\n§0Thistle was very clever and cunning.\n\nTogether, they would go on adventures.§0\n§0Nettle and Thistle roamed through the woods together every day.§0\n§0They were the closest of friends.§0\n§0They explored dark caves, climbed high mountains, and chased animals.§0\n\nThen, on one day like any other, they found something rather peculiar.§0\n§0\n§0There was a trail of purple flowers, leading into a cave they had never seen before.\n\n\"Lets follow the flowers!\" Nettle shouted.§0\n§0\"This seems dangerous. Perhaps we should get torches and weapons.\" Thistle replied.§0\n§0So they went back to their cottage for supplies.\n\nWhen they returned, however, the cave and the flowers were gone.§0\n§0The next week, they traveled back into the woods, searching for the cave.§0\n§0\"There it is!\" Nettle shouted, running after a trail of flowers.§0\n§0Only Thistle noticed they were different flowers.\n\nNettle and Thistle followed the flowers until they reached a house.§0\n§0\"We should go inside,\" Thistle said, walking towards the door.§0\n§0\"No! That's trespassing!\" Nettle cried. But Thistle had already opened the door.§0\n\nInside the house was a beautiful home, filled with silk rugs and gorgeous paintings.§0\n§0In the center of the house was a table, upon which rested a golden book.§0\n§0\"We can take this book and sell it,\" Thistle said. \"We'll never be hungry again.\"§0\n§0\"No!\"\n\n\"That's Stealing!\" Said Nettle.§0\n§0The sisters began to bicker. Then they started to yell. Then they screamed and fought.§0\n§0Neither of them noticed that the air in the room started to churn.§0\n§0The Book blew open.\n\non the page the book opened to, there was a poem.§0\n§0TWO YOUNG GIRLS §0\n§0THE CLOSEST FRIENDS§0\n§0ONE PURE§0\n§0ONE DARK§0\n§0WILL MEET THEIR ENDS§0\n§0NEVER TO BE HAPPY AGAIN§0\n§0NEVER TO FEEL LOVE AGAIN§0\n§0AGAIN, AGAIN, UNTIL THE END\n\nThe sisters stopped fighting to read the book.§0\n§0\"Does it mean us?\" Whispered Nettle.§0\n§0\"No way,\" Said Thistle, \"It's impossible that someone could write about us if we've never been here before.\"§0\n\n\"I dunno… It seems suspicious,\" said Nettle, unnerved.§0\n§0\"That's because you're stupid,\" Thistle spat.§0\n§0They fought all the way home, and all of the rest of their lives.§0\n\nAnd when they had children, their children fought.§0\n§0And their children's children.§0\n§0Never to be happy again.§0\n§0Never to feel love again.§0\n\nThe moral of the story is, Don't go into spooky houses.§0\n§0Especially not spooky houses that you've followed strange flowers to.§0\n§0-Bee", ""),
        StaticBook("A tale of death", "§bAlcetra Enn", "There are many great mysterys in this world today we unravel one of the greatest mysterys of them all. What is death like? I was wandering in the grove until i was a man who was afraid of death, a wood elf assured him that death was nothing to be\n\nafraid of, he said that he could take the old man and some curious people to experience the afterlife, i accepted the offer as i was most eager to unravel the mystery. He put his fingers on our heads and we were taken by a great slumber.\n\nWe awoke in sandy shores where he led us to a gateway that entered the afterlife, it was a marvellous experience full of trees, water and animals. He told us that when we die we will have our own afterlife which will be altered to our personality and\n\nmemories. For example a destructive individual will have fire and blood in his afterlife, whilst a elegant peace lover will have silence and peace. We went into a cave filled with animal statues and vines, this most likely means that this afterlife\n\nrepresented a peace loving druid. After this marvellous experience we were awoken back at the grove, the old man who was eternally afraid of death immediately longed for peace. Now you know that death is a place that will suit your personality and a\n\nplace where you will live a happy life full of prosper.", ""),
        StaticBook("Notice", "Cablam", "18th of Sun's Smile, 1567.§0\n§0\n§0I am writing this note for the scholars of the Library of Dragur to notify of some recent changes.\n\nOrithur, the Founder of the Library and a member of family that has been formed here at the library has left the library indefinitely. If you see him continue to treat him with the utmost kindness and respect.§0\n§0\n§0If you wish to know more relating to his\n\ndeparture and where the Library will go from here feel free to speak with myself, Armin Ambertoe. I will be happy to answer any and all questions. §0\n§0\n§0For now regular scholar duties will continue as normal unless otherwise specified.\n\nSafe travels to you all. §0\n§0\n§0-Armin Ambertoe§0", ""),
        StaticBook("A sealed note.", "§bDelonna", "King Maric.\n\nIt is with great displeasure that I am unable to deliver these in person. \n\nWhile hunting the Hydra beneath the Embermoor with my Marked Men brothers, I was gravely wounded.\n\nI'm told the Clerics did -\n\n- their part, however will still be recovering for a long time. \n\nThus I hope this reaches you well, and that you may put it to good use.\n\nIncluded are a number of enchantment scrolls, arrows, and a basic enchanting table which I will give instructions -\n\n- for using.\n\nMuch of the magical energy required has already been applied, so you should be quite capable of applying the enchantments as you wish.\n\nSimply place a single single on the enchantment table, and you will be able to imbue a piece of armor -\n\n- with little effort.\n\nAttempting to apply more than one will result in the enchantments conflicting, negating one. \n\nAttempting to apply the enchantments to Carabarum based armor, or already enchanted armor will require an experienced enchanter.\n\n((Put the book in the enchantment table and shift+rightclick it while holding the item you want enchanted.))\n\nI hope these are of use to your cause.\n- Delonna.", ""),
        StaticBook("-Chirr'siol", "§bChirr'siol Elibar'acal", "Oh sweet Lelien§0\n§0Your beauty scales to a million§0\n§0The way your hair shimmers on a moonlit night, is sure to stop any Uruk fight§0\n§0Your eyes twinkle in the sun's glow, no theatrical troup could put that on that good of a show.§0\n§0\n§0", ""),
        StaticBook("Logbook", "§bElwyn", "Logbook Form - http://goo.gl/forms/zhoH1ABLpQ\n\nLogbook Records - http://tinyurl.com/oj8garp", ""),
        StaticBook("Guide: Alchemy", "§bThe Scribe", "Fund. Alchemy\nby Unknown\nThe Tales of a Travelling Alchemist\nRewritten by: The Scribe\n\nForeword:\nI have heard many a man ask why one such as myself, would take such an interest and dedication in the art of alchemy when there are readymade spells that could do just the same, if not better, a job than an apothecary’s potion.\n\nMy response to this statement is rather simple whereas one must have a certain gift for spell-slinging, rune-craft, and spirit-speaking, one mustn’t need these frivolous commodities to use a balm to menda wound, shroud themselves in a\n\nnigh-invisible cloak with the swig from a bitter bottle, or set someone ablaze with bottle flame. Alchemy is not merely reproducing a wizard’s spell, but it is shaping the spell in a way for common man’s use. While magic still has its place in our world,\n\nAlchemy allows those without the gift to reap the same benefits as any mage might.\n\nChapter 1: The Fundamentals of Alchemy\n\nThe Principle and Law of Equivalent Exchange:\n\nAs I was providing my services to a rather quaint hamlet, which also provided me information of the local flora and more of the same legends I always hear of, I was approached by a man of common repute, and he produced a sarcastic,\n\nyet thought provoking, question. This question was, and I will attempt to reproduce the dialect as best as my phonetic ability will allow, “‘ow come, Mister, ‘hat ye’ can make all ‘dis ‘onderful stuff outta nothin’?” Now as shameful as his speech may be,\n\nthe question remains the same. How is it, that alchemists are capable or reshaping spells of sorcerers for the common folk out of nothing? The answer to this question is simple to state, but fairly difficult to grasp, and this is the\n\n Principle of Equivalent Exchange. Much like the money one gives to the merchant for the apple or slab of meat, one cannot have something for nothing. As the saying goes, “there is no such thing as a free brunch,” there is no such thing as a free potion.\n\nThis fundamental rule applies to old alchemy, through the use of symbols and rituals, far more than what I call “Modern alchemy. Although I will not get into ancient alchemy, I will state why this rule applies to Modern Alchemy. To craft a potion, for\n\nexample, a bottle of alchemist’s fire, one requires the materials. Much like a spell casted by a wizard, one cannot make something out of nothing. Know that to make a potion, and the possibilities are truly endless, one must take plants,\n\n objects, and other representations of what they wish to make. For example, alchemist’s fire could be best produced with an - -, a mixture of - -, - -, and a dash of - -. Why would one need these items?\n\nThe - - provides the basic mixture of what we will make, and of course the better quality of the - - the better quality of the Alchemist’s Fire, but of course we will get further into this in a later chapter. The - -, a - - plant that grows\n\nmost often in deserts, is often - - and often associated with - -. This provides an superb symbol for the element of - -. The - -, another powerful symbol of  - -, mixes well with the crushed - - to produce something truly strong.\n\n The - -, an optional portion, is another symbol of - -, albeit both weaker than blaze - - for both a - - fire burns far greater than - -. Now then, although you have these symbols of flame, rage, and warfare, one mustn’t simply\n\ntoss objects into the metaphorical cauldron. Precision is necessary, as always. One must measure their mixture carefully, or they will give too much. And giving too much will tip the scales of Equivalent Exchange, causing unpredictable results.\n\nWhy is this you might ask? When balance is achieved through Equivalent Exchanged, a mixture of the elements for the bottled spell of your desire, then all is well. But when you disrupt this balance and push on the world,\n\ntaking and forcing in too much of one or more element then the world will push back. Of course a word of caution is required, for one may think taking less is the best option when balance is not a certainty. This is a common misconception for not\n\nproviding enough of one element will result in the same conclusion, imbalance and chaos. This can be easily associated with cooking, too much of one ingredient will spoil the recipe, not enough of an ingredient will produce\n\nsimilar results. Of course it could also produce blander results, and in that case a potion may not be spoiled but it will simply be weak. This is preferable to exploding, of course.\n\nPreparation and You: \nNovice Alchemists often question me, what advice would provide the budding alchemist, what sip of wisdom could you provide one who desires to tap your fountain of knowledge? The answer to this is rather simple\n\npreparation is key to any potion. What is meant by this is that to mix a potion that you would have not mixed before, or do not mix often, you must have all the facts and supplies before you begin. This is best provided in a short list of required\n\nprocedures if one wishes to keep their eyebrows, fingers and life.\n\n1. Have all the Facts\n2. Know the Recipe\n3. Clean and check all your instruments are working order\n\n4. Ensure you have plenty of whatever ingredients you are using\n5. Keep and Calm Disposition\n\nEven having the recipe is not enough to craft a potion, but knowledge of its origins, ingredients, and uses are crucial. Although budding alchemists often overlook this step, it may save your life one day.\n\nThe Tales of a the Travelling Alchemist, Vol 1\n\nCopied by Rosso\nVaerHaven\n\nRecopied by The Scribe\nCloud Temple", ""),
        StaticBook("Basic First Aid", "§bGwyneviere de Savoie", "Basic First Aid\n\nGwyneviere de Savoie\n\n~                        ~\n          Vol 1\n\nIt is important for those who commonly engage in fighting or combat that the wound is more deadly than your enemies' weapon. Even the smallest cut can kill you. Tend to your wounds as soon as you can and ensure that your wound does not become infected.\n\nUnder the circumstance that the patient has been inflicted with a cut or puncture injury, there will be blood. Tourniqet the wound to stop the blood flow, or apply a moss to absorb and clot the wound.\n\nForeign material, such as cloth and dirt\n\nfor example, will need to be removed from the wound. Particularly if the wound is a puncture. Anything inside the wound can lead to infection and then death.\n\nEven the smallest injury can kill the strongest, and it is important to be aware\n\nof how deadly infection is. Animal urine is an efficient way to sterylise an injury of any infection. Cauterizing the wound is a painful method, but an efficient one, to reduce chances of external infection. If perhaps you do not have ready available\n\nanimal urine, or a brand (or a fire), or you do not have herbs or medical plants; wrap the injury as best you can and replace bandaging often. Reach a doctor, or a field medic immediately.\n\nAfter sterylising the injury, you may chose to apply a medical\n\nor herbal paste to the wound if you have it on hand. Otherwise, apply a clean bandage to the wound and seek proper medical aid. Again, change the dressing regularly.\n\nIf a wound is so severe that an internal organ is punctured\n\nor cut by sword or knife- there isn't much first aid can do. Organ damage will most likely lead to a painful death without proffesional care. It is unlikely that the correct attention will be gained if the wound was inflicted in battle. Use tea's or herbs\n\nto ease your pain, or your patients' pain as best you can. It is likely that the patient will be suffering from his or hers' injury.\n\nSide note: Alcohol is good way to sterylise a wound and act as a pain killer.\n\nSevere Head Injury:\n\nIt is likely that in battle a person will come across a head or skull injury. This may also occur after a fall or a slip. If the injury is small, reach an area where you can sit, and check yourself for obvious\n\ninjury. It is likely you will be dazed or nausious. If skin has split, apply a bandage and seek help as fast as possible to ensure that no long term damage is dealt.\n\nIf the injury is severe, such as the crushing of a skull, your\n\npatient is most likely unconcious, or in a coma. Either way, they're in a bad state- and hopefully you're there to save their life. Check their pulse first, they may appear to be dead. Swelling of the face, nose, and eyes is a sign that the patient\n\nhas undergone some serious damage to their head or skull. In this case, it is unlikely that basic first aid can save their life. Do not move them, and if possible, seek proper medical attention. The brain of the patient will swell, causing brain damage\n\nor death. A hole will need to be drilled into the skull of the patient in order to allow the brain to swell. It is still likely that the patient will die.\n\nHead injuries can cause nausia, headaches, dizzyness and physical sickness. Resting will heal these\n\nBroken Bones:\n\nYou can check to see the severity of your patients injuries (or your own) by physical appearance. Your patient or yourself will most likely be in shock because of the injury. State of shock is a dangerous thing. Never\n\nunderestimate the severity of the situation. Broken bones often can be seen externally as lumps in the skin, particularly in limbs. Check for mishape or difference in your limbs, and the limb affected.\n\nYou can use sticks and straps from boots or clothes, or fabric from your clothes also, to create a splint to keep limbs straight. If your patient has a broken leg, it is likely they wont be able to walk. This is a no brainer to identifying a broken bone\n\nor fracture. Splinting or creating a stretcher out of sturdy sticks strapped together in a ladder shape can be used to transport the patient. A splint can be used to keep the injury straight and reduce movement to the affected area\n\nin the case of injuring yourself.\n\nYou can also pack mud around the limb to set the break. Seek proper medical attention as soon as possible.\n\nDislocations can be relocated with pressure to the\n\naffected area. It is painful. Your patient may need pain killers. If you do not feel comfortable tending to a dislocated bone, seek medical attention of another, or man up.\n\nSlings, made out of tied cloth, or a shirt is great for arm or shoulder breaks.\n\nA bone break can also break the skin. There may be blood. Apply a splint or use a stretcher to get the patient to proper medical attention if this is the case.\n\nWater Injuries:\n\nIf a person has found themselves floating face down in the water. The first thing you should do is turn them onto their back. It is important not to apply weight to their body, as you could force them under water.\n\nThere isnt much you can do out in the water, so bring them back to shore quickly, and remember to keep their head above the waters' surface.\n\nLay them on their back and check for a pulse. A persons pulse can be found under their neck or the under-\n\n-side of their wrist. Either way, there is a possibility of saving the patient. Place one hand, a fist, onto the chest of the patient above their heart. Place your second hand atop the other.\n\nPump their chest, with short breaks. Count thirty pumps before\n\nstopping. This can force water out of the lungs of the patient or restart the heart.\n\nOpen the airway of the patient by placing your palm on the persons forhead and gently tilting their head back. Check for normal breathing. If the patient is not breathi-\n\n-ng still, open the persons mouth and transfer air from your own lungs to the patients. Afterwards, continue pumping. Hopefully this will be enough to revive the patient or get the water out of their lungs.\n\nArrow Wounds:\n\nIf your patient has an arrow injury, first be prepared to apply a lot of force to free the person of the arrow. They are very hard to get out safely. Pulling it out without care can sever arteries or break leave splinters\n\nor wood inside of the body. You could die of blood loss or infection. So long as the bolt or arrow is inside of the patient, the blood loss will be lower than without it.\n\nYou can remove the arrow by propulsion, where you force the arrow\n\nthrough the flesh and out the exit point of the wound. Reccomended for limb injuries.\n\nSaw off the shaft, and leave it for a few days so that the wound becomes full of pus and can then be removed with ease. The pus signals infection.\n\nand would then need proper medical attention.\n\nStretch the wound by plugging it with probes of elder pith, soaked in honey and then using a pair of tongs, reach into the wound and slowly, carefully, tug the arrow/bolt free.\n\nWash the wound with wine or liquor, and apply a bandage securely or cauterize the wound. Reduce the amount of strain on the affected area and rest for a few days to prevent pains and quicken the rate of healing.", ""),
        StaticBook("Elemental Thiik", "§bAnonymous", "Thiik  \n\n        Terra\n\nTerra is one of the most commonly used of the Thiik within the Dialect of the Elements. Representing the firm, unmoving Land beneath our feet some of the longest lasting Thiik are Terra Runes.\n\nBenefits\n\nThe positives of Terra runes are many as they can be used to do anything from fix an item to the ground or add reinforcement for support of Carved structures. Many of our halls boast these Thiik along the walls.\n\nDrawbacks\n\nTerra Thiik have issues due to the fact that Stone is naturally abrasive to suggestion. Due to this it's impossible to really summon stone or move it signifigantly or in large portions with the Terra symbol. It can be used to modify\n\nexisting Stone and Land near it, however.\n\nCreation\n\n*Outlined here is a symbol painstakingly drawn in Ink. It seems to be somewhat circular in shape but rough on the outline.*\n\n           Igni\n\nThe rune of Fire this is perhaps one of the most useful of the base runes. Fire, being free willed, is open to suggestion. Be warned however! Fire always needs a source, and if you fail to provide... it will draw\n\nfrom you! Caution, young smith!\n\nBenefits\n\nIgni is commonly used around forges and for weapons due to it's useful nature. Some of the greatest forges of the Great Hall bear Ograhad's own Igni and are thus able to forge metals that we could not previously\n\nreach a melting point of.\n\nDrawbacks\n\nFire is hard to control by nature and as such demonstrates it when you attempt to force it into particular functions. It will fight the entire time and may even cause signifigant damage if you attempt to force it to hold back too much\n\nAs stated before, be wary that you do not overload the Heart rune or it will begin to draw it's toll from you, the Original creator of the Ruhn. This is a swift death.\n\nCreation\n\n*An upsidedown triangle is drawn here, the sides seem rough and somewhat uneven but it was obviously drawn very precisely.*\n\n         Wulgim\n\nThe Ruhn of Wulgim is one of the more subtle of the Elemental Dialect. Wulgim at it's heart isn't something that causes Khrum to shake in their boots. It has it's uses and I have seen many clever Thinkadre using this in\n\nKadrel to cool Stailininn quicker, as well as power wheels to stoke a furnace effortlessly.\n\nBenefits\n\nWuglim has many smaller uses over the obvious of Igni or Kaas but is no less powerful than either. Often used in ports or in Kadrel it's become something of a symbol for clever thinking amonst Thinkadre.\n\nDrawbacks\n\nMuch less than the previous, Wuglim does things in a subtle way and therefore doesn't draw too much energy when it does so or have the potential for sudden death. The only drawback to this Ruhn is while it can effect bodies of\n\nWuglim it has a certain limit - the more desired to be effected will draw more Manok than Amoruk can handle and will do the same as Igni - Draw Manok from your Anym.\n\nCreation\n\n*Carefully drawn here is something of a 'I' but the middle seems to be broken so the two halves aren't bridged.*\n\n          Kaas\n\nKaas is another subtle of the Elemental Ruhn but can be used in big ways... Although be cautious young Thinkadre, Kaas does not take so kindly to being forced to tasks. Much like Karaad in manners Kaas is a bit more violent\n\nin it's escapades for freedom. Some more foolhardy Dwedmar have found themselves suddenly caught in a whirlwind summoned before them and it has been the death of many good Thin'kadre.\n\nBenefits\n\nKaas is a strange Ruhn, it can manipulate Kaas around it or even summon more... but it comes at a heavy risk. Kaas manipulated must be consistantly manipulated - If you were to break a Kaas rune all the Kaas\n\nit has touched will lash out violently and instantly. Do not leave these Ruhn active for long. However, Kaas can be used for long-distance travelling and barriers - it is a potent tool for combat.\n\nDrawbacks\n\nAs stated, Kaas is violent when released and summoned Kaas is hard if not impossible to control. Sudden winds inside of caverns can be deadly... or Kaas can receed entirely, leaving a foolhardy Dwed dead in\n\nthe most painful of ways. Use caution with this Ruhn young Kadre'ruhn.\n\nCreation\n\n*A diagonal U is written here, with a slight extention on the bottom of the arch.*", ""),
        StaticBook("The Last Record", "§bArtorius Thannius", "Personal Records of the Spellcrafter Archon III - Desperate Measures\nWhile it seems the Ravager was defeated and destroyed, it came at the cost of Aegis: the entire continent has crumbled into some sort of Abyss. It is more than a\n\nfracture of matter, it is a collapse of the laws of the world. Rivel failed to escape the blast radius: the city has broken up and now floats on the magical currents as we hover over the Abyss. The cataclysm has pushed us below the minimum population\n\nthe scryers say we need to survive. Up here we cannot even get food. The Lesser Caste’s survivors have mostly starved to death and with food stores dwindling we will soon follow.\nI have no choice. Tonight I will conduct the ritual of lichdom. Rivel is\n\ndead and so are its people. The remaining few will be more than enough to serve as a sacrifice for the ritual. If Rivel must die, its leader shall live on.\nGarzardiel’s name be cursed.\n- Artorius Thannius, Former Spellcrafter Archon of Rivel\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}", ""),
        StaticBook("Dragur Library", "Wretched", "The History And Tale Of The Library Of Dragur§0\n§0                 -Orithur§0\n§0-------------------§0\n§0\n§0The Library of Dragur stands today as a monument to the betterment of our world, no library may ever hope to match its vast expanse of knowledge across\n\na variety of topics from ancient history to modern magic. The library continues to grow each day, as more and more of the world's Descendants discover the blossoming hope that is the Library of Dragur. As its size grows, so does its influence and\n\npopularity. More take notice, more come to its halls, more donations are left. But, this was not always the way.§0\n§0\n§0I am the co-founder of the Library of Dragur, my name is Orithur, and this is my account of my dearest Library's journey:\n\nIt was during the early days of the Descendants arrival to Athera, that I found myself particularly lost, and without purpose. As a fallen Harbinger of the now deceased Drakaar known as Setherien, I was immediately shunned by every civilised culture upon\n\nthe landmass. Many of my kin were driven to suicide, and those that were not either went into deep hiding, or became consumed with the hatred against them, and went on blood thirsty rampages.§0\n§0\n§0This of course, did myself no aid in trying\n\nto find a new place to fit in, and a new purpose to have. §0\n§0\n§0It should be known that I served as Setherien's keeper of tomes, and before that, I existed as the book keeper within the Order of the Golden Lance. Being a librarian, has always been\n\nwithin my nature. And it was this inherent nature toward the preservation, that did draw the most unlikely of friends to my path.§0\n§0\n§0This friend, just so happened to be a Dragaar named Aedrex. For those unaware, Dragaar act, to the best of their abilities,\n\nas guardians of the Descendants. They seek to collect and preserve knowledge in order to further better the descendant races - thus this remains as our library's primary goal and focus.§0\n§0\n§0Aedrex recruited me, and showed me his very well\n\nconcealed home. It was a vast empty hall with many empty shelves. He told me, he intended to fill them, and that he would be honoured if I were to assist him on this great task.§0\n§0\n§0I could never have anticipated what this quest would mean for not only\n\nmyself, but the world. Almost immediately we began to make contact with all known nations, and began to acquire copies of books scattered across the realm. We delved deep into ruins, we scavaged through cities that had fallen to war to salvage their\n\nlibraries. These cities had of course been looted, but none thought of the value within the library.§0\n§0\n§0We began to make significant political ties, and to this day hold these ties to various nations and their leaders, which greatly assists in the\n\npreservation of the library. One such political tie was Tristen Tresery, who later became the Prince of Malinor.§0\n§0\n§0You see as our shelves expanded, the workload became too much for Aedrex and I to manage by ourselves. We recruited\n\nscholars to assist our efforts, I had even tailored a unique red uniform for them to wear which, to my deep surprise they did.§0\n§0\n§0Our numbers expanded, and thus we became the Order Of Dragur. However, we still very much remained a secretive\n\norder. Very few knew of the existence of the hidden library, located behind a complex hidden door, deep underground. We did this out of fear, our order was young and there existed dangerous forces in the world that would surely tear it all apart should we\n\nbe naive enough to reveal our knowledge to the world. It is only now that I write this that I realise how ridiculous that sentiment was, but perhaps it did do us good.§0\n§0\n§0We continued to grow. First to one hundred books, then two hundred, then three\n\nhundred. We ventured and explored the realm, plundering dungeons, recording events, filling in the gaps in history that had been lost or neglected, and we did this with such pride and such purpose. Never before had I felt a joy as pure as recording and\n\ncollecting our broken little world into books and tomes, all organised neatly upon shelves.§0\n§0\n§0Even in these early days of the order, we had already become the largest known library to ever exist throughout the history of the Descendant\n\nraces. But the title meant very little to us, and still we continued to grow and expand.§0\n§0\n§0Even deeper below the library in the library vault, we kept a large Dragaar's Lexicon, belonging of course to Aedrex. This, absorbed all the knowledge upon the\n\nshelves so that in the event that the books were lost, the library would not be. Also in this vault, we safeguarded several powerful objects. And these objects, were Dragon Eggs.§0\n§0\n§0Aedrex, upon entering our realm did so with seven Dragon Eggs that he hoped\n\nto safeguard and nurture until they may hatch. So they sat in our vault, growing slowly. Dragons are some of the most powerful and intelligent creatures in the known realms, it is no surprise that they grow very slowly, and take a significant amount of\n\ntime to hatch. But for the longest time, they were safe.§0\n§0\n§0Some time into the order's existence, Aedrex and I found ourselves in council with the Aengul of Order and Guardianship, Xan. There was a new threat to the realm, and this threat\n\nwas the Arch-Drakaar named Azdromoth. We communicated with the Greater Entity with his clerical and paladin patrons, and this was not the first time our order found itself in contact with an Aengul. §0\n§0The Aengul who called itself 'Wyrvun' made several\n\nvisits to our library for what I believe to be purely curiousity reasons. Ours was a passtime for even Gods, it would seem.§0\n§0\n§0And so it seemed that nothing could go wrong, that we could only ever continue to progress and expand. But then disaster struck.\n\n\n§0We had drawn the attention of Aenguls, we should have expected the same of Daemons. A Daemon who remains unknown in name or purpose, attacked our library with peculiar spectral entities. We defended the library valiently, and were joined by\n\nthe paladins of Xan to fend off the invaders. Alas, Aedrex could not transform into his enormous Dragon form without destroying the library.§0\n§0\n§0The battle was destructive, and took place within the library halls itself. Fires raged, pillars toppled, the\n\nroof began to cave in. The library was destroyed.§0\n§0\n§0Our biggest concerns of course, were the Dragon eggs and the Lexicon. Both were thankfully unharmed but the vault would not stand for long. Another force recognised this, and\n\nXan himself pulled the Dragon eggs out of the library and into his realm, where one of his patrons named Herun Athna informs me they still exist today. Aedrex also managed to safely transport the Lexicon to a nearby cave system, but this attack had...\n\nChanged him.§0\n§0\n§0We suspended the duty of the scholars and sent them out into the world. We closed off the entrance to the secretive library for good, and Aedrex saw that the Lexicon remained in a safe, inaccessible location. After this was done, Aedrex came\n\nto me with a heavy heart. With the attack, the threat of unknowns and the obvious threat of the Arch-Drakaar, Aedrex told me he was leaving, likely forever. Where to, he never did specify. Another land, another realm, I could not say for certain. All I\n\nknew was that he left me with the Lexicon, and a duty to see the Library of Dragur not only restored, but improved.§0\n§0\n§0And it was in Vailor, that I saw to the construction of an enormous structure that would house the knowledge stored within the giant\n\nlexicon. I met many troubles, many groups approached me with distrust, the repuation I still held with certain key political figures was the only thing allowing me to do this.§0\n§0\n§0Of course, this time the library was to exist to the public. I had a theory\n\nthat when people saw the library's potential, the inherent good within people would allow it to flourish, and they would assist it. Much to both my relief and joy, I was correct.§0\n§0\n§0I began to recruit scholars once more, and very soon the old magic\n\nof the library returned to my hollow frame. I began to feel at home once more. It could never replace the sensation of family I felt around Aedrex, but it felt good regardless.§0\n§0\n§0We began to go on expeditions, we recruited scholars as dedicated as I was to\n\ncollecting and documenting knowledge. We wrote much, we made a great number of friends, we formed new political ties. Our collection continued to grow on mostly donation alone from the comman man. I have now grown very close to the scholars working here\n\nthe longest.§0\n§0\n§0But again we are not without our troubles. Hostility has arisen between myself personally and the Holy Orders that I once served under, and once aided us. To draw their attention away from the library so that their rage may not be\n\ntaken out upon it, for some time I left the library to go in hiding. It is only recently from writing this that I make my return, believing the dust to have now settled. However I now understand that my active presence in the public eye of the library\n\nwill only ever cause chaos. I have therefore left it in the very capable hands of my most trusted Scholar, and now Librarian: Aluuvia. Or, Lyeth, as she now goes by.§0\n§0\n§0My role in the library exists in the background now, until such a time that\n\nI believe it safe for my presence on the library floor to become commonplace.§0\n§0\n§0But all of this aside, the library continues to flourish and grow. We now near close to a thousand unique books upon our shelves, and our library's reputation has grown so\n\nsignificantly. Teachers and students alike of many paths of study flock to our halls for a taste of its knowledge - we continue the work of Aedrex most efficiently.§0\n§0\n§0But I miss him dearly.§0\n§0\n§0There exist unsolved mysterious of the\n\nlibrary, such as, what was the creature that destroyed the original library, what was its goal, and will it attack again? What will Xan do with Aedrex's Dragon Eggs? Will they be returned to me, will he destroy them, or simply guard them for all eternity?\n\nAnd of course, where is Aedrex now? I gave up praying for his return some years ago. His duty upon our world was concluded, he is the reason this library stands today, it would not surprise me if I were to never see him again.§0\n§0\n§0The Library of Dragur will\n\nstand strong throughout the events, ages and realms to come. It will remain a beacon of peace and study for all for centuries more, of this I am sure. We will have further troubles ahead no doubt, but we must never forget that no matter what, it will\n\nsurely be worth it.§0\n§0\n§0- Orithur, Paladin of the Golden Lance, Harbinger of Setherien, and co-founder of the Library of Dragur.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Vision One§0\n§0\n§0~ This was sounded by the calls of a thousand children, singing in a droning monotone. There was no sight or touch- No feelings, aside from the voices singing out the poem.\n\nOrigins all but absolute,§0\n§0Weave not past my own dispute,§0\n§0Strands of fate now do entwine,§0\n§0Both mind and soul are my design,§0\n§0Safeguard not my children near, §0\n§0Mine is the vessel of love and fear.§0\n\nPity not the weight of strain,§0\n§0I do this time and time again.\n\nVision Two§0\n§0\n§0~ The second verse of the poem came in a similar tone. There was, again, no other senses but the recounting of the poem.\n\nFruits of life do beg my pardon,§0\n§0roaming astray my endless garden.§0\n§0My will sees metal turn to rust,§0\n§0and men grow tall from all but dust.§0\n§0Find me not in book nor tome,§0\n§0For I'm the breath of life and home.\n\nForge noble beasts and kings that reign,§0\n§0I do this time and time again.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Yuln - Red Headed High Elf usually seen in a white dress.§0\n§0\n§0Arelin - Black hair, Adunian.§0 Silver/Grey Eyes.§0\n§0\n§0Alera - Black robes, silver hair, High Elf, Purple Eyes.§0\n§0\n§0---->\n\nDiana - One green eye, one blue eye. Silver hair, High elf.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Change", "§bThe Old Man", "I found myself sitting there, staring into the dark pit, my heart racing and my brow covered in sweat due to the extreme conditions within the nether. I was encased in some sort of atmospheric bubble which allowed me to maneuver through the nether,\n\nunharmed by its toxic effects. As I looked around, ichor and lava encompassed the surrounding region. It was one of the most beautiful and terrifying places I have ever laid my eyes upon.\n\nThe mere presence of the area itself was breathtaking, yet belittling due to the fact that the sheer power that Iblees’ realm possessed would make any mortal feel inferior. The fortress itself was incredible and the first time\n\nI walked through it with the Undead Overlord Sprat, he showed me around its various rooms and corridors but was obviously focused on the task at hand. He led me to the spot of the pit that would soon change my life forever.\n\nWhen looked into it, I could feel myself having a doubt here and there as to what I was about to give up, life. After conversing with Sprat, I finally made my decision to serve Iblees and become a conduit of which he would use to complete his mission.\n\nI looked into the pit one last time then back to Sprat who had a stern look on his visage before nodding once, so I took a deep breath, and began my first steps forward into a new life. I took upon the name Seraphim the Damned\n\nwhich I would become known as among my fellow ‘brothers’ and mortals of the realm. This domain of Iblees was to be my new home, a safe haven for those who were beyond death… Then my chance to unleash terror on the realm of Athera began… As I look back\n\nupon that experience that occurred so long ago, I can’t help but think that if I could do it all over again, I wouldn’t change a thing.\n\n~Signed \nSeraphim the Damned, Undead of the Chaos Sect.", ""),
        StaticBook("Soul Puppetry", "Fury_Fire", "The following pages in this book cover a sparsely known Dark Magic known as Soul Puppetry. If this book is found by anyone, it is likely stolen and should be placed somewhere safe and where none will find it.\n\nSoul Puppetry, unlike common belief, is not used to control people in any way, sense, or form. There are no set list of spells, and there are drawbacks on the user. The closest control goes is forcing emotions upon people to the point that they may act\n\nupon such. Mages who practice this will notice a percentage of what they cause coming back upon them in return.\n\nA Soul Puppeteer begins by first learning how to make basic puppets, mimicking the race of the target they wish to inflict their magic upon. These puppets would begin by the molding of clay, making the basic shape of the person. Normal humanoid for\n\nhumans, larger puppets for Orcs. Kharajyr would have ears and a tail. Elves just having ears. Once the clay is shaped, the Puppeteer wraps a layer of moss around it. These signify the 'veins' of the target. After, a layer of hay is wrapped around it all\n\nto both act as the 'skin', and keep it from falling apart. The next bit is when it gets tricky though. The Puppeteer must find a way to extract the blood of the person who they wish to inflict. Such can be done however the puppeteer wants to do such.\n\nWhen the blood is got, even so much as a drop, the puppeteer would push eye sockets in and place the blood in each. They would take hold of the puppet and take a breath, holding it into their chest. They would invision the person that the puppet will be\n\ntied to, imagining him or her as the puppet, before letting the breath escape them and imagine it flowing into the puppet. The connection would be made, and the target would feel a dark presence awakening within them.\n\nThe next step would be complicated. In the end, it would also be similar to the first bit. The Puppeteer would imagine either an emotion or feeling- likely starting with something along the lines of anger. They would take a breath and hold it, imagining\n\nthe very concept of the emotion, before releasing it and willing it into the puppet, likely holding and squeezing its head whilst doing such. Holding the head allows for ease when doing mental curses. As a begginer, the Puppeteer would only make the\n\ntarget mildly annoyed, whilst recieving about half of said anger back upon themselves. Other emotions are easily practiced and learned even without the teachers guidance. Later in learning, the Puppeteer may learn how to inflict pain upon their targets as\n\nwell as horrible curses. Anything the puppeteer can think of can be done, and the more skilled they get the less drawback they recieve. Lower skilled Puppeteers would only hold the connection for twenty elven minutes. Higher skilled could hold them for\n\ndays on end, casting different curses upon someone, although only one at a time. Eventually, the Puppeteer is taught how to create new curses, which is when it is harder to tell what is happening with their victims.", ""),
        StaticBook("Pumpkin and Rat", "§bDedicant Popo Sandybanks", "Yay it was,\nThat Cerridwen would make\nsomething so sweet, 'twas\nA pumpkin! Pumpkin!\nSo orange and round\nwhoever saw it made a happy sound!\nThe pumpkin spread\nuntil one day\nCernunnos created a creature that filled\n\nthe pumpkin with dread.\nA rat! A rat!\nIn its hole it sat\nWatching the pumpkin\nHis tail thumpin'\nWaiting for his chance to strike!\nThe rat came out\nNo people around to make a shout\nand burrowed its way into the Pumpkin.\n\nThe rat ate and ate\nhis hunger unable to sate.\nThough the pumpkin was not sad.\nThe pupkin did not cry.\nThe pumpkin was actually glad!\nThough the Pumpkin dies\nThe rat lives.\nAnd through the rat\n\nMore seeds will spread\nAnd with more pumpkins\nMore rats will come, their tails thumpin'.\nAs such is the way of the Green Man and Lady,\nto bring life from death\nA balance made through equality.\nEvery living creature\n\nmust know one simple truth\nLet there be life when you cause death! \nLet the balance be held!", ""),
        StaticBook("Inspiration", "§bRagnar de Autson VI", "JUST DO IT!!!§0\n§0\n§0DON'T LET YOUR DREAMS JUST BE DREAMS!!!§0\n§0\n§0JUST.....DO§0 ITTTT!", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "§0\n§0\n§0\n§0=======================         Dissertation §0\n§0       on theoretical§0\n§0    planal differences§0\n§0     and transverses.§0\n§0=======================\n\n  The existence of alternate planes of existence is at the least a proven notion, having been that we as the descendants have travelled across different planes an indeterminate and debatable number of times in the span of life of the oldest of elves.\n\n   The exact number of times of travel is debatable largely due to the possibility of many of the different lands we have inhabited being in the same plane. The only facts that are definite to us are the following to be set forth:\n\n1) We have traversed at least once, ergo the point when fleeing Aegis via portal and onto Asulon via ship and sea. This was most definitely a transverse across planes for the simple fact that, had it not been, Iblees and his Undead would have easily\n\nbeen upon us, with or without their Nexus, for had Asulon been upon the same plane as Aegis it would have been simply a matter of tempting another set of fools to open a portal to the Nether on Asulon, as the first Prophet and Overlord did upon Aegis.\n\n2) The lands of Aegis and the lands of Athera resided in the same physical plane, at some point, though Aegis itself fell to the rampages of Aengudaemons. This was evident in the Abyss and her Shards, the remnants of the far-flung corners of Aegis,\n\nsome of which was recognizable to those old enough to remember. There were things unmistakable in the Shards as the shattered, tainted remnants of Aegis, though there was evidence they were altered after our flight from the fatherland.\n\n   Having established the fact of trasverse, and the impossibility of knowing the exact number of such travels, we have come across a conundrum. The existance of multiple other physical realms is known, but the exact number is a mystery.\n\n   The lands upon which we are capable of residing aside, it is also known the existence of planes outside of our view or knowing; Planes belonging to Aengudaemons, their springs of power, and theoretical elemental planes.\n\n   The existence of planes per aengudaemon is evident in the very existence and influence of them upon our own plane.  With the Nether as Iblees' plane, it is safe to assume the other aengudaemons are in possession of their own realms as well, visceral\n\nrepresentations of their selves and power, and the wellsprings from which it flows, on occasion into mortals whom have been brought into their service, willingly or otherwise.§0\n§0   The elemental planes are slightly more theoretical, but the evidence points\n\nto their existence as well, being realms entirely compositional of specific elemental forces. What resides and holds power over these, if anything does, is debatable, and the scholarly opinion on the matter has shifted over the centuries. While they were\n\nonce considered the dominion of spirits such as those the Shamans of the Orcs commune with, they were also once theorized to be the source of elemental evocations. §0\n§0   The Void is, debatably, not considered a plane, due in part to the name given it.", ""),
        StaticBook("Meteor Msg 1", "§bLyeth", "May the once dead be of help once more to those who need it. May the foul goals and taint of Iblees be destroyed.§0\n§0\n§0[Aelu has the Tablet with this written on it]§0\n§0*Zar has drawn a mongoose and a temple*", ""),
        StaticBook("Meteor Msg 2", "§bLyeth", "--- -- they appeared. Those bound to death --- -- saved by its purity.§0\n§0\n§0*Zar has drawn a stone temple!*", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Origins all but absolute,§0\n§0Weave not past my own dispute,§0\n§0Strands of fate now do entwine,§0\n§0Both mind and soul are my design,§0\n§0Safeguard not my children near,§0\n§0Mine is the vessel of love and fear.\n\nPity not the weight of strain,§0\n§0I do this time and time again.\n\nFruits of life do beg my pardon, roaming astray my endless garden.§0\n§0My will sees metal turn to rust, and men grow tall from all but dust.§0\n§0Find me not in book nor tome,§0\n§0For I'm the breath of life and home.\n\nForge noble beasts and kings that reign,§0\n§0I do this time and time again.", ""),
        StaticBook("To Lyeth", "§bBelegar Irongrinder", "Lyeth,\nFriend, teacher and not a very good liar. Thank you your kindness and patience with both myself and my Order. I am sorry to say this but it appears I have to make a choice now and the choice has been made. I am sorry Lyeth, but I will no longer be\n\nworking at the library. I hope that we can still maintain our friendship. Once again, thank you for saving Anastasia.\n\nSincerly, Belegar.", ""),
        StaticBook("Hubris I", "§bAluuvia", "§0\n§0\n§0\n§0=======================       The Punishment§0\n§0         for Hubris.§0\n§0=======================§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0Copied from old Archives.\n\n   Let me tell you a story of the children of Malin, Horen, Urguan and Krug; A tale from the time when God's melting pot became a cauldron. And many wonder about his beneficence.§0\n§0\n§0   A tale from when God was first questioned and his mortal children cried\n\nout in anguish.§0\n§0\n§0   Our story takes us back to the beginning, when the Four Brother's children were coming of age in this world known as Aegis. One stood out among the sons of Malin. Born beneath the shade of the canopy under the pale light of a\n\nfull moon, his father named him Zanunder. Proud and strong was his stature, Zanunder stood tall among his brothers and sisters, resembling their Father in many ways. His hair was the color of the noon-day sun and his eyes a vibrant shade of forest green.\n\n  Iblees came, then, as Zanunder grew. Corrupted his father and uncles, in the end unleashing upon the world a devastating curse. They said that Iblees had been a Daemon, a servant of God. Zanunder began to question this.\n\nIf God was omnipotent, surely he would not allow evil to exist. Nor would he do so were he kind, benevolent or even compassionate. But he was not the only being to begin to question the ways of the world. There was also her.§0\n§0She stood tall, her presence\n\nunlike anything he had laid eyes upon before. Hair black as night, lips green as ivy, but the strangest thing about her visage was surely her eyes. The whites of her eyes were a deep, steel blue, and inside they were a light blue, like the clean waters of\n\na gently flowing stream. He came to know her as Nemiisae, a kindred spirit in this world he viewed as cruel. In short time they fell in love, relishing in each other's ideals and presence. They came to the conclusion quickly that they should escape these\n\nlands, hoping that in doing so they would also be able to break free of Iblees' curse upon the lands. They retreated deep underground in order to begin their plans, for Zanunder did not wish others to know what they had planned, lest they be branded\n\nheretics.§0\n§0\n§0   Deep beneath the ground, in a large cavern, Nemiisae created a portal to a world known as the Verge. From there it would be easier for her powers to stretch across the lands, locating a fit place for her,\n\nZanunder, and their soon-to-be-abundant children to settle. It would also protect them from the scourge Iblees unleashed across the lands. Inside the Verge the two thrived, and quickly multiplied, first growing to three, then four, then six, eight, ten,\n\nand continuing on. Nearly a hundred years after they first arrived in that place, Nemiisae had finally found a suitable place to settle. However, during those hundred years, Kings had risen and fallen. The Brothers had all disappeared.\n\nKingdoms and Guilds began to rise to power throughout the lands. The curse seemed to barely affect people's lives. These events had gone unnoticed by Nemiisae and Zanunder, so wrapped up in raising their young and finding a place to flee from Iblees'\n\ncurse. Inside the Verge, Nemiisae created another portal. It reflected the green, verdant lands they planned to §0settle...but§0 not the dangers lurking within. Before they went through with their children, Zanunder stopped his love. He requested that the\n\nportal be closed behind them, as well as the portal of the Verge they had entered through. Zanunder did not wish for their children to be followed, believing that Iblees' curse may seep through the portals and into this new land. Nemiisae agree, but it\n\nwas a farce. In truth she had always planned to leave the portals open, and to leave a way to find them.§0\n§0\n§0   Nemiisae was a compassionate being, feeling love for all the races of Aegis. She believed they could all escape Iblees' curse\n\nand did not wish to deny them the chance to do so. Little did she know that in the end, all who sought her same refuge would be lead to their dooms by it. As soon as Zanunder,Nemiisae and their children stepped through the portal, they realized it had\n\nslammed shut behind them; They could no longer return to the land they came from. At first, this was not a problem to them. They were confident this land would be their paradise, the savior of their children. But their children had spent decades cut off\n\nfrom the light of the sun, while living and thriving within the Verge. They set out immediately for the thickest forest they could find, in hopes of slowly acclimating their children to the light. But this was not to be. This land was rife with dangers,\n\nof all shapes and sizes, lurking behind corners, within trees, in the skies, the oceans, the §0rivers...There§0 was no escaping it. The very least of these dangers were the skittering, venomous spiders of the caverns. Their children began dying off,\n\nfirst one by one, then two by two, then five by five, then by the droves. Finally, Nemiisae chose the least of the threats of this world and blessed her children with that creature's aspects. Her children became a mix of her blood, Zanunder's, and that of\n\nthe spiders that lurked the caverns. They migrated into these same caverns, living amongst and as the spiders did, safe from the beasts and terrors lurking on the surface. Vicious, cruel, the children shifted and changed into horrible beasts, preying\n\nand feeding upon one another.§0\n§0\n§0   Nemiisae knew she had to bless them in this way so that they may survive, yet still she grieved for their corruption. As well, no matter how hard they tried, Nemiisae and Zanunder could no longer procreate.\n\nHorrified, they began to believe it had been a trick all along. A trick by God to punish them for acting out, perhaps?! Or was it just for His own amusement?! Nemiisae demanded that they find a way to re-open the portal and escape from this cursed land.\n\nThere was no escaping the will of God or the curse of Iblees, she insisted. But at least their children would prosper and thrive in their original homeland. Zanunder refused. They argued at length, until finally he threatened to take the children and go\n\nelsewhere. He firmly believed if they travelled far enough they could survive, thrive, beat God and the §0Curse.Frustrated§0 and exhausted, Nemiisae knew she had to stop him somehow, or their children would all perish. She remembered the blessing\n\nshe had given her children and as Zanunder turned to leave, she changed her form to that of a giant Spider. She put him to sleep with her venom and wrapped him within a cocoon. Then, she bade the eldest and closest of her daughters to watch over the\n\n others, before retreating into the deep with Zanunder, to try and find a way to force open the portal and escape this horrid place.§0\n§0\n§0   What Nemiisae did not realize was that the portal had only been closed to prevent their leaving.\n\nThe portal within the Verge to this new land was still open to anyone who could discover it. And discovered it was. The Dwarves were first to uncover the portal into the Verge, as the first group to find it mined into the cavern it had been hidden in.\n\n Seeing the notes left behind by Nemiisae, they sent one dwarf ahead to try and locate the portal inside the Verge. He returned shortly, proclaiming the glory of the land he saw reflected in the portal’s visage! Mountains, gold, diamonds, as far as the\n\neye could see! It was right there, a short walk away! A world of their own, which they could run as they like, without having to share it with anyone! The Dwarves quickly made a decision to gather their families and things and venture through. Within a\n\nday, they were ready to venture to the Verge and then towards their new land. Greedily, they decided they had to prevent others from discovering this mystical, resource rich land. Using good, old fashioned Dwarven ingenuity, they collapsed the tunnel they\n\nhad accidentally discovered the portal via, then quickly passed through. They quickly found the portal inside the Verge leading to this mystical new land and pass through that as well. And again, another group had fallen for the trap. The portal was\n\nclosed behind them, so there was no place left to go but forward. They ventured into the land, ready to settle it and hoping to thrive. But within the next half of a millenia, they had all perished to the cursed land and it’s §0terrors....creatures§0 or\n\notherwise. All they left behind was skeletons, of their homes and of themselves.§0\n§0\n§0   Above ground, as the tunnel to the cavern shifted and collapsed, a huge crevice formed in the landscape. It had not been there the day before and as a human merchant\n\ngroup passed the area they began to wonder what had caused such a crevice. One of them suggested it may have been a tomb, full of riches. Eager to investigate, the Humans spent the next month digging down into the ground,\n\nfollowing the slant of the crevice and eventually discovering the cavern which housed the portal to the Verge. The Humans too spied the notes and messages Nemiisae had left behind, promising them a land free from Iblees’ curse, free for the taking!\n\nThe merchants quickly climbed back to the surface, cutting down the branches of nearby trees to cover the entrance to the land. They all quickly travelled to their homes, to fetch their families and supplies in order to settle\n\nthis new land. The came back less than a week later, descending into the Cavern, through the Verge and into the new world. But they too fell into the trap. The Portal was closed behind them and they had no place left to go but forward. They ventured out\n\nto settle these new lands with their families. But within the next half of a Millenia, the majority of them had perished to the cursed land and it’s terrors. They were not made immortal as they had hoped, but rather had even shorter lifespans than usual.\n\nThey became inbred, sick, ill, and in the end all they left behind were shadows of their former selves, a slow, stupid race incapable of complex thought and cursed by maladies and deformities. §0\n§0\n\n   After the Humans had placed tree branches to cover the entrance to the Cavern, a group of Elves ventured into that area, hearing the call of the wounded trees. They discovered the branches and removed them, wondering what foul creature had despoiled\n\nthe trees in such a way and for what cause. Into the cavern they ventured, discovering the notes from Nemiisae and the portal to the Verge. This group did not go back for more, though more would come later, as they were immediately forced through.\n\nRunning from a threat, they entered the Verge and discovered the portal to the new world. Fleeing still, they had no choice but to enter. They too fell into this trap, the portal closed behind them, no way back, though they would only find this out after\n\nreturning later to investigate. For now, they still had running to do. Immortal though they were, this land was still rife with danger. Forced to settle in it, the Elves would follow in the footsteps of their cousins, perishing within this strange land\n\nwithin half a Millenia. In these lands they were susceptible not just to the terrors, but also their own curse. Far more infertile than they were in their original lands, they died out merely due to a lack of numbers. While some of them eventually\n\ninterbred with Nemiisae’s children, those who refused would only gain numbers through other poor fools coming through the portal. In this way the Elves too, were trapped.§0\n§0\n§0   When the Elves first found the broken and disturbed trees as well\n\nas the secret they hid, they did not realize they were being hunted. Following them from Malinor had been an Orc hunting party, searching for slaves, meat, or just a bit of violent fun. The Orcs assaulted the Elves at the entrance to the crevice, and\n\nhaving no other way to go, the Elves immediately fled into the Crevice and down into the Cavern. The Orcs gave chase, of course, following them through the Verge and the next portal, into the new world. The Elves had gotten ahead, fleeing to the nearest\n\nforests they could find. In the thick cover, the Orcs lost sight of them. They began to investigate the land as their cousins had. They settled, adventured, and fought.", ""),
        StaticBook("Hubris II", "§bAluuvia", "§0\n§0\n§0\n§0=======================      The Punishment§0\n§0        For Hubris.§0\n§0            Part II§0\n§0=======================§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0Copied from old Archives.\n\n   Fighting was what they were good at and they were much better equipped for the trip into this new world, to face the terrors that haunted it. But their own aggression killed them in the end. They perished at the hands of beasts, monsters they should\n\nhave known they would never defeat, at least not in totality. Within a half of a Millenia, the Orcs too were extinct, leaving behind only the skeletal ruins of their previous homes and their old enemies they had died out battling.\n\n   In the course of the first two-hundred and fifty years, as Nemiisae searched for a way to open the portal and leave this place, Zanunder aged within his sleep, eventually dying. Bereaved of her husband she fell into a great remorse for having\n\naccidentally killed him. Her mind became clouded, muddled, perhaps even mad. Then her children came to her with reports of these strangers in their lands.  She realized then what had happened. The portal only shut on their side. It must have been an act\n\nof God, she believed. Then she realized, it was a test! As God had tested the Four Brothers, surely he was testing her and these others to become stronger. But her compassion would not allow her to let others fall victim to this trap she had unwittingly\n\nplayed a part §0in.She§0 could not reopen the portal, but she could close it from her side, sealing off both the portal to the Verge and the portal within it to the new world. Then she retreated back into her Caverns, encouraging her children to live, breath,\n\nfight, grow stronger. Punish and perish the weak and live for themselves and for her, for it was the only way to gain God’s favor. Then she left them to their own devices, her wishes for their future made clear.§0\n\n   It was in this way that hubris was punished, that the sins of those who came before was tested and they were proven guilty. Had these people physically gone to these lands, surely the curse would not have killed them all. But by passing through the\n\nportal of Hubris which Nemiisae had created, they doomed themselves and their kin. Other groups found the portals between the time Nemiisae realized what had happened and closed it off, but anyone who came through was doomed to suffer and die.\n\nThere was no hope for them as soon as they had passed through those portals into that world. But it is far enough removed that between the first settling of it and our time, no one has ever discovered it again. Beyond churning, whirling seas, deadly\n\nislands, leagues and weeks away from the lands settled by the Four Brothers, it would be impossible to reach without the assistance of a very strong force indeed.\n\n   But perhaps it is for the best, that these ruins remain ruins, and the skeletons go undisturbed, lest we awaken old evils long forgot...", ""),
        StaticBook("Dear Aluuvia", "§bGreg", "Dear Aluuvia,\n\nI didn't mean to bring you so much distress. They were behind your plant this entire time, infact I'm quite suprised you never found them. So much for all that revenge and stuff,\n\n\n\nFarewell,\nGreg.", ""),
        StaticBook("Moaning Of Life", "§bJudas Agnarsson", "§2§l§nJudas Agnarssons§0\n§2§l§nLast Memories/thoughts§0\n§2§l§n(Diary)§0\n§0\n§0This book will contain Judas Agnarssons last thoughts/memories prior to his death.§0\n§0\n§0Judas Agnarsson§0\n§0\n§01498-15____\n\n§oEntry #1§0\n§0\n§0Perhaps with this book you'll remember me by.§0\n§0\n§0I haven't really thought about life after death throughout my whole fifty five years of living; but i'd like to think i'd be reconnected with my child and father.\n\n§oEntry #2§0\n§0\n§0I have grown an accustom for an author I have discovered in the Libary Dragur.§0\n§0Arthur Caulfield: He appears to have wrote many books, all in good condition; considering he wrote these extracts in Athera.\n\nOr what I presume was Athera, as the pages have turned a musty yellow.§0\n§0I highly doubt he is living now, but I can only hope he is out there somewhere.\n\n§oEntry #3§0\n§0\n§0Many people ask about my face.§0\n§0\"But why does it look burnt?\"§0\n§0\n§0\"Have you been cooking lately?\"§0\n§0\n§0\"Urk, disgusting!\"§0\n§0\n§0\"Eww!\"\n\nAll of which are reasons why I cannot simply be bothered to explain to each person why my face is disfigured.§0\n§0\n§0However I'll make an exception this once.§0\n§0\n§0My right eye is engraved with a deep scar; it was a lacerated slash from a previous war.\n\nDuring the war I was fighting for Lord Percival Staunton, whom sadly passed away a while ago.§0\n§0\n§0My actual face however was from a hunting trip.§0\n§0I have a camp set up North East of the docks in the Cloud Temple.§0\n§0I was tired of hunting boar so I\n\nhiked a mountain in order to find a Mountain Goat.§0\n§0However as I stalked my prey, traversing the cliffs.§0\n§0I noticed a figure shoot past me, I didn't acknowledge it that much as my eyes were set upon the goat.§0\n\nAs I lined my arrow about to release, I heard a distinctive growl from behind; soon followed by a crunch of the gravel that my feet rested on.§0\n§0I felt a serrated claw pierce into my clothes, and penetrate my skin.§0\n§0My spine froze, yet I collapsed\n\nforward, unable to speak, I was lost of words.§0\n§0An agonizing pain shot up my spine as I felt it claw into my back.§0\n§0I knew if I didn't stop it, I could have been killed...§0\n§0I used the last of my vigrousness to turn onto my back.§0\n§0As I did so, I saw the face\n\nof a beast. It's breath emitted a foul, onion stench I once inhaled from Shrecks Swamp.§0\n§0I slid my hand towards my gilded scabbard, and unsheathed my hunting knife and thrusted upwards towards it's neck. My face dripped with blood as it's teeth sunk into\n\ncheeks and tore my muscle tissue, and flesh with it as it fell from my body twitching.§0\n§0\n§0I clambered to my feet with the adrenaline I had and repeatedly perforated it's fur, and flesh until I could no longer do so.§0\n§0\n\nI allowed myself to fall back onto the cliff, I lay there looking like a ruptured mess.§0\n§0I awoke to my face being wet, and ticklish. As I squinted my eyes open I was blinded almost immediatley by the sunlight.§0\n§0I awaited until my eyes could adjust; until I\n\ncould see what was §0§olicking §0me.§0\n§0I shifted my gaze towards a bearded goat, licking the blood from my face and cleansing me.§0\n§0I whispered a \"Thankyou\"§0\n§0before closing my eyes.§0\n§0It seemed like an eternity  but was a matter of hours before\n\na little boy; sharing an odd resemblance to my son.§0\n§0I was laying there in complete silence; and in a trice moment the furtive scampering footsteps emerged from the side of me.§0\n§0His eyes were a navy blue, similar to mine. His hair had a parting in the\n\nmiddle like my sons.§0\n§0His lips pouted for a breif second, and opened his mouth as if he was about to speak but remained quiet.§0\n§0\n§0He scuttled towards me, a timid grin laid upon his face.§0\n§0He kneeled next to me and gently stroked my cheek with his thumb.\n\nI groaned in response as he continued to stroke my hair. §0\n§0He whispered something in my ear, but I was too weak to hear it.§0\n§0He clambered to his feet and walked off, disappearing in the mist.\n\nMy vigourness and eagerness to get home to safety is what saved me from dying out there.\n\n§oEntry #4§0\n§0\n§0As a young boy, my father and I travelled the vast, open landscapes in search for labour work.§0\n§0\n§0Up until we met a man named Alifer Amice. He owned a ranch on a farm, which me and my father worked on harvesting crops and such\n\nfor money.§0\n§0\n§0After many years, a job opportunity came up for my father Heinrich Windsor.§0\n§0To be a personal squire to Grand King Skippy, and in return we claim a piece of land.§0\n\nWe named the piece of land House Windsor.§0\n§0Unfortunatley Skippy died shortly after he was pronounced King.§0\n§0Meaning Windsors were no longer allowed in the Dwarven Kingdom.§0\n§0We were banished from our land, and our items were reposessed.\n\nAlifer soon departed from us, never to even be heard/seen again.§0\n§0\n§0My father joined the St Amyas, but was killed for breaking the Code Of Chivarly. §0\n§0His tounge was cut off, and left to bleed to death.\n\n§oEntry #5§0\n§0\n§0§o§nFamily§0\n§0\n§0Father: Heinrich Windsor§0\n§0(Human, Highlander)§0\n§0\n§0Mother: Arsa Windsor§0\n§0(Wood Elf)§0\n§0\n§0Son: Judas Windsor (Me)§0\n§0(Human, Highlander)\n\nUncle: Edward Windsor (Human, Highlander)§0\n§0\n§0Cousin, Edwards Son: Richard Windsor (Human, Highlander)§0\n§0\n§0Cousin, Edwards Son: Joseph Windsor§0\n§0\n§0Cousin, Edwards Daughter: Chloe Windsor\n\nBrother: Heinrich Windsor Junior§0\n§0\n§0Brother: Kalf Windsor§0\n§0\n§0My brother Kalf and I changed our second name to Agnarsson due to Richard Windsor dishonouring our name.\n\n§oEntry #6§0\n§0\n§0§o§nJobs/Education§0\n§0\n§0My lifetime of work has gifted me well throughout the years. I have learnt new things and I appreciate all of them.\n\n((Started this persona at age 10))§0\n§0\n§010-14 Years of Age: Laboured Worker: Farming, Lumberjack, Fisherman.§0\n§0\n§015-18: Saint Amyas Guard of Peremont.\n\n22-24: Manager of a bathing house in the Duchy Of Courland; working under Lord Percival Staunton.§0\n§0\n§025-26: Staunton Guard§0\n§0\n§027-29: Felsen Guardsman in the Military.\n\n30-32: Squire for a Knight.§0\n§0\n§034-36: Bartender in Felsen.§0\n§0\n§037: Worked for AMC: Aumont Mercantile Company.§0\n§0\n§039-40: Owner of AMC\n\n40-47: Courland invested in AMC, and ownership given to me. Renamed to TCC (The Courlandic Company)§0\n§0\n§048: Sold the Company to Mithril Goods for 1500 minas.§0\n§0\n§050: Retired from work.\n\n55: Author of diaries/journals.§0\n§0\n\n§oEntry #7§0\n§0\n§0A rumour has been spreading around the Duchy of Courland lately about a Dragon coming to attack?§0\n§0\n§0I highly doubt this; however I remember when I was twenty five and living in Courland,\n\nI saw a Dragon fly over.§0\n§0It attacked the south guard tower, luckily we fought it off.§0\n§0\n\nGod, forgive me for I have sinned. §0\n§0I have to repent for my malicious actions I have just committed. §0\n§0\n§0Our Duke was in danger of being killed by a man named Knwell Quill, whom seeked to avenge his brother who was beheaded.\n\nI found Quill, outside the city and drove my sword into his abdomen. §0\n§0He collapsed onto the ground, a smug look on his face; before dying.§0\n§0\n§0I do not know how to repent for my sins, please guide me; for I am lost in guilt.\n\nI threw my Blade into the river, and watched it float down the stream; releasing the fresh blood, and allowing the blood to spread throughout the river.\n\n§oEntry #8§0\n§0\n§0Sometimes I wonder how a Kharajyr was created...§0\n§0\n§0Somewhere along the way, a human fucked a cat!§0\n\n§oEntry #9§0\n§0\n§0I was on my way to Felsen today, when I noticed a commotion going on the other side of the gate.§0\n§0\n§0It was quite unusual for the gates to be closed; however I assumed a robbery had previously happened.\n\nAs I peered into the other side of the gate I noticed a pack of Orcs had raided Felsen.§0\n§0\n§0Mithril Goods Trading Co.§0\n§0windows had been shattered.§0\n§0\n§0My gaze was immediatley drawn to a red ork, who held a pitch fork.\n\nthe lustre of his fierce red eyes shot straight through me.§0\n§0\n§0He had an old fellow by the scruff of his neck, and forced him onto his knees.§0\n§0\n§0The old man whimpered and sobbed, until the Red Ork drove his pitch fork into his spine, killing him almost\n\ninstantly.§0\n§0\n§0I pivoted on my heel, promptly turning to flee the scene; as I didn't want to risk my life.§0\n§0\n§0I heard cheers from the other Orks as the Red Ork held the old mans head up in the air in pride of his glory, and winnings.\n\n§oEntry #10§0\n§0\n§0This might just be my last entry, due to the book having limited pages.§0\n§0\n§0Perhaps I will write another book; fiction next time?§0\n§0\n§0I must admit this journal has taken up most of time because\n\nI have forced myself to go out and do something interesting so I can write about.§0\n§0\n§0Do not worry if I do not write for a long period of time.§0\n§0\n§0I am not §0dead....not§0 just yet.§0\n§0\n\nI don't know whether or not this will be a hobby of mine.§0\n§0\n§0Perhaps this book will bring me fame.§0\n§0\n§0If you have any more questions about my life, I suggest you find me in Courland.\n\nAt Cranfield PLC door number 3.§0\n§0\n§0I will give you advice on just about §0anything...so§0 as long as it's a logical question.§0\n§0\n§0I will hand this book into the Libary of Dragur if I can find it....§0\n\nI'm soon to be opening The Courlandic Company again, so perhaps that will make up most of the time I have left.\n\nFarewell all.\n\nSigned- Judas Agnarsson", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "The egg of a Hippogryff.§0\n§0\n§0((Signs of aging show upon the shell of the egg, however, it remains shiny and quite delicate looking.))", ""),
        StaticBook("It is. (Time)", "§bDaeris Maehr'onn", "Oh, but an unfathomable sea doth approach me.\n\nThis sea be not of coastal waves and sandy banks, but of progression.\n\nThis sea be not of ebb and flow, carrying salt to the shore, but of the tears of lifetimes past.\n\nThis sea be not of the fish and seaweed that tangle amongst your hair, but of the bodies cycling through their very mortality that brings about the end.\n\nThis sea be not of the anchored ships that lurk, but of those who cannae see past the present ahead.\n\nThis sea be not a challenge nor a help, for this sea just is. It is. And it will be until there is no more sea until there is no more me until there is no more thee.\n\nThis sea is time and it is. It is. It is.\n\n- Daeris", ""),
        StaticBook("The Tradition.", "§bAnthron", "The tradition of saying goodbye to those who have passed.§0\n§0-------------------§0\n§0Every sixty years, you say the confessions that need to be said§0\n§0to the people closest to you, then head to the top of a high mountain ridge, set up camp, and take an elven day\n\nto say goodbye to those who have passed with a prayer, and then a song, §0\n§0the song goes like this.§0\n§0((next page))\n\nI saw the light§0\n§0Fade from the sky§0\n§0On the wind I heard a sigh.§0\n§0As the snowflakes cover, my fallen brothers,§0\n§0I will say this last goodbye....§0\n§0Night is now falling§0\n§0So ends this day§0\n§0The road is now calling\n\nand I must away....§0\n§0Over hill, and under tree.§0\n§0Through lands where never light has shone.§0\n§0By silver streams that run down to the sea.§0\n§0Under cloud, beneath the stars§0\n§0Over snow and winter's morn§0\n\nI turn at last§0\n§0to paths, that lead home..§0\n§0And though where the road then takes me§0\n§0I cannot tell.§0\n§0We came all this way§0\n§0And now comes the day§0\n§0To bid you farewell....§0\n§0Many places I have been§0\n§0Many sorrows I have seen\n\nBut I don't regret, nor will I forget§0\n§0All who took that road with me.§0\n§0Night is now falling...§0\n§0So ends this day..§0\n§0The road is now calling... and I must away...§0\n§0Over hill and under tree§0\n§0through lands where never light has shone\n\nBy silver streams that run down to the sea..§0\n§0To these memories I will hold...§0\n§0With your blessing I will go..§0\n§0To turn at last§0\n§0to paths§0\n§0that lead home...\nAnd though where the road then takes me,\nI cannot tell..\n\nWe came all this way\nAnd now comes the day\nTo bid you farewell...\n(A bit of a long wait)\nI bid you all... A very fond farewell....", ""),
        StaticBook("Bread for a ?", "§bDaeris Maehr'onn", "~'~'~' Bread '~'~'~\n~'~'~' And A '~'~'~\n~'~'  Question '~'~\n\nBy:\n\nDaeris Maehr'onn\nDedicant\n\nQ: What do you think\nof the Druidic Order?\n\n1.\n\"The Druidic Order is an order that has done much for the descendants. It is an honorable Order with a rich history and righteous passion for the defense of the world's balance.\"\n\n- Heshakomeu Sohae\n\n2.\n\"I have known many among your Order and none disappoint. You are a noble Order with a purpose and cause. The realms of present and past owe you a debt. And Gi' owes me a cup of tea.\"\n\n- Hosper Blackthorn\n\n3.\n\"I think it's a bunch of fuckers who sit in rooms all day making babies.\"\n\n- Kairn Calithil\n\n4.\n\"A bunch of scum who aggrandize false gods.\"\n\n- Allynial Rosenheart\n\n5.\n\"The Druidic Order has failed in their task of maintaining balance.\"\n\nQ2: \"How come?\"\n\nA: \"Well, they just have. They're too carefree.\"\n\n- Archibald\n\n6.\n\"I think the Druidic Order has the right mindset on what's truly important.\"\n\nQ2: \"And what mindset is that?\"\n\nA: \"Protecting our home.\" She motioned to nearby nature.\n\n- Indra\n\n7. \n\"Well.. I don't really know much about them.\"\n\nQ2: \"What do you know?\"\n\nA: \"The one's I've seen seem kind, I suppose.\"\n\n- Anastasia Steelwall\n\n8.\n\"I think it's Great.\"\n\nQ2: \"How come?\"\n\nA: \"Aspects, I can't decide. I'd have to say the people. The Druids are some of the most friendly people I have met.\"\n\n- Raine Winterleaf", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "ee", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "*The journal has blood stains on the pages*§0\n§0\n§0No one must know who I am.§0\n§0\n§0Bjornfort", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Sarudunan§0\n§0\n§0Minas - 50§0\n§0\n§0Climbing on palace walls§0\n§0\n§0due date 1539\n\nName - John Adams§0\n§0\n§0Job - Mine§0\n§0\n§0Weapons - None", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "=======================Dear Owners of this establishment.§0\n§0I think that you are all doing a very good job in running the finest inn, in all of Vailor. §0\n§0Therefore you will be delivered free bread to sell at your inn almost every Elven day!§0\n§0\n§0\n\n=======================§0\n§0\n§0\n§0Keep up the wonderful work!§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0Regards§0\n§0          -N§0\n§0\n§0\n§0======================", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "6 ales", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "We need:§0\n§0Gold, Emeralds, Coal, Iron and Redstone§0\n§0-Royce§0\n§0\n§0I will get some - Carle", ""),
        StaticBook("Hymms Of Canon", "§bCarleton Cassius", "Sanctus§0\n§0 §0\n§0 §0\n§0Sanctus, Sanctus, Sanctus, Dominus Deus Sabaoth.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Pleni sunt caeli et terra gloria tua.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Hosanna in excelsis.\n\nBenedictus, qui venit in nomine Domini.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Hosanna in excelsis.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Amen.\n\nLuceat Eis Lux Invicta, Luceat Eis,§0\n§0Cum tui gratia, Sancte Domine§0\n§0Lux vestra.§0\n§0ac ostende eis via tua.§0\n§0Sanctum sanctorum viam§0\n§0Amen\n\nPater Noster,§0\n§0 §0\n§0Benedictum sit nomen tuum.§0\n§0R.Amen§0\n§0 §0\n§0Benedictum sit nomen tuum§0\n§0R.Amen§0\n§0 §0\n§0Et dabit, nobis Messiarum\n\nHoren, Owyn, Godfrey, Seigmund.§0\n§0R.Benedicatur nomen eorum§0\n§0 §0\n§0Venerunt omnes in nomine tuo,§0\n§0R.Amen, Amen, Amen.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Et expanderunt verbum tuum et pro illo rogamus, ut pro nobis orent.\n\nR.Ita faciemus.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Atque ponant omnes beatos et angelos nostre pietatis testimonium.§0\n§0R.Nos permanebunt pio modis.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Amen.§0\n§0R.Amen\n\nAttende Domine, et miserere, quia peccavimus tibi.§0\n§0Attende Domine, et miserere, quia peccavimus tibi.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Ad te Rex summe,§0\n§0oculos nostros§0\n§0sublevamus flentes:§0\n§0exaudi et exaltabitur,§0\n§0supplicantum preces.\n\nAttende Domine, et miserere, quia peccavimus tibi.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Dextera Patris,§0\n§0lapis angularis,§0\n§0via salutis,§0\n§0ianua caelestis,§0\n§0ablue nostri§0\n§0maculas delicti.\n\nttende Domine, et miserere, quia peccavimus tibi.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Rogamus, Deus,§0\n§0tuam maiestatem:§0\n§0auribus sacris§0\n§0gemitus exaudi:§0\n§0crimina nostra§0\n§0placidus indulge.\n\nAttende Domine, et miserere, quia peccavimus tibi.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Tibi fatemur§0\n§0crimina admissa:§0\n§0contrito corde§0\n§0pandimus occulta:§0\n§0tua, Redemptor,§0\n§0pietas ignoscat.\n\nAttende Domine, et miserere, quia peccavimus tibi.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Attende Domine, et miserere, quia peccavimus tibi.\n\nDa pacem, Domine, in diebus nostris§0\n§0Quia non est alius§0\n§0Qui pugnet pro nobis§0\n§0Nisi tu Deus noster.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Fiat pax in virtute tua: et abundantia in turribus tuis.\n\nDa pacem, Domine, in diebus nostris§0\n§0Quia non est alius§0\n§0Qui pugnet pro nobis§0\n§0Nisi tu Deus noster.\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nPropter fratres meos et proximos meos loquebar pacem de te:§0\n§0 §0\n§0Da pacem, Domine, in diebus nostris§0\n§0Quia non est alius§0\n§0Qui pugnet pro nobis§0\n§0Nisi tu Deus noster.\n\nPropter domum Domini Dei nostri quaesivi bona tibi.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Da pacem, Domine, in diebus nostris§0\n§0Quia non est alius§0\n§0Qui pugnet pro nobis§0\n§0Nisi tu Deus noster.\n\nRogate quae ad pacem sunt Palatinus:et abundantia diligentibus te.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Da pacem, Domine, in diebus nostris§0\n§0Quia non est alius§0\n§0Qui pugnet pro nobis§0\n§0Nisi tu Deus noster.\n\n. Gloria Deus, sicut erat in principio et nunc et semper, et in saecula saeculorum. Amen§0\n§0\n\nDominus regnavit,§0\n§0decorem indutus est:§0\n§0Indutus est Dominus fortitudinem,§0\n§0et praecinxit se.§0\n§0Etenim firmavit orbem terrae,§0\n§0qui non commovebitur.§0\n§0Parata sedes tua ex tunc;§0\n§0A saeculo tu es.\n\nElevaverunt flumina, Domine,§0\n§0Elevaverunt flumina vocem suam,§0\n§0Elevaverunt flumina fluctus suos,§0\n§0A vocibus aquarum multarum.\n\nMirabiles elationes maris;§0\n§0Mirabilis in altis Dominus.§0\n§0Testimonia tua§0\n§0credibilia facta sunt nimis;§0\n§0Domum tuam decet sanctitudo,§0\n§0Domine, in longitudinem dierum.\n\nGloria Deus,§0\n§0et Spiritui Sancto.§0\n§0Sicut erat in principio,§0\n§0et nunc, et semper,§0\n§0et in saecula saeculorum, Amen.\n\nHosanna Filio Horen,§0\n§0Benedictus qui venit§0\n§0In nomine Domini§0\n§0Rex Oren§0\n§0Hosanna in excelsis.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "The Diary Of the Felsen Mafia:§0\n§0We have recruited 3 elven women nice lookin ones to they sit here waitin for blades and its funny our smith has taken a likin to the dark elf ive taken a likin to that wood elf\n\nEntry 2:§0\n§0We have made a base the new sewer system leads to it but we have it baracaded incase the mayor breaks in but we will survive\n\nEntry 3:§0\n§0Mistrusted petty criminals time to strike back", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Mugging§0\n§0Victim:Erendiel Thoren§0\n§0\n§0Suspect: 1.MaulHawk§0\n§0           2.A Lorrian§0\n§0                Guard§0\n§0\n§0Crimes: 1.Assualt§0\n§0         2. Mugging§0\n§0         3. Possible§0\n§0            Slavery§0\n§0\n§0Witness; 1. Eirik\n\nStatement 1§0\n§0\n§0Speaker: Eirik§0\n§0\n§0Statement in another book\n\nInvestigator Logs:1§0\n§0\n§0Either our vic is lying or A Count is lying.§0\n§0\n§0At the moment i require a talk with MaulHawk to get more details.§0\n§0Could our vic really be a thief?§0\n§0And who is this Lorianne Guard?\n\nStatement 2 :Maulhwak§0\n§0\n§0Statement in seperate book\n\nInvestigation Logs: 2§0\n§0\n§0Things don't add up and Maulhawk's unwillingness to help i  suspicious", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Felsen Mafia Mystery§0\n§0\n§0Suspect : John, a female wood elf and a female dark elf and a blacksmith\n\nCrimes: Criminal orginization", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Statement 2:MaulHawk§0\n§0\n§0Maulhawk left her in an alleyway deeming her not worthy to be a throphy§0\n§0\n§0This one protected Lord Colburn, and spared the woman who tried to kill him during an unlethal brawl\n\nMaulhawk recieved no aid as. He needed none as it was a woman§0\n§0\n§0This One has no-need for pesant grade weapons and attire§0\n§0\n§0For MaulHawk is armed by his superiors and friends§0\n§0\n§0She attacked MaulHawk\n\nMaulHawk", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Entry 1§0\n§0-------------------I myself think about this mafia as a bad idea I dont think it will go well for us but why not try and give it a shot even if we could get killed for this I always have a plan. The only thing hold me back though is my parents\n\nI dont want them to come looking for me one day and see me dead or in prison because I want to make them proud but hey its not to late to go back.\n\nEntry 2§0\n§0-------------------So far so good I made a bit of money earlier but it wasnt to much. We also got a new person who's name is john I belive and I think he took a horse or 2. §0\n\nEntry 3§0\n§0-------------------Now only the 3 elf women that we let in a few days ago can be let in because §0well...there§0 women and they dont steal our things from us. Also they very pretty so who knows we might end up just going out with them if we\n\nplay out cards right.\n\nEntry 4§0\n§0-------------------", ""),
        StaticBook("Annabelles Diary", "§bAnnabelle d'Amaury", "--------------------------------------           This Book\n         Belongs To\n          Annabelle\n--------------------------------------\n\n((This is my OOC Journal, which can be found on the forums. If you have legitimate access to this item, you may use the information from the forum post.))", ""),
        StaticBook("Blind Man's Book", "§bReggie", "{\"text\":\"\"}", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "magic. I was asked about the art and I took intrest in it. I am yet to find anyone who practices in the art but I am sure if I look I can find someone. Now I am off to go and try and find one of these magic people and see what I can learn.\n\n10th of The Deep Cold, 1538-§0\n§0I have been minning resently to try and aquire the materials for enchanting. It is a hard work but I am growing stonger because of it. I have not found anyone who practices in magic yet but I continue looking.", ""),
        StaticBook("Im home!", "§bFi Ayda", "Hey Gar, Sorry about me being gone for so long (Ive been grounded recently) But im back, And iv leanred some dark news, so... Kivvy might be dead next time you see him. Shh, I didnt kill him... yet. But ya know I knew that relationship wasnt going\n\nAnywhere. Anyway, im back home. So ill see you around Gar. -Fi", ""),
        StaticBook("Letter To Fi :D", "§bGarsto 'Spark' Leaf", "Dear Fi,§0\n§0\n§0Ok, Glad to have you back. Its been... Quiet. ((OOC: Sorry to hear you were grounded. :( )) And... I trust you know what and why you are doing the thing with Kiv. Alot of things has happened, Ill tell you when I see you.\n\nSee you soon! Gar", ""),
        StaticBook("Canonism Hymms", "§bVarygian Berserker", "Sanctus, Sanctus, Sanctus, Dominus Deus Sabaoth.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Pleni sunt caeli et terra gloria tua.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Hosanna in excelsis.\n\nBenedictus, qui venit in nomine Domini.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Hosanna in excelsis.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Amen.§0\n§0____________________________\n\nLuceat Eis Lux Invicta, Luceat Eis,§0\n§0Cum tui gratia, Sancte Domine§0\n§0Lux vestra.§0\n§0ac ostende eis via tua.§0\n§0Sanctum sanctorum viam§0\n§0Amen\n\nPater Noster,§0\n§0 §0\n§0Benedictum sit nomen tuum.§0\n§0R.Amen§0\n§0 §0\n§0Benedictum sit nomen tuum§0\n§0R.Amen§0\n§0 §0\n§0Et dabit, nobis Messiarum\n\nHoren, Owyn, Godfrey, Seigmund.§0\n§0R.Benedicatur nomen eorum§0\n§0 §0\n§0Venerunt omnes in nomine tuo,§0\n§0R.Amen, Amen, Amen.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Et expanderunt verbum tuum et pro illo rogamus, ut\n\npro nobis orent.§0\n§0R.Ita faciemus.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Atque ponant omnes beatos et angelos nostre pietatis testimonium.§0\n§0R.Nos permanebunt pio modis.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Amen.§0\n§0R.Amen\n\nAttende Domine, et miserere, quia peccavimus tibi.§0\n§0Attende Domine, et miserere, quia peccavimus tibi.§0\n§0 Ad te Rex summe,§0\n§0oculos nostros§0\n§0sublevamus flentes:§0\n§0exaudi et exaltabitur,supplicantum preces.\n\nAttende Domine, et miserere, quia peccavimus tibi.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Dextera Patris,§0\n§0lapis angularis,§0\n§0via salutis,§0\n§0ianua caelestis,§0\n§0ablue nostri§0\n§0maculas delicti.\n\nAttende Domine, et miserere, quia peccavimus tibi.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Rogamus, Deus,§0\n§0tuam maiestatem:§0\n§0auribus sacris§0\n§0gemitus exaudi:§0\n§0crimina nostra§0\n§0placidus indulge.\n\nAttende Domine, et miserere, quia peccavimus tibi.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Tibi fatemur§0\n§0crimina admissa:§0\n§0contrito corde§0\n§0pandimus occulta:§0\n§0tua, Redemptor,§0\n§0pietas ignoscat.\n\nAttende Domine, et miserere, quia peccavimus tibi.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Attende Domine, et miserere, quia peccavimus tibi.§0\n§0\n\nDa pacem, Domine, in diebus nostris§0\n§0Quia non est alius§0\n§0Qui pugnet pro nobis§0\n§0Nisi tu Deus §0noster.Fiat§0 pax in virtute tua: et abundantia in turribus tuis.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Da pacem, Domine, in diebus nostris§0\n§0Quia non est alius§0\n§0Qui pugnet pro nobis§0\n§0Nisi tu Deus noster.\n\nPropter fratres meos et proximos meos loquebar pacem de te:§0\n§0 §0\n§0Da pacem, Domine, in diebus nostris§0\n§0Quia non est alius§0\n§0Qui pugnet pro nobis§0\n§0Nisi tu Deus noster.\n\nPropter domum Domini Dei nostri quaesivi bona tibi.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Da pacem, Domine, in diebus nostris§0\n§0Quia non est alius§0\n§0Qui pugnet pro nobis§0\n§0Nisi tu Deus noster.§0\n§0\n\nRogate quae ad pacem sunt Palatinus:et abundantia diligentibus te.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Da pacem, Domine, in diebus nostris§0\n§0Quia non est alius§0\n§0Qui pugnet pro nobis§0\n§0Nisi tu Deus noster.. Gloria Deus, sicut erat in principio et nunc et semper, et in saecula saeculorum. Amen\n\nDominus regnavit,§0\n§0decorem indutus est:§0\n§0Indutus est Dominus fortitudinem,§0\n§0et praecinxit se.§0\n§0Etenim firmavit orbem terrae,§0\n§0qui non commovebitur.§0\n§0Parata sedes tua ex tunc;§0\n§0A saeculo tu es.\n\nElevaverunt flumina, Domine,§0\n§0Elevaverunt flumina vocem suam,§0\n§0Elevaverunt flumina fluctus suos,§0\n§0A vocibus aquarum multarum.\n\nMirabiles elationes maris;§0\n§0Mirabilis in altis Dominus.§0\n§0Testimonia tua§0\n§0credibilia facta sunt nimis;§0\n§0Domum tuam decet sanctitudo,§0\n§0Domine, in longitudinem dierum.§0\n§0Gloria Deus,§0\n§0et Spiritui Sancto.\n\nSicut erat in principio,§0\n§0et nunc, et semper,§0\n§0et in saecula saeculorum, §0Amen.Hosanna§0 Filio Horen,§0\n§0Benedictus qui venit§0\n§0In nomine Domini§0\n§0Rex Oren§0\n§0Hosanna in excelsis.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Prophecy", "§bCeruberr Asul'Ailer", "Origins all but absolute,§0\n§0weave not past my own dispute.§0\n§0\n§0Strands of fate now do entwine§0\n§0both mind and soul are my design.§0\n§0\n§0Safeguard not my children near,§0\n§0mine is the vessel of love and fear.\n\nPity not the weight of strain,§0\n§0I do this time and time again.\n\n\n\n- Thurak'Yar\n   Farseer of the Uruk.\n \n   A mysterious\n   prophecy.", ""),
        StaticBook("A letter", "§bGarsto", "Dear Bishop Piers,§0\n§0\n§0Im travelling and so may not pay the Tax, I wrote this so you know why!§0\n§0\n§0From, Garsto.", ""),
        StaticBook("Ribbon Tied Note", "§bAnonymous", "§d[!] The letter would be neatly sealed with a lavender wax seal, a rose would be imprinted on the wax. As an additional measure, a silk ribbon of similar shade to the wax but tipped with silver would have been tied around the parchment.\n\n§dThe script within would be elegant; clearly penned by a nimble hand. The pages would lightly smell of roses.\n\n\"Dearest Ceruberr, §0\n§0\n§0I pen this now with hopes that this humble letter finds you well. Who I am matters not presently as I simply wished to take this veiled moment and confide in you the joy your presence brings me.\n\nI idolize you. Your grace, your stature, your ability. You are charming, without doubt. Your smirk is an unmistakable feature I cherish.§0\n§0\n§0Your wisdom is not lacking, you carry it with subtle pride. You carry yourself with the refinement of Purity.\n\nThe ability and class you use when displaying your magic is breathtaking.§0\n§0\n§0Need I go into your intelligence? I have rarely found myself wanting to listen to one speak, but when you do I could remain silent for hours and take in you words.\n\nWhen I look onto you I see perfection by all means we ‘Aheral stand for.§0\n§0\n§0Perhaps someday I will confide in you who I am. I simply had to get this off my chest.§0\n§0\n§0I wish you the very, very best.§0\n§0-§0\n§0Just another ‘Aheral\"", ""),
        StaticBook("Rolled Parchment", "§bAnonymous", "[!] The two parchments would be rolled neatly together, tied by a simple silk ribbon of lavender with silver tips. The handwriting would be unmistakable at this point.\n\n“Dearest Ceruberr,§0\n§0\n§0I often think that perhaps I should simply walk up to you next I see you about and tell you that it is I that have penned all of these letters. Maybe it would be well of me to do such. §0\n\nI could pen letters to you for days but the point is well known, is it not? I have nearly done so once, but as I neared I fell my courage give.\n\nI do not like to hide behind these pages when truth could be outed in just a moment’s time. Perhaps I will come to you! It seems only right.§0\n§0-§0\n§0Yours truly.”§0", ""),
        StaticBook("A left note...", "§bAnonymous", "[!] The scroll would be tied with a simple silk ribbon of lavender color, the tips would be silver. The handwriting would be crisp and clean. A certain fluidness marks it. Lightly, the scent of roses mark the pages.\n\nIn this garden that I wonder,§0\n§0\n§0Often my thoughts drift asunder,§0\n§0\n§0Tore to ribbons with worry and woe,§0\n§0\n§0My hopes often long to grow.\n\nIn the distance I spot a glimmer,§0\n§0\n§0In the light, silver it shimmers.§0\n§0\n§0Creeping nearer to this sight, §0\n§0\n§0My breath sucks in with mild delight.\n\nIt be but a blossoming rose,§0\n§0\n§0A beauty lost in this barren grove. §0\n§0\n§0 In its sight my heart does groan, §0\n§0\n§0For here it sits all alone.\n\nMany times I do return,§0\n§0\n§0In its presence my heart does yearn.§0\n§0\n§0Such beauty here and yet it is not shared,§0\n§0\n§0For me alone it seems prepared.\n\nOr perhaps I am the only one to see,§0\n§0\n§0What a charming creature you can be.§0\n§0\n§0Oh, dashing rose of silver sheen,§0\n§0\n§0The only one I have ever seen.\n\nPlease, never leave from my sight,§0\n§0\n§0You jewel amongst this dreadful blight,§0\n§0\n§0You gift of Purity and of grace,§0\n§0\n§0You lovely Mali with porcelain face.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Sealed Letter", "§bAnonymous", "[!] The envelope would be sealed with lavender wax, the image of a rose is imprinted on the wax. The handwriting of the letter within would of course be nimble and curvy;\n\nthe person penning the note had taken time and care to prepare it. The pages would smell lightly of roses.\n\n\n§0“Dearest Ceruberr,§0\n§0\n§0How I wish I could simply come to you and tell you all these things, but alas not yet. It would seem that still I must wait.\n\nIn trueness I worry what you would think if you knew who I was. Perhaps sooner than later I will come and confess in person, but for now, this must do.§0\n§0\n§0I find it hard to deny how your wondrous charm has lured me in.\n\nMy heart does flutter at the sight of you. In your presence I find my knees weak. You so easily have me hanging onto every smooth word that escapes your lips.§0\n§0\n\nI have never before been so entranced, but before had I had the honor to know such a striking and talented Mali such as yourself.§0\n§0\n§0With this letter I leave with you a gift, though humble.\n\nA rose bush raised and tended by myself, taken from my very garden. I hope it grows lush and beautiful under your care. §0\n§0\n§0Sincerely, §0\n§0Yours truly.”§0", ""),
        StaticBook("A Simple Scroll", "§bAnonymous", "[!] The scroll was tied with a silk ribbon of lavender color, the tips colored silver. §0\n§0\n§0The light scent of roses mark the parchment. The script of it is neat and nimble.\n\nSuch a wise Mali§0\n§0Intelligent and charming§0\n§0Standing out amongst the crowd§0\n§0\n§0\n§0One day I’ll confess§0\n§0Lips parting to speak the truth§0\n§0How this Mali fancies you §0\n\nWhispers in the dark§0\n§0Unseen smiles from afar§0\n§0Swift moving rumors do spread§0\n§0\n§0\n§0Sweet intent at bloom§0\n§0A desire to be nearer§0\n§0How unfair we stand apart§0", ""),
        StaticBook("Neatly Tied Note", "§bLanavia Dreathen", "[!] The scroll would be neatly rolled, tied with a lavender ribbon with silver tips. The script is well known by this point and the parchment smells lightly of roses.\n\n“Dearest Ceruberr,\n\nLike dew drops on the ever green leaves of spring flowers, you stand so absolutely dashing without even trying. Your presence brings me warmth and thus I come now to stand before you, hoping beyond hope I’ll find you in good spirits.\n\nHere I stand, offering you my affection in the openness of the day, no longer hiding behind the vail of secrecy. \n\nYours truly, \nLanavia Dreathen\"", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Dwarves:§0\n§0\n§0Apparently Dwarves have a complete disreguard for race§0\n§0and more care about §0\n§0respective Clans. There are two types of clans, elder clans and common clans. Common being newer and elder being older. The elder clans are the Grandaxes,\n\nIrehearts, Ironhearts, Goldhand, Frostbeards, Starbreakers, Ironborn, and Treebeards. The common clans are the Doomforge, Tunnelsmasher, Irongrinder, Thunderfist, Grayhammer, Thunderfist, Strongbow and Oathcast", ""),
        StaticBook("Cadence's Diary", "§bCadence Tundrak", "Diary of Cadence Tundrak~\n\n10th The Deep Cold 1528\nDear Diary,\nHello! My husband's name is Lucien Dreden Tundrak. We live in the Princedom of Fenn.\nIf anyone is reading this, this is very personal and I prefer you didn't.\n\nI want to have children. If I were to have a boy, I would name him...I don't know. And if it's a girl...I don't know! All I know is that I want to have kids!\nO, how thy wind blow,\nhow thy snow collects, stay strong, Princedom.\n\nWhat would thy do, if I never existed? Probably live on life.\n\n14th The First Seed 1529\n\nDear Diary,\nToday I feel lonely. Part of me wants to run back to Lucien and spend the day with him, but he's busy. If that were an option, I would all over it. Right now I'm in this flowery area. HUGE flowers. The size\n\nof wood elven trees! I love it, but I think a young girl used to \"live\" here. She is dead. I grieve her death. I just know she's dead. I'm not sure how I know, but I do. I also know her ghost haunts places. I grieve her. I feel as if she was around eleven\n\nwhen she died. It's so sad. \nAnyway, I'm going to rap this up so you don't have to listen to my boredom and lonelyness.\n\n~Cadence L. Tundrak\n\n10th of Malin's Welcome, 1533\n\nDear Diary,\nI'm back again! Nothing much has happened. I slapped a thousand year old dark elf who was pregnant with Aelthir's son (who died before he was born). It was fun. She was teasing me saying she\n\nslept with Maloch (Lucien). She's never\nsaid anything since. Not that I've met her again. My son, Ilius, is healthy as ever, and my husband is doing fine. As for me...I'm really not sure. To be honest, I'm not really quite so well.\n\nMaloch has died. I'm not sure of what, all I know is that he is dead. So is my soul. This'll probably be my last entry. I know Maloch wanted to see my diary...but...I didn't get a chance to show him. I wish he didn't die. I'll probably die after writing\n\nthis. Either by my own hand, or by another's. I'm going to die. Goodbye.\n\n~Cadence L. Tundrak", ""),
        StaticBook("A Note", "§bUnknown", "Hope you didn't get\ncaught up in this. If you find this, you know we're worried about you.\n\nWe need to hear from you soon, before we assume the worst.\n\n-Lothiriel", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "1539 : 15th of Snow's Maiden§0\n§0\n§0It seems as if I, Rivena Scullathar, am the last of the Sillumir. Now if indeed, you the reader, live in an age at which you have never heard of such an order, then let me briefly explain. The sillumir are the guards of\n\nthe mali'aheral, wherever they may dwell, and only act for the betterment of elvish society. In the years following the departure of Laethis Izalith, a corrupt high council elf who has intimate affairs with a frost witch, the council had a major shift in\n\npower. Taeleh Elibar'cal, leader of the Golden Owls, an organization I will explain later, took over Laethis's seat as Tilruir'mali. While some considered Taeleh to be just as bad as Laethis, he was not visibilly impure, and was oft friendly toward many.\n\nHowever, I suspect he was one of the prime instigators of the abandoment of a magnificent citadel we used to call home, in favor of moving to a swampy island called Fi'ceru. Remember those owls I spoke of earlier? Well, they are very similar to the\n\nSillumir except deeply steeped in politics. They run things in Fi'ceru.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "DEATH", ""),
        StaticBook("UFOs: A Study", "Spoduminister", "15th of Malin's Welcome, 1569. §0\n§0I saw a strange object appear floating above the sea east of the Cloud Temple. However, it disappeared as quickly as it appeared. It had an odd, amorphous shape, appeared jet black in color. I'm not sure if it\n\ntranslocated, or if it flew at speeds faster than I could observe. What I'm certain of is that I saw something.§0\n§0\n§0I waited around for some time, but it did not return.  Its location was, if looking from the central tree, up between the boat to the Mali'ame\n\nand Mali'aheral. I've decided to interview some of my acquantances, and maybe some citizens or nobles. §0\n§0\n§0The first man I interviewed had no knowledge or sightings of odd, flying objects. The second man said he saw something mysterious flying\n\nobjects, however they were skywhales. I'm unsure if these creatures are what this unidentified flying object could've been, but I was told the whales were not moving as fast as I believe the object was. The third I interrogated mentioned some giant ravens\n\nthey had seen a few years back, though I doubt that is what I am looking for. Multiple more interviews held nothing except more reports of nothing, skywhales, and a Drake. §0\n§0\n§0I have yet to come up with a clear conclusion, though it seems like the\n\nmost likely scenario would be these skywhale creatures can move faster than originally predicted, or can translocate themselves. §0\n§0\n§0Readers, I urge you to keep an eye on the sky. Van'ayla.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Reformed kingdom§0\n§0\n§0kiveck eriela §0\n§0\n§0-No arms§0\n§0\n§0residence", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "OKUMA YAZMA BİLMİYORUM.", ""),
        StaticBook("..", "§bMatthias", "I have a son... \n\nIt must of been when I was away from the Vanguard. I made a lot of bad choices back then but....a son?\n\nIt doesn't matter, what matters is that I keep composed. I must lead this town to a greater security, whatever it takes.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Eskiden... Çok eskiden... Himfea ve Eorden denilen 2 yiğit,biri elf biri insan bilekleri pek.§0\n§0Derler ki bunlardan Eorden,Rohan adındaki bir krallığın kralı,Himfea ise Orman Elflerinin lordu.§0\n§0\n§0Bu iki karındaş,elf ve insan topluluklarının birbirlerine yak-\n\nınlaşmasını sağlamış.§0\n§0Derler ki kötü ork beyine karşı bu iki dost,uzun süre dayanmış§0lar.Lakin§0.§0.§0. bu pek sürmemiş.§0\n§0Bu Himfea adındaki Elf beyinin bir adamı varmış,derler ki adı Volkan.§0\n§0Bu Volkan,kendisine bir suikastçı tutmuş,Ork\n\nBeyi Elf kasabasını kuşatmışken Himfea'yı öldürmüşler.§0\n§0Bunu duyan Eorden,§0\n§0epey hiddetlenmiş.§0\n§0Rohan askerlerini toplayıp Elflerin şeh-§0\n§0rine gitmişler.§0\n§0Elflerin şehrinde,§0\n§0Rohan askerleri isyancı elfleri öldür-§0\n§0müşler ama Volkan§0\n§0kaçmayı başarmış.\n\nRohan beyi çok üzül-§0\n§0müş §0derler.Himfea'nın§0\n§0anısına Miğferdibi adlı Rohan kalesinin yanı-§0\n§0na bir tapınak yaptı-§0\n§0rmış.Derler ki Orman Elfleri arada bir o§0\n§0tapınağa gider ve adaklarını sunarlar.§0\n§0...", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Romanen - Suspicious", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "To GOD's grace, and loyal combatants, may my memories be guarded in tee book.\n\n*Axel's handwriting would appear neat. This paper would seem to be for writing memories.*\n\nThe Stauntons§0\n§0-----------------§0\n§0Gallant and strong, I record my first memory through hardship in tee book. I remember quite well the first time joining tee Staunton Duchy, or the Duchy of Courland. 'Ave Courlandia as they say, I am still one of their men.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "The Marked Code§0\n§0-Steel for men, Aurum for monsters§0\n§0-Never to marry§0\n§0-Never to produce offspring§0\n§0-Never to ascend beyond the rank of commoner§0\n§0-To remain LOYAL to the Code§0\n§0-To remain neutral when possible§0\n\n-To never fight in a war against men unless provoked§0\n§0-To not solve with swords what may be solved with words§0\n§0-To protect the innocent and mundane§0\n§0-To persevere to the end of any hunt begun\n\n-To slay those who interfere with a hunt by any means§0\n§0-To slay those who refuse to uphold their bargains§0\n§0-To defend the lives of your brothers with your own§0\n§0-Not to become a liability\n\n-Never to choose between a greater and lesser evil", ""),
        StaticBook("Autograph", "§bBlundermore P. WindRaker", "{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n§oBlundermore P. WindRaker.§0\n§0§oX", ""),
        StaticBook("Tower, v?", "§bUnknown", "\"Captain!\" the man called out in disgust. \"Why are you here… Why do you do this? Betray your own people in the name of this monster?\" The captain scoffed, looking down at him from above. \"I have served Horen for nearly all my life. I received nothing\n\nbut a meager pittance in exchange! I risked my life on a daily basis to defend the Capital, and I am repaid with nothing. Only kind words and thoughts. I never grew in power when I was a soldier...but when I turned mine eyes towards the powers of magic,\n\nand the Mages, well...\"   The Captain laughed heartily now, throwing his head back before leaning forward to look down upon the man scornfully. \"The Mages are weak. I chose my own magic to use. I found forbidden tomes, researched and plotted, waiting\n\nfor the perfect opportunity to strike. I learned the arts well, better than any of the Guild would know. Now, I am ready to strike against you filthy humans and join in Iblees' service. You fools are too weak, too cowardly. Too afraid to deal with\n\nsomething you do not understand, or learn about something your forefathers have deemed 'forbidden.' Hah! You are just a cowardly lot who will die easily at my hands, as Horen will die at the hands of Iblees!\"", ""),
        StaticBook("Tower, v?", "§bUnknown", "The man would take these atrocious words no longer. He gripped his sword tightly, gritting his teeth , and prepared to bound up the stairs of the crippled tower, to slay the man who hid on the top of it. But before he could make a move...the\n\nCaptain, now a self-taught mage, struck him with a firey bolt of dark magic. The man was barely able to dodge it, the bolt striking his legs as he hit the ground. The New-Mage leaned down now, grinning, hoping to hear a yelp of pain, a shriek of fear,\n\na scream pleading for mercy...but nothing came. The Mage's smile slowly faded as he leaned over the edge of the tower, listening more intently. \"Dead already...?\" he asked quietly to himself, and the anwer came in the form of a quiet laugh from below.\n\nThe Mage's eyes widened as he looked down, mouth slowly taking a frown of disappointment, then anger as the laughter grew louder. \"What...what are you laughing about, knave?!\" the Mage yelled, glaring at the crumpled body of the man who\n\ndared to attack him as he slowly raised his head towards the top of the tower. He was beaten, bruised , bleeding, but he...smiled. The Mage didn't understand. Did the man not know his wounds were fatal? Did he not understand that once the Mage\n\ngrew bored of him, he would leave and kill off all his companions, then likely everyone in the city?! Did this foo lish human not understand the power he now wielded ?! Did he not...wait. A noise...from below...", ""),
        StaticBook("Tower, v?", "§bUnknown", "The Mage saw it coming too late. He was in too much of a rage to concentrate on a defensive spell, a shield, something which might protect him, save him from the blade! And he realized...it was the man's plan all along! The bastard knew he wouldn't be\n\nable to concentrate enough to save his own skin if he was in such an upset....But the man also must have known the Mage wouldn't leave his death to chance. In one last stand, the Mage muttered a quick spell, casting it u pon the man as the blade flew\n\ncloser. Just as the blade embedded itself in the Mage's chest, another firey bolt of magic hit the man, killing him in one fell stroke.\nBut as the Mage yelled and cried and screamed, clawing at the blade in his chest as he slowly died, the man made no\n\nsuch noises. He lay quietly, and as the Mage's cries drew the men back to the tower, they saw both of them dead. The Mage lay in death sprawls at the top of the ruined tower, his face twisted in an unrecognizable horror, the likes of which the men has\n\nnever seen before. The man, meanwhile, lay down at the bottom of the tower...his body burned and bleeding, but his face untouched....and on his face, a smirk, and peaceful, closed eyes.\nFrom a woodcutter who was working nearby and had\n\nhid amongst all the noise, the soldiers heard the story of what happened. They returned to the city victorious yet downtrodden, and spread the story of the man's fall to the citizens. A renewed hope was one of the gifts he gave them in his death, and a\n\nrenewed desire to regain their lost works. The humans immediately set out to the ruins of the watchtower. Even as Horen fought Iblees with his brothers, they began to rebuild the tower. Deep beneath it, the y created a large crypt. At the\n\nbottommost portion they buried the man who had given his life in ser vice of them. Yet on his tombstone reads no name...for none had thought to ask it before the fight began. Instead, a message is writ, and he is buried with the sword which he used to\n\nslay the Mage. They would have named the new Tower after the man, had they known his name. Instead, it was dubbed t he Tower of Heroes, and it was agreed silently by the Priests that anyone of substantial abilities w ho sacrificed much in service to\n\nOren or Aegis should be buried there, to honor them. A silent pact was made to ignore race or gender in these burials, though they knew very few people of other races would request their heroes to be buried there.", ""),
        StaticBook("Tower, v?", "§bUnknown", "The man scoffed, spitting on the ground in front of him. \"Leave them be?! Every person that joins Iblees increases his power. Would you sit idly by in a forge, while your enemy commissions swords with which to run you through?! They must be killed\n\nbefore they can cause any real damage. This is the least we can do for our Father, Horen, as he risks his life with his brothers to fight off Iblees! Did Horen just ignore the problem, hoping it would go away?! No! He joined the fight, and we can too,\n\nin our own way!\" The crowd thusly began to rile behind him, many of the still-able soldiers of the city gathering arms for an attack on the worshippers. At dawn they left for the ruins of the tower. Freezing cold from the sleet and hail, the men set out\n\nthrough the woods and towards the ruins of the tower. The worshippers outnumbered the men from the city, but they were weak of body and spirit. Cowards, all but one of them fled at the sight of the soldiers, running into the woods where they were chased\n\ndown to be slaughtered easily. But the one who commanded the worshippers was recognized by the leader of the soldiers, the man who riled them together, as being a former Captain of the army. He had turned traitor, leading a\n\ngroup of worshippers and sacrificing people in the name of Iblees, in the hope he would be given immortality in exchange for his actions.", ""),
        StaticBook("Tower, v?", "§bUnknown", "\"Hahaha...You...are a coward. A fool. An ignorant, idiotic man as there ever was. Ah, but no. Not a man. Not any longer...\" The man stopped for a moment, coughing, before raising his head to address t he Mage again. \"No! You are the fool! You are the\n\ncoward! I serve Iblees...I am stronger than you!\" the Mage yelled down below, once calm voice now raising to a higher pitch in angery and frustration. \"No. I fear death, yes. I fear pain, of course. But you....you fear life itself. Are you so weak that\n\nyou cannot bear to live in this world? Is that why you seek to join the undead hordes which rampage about the land at Iblees' command?  The fact that you are too weak to even live in this world?!\" the man retorted, smile still playing across his\n\nface as he examined the Mage, who now shook with rage. \"No..no...I am the strong one! I am victorious! I have all the power, and you have nothing! You will die! Your men will die! Your family, your friends, all of Horen's children will die!\" The Mage\n\nscreamed, energy welling up around him in his rage...and this time, the man's smile faded.\n\"Yes. We will die. As does everyone, eventually. Your magic is impressive, yes. It will take my life ...\" The man sat up slowly, looking down at the ground and\n\nhis battered, broken, and burned legs. \"But...\" The man looked back up at the Mage, smirking. \"My blade will take yours!\" In a single fell swoop, th e man flung his blade upwards towards the Mage. Only his legs were injured...he had lost a deal of\n\nblood, yes....even if he did live, he would never walk again... but he still had the use of his arms and chest. He flung his blade with all his might, praying to God and to Horen that it would strike true.", ""),
        StaticBook("Melunis the Mad", "§bSofetios Jayamen", "Melunis the Mad\n\n             -By Sofetios\n                  Jayamen\n\nI was travelling to a place called Geedin in search of a philosopher called Melunis. This man was said to be the wisest in the land, according to my friend who went there a while back. So I figured I would see what wisdom he could offer me.\n\nI arrived in the town and walked up to a guard. I said,\n\n“Excuse me sir, could you direct me to the man named Melunis?” The guard just laughed and said,\n\n“The only Melunis I know is a madman, you can find him over by the alley.” I thought to myself, this madman couldn’t be the one that my friend was talking about. My friend was a very smart person and wouldn’t confuse a wise man with a madman.\n\nSo to satisfy my knowing of whether or not this was the correct man, I went to the alleyway and saw an old man in rags. I said to the man.\n\n“Are you the man known as Melunis?” to which he looked at me, smiled, and said,\n\n“Indeed so! How can I help you this fine day!” The day was overcast, and cold. I couldn’t see how it was fine.\n\n“Would you happen to know my friend, James Linson?”  The man looked overjoyed at the name and said,\n\n“Why yes! I remember the lad. I do hope he is doing well.”\n\n“Well he told me you were the wisest in the land…” I said, to which Melunis replied with a frown,\n\n“Wise? No I’m not wise. I am just a ragged old man in an alleyway. Do I look like I’m Wise?”\n\n“No you don’t.” I said. “You don’t look wise at all.” The man then got a sparkle in his eyes.\n\n”Not wise at all?” said the man. “Well I must agree. You being very well off must know what it means to be wise, as you are in a far better place then I.” The man thought for a second, then said, “Tell me, what does it mean to be wise.”\n\n“Well it means that you know a lot about things.” I said, “Like a smith knows how to smith well, he is wise in smithing.”\n\n“Ah..” said the man. “ So in a sense, being wise is having knowledge.”\n\n“Yes.” I said.\n\n“ Alright, well what is knowledge.” Said Melunis. “As in how would I be able to obtain it to become more wise.” I thought for a second, then said,\n\n“Knowledge is knowing something, and obtained by learning.”\n\n“I see!” Said the man. “But… how do I know I know something.” At this I was a bit perplexed. I said,\n\n“Could you explain what you mean?” The man nodded and said,\n\n“In other words, How do you know what you know is true.”\n\n“Because you know it works when applied to things.” I said.\n\n“Alright.” Said the man. “So knowledge is what you know works when applied to things, but how does that make it true?”\n\n“What do you mean?” I said.\n\n“I mean for example, we could see an abstract painting.” Said Melunis. “You could think it’s one thing, I could think its another. Both works when applied, at least to the individual, but who is right?”\n\n“I guess we would both be right.” I said. “It would be up to the individual.”\n\n“Ah I see now!” Said the man. “So knowledge is what works with the individual. But still, how do we know it is true? Because isn’t the truth universal?”\n\n I began to get frustrated. But… Then I came to a realization.\n\n“I guess the truth is up to the person.” The man looked at me and said,\n\n“So there is no real truth? But what about common knowledge?” I thought for a second and said,\n\n“The common knowledge is what people have a common belief in.” Melunis nodded, seeming to understand.\n\n\n“But then what is the real truth?” The man said.\n\n“The real truth…” I began, “The real truth is… unknowable. Even if we got to it, people still would have different opinions. And those opinions might have more people believing in it than the real truth.”\n\nMelunis smiled at me and said,\n\n“Thank you, I think I understand now what it means to have knowledge.”\n\nSoon after this conversation, I left. Slightly confused, but more understanding.\n\n Though now I realized what my friend meant by him being the wisest in the land.\n\nThe End", ""),
        StaticBook("A Tome", "Anonymous", "Aldron Festivus was a known apprentice to Ezekiel Cunningham although his power is claimed by many to have exceeded that of his master in many ways. After the Massacre of 1183 Aldron is known to have disappeared for\n\n10 years— Though precisely where he went is entirely unknown. Some speculate it was to his master’s training grounds he returned to in order to harness the power which Ezekiel had only begun to comprehend.\n\nOn the Second of the Grand Harvest of 1194 Aldron returned to prominence where he terrorized elven and human alike— Killing far more than his master ever had. It is rumored he took upon a half- spectral form,\n\nwhere his soul was bound to a far more malevolent and powerful creature than he could ever have been alone which permitted him all manner of eldritch powers including the often sought ability to fly— A power which was\n\nmuch the envy and ire of those benevolent mages and clerics who wished to smite him.\n\nAldron is known to have been killed by a dwarven order dispatched to kill him early on in the 1210s. What happened to the\n\nthe artifact which powered him is unknown.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Tome", "Anonymous", "The World's Mysteries: Volume IX-\nPart 2\n\n-Continued from Part 1-\n\nThe purpose of the Word Altars was two fold: With the altars and the language of the\n\nelves, a druid could commune with the entire forest at once, literally knowing the thoughts and feelings of every animal and plant within their woodland domain. The second purpose was perhaps far more\n\ndangerous; through the use of their connection to the Aspects, the Word Altars and ancient elven the druids could forge, or rather in their case \"grow\", sentient plants of size and stature which would slumber deep in\n\nthe forests until a time of need.\n\nBut what of the descendants who found themselves in Aegis? Alas, it is said in ancient times that it was Malin who commanded the altars be sent to the East with a\n\nsmall contingent of elves to guard them until their deaths. It is believed that, after the first great war with Iblees, he himself feared their power should they have come under his control. Thus the elves of Aegis, over time,\n\nwould forget their tongue as it served them no purpose... and the story of the altars would be lost to time.\n\nYet the altars were not to have the longevity that the Dwarven Anvils did- they would meet their demise in\n\ntime. It was fear which caused the demise of the altars, for in the year 1200 the leader of the elves was to hear of a great temple in the West and the wrath of a being- a story foretold by those who brought the altars from\n\nMalin's land. The elves, controlled by fear and the words of Malin, vowed that the great Word Altars were too powerful to control for those who would harm their kin, and thus they were destroyed.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Tome", "Anonymous", "In the year 1183 the practitioner of the dark arts known as Ezekiel Cunningham and his apprentice, Aldron Festivus, struck and killed individuals in a settlement near Fiandria. Curiously there are reports of the mage\n\nmanipulating a dark spectral force at other attacks— Though details on this creature are unknown.\n\nLittle is known precisely of what happened on that day as there was not a sole survivor of the massacre.\n\nIn the beginning of 1183, the Order of Nine was sent to investigate the reports of the massacre. All that was found within the town were the bodies of the dead and a newly formed apparition pillar within a nearby cave. The battle\n\nto seal the pillar which ensued left Ser Rowyn Villinus, the then most powerful cleric of the order, dead. The apparition is known to have been maimed and sealed within its pillar.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Tome", "Anonymous", "The World's Mysteries: Volume IX -\nPart 1\n\nElven Word Altars\n\nFor years scholars have puzzled over the few remaining writings in elven of the ancient elves.\n\nIn ancient times the elven tongue was almost exclusively spoken by the elves and yet over time, inexplicably to scholars, the language became outlawed.\n\nWho imposed this ban or taboo on the language is\n\nunknown and it is particularly remarkable given the long lives of elves which would presumably slow the change of language tremendously. Some scholars have postulated that the words held ancient and eldritch\n\npower, yet no proof has ever come forth to prove such a notion.\n\nIn truth, elven was indeed a powerful tool for the elves- Yet only in particular circumstances. Just as the\n\ndwarves were gifted the ancient Dwarven Anvils by Aenguls, it is said that the elves were gifted Word Altars for the protection of the elves.\n\nThese powerful relics were placed in the safekeeping of the great elven\n\ndruids. With their druidic powers aided by the Aspects and the power of the ancient elven language, the druids held mastery over the most powerful relic ever known to the elves.\n\n-Continued in...\n\nThe World's Mysteries: Volume IX-\nPart 2", ""),
        StaticBook("A Poem", "Anonymous", "The Fabled Forge\n\nDeep within the dwarven mountains cold,\nMiles under ancient earth and caverns untold,\nLies the ancient dwarven smithy of wealth long lost,\nA place of\n\ndwarven dreams,\nInto where flow rivers of gold,\nThe ancient smithy an awe to behold,\nMany a dwarf lost in the caves,\nThose who found it took the secret to their graves,\nKnow sons of Urguan,\nHeed these words\n\nand take hold,\nFor one day you might find this wealth foretold.", ""),
        StaticBook("Jayas Journey C4", "§bSofetios Jayamen", "Jaya's Journey\n             -By Sofetios\n                  Jayamen\n\n((This is a book I'm actually writing in real life. I really need motivation to continue, so please let me know what you think.))\n\n    Chapter Four\n  The Power of Lin\n\nAs we set of on the path leading out from the town, Sofetios began to tell me about Lin. \n\n“To use Lin, you will need to be able to understand what it is.” Said Sofetios.\n\n“Ok then.” I said. “What is it?”\n\n“I think the better question is what isn’t it.” Said Sofetios. \n\n“Lin is like an elemental life force to which you can use to manipulate the area around you.\n\n For instance, Sofia uses Lin as a weapon, literally. She can make weapons out of her Lin and use it to add force to her blows.”\n\n“Wait so you mean that I could make a sword right now out of Lin?” I said astounded.\n\n“I don’t think you would be able to do it right away since you’re just beginning, but yes you could. I would ask Sofia to train you in that type of style since I don’t know much about it.”\n\n“Ok.” I said. “So what can you teach me?”\n\n“I can teach you another style of using Lin.” Said Sofetios. “As you saw before I levitated things around Linink’s office. But you can do much more. You can also make things into different forms.\n\n Imagine you holding clay with your minds hands and holding it from 5 feet away. Then being able to mold it to what you would like. Sofia can we stop for a second?” \n\n“Come on.” Said Sofia. “What for? We’re on a tight schedule.”\n\n“It will only take a second.” Said Sofetios as he picked up a pebble. He walked over to me and put it in my hand. “Something to start with. Try levitating it, transforming it, things like that.”\n\nAs we began walking again, I held the pebble in my hand and levitating it. I thought I saw it wiggle a bit but thought that it was just my movement walking. \n\nWe arrived at a camp site as the full moon was up.\n\nAs we set up a camp sight for the night, Sofetios walked over to me.\n\n“How is the stone coming along?” Said Sofetios\n\n“I just can’t get it to budge.” I said frustrated.\n\n Sofetios appeared to be thinking. Then he said,\n\n“I think you may be thinking about it a different way then you should for levitating. If I’m right in saying your trying to force it to, it won’t work.”\n\n“Wait why won’t force work?” I said. “I’m trying to force the stone up right?”\n\n“Force works for stuff like… well… forceful stuff.” Said Sofetios. “What you want to try to do is lifting it not with force alone, but thought.”\n\n“Thought.” I repeated.\n\n“Yes.” Said Sofetios. “Instead of the thought of trying to lift an impossible stone, just think of it as possible it might help. A lot of it is in what you believe.” There was the word again. Believe.\n\n This gave me confidence. I held out the stone in my palm and began to try to levitate it. After a moment nothing happened, and then it wiggled.\n\n“Yes!” I yelled.\n\n Just as I yelled, the stone shot into the air and then landed on the ground. “Hey Sofetios you don’t have to show off.”\n\n“That wasn’t me.” Said Sofetios. “It was you.”\n\n“I did that?” I said confused. “But all I could do was make it move a little. How could I have made it shoot in the air like that?”\n\n“Your excitement in moving the pebble while still trying to move the pebble.\n\nEmotion counts as thought when it comes to Lin.”\n\n“Hey you two.” Said a voice from behind. We turned and it was Sofia. She had a bag on her bag that seemed full of a certain type of fruit. “Dinners up.”\n\nSofetios put a metal pot on the unlit fire pit. \n\n“Hey Jaya could I get the water sphere?” Said Sofetios.\n\n“Yea, sure.” I said taking it out and giving it to him. He put it in the pot when I asked,\n\n“Who has the fire maker?” I said. Sofetios just smiled at me and said,\n\n“We don’t need one.”\n\n“But how-“I began as I saw Sofetios put his hand near the fire pit and snapped his fingers.\n\n Instantly, a small flame appeared on his thumb which he left sticking out. He started to light the fire until it started to flame. Then in moments there was good sized fire in the pit. “Doesn’t that hurt?”\n\n“Not when you know how to control it.” Said Sofetios. \n\n“O stop showing off Sofetios.” Said Sofia. I saw water starting to slowly rise in the pot. I got closer to get a better look.\n\nIt seemed at if the Water sphere was melting water but wasn’t shrinking in the slightest.\n\n“It’s like ice.” I said.\n\n“I guess you could look at it that way.” Said Sofetios as he picked up the water sphere out of the pot and gave it to me.\n\n“Come on guys, let’s eat.” Said Sofia tossing an apple my way. The apple had was red with an odd purplish tinge.\n\nI put the water sphere away then was about to take a bite when an arrow came from my right and hit the apple, sending it flying from my hand into a tree. We all sprang up and drew our weapons.\n\nSofia Looked to where the arrow came from but there was no one there. \n\n“Come out coward!” Said Sofia. There wasn’t a noise. The moon was very bright, bright enough for me to make out the shadow of a figure from behind a large rock.\n\n“There!” I said pointing to the shadow. We ran toward the rock and when we got to the side, No one was there. “Wait, what?” I looked down to where the shadow was and there was nothing there.\n\n“We’ve been tricked!” Said Sofia pointing back at the sight. “Who ever it was stole the apples!” We ran back to the camp sight and Sofetios pointed to the ground. Where he was pointing there as a piece of paper. He went down and picked it up and read,\n\n“ ‘I’m Sorry for having to have delayed your meal. I left some other fruits by one of your bags. Those apples where poisoned. Local hunters nearby here use the poison on those apples so that once an animal eats it, it dies in a couple of days.\n\nOnce the animal dies, the poison wears off. Once again I’m sorry.’ ”\n\n“Well whoever it was did leave us fruit.” Said Sofia with a smaller bag of fruit in her hands.\n\n“All there is on the bottom of the note is a big C.” Said Sofetios.\n\nSofia took out some of the fruits and I said,\n\n“Hey, those are the kind of berries that are in the woods back home.” \n\n“Well, I guess they are safe then.” Said Sofia.\n\nAfter discussing what just happened a while we ate, we agreed that I would take first watch. After a while when everyone but me was sleeping, I began to get tired so I sat on a nearby rock.\n\n When I sat down I felt something crinkly under me. I sat up and there was a piece of paper, Identical to the one we found a bit earlier except it had a different message. I read,\n\n‘If you want to learn how to use his weapons. Come see me at The Purple Café in Northenway. When you get there ask the waitress for the basement level table. I’ll be waiting there. Please do not tell your friends. I would like this to be private. C. ‘\n\nEND OF CHAPTER FOUR\n\nThank you for taking the time to read this book! I will be writing more in the future!\n\nIf you like my writing, please let me know in person or by bird!\n\n~Sofetios Jayamen\n((Sofetios))", ""),
        StaticBook("A Poem", "Anonymous", "The Crumbling Tower\n\nThe old King's daughter,\nDay by day she waits,\nHer cheeks ruddy as a rose,\nHer voice sweet as a lark,\nShe moves with a lilly's grace,\n\nBut it is there she waits,\nIn her ivory tower,\nPassing her days until her prince is to come.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Tome", "Anonymous", "The Order of Nine: A History\n\nThe Order of Nine was founded in the year 1143 by a group of humans to combat what was apparently an increase in the number of attacks by those who practiced Dark\n\nMagics. Although the reason for this increase is unknown it is known the Order was led by Ser Maximillion Vegasus and the High Priest Paul Rutherford.\n\nIn the year 1144 the Order began its formal duty and\n\npurged 27 suspected heretics and practitioners of Dark Magic in that one year. Their methods, while unorthodox appeared to yield results for this was the greatest number of dark practitioners purged in many\n\nyears— Scholars of later years have suspected this to be the result of inaccurate detection methods.\n\nThe Order is known to have come to an abrupt end on the 9th of Snows Maiden 1302 when they attempted\n\nto purge Ezekiel Cunningham— Known for at least 2 magical massacres in the first half of the decade of 1390. The Order is known to have sealed the Apparition which arose from the Massacre of 1183 which killed the\n\nCleric which undertook the task. Thus the pillar was only sealed in gold and the apparition maimed and not banished proper.\n\nAlthough he was eventually killed and sealed within his cavern home as a tomb, eight of\n\nthe order perished within, and the ninth, he who oft created wards, spoke not of that day or the location of the tomb afterwards..\n\nThe location of the final resting place of the Missing Eight remains unknown\n\nto this day. However, it is said that the ring which gave Ezekiel power still rests there to this day. It is also rumored that he who acquires the ring and brings it to the southern elven isle on the eve of a new year will gain the power\n\nof Ezekiel.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Tome", "Anonymous", "The Vermianae\n\nThe Vermianae is a behemoth of both land and and sea rumored to first have arrived in Urguan’s lands in the year 1002. It is suspected to be responsible for many of the long subterranean\n\ntunnels which span the great landmass. Those who reported it noted that it was not as large as the tunnels which it had seemingly created, leading some to believe they were forged through many trips of the creature through\n\nthe tunnels— Widening it slightly more each time.\n\nSightings were first reported by dwarven explorers in the year 1002. The last known sighting of the beast was in 1203 after a particularly\n\nbloody feeding on a small dwarven hill-town to the north. The brave dwarves of the area did rally and chase the beast, wounding it. Some suggest it retreated underground, biding its time until it could strike\n\nonce more.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Tome", "Anonymous", "Perilous Locations: Volume VII\n\nEmbermoor\n\nEmbermoor is a place where the immaterial boundary between our world and the others is at its thinnest. It was\n\nthe site of an ancient magical catastrophe and the swamp has forever been a place where magic is both strong and dangerous. Elemental spirits can take physical form, and demonic creatures skitter between the\n\ntrees. It is the only place in the world where just about any eldritch horror can appear, stalking beneath the swampy canopy. Wisps of magical energy float lazily through the air, giving the area its name.\n\nIn the centre of Embermoor is an ancient ritual site, a nexus of dark magic. Historically, it was the meeting ground for ancient practitioners of the Dark Arts. Here, they attempted to pool their insidious and powerful magics\n\nto force a Daemon to manifest so they might enslave its power for their own uses. This went catastrophically wrong: the dark mages that \"survived\" now walk Embermoor as sinister spectral creatures and twisted horrors,\n\nand it is thought what remains of the daemon is what has left Embermoor in its beyond unnatural state.\n\nIn Embermoor, ghosts find they have the ability to physically interact in a way they cannot in the rest\n\nof the land. They can touch the world, for Embermoor is not entirely part of our plane. However, it is no paradise for them: Embermoor is a very dangerous place where none should linger for long.", ""),
        StaticBook("Poetry V1", "§bSofetios Jayamen", "Poetry\n       Volume One\n\n             -By Sofetios\n                  Jayamen\n\n         Courage\n\nIt is a thing you use\nWhen you can't go to the end,\nWhen all seems lost\nAnd you can't defend.\n\nYou use it as\nIt combines with your will,\nAll you need to do is\nLet it give its fill.\n\nThe choice is yours,\nGive in or go,\nThough in the end\nOnly you will know.\n\nSee with your spirit\nAnd not with fear,\nfor then you will know\nThat courage is here.\n\n     The Dreamer\n\nThe Dreamer,\nThe one who dares not be the same,\nFor in dreams mysteries unravel,\nAnd endless space you do travel,\nAs your dreams you try to tame.\n\nAs the Dreamer,\nDreams above the sky,\nAnd looks down below,\nTo the people that show,\nWho know not what lies that high.\n\nUnawakened, the Dreamer,\nIn a maze of thoughts,\nWandering the mists,\nWith endless twists,\nAs the path on minds paper he jots.\n\nThough be wary dreamer,\nAs thoughts tend to run away,\nOnly the ones you really need,\nAnd the ones you often feed,\nAre the ones that often stay.\n\n      The Lake\n\nI wander upon a path by a lake,\nWondering where it’s all gone.\nEvery day I used to come here,\nFrom the morning to the evening's yawn.\n\nEvery now and again I wonder,\nWhy the days have passed.\nAnd think very deeply,\nWhy they don't seem to last.\n\nDay by day, night by night,\nThe Lake becomes smaller still.\nThe thoughts of it become thinner,\nEven to my strongest will.\n\nIt has become ten times smaller and ten times shallower,\nEven thought it has not changed.\nTo other people it might be normal,\nbut to me it is considered strange.\n\n   An Uncommon Sight\n\nI go about my day,\nDoing the same things as before,\nThe same old road,\nFrom my humble abode,\nNot realizing it is a bore.\n\nI walk ever onward,\nKnowing all my tasks by heart,\nA straight narrow trail,\nWith nothing new to avail,\nAll I do is my part.\n\nThen one day as I'm going past,\nA park once again,\nI see a boy,\nwithout even a toy,\nwho couldn't be past ten.\n\nThe boy was doing something abnormal,\nsomething I hadn't seen before,\nHe was looking in the sky,\nNot even blinking an eye,\nthey didn't even seem sore.\n\nI wondered if there was a hawk,\nOr something in the sky,\nI didn't hear a thing,\nOnly the birds that always sing,\nSo I did wonder why.\n\nI looked over,\nto the boy sitting by,\nHe was being bullied by his peers,\nAnd was starting to go in tears,\nJust for looking in the sky.\n\nI was about to go over,\nTo try to help if I could,\nbut the parents went first,\nBefore I feared the worst,\nTo help as they should.\n\nI walked on my way home,\nWondering why what I saw came to pass,\nI had a thought why,\nso I looked to the sky,\nand saw it in it's great mass,\n\nAs I looked to the sky,\nI was transfixed,\nI hadn't done this in a while,\nAnd it did make me smile,\nAs past and current memories mixed.\n\nI saw the vast expanse,\nIn all its mystery,\nI watched the clouds in the sky,\nAs they changed shape and passed by,\nAs they had for all of history.\n\nSuddenly someone laughed to my left,\nEven thought it was mild,\nSomeone looking at me,\nLaughing at what I appeared to be,\nJust as the others had done to the child.\n\n      Friend\n           or\n           Enemy?\n\nEverywhere I am\nI hide from your sight,\nIf it's in the day,\nAnd especially at night.\n\nI am one of sleep's worst enemies,\nAnd one of fear's best friends,\nI grow ever more larger,\nAs your will begins to bend.\n\nIn the day I am more friendly,\nEspecially when its hot and I'm in sight,\nBut when it turns to night time,\nI'm sure to give you a fright.\n\nI am a mimic of sorts,\nI try to copy you while you walk,\nI even mimic other people,\nAlthough I cannot talk.\n\nEven when you go about the day,\nI skulk and kind of stalk,\nFear or fear not, I am always here,\nI am the shadow on which you walk.\n\n       The Cobbler\n            and\n         The Crow\n\nI was working in the night,\nAs I bent over a shoe,\nI was working very hard,\nto finish before the morning dew.\n\nI was just about finished,\nWhen what was this I hear?\nA tapping, slight tapping,\nAnd it seemed to be so near.\n\nI turned to the window,\nAnd what did I see?\nA crow tapping at my glass,\nStaring at me.\n\n\"Go away.\" I said,\nSternly as I did stand.\n\"Don't you know I have work to do?\"\nThough I know it didn't understand.\n\nBut the crow kept tapping at the glass,\nAnd I began to get mad,\nThis crow just would not stop,\nNot even a tad.\n\nI turned to my unfinished shoe,\nTo work as long as I could bear,\nBut then I noticed something missing,\nThat would stop me from completing the pair.\n\nThe golden lace that I needed,\nFor this order to be complete,\nI couldn't find it anywhere,\nAs the glass the crow did beat.\n\nI checked my table, checked my pockets,\nAnd then to my surprise,\nThere was a hole in one,\nThat was just the right size.\n\nI would never finish this shoe,\nBy the morning light,\nNow things were dire,\nand my situation was quite tight.\n\n\"I must have dropped it,\nOn the way back from the store!\"\nBut it was too far I thought,\nAs my confidence tore.\n\nBut then I realized \"What's this?\"\n\"Where was the tapping I couldn't bare?\"\nI looked to the window,\nAnd the crow wasn't there.\n\nI went to the window,\nAnd what was in its place?\nIt was simple and divine,\nThe golden lace.\n\nI opened the window,\nAnd took it with care,\nBecause of who gave this gift,\nI was certainly aware.\n\nI went to my table,\nAnd finished the shoe.\nBut then thought of the crow,\nIf I only knew!\n\nI then went to bed,\nWith not much I could say,\nI only did hope,\nThe crow would come back some day.\n\n         Imprint\n\nI stand in a forest,\nOne that I know quite well,\nWhere the trees are tall,\nAnd they never do fall,\nAt least that's what I like to tell.\n\nI came here,\nWhen I was a boy,\nUsually in the day,\nBut any time I would say,\nThe forest was my favorite toy.\n\nI came here again,\nA few years ago,\nIt was the same place I knew well,\nAs far as I could tell,\nThere was nothing different to show.\n\nI open my eyes now,\nTo see the trees,\nMy eyes see an empty place,\nAs if leaving no trace,\nThough this is not what my mind sees.\n\nThank you for taking the time to read this book! I will be writing more in the future!\n\nIf you like my writing, please let me know in person or by bird!\n\n~Sofetios Jayamen\n((Sofetios))", ""),
        StaticBook("A Tome", "Anonymous", "Urguan's Hall\n\nThe ancient city once known as Urguan's Hall was the capital of the prehistoric dwarves (known to scholars as the Forgotten Dwarves) and the centre of their civilisation.\n\nDuring the Iblees War, the dwarves experienced a schism. The dwarf known as Urguan led a faction of dwarves to join up with the other races. The larger body of dwarves were distrustful of the other races and prideful of\n\ntheir own abilities. They sought their own solution to Iblees’ invasion of the world. They united their best runesmiths and technomancers and over ten years built their masterwork with which to defeat the Fallen One.\n\nThe huge device is known as The Seal, a room-sized machine capable of creating a pocket dimension. \n\nThe machine has four parts. Two colossal engines called Planeforges generate the pocket dimension.\n\nIn the centre of the room is a huge horizontal gateway into the pocket dimension. On the far size is an enormous spherical disc known as the Ethereal Magnet. With enough power, it can draw any aengudaemon that corporealises in\n\nthe city into the machine’s maw. In the corners of the room are massive arcane towers known as Soul Capacitors. They are aptly named, for the power draw of this machine is so great it requires living souls. Many, many\n\nliving souls.\n\nThe Forgotten Dwarves tested this machine extensively. The power draw to create another world is colossal, and a huge number of dwarves had to be killed and their souls harvested\n\nto power it. In the first stage, only criminals were used, but soon the dwarves were slaughtering their own people en masse. A civil war broke out as the population resisted, but the death only provided more\n\nfuel for the machine.\n\nThe architects of the Seal tested it extensively. Firstly they used one of their own people. The test proved the machine worked, but as the pocket dimension was empty of\n\neverything it proved fatal to the dwarf they tested it on too. Later, they managed to deceive a minor aengudaemon and test the machine on it too. The wrathful entity broke free: the machine was not strong enough to hold it. The few\n\nForgotten Dwarves left realised that more power was needed, more souls, and began to plan to raid the surface to bring back more sacrifices. They began building an army of golem constructs. However, the Seal machine was\n\ndamaged during the aengudaemon’s escape. Vengeful souls managed to manifest as spectral creatures, possessing the dead, the golem constructs and some of the machinery. The surviving\n\ndwarves, consisting of the machine architects, the lords and king and the few surviving military were too few to fight them and were killed. The spectral manifestations of the sacrificed dwarves decided that the Seal\n\ncould not be allowed to consume more souls. While they could influence the world, they did not possess the power to destroy the machine. Instead, they sealed the city and took the five keys to the far corners of\n\nthe world so that the city could never be opened again.", ""),
        StaticBook("Presuming", "§bAdrian de Bar", "{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIt is unwise, my lady, to\nPRESUME IN POLITICS.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Poem", "Anonymous", "The Great Serpent of Earth and Stone\n\nAn unquenchable thirst for earth and stone,\nIt sits down low beneath the soil,\nFar beyond the mortal’s reach,\nWide it may open its vicious maw,\n\nWide it may open its vicious maw,\nShaking the mountains as it moves,\nIt slithers far off and devours many,\nBeware the Vermianae, children of mine,\nFor it asks not even a name before it turns dwarven\n\nflesh to gore.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Tome", "Anonymous", "The World's Mysteries: Volume III\n\nThe orcs do not build to last the centuries. They know that they could be driven from their camps any day, and their wooden huts are designed to be\n\nboth built, repaired and torn down as quickly as possible. Having structures future civilisations can uncover, caked in dust, doesn't do THEM any good.\n\nThe orcs of ancient times were nomadic, and next to nothing remains\n\nsave for a few carvings on cave walls. Only one artifact of the ancient orcs remains, a stone henge used as a meeting ground between ancient orcish clans. \n\nWhy the henge would be built\n\nthere instead of anywhere else was unclear at first, but the significance became clear once explored. The place is a proving ground; nearby lies a deep cave, filled with traps and hostile spiders. At its deepest depth\n\nlies the cave of a great mother Scaddernak, far too big to ever leave the cave and tended to by her young. This creature must be ancient, hundreds if not thousands of years old. It isn't clear if the orcs worshipped this\n\nScaddernak before the time of Krug, sought to prevent its escape, or used the cave as a proving ground, a trial of strength and skill to descend into the cave and recover the creature's eggs, then escape alive.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Book", "Anonymous", "*You happen upon a small, crumbling book. Its faded colours look as if they were once colourful and vibrant, implying that it may have been for a child. A simple picture of a snail can be made out on its rotting cover. Within is\n\nterrible poetry.\n\nOn the inside cover, however, there's some handwriting in ink scribbled in barely legible cursive*\n\n\"I remember you loving this book when you were the size of a pickle,\n\nDamni. I hope it gives you a little smile in the coming days, despite the dreadful rhyming.\n\nMuch love, your brother, Herbert.\n\nP.S. Don't stop making your pumpking pies!\"\n\n*On the next page, the book itself begins.\n\nFar back in the days of the silliest yore,\nWhen knights would woo ladies, and fight nothing but boar,\nThere was an old wizard; a white\n\nbeard to his ankles,\nHis name were Migweed Berleuth-\nPerrydankles.\n\nOld wizened Migweed were in a slump,\nWhilst in his chair resting his wrinkly rump.\nHe were tired of all these youngin's\n\na-many,\nRushing about and bothering him a-plenty.\n\nHe wanted to set a good example,\nFor all the chil’rens against their rushful debacle.\nSo he scratched his chin, and\n\nthunk’d a bunch,\nAnd decided instead he’d have some lunch.\n\nAnd ‘lo, he sat down with a pumpkin pie,\nAnd ate his fill with a serving of lye,\nThen slowly it came from across the old table,\nA small sloshing\n\nsnail, most slow and graceful.\n\n“Aha!” Exclaimed Migweed with a grin most cunning.\n“Ye will be he to send my message a-pummeling,\nInto the mind of many a young stoat!”\nThe snail, in reply,\n\nsaid nothing of note.\n\nThe snail were small, no greater than a mere finger,\nBut on such details Migweed weren’t one to linger.\nHe enchanted his magiks into his pumpkin pies,\nThat any who\n\ndined would grow ten times their size!\n\nMigweed fed the snail a piece most fat,\nAnd the snail were grown to the size of a cat!\nBut the snail loved the taste, and was the greedy sort,\nAnd ate and ate\n\n‘til he were the size of a fort!\n\nMigweed named his fort-snail Sluthery,\nAnd rode upon his shell through miles of shrubbery.\nBut at the first town, he met the sum of his fears.\nHe had died during\n\nthe trip--it had taken ten years!\n\nNot only were size that Sluthery did gain,\nBut also did grow his sluggish snail-brain.\nAnd what did he ask the scared townsfolk within?\n“Poompkim Poie!”\n\nhe glubbled with a grin.\n\nTown after town did he demand of pie,\nAnd fatter he grew as years went by.\nUntil one day he felt a wee-bit woozy.\nSo he found a nice field, and had himself a snoozy.\n\n\nBut this snooze were nary a quiet little matter,\nHe’s yet to wake up, and not getting any fatter!\nPlants and trees grew while he was so still.\nHis shell is bright green! He has become a hill!\n\n\nSo ends the tale of Sluthery the Slothful.\nBut, sweet reader, always be mindful;\nGrow no pumpkins, those who would tempt fate,\nOr you may find him sloshing towards -your- gate!", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Yuglix's battle stories.§0\n§0\n§0\n§0Hunt #1§0\n§0------------------§0\n§0Mi was new to the Uzg,§0\n§0nub clan, nub family, nub friends. Completly clean slate. Mi was blahin with eath and some others about a clan, agh mi decided mi\n\nwould join the lurs. He blah'd mi the asht trial was the trial of strength; Mi had to prove miself on the battlefield. Ironically, just at that time, suddenly bubhosh bugs were everywhere, each at least the size of a cat. They pushed us back toward the\n\nstatue at the start of the goi. Mi ran to get a sword, returning rather quickly to hold back the monstrous beasts that threatened to take over the goi. Mi cut through the crowd, flatting tyties ((Hundreds)) of the monsters. Eventually, after many bites to\n\nthe legs, all the bubhosh bugs were flat. But there was one last, the bubhoshest and ugliest of them all- A BUG THE SIZE OF A HORSE! Everyazh charged it and swung their zultz furiously, but it would nub go down! We eventually brought the beast to its\n\nknees, and flatted it in a flurry of zultz agh ligz.§0\n§0\n§0Great battle§0\n§0-------------------It was a cool day in the Uzg, as mi bruddahz agh ziztahz gathered- prepped for waaagh. Mi was there, armored agh armed. A hozh h'ty bruddahz agh\n\nziztahz were there! We were klompen a war for rebel sharas, trying to free themselves from the reign of their oppressive shara overlords. We gathered in courland, marching toward the battlefield. Through the battlefield, people peep'd for their\n\nfriends and family amongst the sea of faces. Mi was still very new to the uzg, and barely knew anyazh. Through the haze, mi peep'd a beacon- a beautiful fe-uruk. Mi agh that fe-uruk spent the battle together, more or less. She blah'd her name was Nurena.\n\nLittle did mi gruk then, she would someday be mi mate. Throughout the battle, we ran through a fog, swinging our zultz agh various other weapons at everything we peep'd. Mi was lost in the battlefield, trekking an uphill battle. Mi counted that mi brought\n\ndown at least h'. Most everyash on our side lived through the bloody fray, due to the fact the oreners were sorely outnumbered. They could have surrendered, but they did nub. Mi came out with only a few scars, and we returned to the Uzg\n\ntriumphantly.§0\n§0\n§0\n§0Tavern Raid§0\n§0___________________§0\n§0\n§0It was a warm summer day, mi agh some bruddahz were planning on attacking a popular tavern. We seeked sacrifices for different spirits. We entered the tavern\n\nagh everyash froze. The staff of the tavern blah'd us to get out, grukking they had any blah over us. A young kitty knocked a lig, and aimed an o'lig at mi. Mi caught this, agh chased dah kitty onto the steps, right into mi friend mugath. Mi began to\n\nclose in on him, but he hurled himself over the side of the steps. Mi chased him onto a stage for performing musicians, agh he drew a zult. Our zultz clashed as we were locked in a flatly dance of steel agh sparks. Eventually, mi managed to knock him down\n\n, so mi picked up his zult agh threw it away. Mi threw him through the window, agh mi managed to knock him out. We returned to the Uzg with dub hozh sacrifices.§0\n§0\n§0\n§0Death to whitewashes!§0\n§0------------------§0\n§0There was commotion in the goi. This\n\nilluded mi, so mi decided to figure out what was going on. They explained that some whitewashes had made a camp out in the deserts of the Uzg, agh had captured mi dear friend Gholug'Braduk! So, with Futh or H' bruddahz agh ziztahz, we charged the camp.\n\nWhen we got there, there were Gakh well armored whitewashes. They peep'd fierce, but they stood nub chance against us! Mi confronted ash who wielded a spear, and we scuffled for a moment. Mi landed a single slash on dem, but mi got stabbed with a pike in\n\nthe side- sending mi tumbling right behind their leader. With the life draining from mi, mi took this opportunity to plunge mi zult into their leaders back, knocking him to the ground and putting him on the losing defensive. Unfortunately, mi was stabbed\n\nin the side again, and mi fell to the ground. Mi called out for buurztuuth, mi lur wolf, agh eventually he arrived agh picked mi up. Mi rode him over to the goblin whitewash, agh the gobo was trampled as his head was crushed like a fresh watermelon.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "How to be a hozh Uruk§0\n§0An all purpose guide by Yuglix'Lur.§0\n§0__________________§0\n§0\n§0Throughout mi travels, mi have noticed many urukz, gobos, agh ologs acting nubhozh. Mi gruk it would be hozh to have a written guide. If lat doubt latz hozhness as an uruk, then\n\nread this.§0\n§0\n§0HONOR§0\n§0------------------§0\n§0Honor is wub krug was blessed with, agh hopefully it lasts today despite a few nubhozh eggs. Honor is a complicated thing, but if lat listen to latz heart it will come easily.\n\nA hozh uruk never backs down from a klomp, except for in a few cases.§0\n§0\n§0-The person the uruk is klomping has to be able bodied and of age.§0\n§0\n§0-From mi experiences, turning down a klomp for a mate is acceptable\n\nif blah'd mate denies the klomp.§0\n§0\n§0Remember uruks, never lie! Loose lips may sink ships but fibs break ribs.§0\n§0\n§0Respect lats brothers, sisters, agh allies!§0\n§0\n§0Violence should nub be lats azht resort, but\n\nnub latz last resort either, if lat gruk wub mi mean.§0\n§0-------------------Whitewashism§0\n§0\n§0This is a bubhozh problem among uruks- misconception of the definition of whitewash.\n\nWub people gruk whitewashes are§0\n§0___________________§0\n§0\n§0-Orcs who do not instantly resort to klompen§0\n§0\n§0-Orcs who do nub flat everything.§0\n§0\n§0-Orcs who socialise (Not live) outside the Uzg\n\nwith allies (I.E in these, tiks, visiting the aethermoorian tavern)§0\n§0\n§0-Agh, of course! Uruks who hurt other Uruks.§0\n§0\n§0What whitewashes actually are§0\n§0------------------§0\n§0-Orcs who...§0\n§0-Live outside of the Uzg\n\n-Mate with non-uruks§0\n§0-Disobey honor§0\n§0-Steal from uruks§0\n§0-Hurt other uruks.§0\n§0-------------------How to treat an unruly olog.§0\n§0\n§0Ologs rarely listen to traditional reason, they only listen to grub. Lat have gakh options when dealing with\n\nrude ologs.§0\n§0\n§0Option azh- Try agh show the olog the error in their ways. This option rarely works.§0\n§0\n§0Option dub- Offer the olog grub to stop.§0\n§0\n§0Option gakh- Punish the olog by not\n\nfeeding them for a day or dub.§0\n§0\n§0\n§0The second dub are the reccomended methods.§0\n§0-------------------Choosing a clan!§0\n§0\n§0Choosing a clan is nub something to rush into like a bull charging an opponent.\n\nIt is a decision that will affect all of latz life.§0\n§0There are a few things azh must consider§0\n§0\n§0\n§0-Heritage§0\n§0Wub clans were lats popo, momo, agh other ancestors in? Family is important, agh lats ancestors would be\n\nproud if lat joined a clan they founded, helped, or simply were a part of.§0\n§0\n§0-Personal interests§0\n§0What do lat like to do? That is an important step, as lats clan may affect lats lifestyle agh career greatly. Agh wub ancesters agh immortal spirits\n\ndo lat appreciate?§0\n§0\n§0-Agh, lastly- where does lat gruk lat belong? Mi gruk that sounds silly agh cliche, but it is truly helpful.§0\n§0-------------------Buurz Mojo§0\n§0\n§0A common question pinkiez will ask urukz, is why\n\ndo we gruk their pinki mojo is nubhozh, agh it can be answered in a few short sentences.§0\n§0Krug was a hozh zhomo, who believed spirits agh shamanism were the greatest of mojo agh religion. Krug is our racial popo, agh he must be respected. A mojo that is\n\nnub his way, should be condemned.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "It appears you have taken Inaijo's life.§0\n§0\n§0If you find it in your soul to forgive Inaijo for whatever he had done to cause his death. Please tell Inaijo's little kitten, Muja and all the other Kharajyr about his fate.§0\n§0\n§0Mul'ta.   Signed Inaijo", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Diry uf Gobo,§0\n§0Mi is Gobo MieGal, Gobo Miegal is by mi Gobo selfes... MieGal wizhez far Uruk Bruddahs.§0", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Orcish Popular Tales§0\n§0=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-§0\n§0As the writer of this book, I can confirm the existence of everything listed here. Because my grandfather told me it all exists.\n\nThe Red Eyes Jabbernack§0\n§0-=-=-=-=-=-§0\n§0Thousands upon thousands of uruk farmers confirm to have seen the red eyes jabbernack staring right at them, specially the ones high on cactus green, and I swear it's real, believe me.\n\nThis jabbernack is far, far more different from other jabbernacks. His size is the size of twenty two ologs, two uruks and a goblin child.§0\n§0\n§0He has red eyes, and he takes aways the eyes of the children whom go out in the desert at midnight.\n\nOnce midnight is hit, the evil red eye jabbernack appears; far in the sands, he shall stare at you. Glowing red eyes of the size of three quarters of an olog will make even the most insane uruk to think and shake.\n\n[!] The following page has a better handwriting, like it was copied from somewhere else§0\n§0\n§0\"Za Illska Mubaram\"\n\nOnce midnight comes§0\n§0Shall the sandstorm growl...§0\n§0The comet eyes...§0\n§0Peek you from it's cowl...§0\n§0-=-§0\n§0My child, if you go...§0\n§0For the night, he won't go...§0\n§0The sun will grow...§0\n§0But the light will bestow\n\nNow close your eyes...§0\n§0So he won't stare...§0\n§0Say yes to Drelthok§0\n§0And let him take care...§0\n§0-=-=-§0\n§0But If you go...§0\n§0Shall the light go...§0\n§0Shut your eyes§0\n§0So the night will go...§0\n§0\n\nIf you ever come close to the Evil Jabbernack, he shall take your eyes and claim them to himself, his eyes are made of all the eyes of curious child he has harvested.\n\n[!] A picture has been drawn to show how the Evil Jabbernack looks like.§0\n§0\n§0\n§0https://goo.gl/6IIwtw\n\nThe Shadows of the Sands§0\n§0-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-§0\n§0The shadows of the sands. . . oh, what a fancy name...§0\n§0\n§0But it's not exactly shadows.\n\nA long, long time ago, the many orc clans split and began to clash against eachother and the Dom clan; the infamous Clan Wars.§0\n§0\n§0During that day, many and many orcs died, from both sides, bodies were left on the battlefield, generally\n\nbecause the other orcs wouldn't have time to burn the dead.§0\n§0\n§0But the dead ones - Lur or Dom - never ascended to the ancestral plane. They were stuck for some reason, bound to new tasks.\n\nWith time, they developed hate and anger towards the other orcs. Their bodies were burried by many sandstorms, but their spirits weren't, so one day, they all came together and began to writhe against eachother in spirals, fusing after it.\n\nThe result was an unstable, ugly creature, created by hate, anger and fear, they all became one, single entity. But it began to develop toughts.§0\n§0\n§0He swore. Swore to take revenge on Krug.\n\nNamed as \"Ashes\", the monstruosity opened a portal to the ancestral plane and aimed for Krug, clashing a battle that lasted two days against him.§0\n§0\n§0For his victory, Krug had to chose the future of Ashes.\n\nHe couldn't bring their spirits to the ancestral plane, this power was beyond his, even for the almighty Krug, but he gave him a purpouse.§0\n§0\n§0So he was sent, to the chain that links the ancestral plane to our plane to punish the wrong.\n\nThe whitewashes, the dark shamans and the those whom tried to somehow get on the ancestral plane to disturb the spirits was always met by him.§0\n§0\n§0When they got to Ashes, the horrendous creature raised his mace to pulverize them and claiming their souls\n\nfor himself.§0\n§0\n§0[!] Pixel Art comming soon!\n\nThe Night Light§0\n§0-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-§0\n§0The night lights are certainly the most uncommon yet most beautiful things an orc can see. But it is reserved to an orc only, and he might never see it again on his life. Maybe two, maybe none.\n\nRegardless of being the most liked Rex, the most violent uruk, the best shaman of the entire history, they may possibly never see this beautiful, dancing light. It's so beautiful that it signs for you. Truly heartbreaking.\n\nWhen the bond between two uruks are so deep and so strong that it's unbreakable. When two orcs trust eachother so much is when one can see this. But the saddest part is that it's when someone dies, and the dead won't even be able to see it.\n\nNight light doesn't refers itself to night, but to the dusk or night of your ultimately most beloved one. Once he dies and if he ascends to the ancestral plane, he'll be allowed to grant a single wish to Drelthok for their most beloved one.\n\nThen, from it's deepest desires, Drelthok shall weave a misty line to connect it to the other orc once his state ascends to Drelthok's care; in other words; sleeps. The light will shine, vibrant and strong with the emotions of the deceased one flows\n\nto the other orc, making it some sort of spetacular.§0\n§0\n§0When the orc is sleeping, everything goes black on his dream, then he sees himself, watching the  night skies with the one he loved. There'll be a pile of burning green between them and\n\nthe tales or histories the deceased one, it's a beautiful moment. The peace's absolute and the moment is remarkable. Once the talking ends, the orcs will klomp. Klomp to teach the other orc a lesson or to be teached a lesson.\n\nOnce they finish the klomp, the deceased orc begins to vanish into blue or yellow dust, ascending to the skies, where he becomes a new, beautiful shining star and watches us all during the night. And when you look at that shiny star, you'll know it's him.\n\nHim, the man, or her, the woman.\n\nThe Ever-Growing Scaddernack.§0\n§0-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-§0\n§0It sounds and it is insane. A race of scaddernacks that never stop growing.§0\n§0\n§0Blessed by Freygoth himself, there was one small scaddernack that always wanted to be big.\n\nSo one night, when the scaddernack was sleeping, Freygoth made a link to our plane by walking on a bridge weaven by Drelthok and gave the little scaddernack a blessing.§0\n§0\n§0And when it woke up, he was feeling bigger, so he went outside\n\nand hunted as much as he could, returning with it's belly full to her mother scaddernack. The once little scorpion was already about the size of his mother. So he slept, and he woke up again, much more bigger, so he went, back to the deserts.\n\nHe was huge, enormous, so he devasted everything he saw, but, at the end of the day, he wasn't satisfied, so on that night, he attacked an entire clan, killing every single member that once lived on the fort, making it's name forgotten, and now, lost.\n\nSo he returned. And slept after eating all these uruks. He was twice the size of his mother. But, when he woke up, he wasn't yet satisfied, so he killed and had commited cannibalistic acts with every scaddernack on that cave, including the eggs.\n\nSo then, he left the cave, still hungry, he went to the Lur fort and broke the walls, going for Lurgoi pit, where they clashed the mightiest combat of titans of the history. In the end, being smaller but more experienced and quickier, Lurgoi won the fight\n\nby quickly going to sprint towards it's flank and sting the large scaddernack's rib, incapacitating him. To thank Lurgoi, the orcs on the next day gave him one hundred elves to feast upon as a reward for it's bravery.", ""),
